== CHAPTER 2: The Five-Fingered Hand and Bundles of Ten
<chapter-2-the-five-fingered-hand-and-bundles-of-ten>
#quote_box[
#emph[“If we had had six fingers on each hand like creatures in fairy
tales, the entire world would count in a completely different way. Human
mathematics was born at the tips of our ten fingers.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-1>
- Why almost the entire planet counts in bundles of #strong[10] (the
  decimal place-value system).
- Civilizations that also counted with their toes in bundles of
  #strong[20] (vigesimal systems).
- The magic trade: exchanging 10 small pebbles for 1 cork marker or
  white seashell (the #strong[ten]).
- How to construct #strong[hundreds] and #strong[thousands] using the
  egg carton model.
- The secret of #strong[place value]: why the 1 in 14 does not mean the
  same thing at all as the 1 in 41!
- The indispensable role of #strong[zero] to hold the empty column (the
  difference between 45 and 405).
- Walking along the number line from 0 to 100, and comparing numbers
  using the signs #strong[$<$] and #strong[$>$].

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: A Pouch That Weighs a Ton
<the-problem-a-pouch-that-weighs-a-ton>
The flock of sheep has flourished across the seasons. New lambs were
born in spring, and other shepherds have joined your settlement. This
morning, you no longer have just seven or eight sheep to lead out.

You have #strong[forty-five]!

You open the gate and begin your item-by-item pairing with pebbles in
your leather pouch: #emph[Plop, plop, plop…] By the fortieth sheep, your
bag is heavy as a paving stone. It bumps against your knee, pulls down
on your belt, and threatens to tear in two!

You then try replacing the pebbles with notches carved onto a wooden
tally stick, like ancestral hunters:

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_02_baton_45_traits.svg", alt: "Le bâton d’entailles : saturation cognitive des 45 traits continus"),
  caption: [
    Le bâton d'entailles : saturation cognitive des 45 traits continus
  ]
)

Look at this long, unbroken row of notches. Can you tell at a single
glance whether there are 44, 45, or 46 marks? It is impossible! Your
eyes blur, the lines seem to swim, and if a fly distracts you while you
are pointing your finger, you are forced to start all over again from
the very first mark.

To count large quantities without carrying a mountain of stones or
straining your eyes, a new trick had to be invented. And that trick you
have carried with you from the very first day of your life.

#divider()

=== 2. The Secret at the Ends of Your Arms: The Ten
<the-secret-at-the-ends-of-your-arms-the-ten>
Hold up both hands open before your eyes. Look at your fingers:

- On your left hand: 5 fingers (thumb, index, middle, ring, pinky).
- On your right hand: 5 fingers.
- Together: $5 + 5 = 10$ fingers!

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_02_deux_mains_dix.svg", alt: "Les deux mains ouvertes : 5 + 5 = 10, fondement de la base décimale"),
  caption: [
    Les deux mains ouvertes : 5 + 5 = 10, fondement de la base décimale
  ]
)

One morning, watching the sheep stream past, a young shepherd has an
epiphany. Along the trail, he has gathered ordinary small gray pebbles,
but in his pocket, he carries several lightweight cork stoppers carved
from the bark of an oak tree (or smooth white sea shells).

He invents a brilliant rule of exchange: 1. Each time an animal crosses
the gate, he raises a finger: one, two, three, four, five, six, seven,
eight, nine, #strong[ten]! 2. All ten fingers are raised. His hands are
full! He has no finger left for the eleventh sheep. 3. So, he drops all
ten fingers at once. 4. And instead of keeping 10 heavy pebbles in his
bag, he makes a trade: he places the 10 small pebbles into a ground
basket, and #strong[slips 1 single cork stopper into his pocket]!

This cork stopper is no ordinary pebble. It possesses a superpower: on
its own, it whispers: #emph[“I am worth ten small pebbles. I am worth
ten sheep!”]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
10 small pebbles = 10 units / ones)
Magic trade!)
🥫 ]         (1 cork stopper = 1 ten)
    ```
  ]
]

The shepherd can now continue with free hands:

- Ten more sheep pass? A second cork stopper into the pocket.
- Ten more? A third cork stopper.
- Ten more? A fourth cork stopper.
- And for the final five sheep bringing up the rear? There are not ten
  of them; his hands are not full: he simply holds #strong[5 small gray
  pebbles] in his palm.

When all 45 sheep have crossed the threshold, what does the shepherd
have in his pocket?

- Only #strong[4 cork stoppers] (worth $4 times 10 = 40$),
- And #strong[5 small gray pebbles] (worth 5).

In total: 9 small, featherlight items instead of 45 heavy stones that
were bruising his hips!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Unit (One) and Ten
]


- A #strong[unit] (or #strong[one]) is an isolated, individual item (1
  pebble, 1 marble, 1 finger).
- A #strong[ten] is a closed bundle of #strong[10 units] grouped
  together ($1 upright(" ten") = 10 upright(" ones")$).
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? --- The 10-Egg Carton
]
 In your kitchen, look at
a standard cardboard egg carton. It contains two rows of 5 compartments,
exactly 10 spaces. One full, closed carton is #strong[1 ten of eggs]. If
you have 3 full cartons and 4 loose eggs on the counter, you know
instantly that you have 34 eggs, without having to count them one by
one!
]

#divider()

=== 3. Civilizations That Counted with Their Toes: Base 20
<civilizations-that-counted-with-their-toes-base-20>
Not all civilizations across history stopped at 10 fingers.

In the warm climates of Mesoamerica thousands of years ago, the
#strong[Mayas] and #strong[Aztecs] walked barefoot year-round. To count,
they used their 10 fingers… plus their #strong[10 toes]!

They therefore counted in bundles of #strong[20]: this is known as a
#strong[vigesimal system] (from the Latin #emph[vicesimus], twentieth).

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True Story --- Why Does French Say “Quatre-vingts”?
]
 Ancient
Celts and Gauls also counted in bundles of twenty! That is why in
France, instead of a single word for eighty, people still say:
$ upright("“quatre-vingts”") = 4 upright(" twenties") = 20 + 20 + 20 + 20 = 80 $
And for 90, they say “quatre-vingt-dix” ($4 times 20 + 10$). Languages
preserve living traces of those barefoot ancient hunter-gatherers!
]

#divider()

=== 4. The Grand Hierarchy: Tens, Hundreds, and Thousands
<the-grand-hierarchy-tens-hundreds-and-thousands>
What happens when a flock becomes enormous? Suppose you have collected
ten cork stoppers in your pouch:
$ 10 upright(" cork stoppers") = 10 upright(" tens") = 100 upright(" sheep!") $

Your pocket of stoppers is now full. What do you do? You invent another
trade:

- You exchange your #strong[10 cork stoppers] for #strong[1 golden
  pinecone]!
- This pinecone is called #strong[one hundred]. It represents 100 sheep.

And if your settlement one day gathers ten golden pinecones?
$ 10 upright(" hundreds") = 1 thin 000 upright(" sheep!") $ You exchange
them for #strong[1 large painted river stone]: this is #strong[one
thousand].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
1 gray pebble      = 1 unit (one)
1 cork stopper     = 1 ten       = 10 units
1 golden pinecone  = 1 hundred   = 10 tens      = 100 units
1 painted stone    = 1 thousand  = 10 hundreds  = 1 000 units
    ```
  ]
]

Every time you accumulate 10 items of one level, you bundle them into a
single item of the next level! This is the core of our #strong[decimal
place-value system] (from the Latin #emph[decem], meaning ten).

#divider()

=== 5. Place Value: Position Dictates Power!
<place-value-position-dictates-power>
To write these large quantities using only our digits 0 through 9
without drawing pinecones or corks all day long, ancient mathematicians
discovered a master principle: #strong[place value].

Look carefully at these two numbers written with the exact same digits,
1 and 4:

$ upright(bold(14)) #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) upright(bold(41)) $

Are they the same? Absolutely not!

- In #strong[14]:
  - The rightmost digit (#strong[4]) indicates loose units: 4 single
    pebbles.

  - The leftmost digit (#strong[1]) indicates tens: 1 bundle of 10.

  - In total: $10 + 4 = 14$.
- In #strong[41]:
  - The rightmost digit (#strong[1]) indicates 1 small single pebble.

  - The leftmost digit (#strong[4]) indicates 4 tens, that is, 4 bundles
    of 10 ($10 + 10 + 10 + 10 = 40$)!

  - In total: $40 + 1 = 41$.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Place-Value Chart:
Tens       Units
bundles)   (singles)
1           4        = 10 + 4 = 14
4           1        = 40 + 1 = 41
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Golden Rule --- Place Value
]
 In mathematics, the true value
of a digit depends entirely upon the #strong[place] (the column) it
occupies within the number:

- Furthest to the right: the #strong[ones] (units) column ($u$).

- Immediately to the left: the #strong[tens] column ($t$).

- Further to the left: the #strong[hundreds] column ($h$).

- Further still: the #strong[thousands] column ($t h$).
]

#divider()

=== 6. The Invisible Guardian: Zero Holding the Empty Place
<the-invisible-guardian-zero-holding-the-empty-place>
Imagine you have in your collection box:

- #strong[4 golden pinecones] (4 hundreds = 400),
- #strong[0 cork stoppers] (no tens at all),
- #strong[5 gray pebbles] (5 ones).

How do you write this number? If you simply write the 4 and the 5 side
by side: #strong[45], anyone reading your note will interpret it as 4
tens and 5 ones, meaning 45 sheep! Four hundred sheep have vanished into
thin air!

To ensure that the 4 remains anchored in the hundreds column, we must
station a sentry in the empty tens column: #strong[the digit zero (0)].

$ 405 = 4 upright(" hundreds, ") 0 upright(" tens, and ") 5 upright(" ones") $

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Hundreds     Tens       Units
4          0          5       ---> 405
Zero prevents the 4 and 5 from collapsing together!
    ```
  ]
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? --- The Invention of Zero
]
 Zero is one of the
greatest conceptual breakthroughs in human history! It was perfected by
mathematicians in #strong[India] around 1,500 years ago. Its Sanskrit
name was #emph[shunya], meaning “void” or “empty.” It traveled into the
Arabic world as #emph[sifr] (which gave rise to both our English word
“cipher” and the French word “chiffre”).
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! --- The Missing Middle Zero
]
 Two hundred three is
never written 23! $23 = 2 upright(" tens and ") 3 upright(" units")$.
Two hundred three is written $203$: there are 0 tens in the middle.
Never let an empty column collapse without its sentry zero!
]

#divider()

=== 7. The Number Line and Inequality Signs: $<$ and $>$
<the-number-line-and-inequality-signs-and>
To organize numbers like beads along a string, we draw a straight
graduated axis: #strong[the number line]. It originates at 0 on the left
and extends toward the right, growing step by step:

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_02_droite_100.svg", alt: "La droite numérique orientée de 0 à 100"),
  caption: [
    La droite numérique orientée de 0 à 100
  ]
)

The further a number is located to the right, the greater its value.

==== The Hungry Alligator: $<$ and $>$
<the-hungry-alligator-and>
To compare two numbers and declare which is greater, we use two
convenient mathematical symbols:

- #strong[The sign $<$] is read “#strong[is less than]\.”
  #emph[Example:] $3 < 8$ (3 is less than 8).

- #strong[The sign $>$] is read “#strong[is greater than]\.”
  #emph[Example:] $9 > 4$ (9 is greater than 4).

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- The Hungry Alligator
]
 Imagine that the symbols $<$
and $>$ are the #strong[wide-open jaws of a hungry alligator]. The
alligator always snaps toward the larger feast (the greater number)! The
narrow point points toward the smaller number.
]

#quote_box[
#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_02_crocodile_comparaison.svg", alt: "Le crocodile de comparaison : la gueule s’ouvre vers la plus grande quantité"),
  caption: [
    Le crocodile de comparaison : la gueule s'ouvre vers la plus grande
    quantité
]

)
]

#divider()

=== 8. Five Step-by-Step Solved Examples
<five-step-by-step-solved-examples>
#quote_box[
#strong[Solved Example 1 --- Decomposing a Number into Tens and Ones
]

#emph[Problem:] Decompose the number #strong[63] using 10-egg cartons
and loose eggs. #emph[Step-by-step resolution:] 1. In 63, the left digit
is 6: it represents #strong[6 tens].
$6 upright(" tens") = 6 upright(" cartons of 10") = 60 upright(" eggs")$.
\2. The right digit is 3: it represents #strong[3 loose units]. 3. We
express this in expanded form: $ 63 = 60 + 3 =\(6 times 10\)+ 3 $
#emph[Conclusion:] 63 eggs means 6 full cartons plus 3 loose eggs on the
side.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[Solved Example 2 --- Comparing 37 and 52
]
 #emph[Problem:] Which
number is greater between 37 and 52? Express the comparison with the
correct sign. #emph[Method:] 1. Always inspect the #strong[tens digit]
first (the larger bundles):

- In 37, there are 3 tens (30).
- In 52, there are 5 tens (50).

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Because $5 upright(" tens") > 3 upright(" tens")$, the number 52
  contains more tens than 37. We do not even need to examine the units!
+ The alligator opens its jaws toward 52:
  $ 37 < 52 #h(2em) upright("(37 is less than 52)") $
]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[Solved Example 3 --- Comparing Numbers with Equal Tens: 74 and
79
]
 #emph[Problem:] Compare 74 and 79. #emph[Method:] 1. Inspect the
tens digits: 7 tens in 74, and 7 tens in 79. It is a tie! 2. Since the
tens are identical, we break the tie using the #strong[ones digit]:

- 74 has 4 ones.
- 79 has 9 ones.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 3)
+ Since $4 < 9$, we conclude: $ 74 < 79 $
]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[Solved Example 4 --- Rounding Bounds to the Nearest Ten
]

#emph[Problem:] Bound the number #strong[47] between the multiple of ten
immediately preceding it and the one immediately following it.
#emph[Method:] 1. 47 has 4 tens and 7 ones. 2. The full ten immediately
preceding 47 is #strong[40] ($4 upright(" tens")$). 3. The full ten
immediately following 47 is the next multiple: #strong[50]
($5 upright(" tens")$). 4. We express the double inequality:
$ 40 < 47 < 50 $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[Solved Example 5 --- Discovering the Mystery Number
]

#emph[Problem:] “I am a number between 60 and 70. My units digit is
double of 3. Who am I?” #emph[Method:] 1. The number lies between 60 and
70: its tens digit must be #strong[6]. 2. Its units digit is double of
3: $3 + 3 = 6$. The units digit is therefore #strong[6]. 3. Combining 6
tens and 6 units yields: #strong[66].
]

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-1>
+ Our numerical system is in #strong[base ten] because we possess ten
  fingers on our two hands.

+ #strong[1 ten = 10 units (ones)]. 1 hundred = 10 tens = 100 units. 1
  thousand = 10 hundreds = 1,000 units.

+ #strong[Place value]: A digit's value depends on its column. In 82,
  the 8 represents 80 (eight tens). In 28, the 8 represents 8 (eight
  ones).

+ The digit #strong[zero (0)] is an indispensable placeholder: it
  reserves the space of an empty column (e.g., 306 contains 0 tens).

+ To compare two two-digit numbers:

  - First compare the #strong[tens digit]. Whoever has more tens always
    wins!
  - If the tens digits are equal, compare the #strong[units digit].

+ Inequality symbols: #strong[$<$] (less than) and #strong[$>$] (greater
  than). The alligator's mouth always opens toward the greater number.

#divider()

