== CHAPTER 4: The Merchant's Abacus and the Traveling Carry
<chapter-4-the-merchants-abacus-and-the-traveling-carry>
#quote_box[
#emph[“When numbers grow too large to hold in the palm of your hand, we
build them a house with stories and columns. And when a room overflows,
a guest climbs the secret staircase to knock at the neighboring door:
this is the journey of the carry.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-3>
- The secret of the #strong[abacus] and the partitioned shoe box (the
  Ones column and the Tens column).
- Setting up and executing #strong[column addition without regrouping
  (no carry)].
- The mystery of #strong[addition with carrying]: when $7 + 8 = 15$, the
  ones compartment overflows! Why and how 1 brand-new ten travels to the
  top.
- Setting up and executing #strong[column subtraction with borrowing
  (decomposing a ten)] by opening a sealed bundle of 10.
- The detective's superpower: #strong[verifying calculations using the
  inverse operation] to be 100% certain of eliminating all mistakes.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Merchant's Heavy Cargo
<the-problem-the-merchants-heavy-cargo>
We stand on the river wharves. A sturdy wooden river barge has just
docked, laden with sacks of grain.

The dock foreman holds a clay tablet. He must tally the shipments from
two separate agricultural estates:

- The Sun Estate brings #strong[47 sacks of wheat].
- The Moon Estate brings #strong[38 sacks of barley].

The city governor demands the grand total before nightfall. The foreman
begins carving small notches on his slate: $1\,2\,3\,4 dots.h$ It is far
too slow! His vision blurs, the lines cross, and dockworkers shout with
mounting impatience: #emph[“How many sacks in total? We want to unload
the ship!”]

To calculate without error, even with large quantities, ancient
merchants did not line up loose stones on the dirt. They utilized an
ingenious device: #strong[the counting abacus].

#divider()

=== 2. The Calculating Machine: The Abacus and the Two-Column Box
<the-calculating-machine-the-abacus-and-the-two-column-box>
Let us construct the earliest precursor to the computer! You only need a
shoe box and a cardboard partition dividing it into two compartments:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
THE COUNTING BOX
TENS              ONES
t )             ( o )
cork stoppers]  [pebbles/beads]
    ```
  ]
]

Recall the foundational rule discovered in Chapter 2:

- In the #strong[Ones ($o$)] compartment, we only place small single
  pebbles (1 pebble = 1 sack).
- In the #strong[Tens ($t$)] compartment, we only place cork stoppers (1
  stopper = 10 sacks).
- #strong[Absolute Rule:] The moment #strong[10 pebbles] accumulate in
  the ones compartment, the bin is completely full! We must immediately
  exchange those 10 pebbles for #strong[1 cork stopper], which shifts
  across into the tens compartment.

On grid paper, we draw this exact structure in vertical columns: this is
known as #strong[setting up a standard vertical algorithm].

#divider()

=== 3. Vertical Addition Without Regrouping: Perfect Alignment
<vertical-addition-without-regrouping-perfect-alignment>
Let us begin with a gentle addition: $ 23 + 45 $

To set up this calculation on grid paper, follow the golden rule of
alignment:

#quote_box[
#strong[🧭 Alignment Rule --- Everyone in Their Column!
]
 Write the
numbers one directly above the other, aligning vertically:

- #strong[Ones under ones] (the right-hand column).

- #strong[Tens under tens] (the left-hand column).

- Draw a straight horizontal line beneath: this line represents the
  equal sign $=$.

- #strong[ALWAYS begin by adding the ones column first] (from right to
  left)!
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   o
    2   3      (2 tens and 3 ones)
+   4   5      (4 tens and 5 ones)
----------
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[Step 1: Add the ones.] In the right-hand column: $3 + 5 = 8$.
  Write #strong[8] below the line, in the ones column.

+ #strong[Step 2: Add the tens.] In the left-hand column: $2 + 4 = 6$.
  Write #strong[6] below the line, in the tens column.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   o
    2   3
+   4   5
----------
    6   8      The sum is 68!
    ```
  ]
]

Everything remained orderly: neither column reached 10, no pebbles
overflowed.

#divider()

=== 4. Addition with Regrouping: The Traveling Carry
<addition-with-regrouping-the-traveling-carry>
Now let us return to the barge cargo: $ 47 + 38 $

Set up the columns:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   o
    4   7
+   3   8
----------
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[Inspect the ones column:] We unite 7 pebbles and 8 pebbles.
  $ 7 + 8 = 15 $ #strong[Alert!] 15 is a two-digit number! We cannot
  write 15 in the ones box, because each column can hold only a single
  digit from 0 to 9.

+ #strong[The magic trade:] What do 15 pebbles represent?
  $ 15 upright(" ones") = 1 upright(" ten") + 5 upright(" ones") $

  - The #strong[5 ones] remain seated at the bottom of the column, below
    the line.
  - The #strong[1 brand-new ten] cannot stay in the ones bin: it packs
    its bag and #strong[travels up to the top of the tens column]!

This traveling ten is called #strong[the carry] (or regrouped ten). We
write it as a small superscript numeral circled at the top of the tens
column:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
   (1) <----- The traveling carry!
    t   o
    4   7
+   3   8
----------
        5      (7 + 8 = 15: write 5, carry 1)
    ```
  ]
]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 3)
+ #strong[Compute the tens column:] Do not forget to include the
  traveling newcomer!
  $ 1 upright(" (carry)") + 4 + 3 = 8 upright(" tens") $
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
   (1)
    t   o
    4   7
+   3   8
----------
    8   5      The total is 85 sacks!
    ```
  ]
]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- The Carry (Regrouping)
]
 In a vertical
algorithm, a #strong[carry] is a ten (or hundred) produced by an
overflow of the preceding column, which travels to join the top of the
next larger place-value column.
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! --- Forgetting the Carry
]
 The most frequent
student mistake is forgetting to add the carry when summing tens! To
guarantee success: 1. Record the carry immediately the moment you write
down the ones digit. 2. Form the habit of starting the tens addition
#strong[with the carry]: “1 + 4 = 5, and 5 + 3 = 8.”
]

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True Story --- Blaise Pascal's Calculator (1642)
]
 To assist
his father, a royal tax collector who spent long nights adding endless
ledgers of figures, 19-year-old French scientist Blaise Pascal invented
the first mechanical calculator: #strong[the Pascaline]. Inside, an
ingenious sautoir gear mechanism automatically carried over a 1 to the
neighboring wheel whenever a dial turned past 9!
]

#figure(image("figures/histoire/ch_04_pascaline.jpg", alt: "Blaise Pascal’s arithmetic machine (Pascaline, 1642): the first mechanical automatic decimal carry mechanism"),
  caption: [
    Blaise Pascal's arithmetic machine (Pascaline, 1642): the first
    mechanical automatic decimal carry mechanism
  ]
)

