=== 5. Vertical Subtraction: Unpacking a Ten
<vertical-subtraction-unpacking-a-ten>
Now consider the reverse narrative: The barge initially contained
#strong[85 sacks]. Merchants sell #strong[38 sacks] on the wharf. How
many sacks remain on board? $ 85 - 38 $

Set up the subtraction vertically:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   o
    8   5

-   3   8
----------
    ```
  ]
]

As always, start with the ones column on the far right: We have 5 loose
pebbles, and we must remove 8: $ 5 - 8 = upright(" ??") $ #strong[That
is impossible!] If you have 5 berries in your pocket, you cannot hand 8
to your friends!

What does the merchant do? He does not panic. He looks to the tens
column, which holds 8 sealed bundles of 10 sacks.

==== The Decomposing Method (Borrowing / Unpacking):
<the-decomposing-method-borrowing-unpacking>
+ The merchant takes #strong[1 bundle of ten] from the tens store. There
  were 8 tens: now only #strong[7 tens] remain! (Cross out the 8 and
  write 7 above).
+ He unties this bundle: it releases #strong[10 fresh ones].
+ He adds these 10 ones to the 5 already sitting in the ones column:
  $ 10 + 5 = 15 upright(" ones!") $ (Place a small 1 beside the 5 to
  form 15).

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    (7)  (15)
     t     o
    \8\   \5\

-    3     8
-------------
    ```
  ]
]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 4)
+ Now subtraction proceeds effortlessly!

  - In the ones column: $15 - 8 = upright(bold(7))$.
  - In the tens column: $7 - 3 = upright(bold(4))$.
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    (7)  (15)
     t     o
    \8\   \5\

-    3     8
-------------
     4     7      47 sacks remain!
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[💡 Physical Action --- Unpacking a Ten
]
 Take 8 matchboxes filled
with 10 matches each (80) and 5 loose matches (5). You must remove 8
matches. You cannot draw 8 from the 5 loose ones. You take #strong[one
closed matchbox], open it, and dump its 10 matches onto the table with
the 5 loose ones: you now have 15 loose matches and 7 closed boxes. You
can remove 8 matches with complete ease!
]

#divider()

=== 6. The Detective's Secret: Verifying with the Inverse Operation
<the-detectives-secret-verifying-with-the-inverse-operation>
How does a skilled mathematician confirm that an answer is accurate
without waiting for an instructor's red pen? By performing #strong[the
inverse operation proof]!

- You calculated the subtraction: $85 - 38 = 47$.
- To verify, execute the reverse: take the 47 remaining sacks and add
  back the 38 sacks sold ($47 + 38$).
- If your work was flawless, you must recover #strong[the exact 85
  initial sacks]!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Executed subtraction:      Verification addition:
8 5                         4 7
3 8                       + 3 8
4 7                         8 5   <--- Exactly 85 recovered: verified!
    ```
  ]
]

If the result does not yield 85, a calculation error occurred: you can
track down the slip before anyone else spots it!

