== CHAPTER 5: The Brick Wall and Magic Rectangles
<chapter-5-the-brick-wall-and-magic-rectangles>
#quote_box[
#emph[“Why count ten times what the eye can see in a single block?
Arranging objects in regular rows gives our mind the superpower of
multiplying space and time.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-4>
- Why counting 24 bricks one by one takes too long, and how
  #strong[multiplication ($times$)] saves precious time.
- #strong[Sensible commutativity]: why rotating a chocolate bar by a
  quarter turn ($90^compose$) changes neither its taste nor its number
  of squares ($4 times 6 = 6 times 4$).
- How to learn multiplication tables without tears thanks to geometric
  patterns and finger tricks (the magical nine-times table).
- #strong[Doubles] and #strong[halves].
- Multiplying by 10 and 100 by sliding digits one place to the left.
- Your first #strong[column multiplication] of a two-digit number by a
  single digit.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Storm Approaches the Brickyard
<the-problem-the-storm-approaches-the-brickyard>
Centuries have rolled by. Humans no longer sleep in caves: they have
built sturdy villages along riverbanks.

To construct a great granary capable of feeding the village throughout
the winter, the masons molded clay bricks and set them out in the sun to
dry.

Suddenly, a cold wind whips up. The blue sky turns dark gray, then
ink-black: #strong[a ferocious summer storm is bearing down!] The master
mason waves his arms and shouts: #emph[“We must cover the bricks with
heavy tarpaulins before the downpour drenches them and turns them into
mush! How many bricks are there on the drying yard? Give me the exact
count, quickly!”]

You run toward the site. The bricks have been neatly aligned on the
ground: There are #strong[4 long rows], and in each row, there are
#strong[6 bricks].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Row 1:  [] [] [] [] [] []   (6 bricks)
Row 2:  [] [] [] [] [] []   (6 bricks)
Row 3:  [] [] [] [] [] []   (6 bricks)
Row 4:  [] [] [] [] [] []   (6 bricks)
    ```
  ]
]

If you start pointing your finger at every brick one by one: #emph[“1,
2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7…”] By the twelfth brick, the first flashes of lightning
rip through the sky. By the fifteenth, the first heavy raindrops
splatter in the dust. You will never finish counting in time before
everything is ruined!

You could perform repeated addition: $ 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 $ $6 + 6 = 12$,
then $12 + 6 = 18$, then $18 + 6 = 24$. That is already much faster. But
ancient builders invented an even more lightning-fast shortcut:
#strong[Multiplication].

#divider()

=== 2. The Giant Leap: Multiplication
<the-giant-leap-multiplication>
Look at the shape traced by the bricks on the ground: this is no
scattered mess, it is a magnificent #strong[Rectangle]!

- Vertically (height): #strong[4 rows].
- Horizontally (width): #strong[6 columns].

Instead of repeating the addition “6 plus 6 plus 6 plus 6,” we say:
#emph[“I have 4 rows of 6 bricks.”] And we write this with a little
cross: #strong[the sign $times$] (times or multiplied by).

$ 4 times 6 = 24 $

This little $times$ sign marks the crossing of two directions: the
horizontal line meets the vertical column to weave a grid. #strong[To
multiply is to forge a rectangular surface in a flash!]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Multiplication
]
 #strong[Multiplication] is an
operation that replaces repeated addition of the same number. The symbol
$times$ is read “#strong[times]” or “#strong[multiplied by]”.
$ 4 times 6 = underbrace(6 + 6 + 6 + 6, 4 upright(" times the number ") 6) = 24 $
The numbers being multiplied are called #strong[factors], and the result
is called the #strong[product].
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Chocolate Bar: Commutativity at 90 Degrees
<the-chocolate-bar-commutativity-at-90-degrees>
Take a chocolate bar in your kitchen. It has 4 rows of 6 squares. Lay it
flat before you horizontally: you see #strong[4 rows of 6 squares]
($4 times 6$).

Now, take the bar and rotate it by a quarter turn on the table (a right
angle of $90^compose$).

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Flat chocolate bar:                  Bar rotated by 90°:
4 rows of 6                                 6 rows of 4
    ```
  ]
]

What do you see now? You see #strong[6 rows of 4 squares] ($6 times 4$).

Did a single square of chocolate melt away? No! Did anyone bite a piece
off during the turn? No! The number of squares before your eyes is
strictly the same:

$ 4 times 6 = 6 times 4 = 24 $

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Property --- Commutativity of Multiplication
]
 We can
#strong[change the order of the factors] without changing the result.
Turning a rectangle on its side never changes the number of objects it
contains! #emph[A wonderful consequence:] When learning your
multiplication tables, you only have #strong[half the work to do]! Once
you know that $3 times 8 = 24$, you already automatically know that
$8 times 3 = 24$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Doubles and Halves
<doubles-and-halves>
Before exploring all the tables, there are two words you hear every day:
#strong[double] and #strong[half].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Starting amount:           (3)
The DOUBLE (× 2):           +       =  6  (twice as much!)
The HALF (÷ 2):        Split 6 into two equal parts: 3 and 3.
    ```
  ]
]

- #strong[The double] is taking an amount #strong[twice] ($n times 2$ or
  $n + n$).
  - The double of 4 is $4 + 4 = 8$.

  - The double of 10 is $10 + 10 = 20$.
- #strong[The half] is sharing fairly into two strictly equal shares.
  - Half of 8 is 4.

  - Half of 20 is 10.

#divider()

=== 5. A Journey Through the Multiplication Tables
<a-journey-through-the-multiplication-tables>
Memorizing tables by rote like a robot can feel dull. But when you look
at their hidden patterns, each table has its own unique personality!

==== 1. The 2-Times Table: Even Numbers
<the-2-times-table-even-numbers>
Multiplying by 2 simply means taking giant leaps of two:
$ 2\,quad 4\,quad 6\,quad 8\,quad 10\,quad 12\,quad 14\,quad 16\,quad 18\,quad 20 $
All results are #strong[even numbers]: they always end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or
\8.

==== 2. The 5-Times Table: The Dance of the Hand
<the-5-times-table-the-dance-of-the-hand>
Every hand has 5 fingers. So two hands make 10, three hands make 15,
four hands make 20… The results of the 5-times table #strong[always
alternate ending in 5 or 0]:
$ 5\,quad 10\,quad 15\,quad 20\,quad 25\,quad 30\,quad 35\,quad 40\,quad 45\,quad 50 $

==== 3. The 10-Times Table: The Awakening of Zero
<the-10-times-table-the-awakening-of-zero>
Multiplying by 10 turns each unit into a ten! The digit shifts one
column to the left, and a 0 is placed in the units column:
$ 3 times 10 = 30 #h(2em) 7 times 10 = 70 #h(2em) 12 times 10 = 120 $

==== 4. The Magical 9-Times Table: The Ten-Finger Trick!
<the-magical-9-times-table-the-ten-finger-trick>
Lay your ten fingers flat on the table before you. Your fingers are
numbered from 1 to 10 from left to right (left thumb is 1, right little
finger is 10).

#quote_box[
#strong[🖐️ The Finger Magic Trick for the 9-Times Table
]
 Want to
calculate #strong[$9 times 4$]? 1. Fold down your 4th finger (the ring
finger of your left hand). 2. Look at your fingers:

- To the left of the folded finger, there are #strong[3 upright
  fingers]: these are the tens (#strong[30]).
- To the right of the folded finger, there are #strong[6 upright
  fingers]: these are the units (#strong[6]).

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 3)
+ Put them together: #strong[36]! And there it is: $9 times 4 = 36$!
  #emph[\(Try it with $9 times 7$: fold down the 7th finger. You have 6
  fingers on the left and 3 on the right: $63$! It never fails!)]
]
]

#divider()

=== 6. Multiplying by 10, 100, and 1,000
<multiplying-by-10-100-and-1000>
What happens when we multiply a number by 10 or 100? Recall our
place-value chart:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Hundreds     Tens       Units
4       = 4
4          0       = 4 × 10 = 40
4          0          0       = 4 × 100 = 400
    ```
  ]
]

- Multiplying by #strong[10]: each unit becomes a ten. The number shifts
  #strong[one place to the left], and we append a #strong[0] on the
  right.
- Multiplying by #strong[100]: each unit becomes a hundred. The number
  shifts #strong[two places to the left], and we append #strong[two
  zeros] (#strong[00]) on the right.
  $ 7 times 100 = 700 #h(2em) 25 times 100 = 2\,500 $

#divider()

=== 7. Column Multiplication: A Two-Digit Number by a Single Digit
<column-multiplication-a-two-digit-number-by-a-single-digit>
Imagine you need to prepare packages for 3 classrooms. Each class needs
#strong[24 notebooks]. How many notebooks must you hand out?
$ 24 times 3 $

Let us set up the calculation in columns, just as with addition:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   u
    2   4
×       3
---------
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[Step 1: The 3 multiplies the units.]
  $ 3 times 4 upright(" units") = 12 upright(" units") $ In 12, there
  are 2 units and 1 ten:

  - Write the #strong[2] under the line in the units column.
  - Keep the #strong[1 ten as a carry] in your mind or in a small circle
    atop the tens column.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
   (1) <--- carry
    t   u
    2   4
×       3
---------
        2
    ```
  ]
]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Step 2: The 3 multiplies the tens.]
  $ 3 times 2 upright(" tens") = 6 upright(" tens") $ Do not forget to
  add the carried ten: $ 6 + 1 = 7 upright(" tens") $ Write the
  #strong[7] in the tens column.
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
   (1)
    t   u
    2   4
×       3
---------
    7   2      The total is 72 notebooks!
    ```
  ]
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out for the Trap! --- Multiply first, add the carry
after!
]
 In the tens column, #strong[never] perform addition before
multiplication! If you do $\(1 + 2\)times 3 = 3 times 3 = 9$, that is
false! The sacred rule: #strong[multiply first ($3 times 2 = 6$), and
add the carry after ($6 + 1 = 7$)].
]

#divider()

=== 7bis. The Great Shortcut: Long Multiplication with Two-Digit Multipliers
<bis.-the-great-shortcut-long-multiplication-with-two-digit-multipliers>
Once you master multiplying by a single digit, merchants invented an
ingenious shortcut for multiplying by a #strong[two-digit number] like
$146 times 23$.

This calculation is done in #strong[two distinct tiers]:

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_05_multiplication_posee_ret.svg", alt: "La multiplication posée à deux étages avec retenue"),
  caption: [
    La multiplication posée à deux étages avec retenue
  ]
)

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
     1   4   6
×        2   3
--------------
     4   3   8      <-- Tier 1: 146 × 3 units
+2   9   2   0      <-- Tier 2: 146 × 2 tens (THE MANDATORY RED ZERO!)
--------------
 3   3   5   8      <-- Final sum of both tiers
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[🧭 The Mechanical Algorithm in 4 Simple Gestures:
]
 1. Multiply
the whole top number by the #strong[units] digit of the bottom number
($146 times 3 = 438$). 2. On the line below, place a #strong[red zero]
in the units column immediately (because we are now multiplying by
tens!). 3. Multiply the whole top number by the #strong[tens] digit of
the bottom number ($146 times 2 = 292$). 4. Draw a line and #strong[add
the two tiers].
]

