=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-4>
+ #strong[Multiplication ($times$)] replaces repeated addition of the
  same quantity ($4 times 6 = 6 + 6 + 6 + 6$).
+ It is #strong[commutative]: you can reverse the order of factors
  ($4 times 6 = 6 times 4 = 24$). It is just like turning a rectangle or
  chocolate bar by a quarter turn!
+ #strong[Double] means multiplying by 2; #strong[half] means dividing
  into two equal shares.
+ To multiply by #strong[10], shift one column left (append one zero);
  to multiply by #strong[100], shift two columns left (append two
  zeros).
+ For long multiplication: multiply the units first, record any carry,
  then multiply the tens and add the carry at the end.

#divider()

