=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-5>
- How ancient peoples measured using their own bodies (the inch, the
  foot, the pace, the cubit) and why this led to gargantuan disputes!
- The invention of a single standard: the #strong[meter ($m$)] and the
  #strong[centimeter ($c m$)].
- How to measure an object with a graduated ruler #strong[without ever
  falling into the zero trap].
- Weighing the world: the Roberval balance scale, the #strong[gram
  ($g$)], and the #strong[kilogram ($k g$)].
- Liquids and capacities: the #strong[liter ($L$)].
- Taming the flow of time: the calendar of seasons, the 12 months, and
  #strong[reading time on an analog clock].
- Everyday currency: coins, banknotes, and euro cents.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Feud of the Egyptian Stonecutters
<the-problem-the-feud-of-the-egyptian-stonecutters>
Under the scorching sun on the banks of the Nile, four thousand years
ago, hundreds of workers are constructing a grand temple for the
pharaoh.

The chief architect traces the blueprints upon a sheet of papyrus:
#emph[“I demand that each block of stone for the monumental gateway
measure exactly #strong[three cubits] in length!”]

Two stonecutters set to work in the quarry:

- The first is named Senned. He is a towering giant over two meters
  tall, with long, powerful arms like oak trunks.
- The second is named Bak. He is a slender, agile young man with short,
  delicate arms.

What is a #strong[cubit] to them? It is the distance from the tip of the
elbow to the tip of the middle finger (the longest finger of the hand)!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Tip of the elbow                                Tip of the middle finger
1 CUBIT
    ```
  ]
]

Each mason measures three cubits using his own arm, carves his limestone
block from the white bedrock, and hauls it to the construction site.

When the architect attempts to assemble the lintel of the gate, disaster
strikes! The stone carved by the giant Senned juts out by twenty
centimeters, while the block carved by little Bak is far too short and
crashes down into the dust! The two stonecutters seize each other by the
collars, each accusing the other of cheating.

Why did this disaster occur? Because #strong[the body of every human
being is different]. If a giant's stride is wider than a child's stride,
and if a grandfather's foot is longer than his grandson's foot, no one
on Earth will ever be able to build a straight house!

#divider()

=== 2. From the Body to the Ruler: The Meter and the Centimeter
<from-the-body-to-the-ruler-the-meter-and-the-centimeter>
To put an end to endless disputes, the pharaoh decreed a royal law:
#emph[“Henceforth, there shall exist only one single cubit throughout
all the land: #strong[the cubit of my own arm]!”]

Royal sculptors carved this length into a rod of polished black granite,
preserved within the sacred royal treasury. Wooden copies were crafted
for every job site: this was a #strong[standard of measurement].

Centuries later, during the French Revolution, scientists sought
something even grander: a standard belonging to no single monarch, but
to all inhabitants of the Earth. They created #strong[the Meter].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
The inch:        The width of an adult's thumb (~ 2 to 3 cm)
The foot:        The length of a shod foot     (~ 30 cm)
The pace:        A long adult walking stride   (~ 1 meter)
    ```
  ]
]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- The Meter and the Centimeter
]


- #strong[The meter ($m$)] is the official base unit of length. It is
  roughly the width of a bedroom doorway, or the length of a long adult
  walking stride.

- To measure small objects (such as a pencil or an eraser), one meter is
  divided into 100 equal slices: each slice is called a
  #strong[centimeter ($c m$)].

- The prefix “centi-” means: #emph[one hundredth].
  $ 1 upright(" meter ")\(m\)= 100 upright(" centimeters ")\(c m\) $
]

==== How to Measure with Your Ruler Without Falling into the Trap
<how-to-measure-with-your-ruler-without-falling-into-the-trap>
Pick up your plastic school ruler.

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out for the Trap! --- The plastic edge is not zero!
]

Many students press the plastic end of their ruler against the start of
the object. That is an error! On most rulers, there is a small blank
margin before the first graduation line. #strong[To measure accurately,
you must align the ZERO ($0$) mark directly with the very start of your
object.] Then, you observe which number lands exactly at the end of the
object.
]

