=== 3. Mass and Weight: The Roberval Scale and the Kilogram
<mass-and-weight-the-roberval-scale-and-the-kilogram>
How heavy is your backpack? How heavy is a bird's feather?

To weigh objects, we use a two-pan balance perfected by French
mathematician and inventor #strong[Gilles Personne de Roberval]. On one
pan, we place the object to be weighed (for instance, a ripe melon). On
the other pan, we place calibrated metal #strong[weights] until the
pointer rests perfectly vertical in the center.

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_06_pesee_melon_1kg.svg", alt: "Pesée du melon : équilibre de masse de 1 kilogramme"),
  caption: [
    Pesée du melon : équilibre de masse de 1 kilogramme
  ]
)

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- The Kilogram and the Gram
]


- For heavy objects, we use the #strong[kilogram ($k g$)]. A bag of
  flour or a carton of milk weighs approximately $1 upright(" kg")$.

- For very light objects, we divide a kilogram into 1,000 tiny parts:
  each part is a #strong[gram ($g$)].

- A paperclip or a kernel of corn weighs roughly $1 upright(" g")$.

- The prefix “kilo-” comes from Greek and means #strong[thousand].
  $ 1 upright(" kilogram ")\(k g\)= 1\,000 upright(" grams ")\(g\) $
  Half a kilogram is $500 upright(" g")$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Capacities: The Liter
<capacities-the-liter>
How much water fills your bathtub? How much orange juice is in your cup?
The volume of liquid that a container can hold is called its
#strong[capacity] or capacity volume.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- The Liter ($L$)
]
 The standard unit of liquid
measurement is the #strong[liter ($L$)].

- A standard bottle of mineral water often holds $1 upright(" liter")$
  (or $1.5 upright(" L")$).

- A carton of vegetable soup contains $1 upright(" liter")$.

- A small soda can contains roughly a third of a liter
  ($33 upright(" cL")$).

- A large bucket holds approximately $10 upright(" liters")$.

- A filled bathtub holds around $150 upright(" liters")$.
]

#divider()

=== 5. Taming the River of Time: From the Hourglass to the Clock
<taming-the-river-of-time-from-the-hourglass-to-the-clock>
Unlike a rope lying motionless on the floor or a stone resting on a
scale, #strong[time never stops]. You cannot touch it with your fingers.

To track time, early humans began by watching the heavens: 1.
#strong[Day and Night]: The Earth spins on its axis. When the sun
shines, it is day; when it sets, it is night. A full day lasts
#strong[24 hours]. 2. #strong[The Week]: It spans #strong[7 days]
(Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday). 3.
#strong[The Month]: The time required for the Moon to cycle through its
phases (roughly 30 or 31 days, except February with 28 or 29). 4.
#strong[The Year]: The time Earth takes to complete one full revolution
around the Sun. It contains #strong[12 months] (365 days) and journeys
through the four seasons (spring, summer, autumn, winter).

==== Reading an Analog Clock
<reading-an-analog-clock>
On a clock face, there are 12 numbers and two hands turning in clockwise
rotation:

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_06_cadran_horloge.svg", alt: "Le cadran circulaire : 12 heures et 60 minutes"),
  caption: [
    Le cadran circulaire : 12 heures et 60 minutes
  ]
)

- #strong[The short hand (small and slow)]: This is the #strong[hour
  hand]. It takes 12 hours to complete one revolution, gliding slowly
  from one numeral to the next.
- #strong[The long hand (tall and fast)]: This is the #strong[minute
  hand]. It completes a full lap in #strong[60 minutes] (1 hour).

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Reading the Clock Step by Step
]
 1. #strong[On the
hour:] When the long hand points straight up at #strong[12], the short
hand indicates the exact hour. #emph[Example: short hand on 4, long hand
on 12 $arrow.r$ it is 4 o'clock ($4 upright(":") 00$).] 2. #strong[Half
past ($30$ minutes):] When the long hand points straight down at
#strong[6], it has completed half a turn. We say “half past”.
#emph[Example: short hand halfway between 4 and 5, long hand on 6
$arrow.r$ 4:30.] 3. #strong[Quarter past ($15$ minutes):] Long hand
pointing right at #strong[3] $arrow.r$ quarter past
($4 upright(":") 15$). 4. #strong[Quarter to ($45$ minutes):] Long hand
pointing left at #strong[9] $arrow.r$ quarter to ($4 upright(":") 45$).

$ 1 upright(" hour ")\(h\)= 60 upright(" minutes ")\(m i n\) $
$ 1 upright(" minute ")\(m i n\)= 60 upright(" seconds ")\(s\) $
]

#divider()

=== 6. Money: Everyday Currency
<money-everyday-currency>
To purchase bread from the baker or buy a book, we no longer trade goats
or bushels of wheat: we use #strong[currency]. In many European
countries, the currency is the #strong[Euro (€)]\; in other places, the
dollar (\$), pound, or yen is used.

- #strong[Coins:]
  - Copper coins: $1 upright(" cent")$, $2 upright(" cents")$,
    $5 upright(" cents")$.

  - Brass coins: $10 upright(" cents")$, $20 upright(" cents")$,
    $50 upright(" cents")$.

  - Bimetallic coins: #strong[$1 upright(" euro")$] and
    #strong[$2 upright(" euros")$].
- #strong[Paper Banknotes:]
  - $5 upright(" €")$, $10 upright(" €")$, $20 upright(" €")$,
    $50 upright(" €")$, $100 upright(" €")$, $200 upright(" €")$.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Currency Standard --- Cents and the Euro
]
 Just like
centimeters and meters:
$ 1 upright(" Euro ")\(euro\)= 100 upright(" cents ")\(c\) $ To make one
euro, you need one hundred cents!
]

#divider()

=== 7. Five Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<five-step-by-step-worked-examples-1>
#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Measuring a Paintbrush with a Ruler
]

#emph[Problem:] A paintbrush is laid beside a ruler. The handle begins
at the $0$ graduation line. The bristle tip touches mark $14$. How long
is the brush? #emph[Solution:] Since the start aligns with 0, read the
length directly from the end: the brush measures
#strong[$14 upright(" cm")$]. #emph[\(If it started at mark 2 and ended
at mark 14, it would measure $14 - 2 = 12 upright(" cm")$\.)]
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Comparing Lengths in Meters and
Centimeters
]
 #emph[Problem:] A blue ribbon measures
$1 upright(" m ") 25 upright(" cm")$. A red ribbon measures
$140 upright(" cm")$. Which ribbon is longer? #emph[Method:] Convert to
the same unit (centimeters):

- Blue ribbon: $1 upright(" m") = 100 upright(" cm")$, so
  $1 upright(" m ") 25 upright(" cm") = 100 + 25 = 125 upright(" cm")$.
- Red ribbon: $140 upright(" cm")$. Since $140 > 125$, the #strong[red
  ribbon] is longer.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Balancing a Basket on a Roberval Scale
]

#emph[Problem:] A bag of apples is placed on the left pan of a balance
scale. On the right pan, we place a $1 upright(" kg")$ weight, a
$500 upright(" g")$ weight, and two $100 upright(" g")$ weights. The
scale balances perfectly. What is the mass of the bag of apples?
#emph[Solution:] Sum the weights:

- $1 upright(" kg") = 1\,000 upright(" g")$.

- Remaining weights: $500 + 100 + 100 = 700 upright(" g")$.

- Total: $1\,000 + 700 = 1\,700 upright(" g")$, which is
  #strong[$1 upright(" kg ") 700 upright(" g")$].
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 4 --- Duration of a Cartoon
]
 #emph[Problem:] A
cartoon starts at $14 upright(":") 15$ and ends at $15 upright(":") 15$.
How long did it last? #emph[Solution:] The minutes match
($15 upright(" min")$). Look at the hours: from 14:00 to 15:00, exactly
#strong[$1 upright(" hour")$] (60 minutes) elapsed.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 5 --- Making Change at the Bakery
]

#emph[Problem:] A loaf of bread costs
$1 upright(" € ") 20 upright(" c")$. You hand the baker a
$2 upright(" €")$ coin. How much change will you receive? #emph[Method:]
Convert to cents:

- $2 upright(" €") = 200 upright(" cents")$.

- Bread cost:
  $1 upright(" € ") 20 upright(" c") = 120 upright(" cents")$.

- Subtract: $200 - 120 = 80 upright(" cents")$. You receive
  #strong[$80 upright(" cents")$] in change (e.g.~a 50c, 20c, and 10c
  coin).
]

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-5>
+ To avoid misunderstandings, everyone uses #strong[standardized units]:

  - Length: #strong[meter ($m$)] and #strong[centimeter ($c m$)]
    ($1 upright(" m") = 100 upright(" cm")$).

  - Mass: #strong[kilogram ($k g$)] and #strong[gram ($g$)]
    ($1 upright(" kg") = 1\,000 upright(" g")$).

  - Capacity: #strong[liter ($L$)].

+ When measuring with a ruler, always align the #strong[$0$] mark with
  the start of the object.

+ Time is structured into days (24 h), weeks (7 d), months (30 or 31 d),
  and years (12 mo).

+ On a clock: short hand indicates #strong[hours], long hand indicates
  #strong[minutes]. $1 upright(" h") = 60 upright(" min")$.

+ $1 upright(" euro") = 100 upright(" cents")$.

#divider()

