=== 1. The Problem: The Queen's Flowerbed
<the-problem-the-queens-flowerbed>
Long ago, in a splendid kingdom of the Orient, the queen summoned her
head gardener: #emph[“I desire a new garden before the palace gates. But
take heed! I will not tolerate a chaotic tangle of wild weeds. I want
four magnificent parterres: - a flawless square parterre for my white
rosebushes, - an elongated rectangular parterre for my crimson tulips, -
a pointed triangular parterre for my fragrant herbs, - and a perfectly
circular pool in the center, where my golden carp may swim!”]

The gardener bowed low. Yet upon stepping onto the vast stretch of bare
earth, he was deeply troubled: How could he trace a perfect circle
without a giant compass? How could he ensure a rectangle had sharp, true
corners and did not look like a crushed, crooked box? And how could he
direct the apprentices to dig the pathways in the right places without
losing their way?

To accomplish this masterpiece, the gardener relied on the simplest,
most brilliant tools in the history of geometry: #strong[a piece of
string, two wooden pegs, and a folded sheet of paper].

#divider()

=== 2. Plane Figures: Polygons and Their Secrets
<plane-figures-polygons-and-their-secrets>
A flat (or plane) figure is a closed drawing that can be traced on a
sheet of paper or on the ground without rising up into the air.

When a plane figure is bounded solely by straight line segments
(straight sides drawn with a ruler), it is called a #strong[polygon]
(from the Greek #emph[polus], many, and #emph[gônia], angle or corner).

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
THE SQUARE               THE RECTANGLE                THE TRIANGLE
4 equal sides             Opposite sides equal             3 sides
4 right angles            4 right angles                   3 vertices
    ```
  ]
]

==== 1. The Square
<the-square>
- It has #strong[4 sides] that measure #strong[exactly the same length].
- It has #strong[4 right angles] (crisp corners like the corner of a
  sheet of paper).
- It has #strong[4 vertices] (pointed corners).

==== 2. The Rectangle
<the-rectangle>
- It has #strong[4 sides], but they are not all equal: it has 2 long
  lengths and 2 short widths.
- Opposite sides (the sides facing each other) are equal in length.
- It has #strong[4 right angles] and #strong[4 vertices].

==== 3. The Triangle
<the-triangle>
- It has #strong[3 straight sides] and #strong[3 vertices] (three
  angles).
- Its name means: “three angles”.

#divider()

=== 3. The Gardener's String: Tracing a Perfect Circle
<the-gardeners-string-tracing-a-perfect-circle>
Unlike the square or the triangle, #strong[the circle has no straight
sides and no corners]! It is a closed, beautifully harmonious curved
line.

How did the gardener trace the queen's great round pool?

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Taut string
Central  Marking
peg                          peg
O)                        (Chalk)
•✎
Full circular turn /
••
    ```
  ]
]

+ He drives a sturdy #strong[wooden peg straight into the ground at the
  center] of the future pool (this is the #strong[center] of the
  circle).
+ He ties a strong cord securely to this peg.
+ To the other end of the cord, he fastens a second pointed peg (or a
  piece of chalk).
+ He pulls the string until it is #strong[completely taut].
+ Keeping the string tight without ever letting it slacken, he walks in
  a full circle around the center peg, letting the point scratch into
  the earth.

When he completes the loop, he has traced a #strong[flawless circle]!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Circle, Radius, and Diameter
]


- A #strong[circle] is the collection of all points located #strong[at
  the exact same distance] from a central point called the
  #strong[center].

- The length of the taut string is called the #strong[radius] of the
  circle.

- The straight line passing through the center from one edge to the
  opposite edge is called the #strong[diameter]. The diameter is always
  twice the radius ($2 times upright("radius")$).
]

#divider()

=== 4. The Right-Angle Gauge: Crafting Your Magic Set Square
<the-right-angle-gauge-crafting-your-magic-set-square>
To verify that a wall rises vertically or that a table has square
corners, carpenters use a triangular tool made of wood or metal: a
#strong[set square]. Yet you can craft an infallible set square in ten
seconds using any scrap of torn paper!

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🖐️ Workshop --- Make Your Own Folded-Paper Set Square
]
 1. Take
any scrap piece of paper, even one with a jagged, crooked edge. 2. Fold
it in half once, pressing firmly along the fold with your thumbnail. You
now have a perfectly straight edge. 3. Now, fold the paper in half a
second time, #strong[bringing the first straight crease directly onto
itself], edge to edge! 4. Crease it firmly. 5. The corner formed by
these two folds is a #strong[flawless right angle of $90^compose$]!
Place this magical corner against the corner of your notebook, your
desk, or a door frame: it fits to the millimeter!
]

