=== 5. Solids: The World in Three Dimensions
<solids-the-world-in-three-dimensions>
On paper, figures are flat. But in the physical world, objects possess
thickness and depth: they take up volume in space. We call them
#strong[solids].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
THE CUBE              RECTANGULAR PRISM              CYLINDER
    ```
  ]
]

Let us learn the vocabulary of master builders:

- A #strong[face]: a flat wall of the solid (the smooth surface upon
  which it can rest).
- An #strong[edge]: the straight line segment where two faces meet (the
  sharp boundary).
- A #strong[vertex]: the sharp corner point where several edges meet.

==== The Cube (like a game die)
<the-cube-like-a-game-die>
- It has #strong[6 square faces], all strictly identical.
- It has #strong[12 edges] of equal length.
- It has #strong[8 sharp vertices].

==== The Rectangular Prism (like a shoebox or brick)
<the-rectangular-prism-like-a-shoebox-or-brick>
- It has #strong[6 rectangular faces] (which may sometimes include 2
  square faces).
- It has #strong[12 edges] and #strong[8 vertices].

==== The Sphere (like a marble, an orange, or a soccer ball)
<the-sphere-like-a-marble-an-orange-or-a-soccer-ball>
- It has no flat faces, no straight edges, and no vertices. It rolls
  freely in every direction!

==== The Cylinder (like a paper towel roll or soup can)
<the-cylinder-like-a-paper-towel-roll-or-soup-can>
- It has #strong[2 circular flat faces] (top and bottom disks) and a
  curved side that lets it roll in a straight line.

#divider()

=== 6. The Magic Mirror: The Axis of Symmetry
<the-magic-mirror-the-axis-of-symmetry>
Take a sheet of white paper. Place a drop of blue ink or watercolor in
the center. Fold the sheet in half along a straight line immediately,
press firmly with the palm of your hand, and unfold:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Fold line (Axis of Symmetry)
o )        ( o )
Left        Right
wing        wing
    ```
  ]
]

You behold a magnificent #strong[butterfly]! The right wing is the
identical reflection of the left wing. If you refold the sheet, the two
wings will overlap #strong[exactly], stroke for stroke, point for point.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Axis of Symmetry
]
 An #strong[axis of symmetry]
is a straight line that divides a figure into two halves that match up
perfectly when folded. It mirrors what you would see if a flat mirror
were placed upright along that line!
]

Countless things in nature display an axis of symmetry: your own face, a
tree leaf, a ladybug, or a snowflake.

#divider()

=== 7. Grids and the Little Robot's Code
<grids-and-the-little-robots-code>
To dispatch an apprentice to plant seeds at an exact spot in the garden,
the master divided the ground into regular squares: #strong[a grid].

#figure(image("figures/vol_1/fig_07_grille_reperage.svg", alt: "Repérage sur quadrillage cartésien discret"),
  caption: [
    Repérage sur quadrillage cartésien discret
  ]
)

To locate any square, we provide its address with two coordinates: 1.
First the #strong[column] (the letter: A, B, C, D). 2. Next the
#strong[row] (the number: 1, 2, 3). #emph[Example:] The robot is in
square #strong[B2]. The golden star shines in square #strong[C3].

==== Coding Movement with Arrows:
<coding-movement-with-arrows>
To navigate the robot from square #strong[B2] to the star at
#strong[C3], we write a program using directional arrows:

- $arrow.r$: move one square to the right.
- $arrow.t$: move one square up.
- $arrow.l$: move one square to the left.
- $arrow.b$: move one square down.

The shortest path from B2 to C3 is: $ arrow.r quad arrow.t $ (One step
right to land on C2, then one step up to reach C3!)

#divider()

=== 8. Five Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<five-step-by-step-worked-examples-2>
#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Distinguishing a Square from Rectangles
]

#emph[Problem:] Consider three shapes, each with 4 right angles:

- Figure A: width 5 cm, length 8 cm.

- Figure B: 4 sides, each measuring 6 cm.

- Figure C: width 3 cm, length 7 cm. Which figure is a square?
  #emph[Solution:] A square must have #strong[4 equal sides] in addition
  to 4 right angles. Only #strong[Figure B] has 4 equal sides (6 cm).
  Figures A and C are non-square rectangles.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Calculating Diameter from Radius
]

#emph[Problem:] The gardener staked a 4-meter taut cord from the central
peg to outline his pool. What is the total diameter of the pool?
#emph[Solution:] The radius is $4 upright(" m")$. The diameter spans
straight through the center from edge to edge, equal to twice the
radius:
$ upright("Diameter") = 4 upright(" m") + 4 upright(" m") = 2 times 4 upright(" m") = upright(bold(8 upright(" meters"))) $
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Counting Features of a Solid
]

#emph[Problem:] Look at a closed cardboard tissue box (a rectangular
prism). How many stickers would you need to put one sticker on every
vertex? #emph[Solution:] Vertices are the sharp corners. There are 4
vertices on the top face and 4 on the bottom face:
$ 4 + 4 = upright(bold(8 upright(" vertices"))) $ You would need
#strong[8 stickers].
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 4 --- Finding Axes of Symmetry of a Rectangle
]

#emph[Problem:] How many axes of symmetry does an oblong (non-square)
rectangle possess? #emph[Practical Method:] Take a sheet of A4 printer
paper. 1. Fold it in half horizontally: the two halves align. That is
the first axis! 2. Fold it in half vertically: the two halves align.
That is the second axis! 3. #emph[What about the diagonal (corner to
corner)?] Try folding corner to opposite corner: the edges jut out and
do not match! The diagonal of an oblong rectangle is #strong[not] an
axis of symmetry. #emph[Conclusion:] A non-square rectangle has
#strong[2 axes of symmetry].
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 5 --- Moving on a Grid
]
 #emph[Problem:] A
treasure is located at square #strong[D4]. A pirate starts at
#strong[A1]. Write the shortest directional code to find the treasure.
#emph[Solution:]

- From column A to column D: 3 steps right
  ($A arrow.r B arrow.r C arrow.r D$): $arrow.r arrow.r arrow.r$.
- From row 1 to row 4: 3 steps up ($1 arrow.r 2 arrow.r 3 arrow.r 4$):
  $arrow.t arrow.t arrow.t$. #emph[Complete Program:]
  $arrow.r quad arrow.r quad arrow.r quad arrow.t quad arrow.t quad arrow.t$.
]

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-6>
+ #strong[A square] has 4 equal sides and 4 right angles. #strong[A
  rectangle] has equal opposite sides and 4 right angles. #strong[A
  triangle] has 3 sides and 3 vertices.

+ #strong[A circle] is the set of points equidistant from the center.
  This distance is the #strong[radius]\; the #strong[diameter] is twice
  the radius.

+ A #strong[right angle ($90^compose$)] is verified with a set square or
  double-folded paper.

+ Solids have #strong[faces] (flat surfaces), #strong[edges] (meeting
  lines), and #strong[vertices] (corner points).

  - Cube and rectangular prism: #strong[6 faces], #strong[12 edges],
    #strong[8 vertices].

+ A figure has an #strong[axis of symmetry] if its two halves overlap
  perfectly when folded.

+ On a grid, a cell is identified by its #strong[column] (letter) and
  #strong[row] (number). Paths are coded using directional arrows.

#divider()

