  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
o o o o o
o o o o o  o   <--- the soldier sticking out!
    ```
  ]
]

The captain sweats under his bronze helmet. No matter how hard he tries:
#strong[with 11 soldiers, no solid rectangle is possible], except for
the long single line of 1 by 11.

It is neither the soldiers' fault nor the captain's. It is the number 11
itself that #strong[refuses] to be broken into rows. Why are some
numbers so flexible, while others are so stubborn? To understand this
mystery, we must learn two crucial words: #strong[multiple] and
#strong[divisor].

#quote_box[
#strong[🖐️ Your Turn ---
]
 Take 12 small objects (buttons, beans, or
coins). Form every possible rectangle. Then remove one object and try
again with 11. Feel the difference with your own fingers!
]

