== CHAPTER 9: The Feast Remainders and the Game Teams
<chapter-9-the-feast-remainders-and-the-game-teams>
#quote_box[
#emph[“To share is to distribute justice turn by turn. But when objects
cannot be broken into pieces, what do we do with what remains in the
center of the table? It is within the remainder that the whole truth of
division lies hidden.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-8>
- Why division arises from two everyday questions: #strong[fair sharing]
  (how many does each person get?) and #strong[grouping] (how many full
  groups can we form?).
- The #strong[four actors] of Euclidean division: the #emph[dividend],
  the #emph[divisor], the #emph[quotient], and the #emph[remainder].
- The #strong[fundamental equation] $a = b times q + r$ and the
  #strong[inviolable golden rule]: the remainder must always be strictly
  less than the divisor ($r < b$).
- The #strong[step-by-step long division method], explained clearly
  without panic.
- How to #strong[verify] your calculation in a single glance using
  multiplication.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Fourteen Sturgeons of the Great River
<the-problem-the-fourteen-sturgeons-of-the-great-river>
Morning mist floats over the slow waters of the great river. The
village's heavy fishing dugout pulls up against the wooden logs of the
landing dock. The fishermen leap into the cool water and heave a
dripping net onto the bank: they have caught #strong[14 large golden
sturgeons], glistening in the morning sun.

Four fishing families gather in the central square around a massive flat
stone table. Each family has sent its representative. The 14 fish are
lined up across the table, their eyes bright and scales sparkling.

The village elder steps forward with a solemn gaze: #emph[“These fish
must feed our children. But take heed: every family must receive exactly
the same share. Not one bone more, not one scale less! Otherwise,
jealousy will tear our village apart.”]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
