    ```text
Round 1:   Family A [><>]   Family B [><>]   Family C [><>]   Family D [><>]
4 fish distributed, 10 remain on the table)
Round 2:   Family A [><>]   Family B [><>]   Family C [><>]   Family D [><>]
8 fish distributed in total, 6 remain on the table)
Round 3:   Family A [><>]   Family B [><>]   Family C [><>]   Family D [><>]
12 fish distributed in total, 2 fish remain on the table!)
    ```
  ]
]

Look at the table after the third round:

- Each family has received #strong[3 large fish]. Everyone is treated
  completely equally.
- But in the center of the stone, #strong[2 whole fish] remain.

Can you deal a fourth round? No! If you give one fish to Family A and
one to Family B, Families C and D will protest loudly: #emph[“Why them
and not us? That is unjust!”]

Because these fish are fresh and must be salted whole to be preserved,
they cannot be sliced into pieces today. Sharing whole objects has
reached its limit.

You have just carried out a fundamental operation in mathematics:
#strong[Euclidean division] (integer division with remainder), named in
honor of the ancient Greek scholar #strong[Euclid], who taught in
Alexandria more than 2,300 years ago.

