== CHAPTER 10: The Broken Galette and the Measuring Sieve
<chapter-10-the-broken-galette-and-the-measuring-sieve>
#quote_box[
#emph[“Until now, our numbers counted whole, solid objects: pebbles,
sheep, gold coins. But the real world is made of bread that is broken,
cloth that is cut, and time that flows in fractions of hours. To measure
the fractured world, the human mind had to invent the fraction.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-9>
- Why whole numbers are no longer enough when we must share things that
  do not divide evenly.
- The two-story anatomy of a fraction: the #strong[denominator] (which
  names the size of the parts) and the #strong[numerator] (which counts
  how many parts are taken).
- How to tell at a glance whether a fraction is #strong[less than],
  #strong[equal to], or #strong[greater than 1].
- How to decompose a large fraction into #strong[whole units plus a
  remaining fractional part] (such as $7 / 3 = 2 + 1 / 3$).
- How to place fractions accurately on a #strong[graduated number line].
- Why $1 / 2 = 2 / 4 = 4 / 8$: the secret of #strong[equivalent
  fractions] revealed through paper folding.
- How to #strong[add and subtract] fractions sharing the same
  denominator without making mistakes.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Baker's Barley Galette
<the-problem-the-bakers-barley-galette>
The mouth-watering aroma of warm, golden bread drifts from the mouth of
the communal clay-brick oven. The baker pulls out a magnificent flat
barley galette onto a large wooden board---round like a golden disk,
steaming and crispy.

Around the table, #strong[four children] have bellies rumbling with
anticipation. There is only one single galette for four hungry diners.

#figure(image("figures/vol_2/fig_10_galette_quarts.svg", alt: "Le partage de la galette : les fractions de disque"),
  caption: [
    Le partage de la galette : les fractions de disque
  ]
)

If the baker applied Euclidean division from the previous chapter:
$ 1 =\(4 times 0\)+ 1 $ The quotient is 0, and the remainder is 1! The
children would receive 0 galettes each, and the whole galette would sit
on the table growing cold. That would be absurd and sorrowful!

The baker smiles, draws a sharp steel knife, and rests the blade right
at the center of the galette: 1. #emph[First straight cut:] the blade
slices cleanly from edge to edge. Behold: #strong[two identical
half-galettes]. 2. #emph[Second perpendicular cut:] the blade cuts
across at a right angle. The galette is now divided into #strong[four
strictly equal quarters].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Slice 1                   Slice 2
Slice 3                   Slice 4
    ```
  ]
]

Each child holds out a hand and receives their share. How much did each
child receive? They did not receive 0 galettes. Nor did they receive 1
whole galette. They received #strong[one part coming from a whole cut
into 4 equal pieces].

To record this new kind of quantity, humans invented a two-story
notation, separated by a horizontal bar:

$ 1 / 4 $

This notation is read: #strong[“one fourth”] (or #strong[“one
quarter”]). The word comes from the Latin #emph[fractio], meaning “the
act of breaking into pieces”. You have just stepped into the world of
#strong[fractions].

