=== 3. Comparing a Fraction to One: Smaller, Equal, or Greater?
<comparing-a-fraction-to-one-smaller-equal-or-greater>
Look at a box of 6 chocolates. The whole unit is the complete box ($6$
chocolates).

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Découverte --- L'Unité Complète comme Fraction $6\/6$
]


- o | o | o | o | o | o \] \<-- 1 full box = 6/6
]

+ #strong[If you eat 4 chocolates:] you have eaten $4 / 6$ of the box.
  The numerator ($4$) is smaller than the denominator ($6$). You have
  eaten #strong[less than one whole box]: $4 / 6 < 1$.

+ #strong[If you eat all 6 chocolates:] you have eaten $6 / 6$ of the
  box. The numerator equals the denominator. You have eaten
  #strong[exactly 1 whole box]: $6 / 6 = 1$.

+ #strong[If you are ravenous and eat 11 chocolates] (by opening a
  second box): You have eaten $11 / 6$ of a box. The numerator ($11$) is
  larger than the denominator ($6$). You have eaten #strong[more than
  one whole box]: $11 / 6 > 1$.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Property --- Position Relative to 1
]
 For any fraction
$a / b$:

- If the numerator is strictly less than the denominator ($a < b$), then
  $a / b < 1$.

- If the numerator is equal to the denominator ($a = b$), then
  $a / b = 1$.

- If the numerator is strictly greater than the denominator ($a > b$),
  then $a / b > 1$.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Comparing to 1
]
 Let us compare the
following fractions to 1: $3 / 8$, $7 / 7$, $9 / 4$, $5 / 6$.

- For $3 / 8$: $3 < 8$, so $3 / 8 < 1$.

- For $7 / 7$: $7 = 7$, so $7 / 7 = 1$.

- For $9 / 4$: $9 > 4$, so $9 / 4 > 1$.

- For $5 / 6$: $5 < 6$, so $5 / 6 < 1$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Decomposing a Fraction: The Bridge Between Division and Fractions
<decomposing-a-fraction-the-bridge-between-division-and-fractions>
When a fraction is larger than 1, it hides complete whole units inside!
Consider the fraction $7 / 3$ (seven thirds of a galette). Each whole
galette contains 3 thirds ($3 / 3 = 1$). How many whole galettes can we
assemble with 7 thirds?

Recall Euclidean division of 7 by 3: $ 7 =\(3 times 2\)+ 1 $ In 7, there
are #strong[2 groups of 3], with #strong[1 left over]. This means that
with 7 thirds, we can build #strong[2 full galettes], with #strong[1
third remaining]!

$ 7 / 3 = 3 / 3 + 3 / 3 + 1 / 3 = 1 + 1 + 1 / 3 = 2 + 1 / 3 $

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Writing a Fraction as “Whole Number + Fraction \<
1”
]
 To decompose an improper fraction $a / b$: 1. Carry out the
Euclidean division of $a$ by $b$: find quotient $q$ and remainder $r$
($a = b times q + r$). 2. Write directly: $ a / b = q + r / b $ Since
$r < b$, the fraction $r / b$ is guaranteed to be strictly less than 1!
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Decomposing an Improper Fraction
]
 Decompose
$23 / 5$.

- Division of 23 by 5: $23 =\(5 times 4\)+ 3$ (quotient 4, remainder 3).
- We therefore have: $23 / 5 = 4 + 3 / 5$. This shows that $23 / 5$ lies
  strictly between 4 and 5: $4 < 23 / 5 < 5$.
]

#divider()

=== 5. Placing Fractions on a Graduated Number Line
<placing-fractions-on-a-graduated-number-line>
A graduated number line is a measuring path. To place fractions upon it:
\1. Identify the unit interval between 0 and 1 (and between 1 and 2, 2
and 3, etc.). 2. Look at the denominator: it indicates the number of
equal sub-intervals to create within each whole unit. 3. Count steps
forward according to the numerator.

Let us draw a line where each unit is partitioned into #strong[4 equal
parts] (quarters):

#figure(image("figures/vol_2/fig_10_droite_graduee_quarts.svg", alt: "La droite numérique graduée en quarts"),
  caption: [
    La droite numérique graduée en quarts
  ]
)

- Point A is located 1 quarter from the start: it represents $1 / 4$.
- Point B is located right on 1: it represents $4 / 4 = 1$.
- Point C is located 6 quarters from the start: it represents
  $6 / 4 = 1 + 2 / 4$.

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Reading and Placing on the Line
]

#emph[Question:] On a number line where each unit is divided into 5
equal parts (fifths), where does $8 / 5$ lie? #emph[Answer:] Start at 0
and move forward 8 tick marks of size $1 / 5$. Since
$8 / 5 = 1 + 3 / 5$, the point is located between 1 and 2, exactly 3
ticks past 1.
]

#divider()

=== 6. Equivalent Fractions: The Secret of Paper Folding
<equivalent-fractions-the-secret-of-paper-folding>
Take a strip of white paper. Fold it exactly in half. Color one half
blue. You have colored #strong[1 part out of 2]: that is $1 / 2$.

Now, without cutting anything or adding any ink, fold the folded strip
in half once more. Unfold it. What do you notice? The whole strip is now
divided into #strong[4 equal sections]. And the blue part now contains
#strong[2 sections out of 4]!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Strip folded in 2:              Strip folded in 4:
Blue          White       Blue  Blue White White
1/2 )       ( 1/2 )     (1/4) (1/4)
1 part out of 2                 2 parts out of 4!
    ```
  ]
]

Did the amount of blue ink increase? No! Is the strip any longer? No!
Only the fineness of the division changed. You multiplied the total
number of slices by 2 (at the bottom), but you also multiplied the
number of blue slices by 2 (at the top):

$ 1 / 2 = frac(1 times 2, 2 times 2) = 2 / 4 $

If you fold the strip in half a third time, you obtain:
$ 2 / 4 = frac(2 times 2, 4 times 2) = 4 / 8 $

All these fractions describe the exact #strong[same quantity of matter].
We call them #strong[equivalent fractions] (or equal fractions).

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 The Golden Rule of Equivalent Fractions
]
 You do not alter the
value of a fraction if you #strong[multiply] (or #strong[divide]) both
its numerator and denominator by the #strong[same non-zero whole
number]:
$ a / b = frac(a times k, b times k) #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) a / b = frac(a div k, b div k) $
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 4 --- Generating Equivalent Fractions
]


- Find a fraction equal to $3 / 5$ with a denominator of 20: To go from
  5 to 20, we multiply by 4 ($5 times 4 = 20$). We must also multiply
  the top by 4: $3 times 4 = 12$.
  $ 3 / 5 = frac(3 times 4, 5 times 4) = 12 / 20 $

- Simplify $6 / 8$: 6 and 8 are both divisible by 2:
  $ 6 / 8 = frac(6 div 2, 8 div 2) = 3 / 4 $
]

#divider()

=== 7. Adding and Subtracting Fractions with the Same Denominator
<adding-and-subtracting-fractions-with-the-same-denominator>
Imagine a large cake cut into 8 equal slices (eighths).

- At lunch, Leo eats 2 slices: he eats $2 / 8$ of the cake.
- At afternoon snack, his sister Sarah eats 3 slices: she eats $3 / 8$
  of the same cake.

How much cake did they eat altogether? They ate
$2 upright(" slices") + 3 upright(" slices") = 5 upright(" slices")$ of
size “eighth”. Therefore, they ate $5 / 8$ of the cake!

$ 2 / 8 + 3 / 8 = frac(2 + 3, 8) = 5 / 8 $

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Cake cut into 8 slices:
Leo | Leo | Sarah | Sarah | Sarah |     |     |     ]
2/8 ---> <------- 3/8 ------->
5/8 ------------>
    ```
  ]
]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Rule --- Addition and Subtraction with the Same Denominator
]

To add (or subtract) two fractions that have the #strong[same
denominator]: 1. #strong[Keep the same denominator] (do not touch the
bottom!). 2. #strong[Add (or subtract) the numerators] (compute only on
the top).

$ a / c + b / c = frac(a + b, c) #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) a / c - b / c = frac(a - b, c) $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning --- The Forbidden Mistake
]
 Never add
denominators together!
$ 1 / 4 + 2 / 4 eq.not frac(1 + 2, 4 + 4) = 3 / 8 quad upright("TOTALLY FALSE!") $
If you combine 1 quarter of a pie and 2 quarters of a pie, you get 3
quarters of a pie ($3 / 4$), not 3 eighths! The size of the slice does
not shrink when you place several on the same plate.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 5 --- Simple Calculations
]


- $A = 3 / 7 + 2 / 7 = frac(3 + 2, 7) = 5 / 7$

- $B = 9 / 10 - 4 / 10 = frac(9 - 4, 10) = 5 / 10 = 1 / 2$

- $C = 1 - 3 / 5$: Rewrite the integer 1 as a fraction with denominator
  5: $1 = 5 / 5$. $ C = 5 / 5 - 3 / 5 = frac(5 - 3, 5) = 2 / 5 $
]

#divider()

=== What You Need to Remember
<what-you-need-to-remember-1>
+ #strong[A fraction] $a / b$ represents an equal division: the bottom
  ($b$, denominator) gives the number of equal parts in the whole; the
  top ($a$, numerator) counts the parts taken.

+ #strong[Vocabulary:] $1 / 2$ (one half), $1 / 3$ (one third), $1 / 4$
  (one quarter / fourth), $a / b$ ($a$ $b$-ths).

+ #strong[Comparison to 1:]

  - $a < b arrow.r.double.long a / b < 1$

  - $a = b arrow.r.double.long a / b = 1$

  - $a > b arrow.r.double.long a / b > 1$

+ #strong[Decomposition:] For $a / b > 1$, perform Euclidean division
  $a = b times q + r$, yielding $a / b = q + r / b$ (a whole number plus
  a proper fraction).

+ #strong[Equivalent fractions:] Multiplying or dividing both top and
  bottom by the same non-zero number leaves the fraction's value
  unchanged ($1 / 2 = 2 / 4 = 5 / 10$).

+ #strong[Addition / Subtraction:] With identical denominators, compute
  only across the numerators: $a / c + b / c = frac(a + b, c)$.

