=== Solutions
<solutions-8>
#strong[\1.] a) $3 / 5$\; b) $7 / 8$\; c) $1 / 2$\; d) $4 / 3$.
#strong[\2.] a) The numerator is 5: it indicates that 5 parts have been
taken. b) The denominator is 9: it indicates that the unit was divided
into 9 equal parts. #strong[\3.] - Less than 1: $4 / 7$, $1 / 10$ (since
the top is smaller than the bottom). - Equal to 1: $9 / 9$, $15 / 15$
(since top = bottom). - Greater than 1: $12 / 5$, $8 / 3$ (since the top
is larger than the bottom).

#strong[\4.] Same numerator (1): the larger the denominator, the smaller
the fraction. $ 1 / 100 < 1 / 9 < 1 / 5 < 1 / 3 < 1 / 2 $

#strong[\5.] a)
$1 / 3 = frac(1 times 2, 3 times 2) = upright(bold(2)) / 6$. b)
$2 / 5 = frac(2 times 2, 5 times 2) = upright(bold(4)) / 10$. c)
$3 / 4 = frac(3 times 3, 4 times 3) = 9 / upright(bold(12))$. d)
$10 / 20 = frac(10 div 10, 20 div 10) = 1 / upright(bold(2))$.

#strong[\6.] a) $frac(2 + 3, 7) = 5 / 7$\; b) $frac(5 - 1, 9) = 4 / 9$\;
c) $frac(4 + 7, 12) = 11 / 12$\; d) $frac(8 - 5, 10) = 3 / 10$.

#strong[\7.] a) 1 tick = $1 / 6$\; 5 ticks = $5 / 6$\; 8 ticks =
$8 / 6$. b) $8 / 6 = 6 / 6 + 2 / 6 = 1 + 2 / 6$ (or $1 + 1 / 3$).

#strong[\8.] a)
$11 = 4 times 2 + 3 arrow.r.double.long 11 / 4 = 2 + 3 / 4$. b)
$17 = 5 times 3 + 2 arrow.r.double.long 17 / 5 = 3 + 2 / 5$. c)
$26 = 8 times 3 + 2 arrow.r.double.long 26 / 8 = 3 + 2 / 8$ (or
$3 + 1 / 4$). d)
$31 = 6 times 5 + 1 arrow.r.double.long 31 / 6 = 5 + 1 / 6$.

#strong[\9.] a) $1 - 2 / 3 = 3 / 3 - 2 / 3 = 1 / 3$. b)
$1 - 5 / 8 = 8 / 8 - 5 / 8 = 3 / 8$. c)
$2 + 3 / 4 = 8 / 4 + 3 / 4 = 11 / 4$.

#strong[\10.] a) One pizza = 8 slices. They ate #strong[$22 / 8$] of a
pizza. b) $22 = 8 times 2 + 6 arrow.r.double.long 22 / 8 = 2 + 6 / 8$.
Therefore #strong[2 whole pizzas] were completely eaten. c) On the third
pizza, they ate 6 out of 8 slices. Thus $8 - 6 =$ #strong[2 slices]
remain.

#strong[\11.] a) #strong[False], because $3 < 5$ with the same
denominator, so $3 / 4 < 5 / 4$. b) #strong[True], because
$frac(2 times 3, 3 times 3) = 6 / 9$. c) #strong[True], because
$frac(1 + 1, 2) = 2 / 2 = 1$. d) #strong[False], we never add
denominators! $3 / 5 + 1 / 5 = 4 / 5$, not $4 / 10$.

#strong[\12.] - Manon: $1 / 2$ of 24 squares = $24 div 2 =$ #strong[12
squares]. - Arthur: 1 eighth is $24 div 8 = 3$ squares. Thus 3 eighths
are $3 times 3 =$ #strong[9 squares]. - Chloe: 1 twelfth is
$24 div 12 = 2$ squares. Thus 5 twelfths are $5 times 2 =$ #strong[10
squares]. #strong[Manon] ate the most (12 squares).

#strong[\13.] - $14 = 3 times 4 + 2 arrow.r.double.long 4 < 14 / 3 < 5$.
\- $37 = 5 times 7 + 2 arrow.r.double.long 7 < 37 / 5 < 8$. -
$49 = 8 times 6 + 1 arrow.r.double.long 6 < 49 / 8 < 7$.

#strong[\14.] a) The whole trail is $6 / 6$. At the second stop, she
completed $5 / 6$. There remains $6 / 6 - 5 / 6 = 1 / 6$ of the trail.
b) The full course is 1,200 m. $1 / 6$ of 1,200 m is $1\,200 div 6 =$
#strong[200 meters].

#strong[\15.] - 1st cut in half: pieces of $1 / 2$. - 2nd cut in half:
pieces of $1 / 4$ (since $1 / 2 div 2 = 1 / 4$). - 3rd cut in half:
pieces of $1 / 8$. - 4th cut in half: pieces of $1 / 16$.

The smallest piece measures #strong[$1 / 16$ of a meter] (or 6.25 cm).

#divider()

=== The Question that Opens What Lies Ahead
<the-question-that-opens-what-lies-ahead-1>
Thanks to fractions, you now know how to cut any galette into 3, 4, 7,
or 12 equal parts. Yet Renaissance merchants and engineers faced a
persistent, frustrating headache: To compare $3 / 7$ and $4 / 9$, or to
add mixed thirds, fourths, and fifths, they constantly had to search for
awkward common denominators.

In 1585, a brilliant Flemish engineer and mathematician named
#strong[Simon Stevin] cried out: #emph[“Why burden ourselves with all
these disparate denominators? Our number system already counts in
bundles of 10: units, tens, hundreds. Let us simply extend this very
scale below the number 1: tenths, hundredths, thousandths!”]

To separate the realm of whole items from the realm of decimal crumbs,
all that was needed was a single magic punctuation mark: #strong[the
decimal point] (or comma). Prepare to discover decimal numbers in
#strong[Chapter 11: The Tenths of Simon Stevin]!

#divider()

