#pagebreak(weak: true, to: "odd")
#metadata("chapitre") <debut-chapitre>
#metadata("debut-section") <debut-section>
== CHAPTER 11: The Tenths of Simon Stevin
<chapter-11-the-tenths-of-simon-stevin>
#quote_box[
#emph[“Everyone admires the perfection of our number system that counts
by tens, hundreds, and thousands. Why stop at the unit as if standing
before an insurmountable wall? By crossing this threshold into tenths
and hundredths, all measurements become as simple as whole numbers.”
]

--- Adapted from Simon Stevin, #emph[The Decimal] (#emph[De Thiende],
1585).
]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-10>
- The incredible stroke of genius by Flemish engineer #strong[Simon
  Stevin] in 1585.
- What #strong[decimal fractions] are
  ($1 / 10\,1 / 100\,frac(1, 1 thin 000)$) and how they extend the place
  value chart.
- The role of #strong[the decimal point] (or comma): the watertight
  border between whole units and decimal fractions.
- The classic pitfall: understanding why #strong[$3.2$ is greater than
  $3.18$], even though 18 is greater than 2!
- #strong[Bounding and interpolating] decimals: discovering that between
  any two numbers, there are always infinitely many other numbers.
- How to set up #strong[addition and subtraction] of decimals by lining
  up decimal points like buttons on a shirt.
- How to multiply by 10, 100, and 1,000 in the blink of an eye by
  shifting place value ranks.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Nightmare of the Merchants of Bruges
<the-problem-the-nightmare-of-the-merchants-of-bruges>
The year is 1585 in the bustling Flemish city of Bruges. The canals are
crowded with barges laden with grain, spices, barrels of beer, and rolls
of fine Flemish cloth.

On the quays, merchants and tax collectors are tearing their hair out.
To calculate the total cost of a shipment of wheat, a merchant must add:

- $14$ whole florins,
- plus $3 / 7$ of a florin for cart transport,
- plus $5 / 12$ of a florin for the canal bridge toll,
- minus $1 / 6$ of a florin for a trade discount!

Every trader uses fractions with completely different denominators:
thirds, sevenths, twelfths, sixteenths. They constantly have to find
common denominators, convert fractions, simplify… Calculations drag on
for hours, errors multiply, and bitter arguments erupt across the
marketplace.

One man observes this chaos with calm clarity. His name is #strong[Simon
Stevin] (1548--1620). He is an engineer, mathematician, and
quartermaster general of the Dutch army.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Discovery & History --- Simon Stevin (1548--1620)
]


#emph[“Why make complicated what can be simple? We count by tens,
hundreds, and thousands. Let us do the exact same thing BELOW the
unit!”]
]

Stevin asks an illuminating question: #emph[“When we write 111, the
first 1 represents 100, the second represents 10, and the third
represents 1. Each rank is 10 times smaller than the one before it. Why
stop abruptly at 1? Let us continue! The next rank will be 10 times
smaller than 1, and the one after that 10 times smaller still!”]

In 1585, Stevin publishes a slim 36-page booklet written in Flemish
entitled #emph[De Thiende] (“The Tenth”), translated into French as
#emph[La Disme] and English as #emph[The Art of Tenths] or #emph[The
Decimal]. This marks the official birth of #strong[decimal numbers].

#divider()

=== 2. From Sliced Cake to Place Value: Decimal Fractions
<from-sliced-cake-to-place-value-decimal-fractions>
To understand the journey below the number 1, imagine a long measuring
strip of length 1 meter.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
0                                                             1 meter
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[The Tenths:] Divide this meter into #strong[10 equal
  sections]. Each piece is a decimal fraction:
  $ 1 / 10 upright(" of a meter (one tenth of a meter, or ") 1 upright(" decimeter)") $
  It takes 10 tenths to make 1 whole unit: $10 / 10 = 1$.

+ #strong[The Hundredths:] Take one of these tenths and divide it into
  #strong[10 tiny sub-pieces]. Since there were 10 tenths, the whole
  meter is now partitioned into $10 times 10 = 100$ small pieces! Each
  tiny piece is called a #strong[hundredth]:
  $ 1 / 100 upright(" of a meter (one hundredth of a meter, or ") 1 upright(" centimeter)") $
  It takes 10 hundredths to build 1 tenth ($10 / 100 = 1 / 10$). And it
  takes 100 hundredths to form 1 whole unit ($100 / 100 = 1$).

+ #strong[The Thousandths:] Divide each hundredth into 10 even finer
  dust-like specks. The entire unit is now cut into
  $100 times 10 = 1 thin 000$ parts! Each speck is called a
  #strong[thousandth]:
  $ frac(1, 1 thin 000) upright(" of a meter (one thousandth of a meter, or ") 1 upright(" millimeter)") $

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Decimal Fraction
]
 A #strong[decimal fraction]
is any fraction whose denominator is
$10\,100\,1 thin 000\,10 thin 000 dots.h$ (a power of 10).
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Decimal Point: The Border Guard of Place Value
<the-decimal-point-the-border-guard-of-place-value>
How can we write these fractions without drawing two-story fraction bars
every time? Look at our grand place-value chart extended to the right:

