]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Decimal Number, Whole Part, and Decimal Part
]

A #strong[decimal number] is a number that can be expressed with a
finite number of digits after the decimal point.

- The #strong[whole-number part] is located to the left of the decimal
  point (units, tens, hundreds…).

- The #strong[decimal part] is located to the right of the decimal point
  (tenths, hundredths, thousandths…).

- The #strong[decimal point] (or comma) is the border marking where the
  single units column ends.
]

==== How to Read $3.47$ Aloud?
<how-to-read-3.47-aloud>
- In casual speech: people often say “three point four seven”.
- But the precise mathematical reading is: “#strong[3 units and 47
  hundredths]” or “#strong[3 units, 4 tenths, and 7 hundredths]”.

$ 3.47 = 3 + 4 / 10 + 7 / 100 = 3 + 47 / 100 = 347 / 100 $

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Simon Stevin did not yet use our modern
decimal point! He drew tiny circled numbers above the digits:
\$3\\text{ \\textcircled{0} } 4\\text{ \\textcircled{1} } 7\\text{ \\textcircled{2} }\$
to designate whole units, tenths, and hundredths. It was the Scottish
mathematician #strong[John Napier] (the inventor of logarithms) who
popularized the decimal point and comma in 1617. Today, English-speaking
countries use a dot ($3.47$), while France and many European nations use
a decimal comma ($3\,47$). The mathematical meaning is identical!
]

#divider()

=== 4. The Great Pitfall: Why $3.2$ Is Greater Than $3.18$!
<the-great-pitfall-why-3.2-is-greater-than-3.18>
This is the number-one pitfall in elementary school mathematics---one
that almost every student stumbles into at least once!

Look at these two numbers: $ 3.2 quad upright("and") quad 3.18 $ An
untrained eye glances at the decimal part and thinks: #emph[“18 is
bigger than 2, so 3.18 must be bigger than 3.2!”] #strong[That is
completely wrong!]

Why? Because you are comparing slices of completely different sizes!

- In $3.2$, the 2 represents #strong[2 tenths] of a cake (2 large, thick
  slices).
- In $3.18$, the 1 represents #strong[1 tenth] and the 8 represents
  #strong[8 tiny hundredths].

To compare fairly, we must express both in the same unit of measure
(hundredths). Add a #strong[trailing zero] at the end of $3.2$:
$ 3.2 = 3.20 quad upright("(3 whole units and 20 hundredths)") $

Now compare:

- $3.20$ is 3 cakes and #strong[20 hundredths].
- $3.18$ is 3 cakes and #strong[18 hundredths].

Since $20 > 18$, it is crystal clear that: $ upright(bold(3.2 > 3.18)) $

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
3.20:  [ Cake 1 ] [ Cake 2 ] [ Cake 3 ]  +  [ 20 hundredths ]  <-- LARGER!
3.18:  [ Cake 1 ] [ Cake 2 ] [ Cake 3 ]  +  [ 18 hundredths ]
    ```
  ]
]

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Comparing Two Decimal Numbers Without Error
]
 1.
First compare the #strong[whole-number part]: $7.1 > 5.99$ because
$7 > 5$. (No need to even look past the decimal point!). 2. If the whole
parts are equal, compare the #strong[tenths digit]: In
$3 . upright(bold(2))$ and $3 . upright(bold(1)) 8$, the tenths digits
are $2$ versus $1$. Since $2 > 1$, it follows that $3.2 > 3.18$. 3. If
the tenths are equal, compare the #strong[hundredths], and so on.
#emph[Foolproof tip:] Pad the numbers with trailing zeros on the right
so both have the same number of decimal places!
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning --- Meaningful vs.~Superfluous Zeros
]


- #strong[Superfluous zeros:] You can freely add or remove zeros at the
  very end of the decimal part without changing the number's value:
  $ 4.5 = 4.50 = 4.500 $

- #strong[Indispensable zeros:] A zero trapped between the decimal point
  and another non-zero digit can NEVER be removed: $ 4.05 eq.not 4.5 $
  $4.05$ is 4 units and 5 hundredths. $4.5$ is 4 units and 5 tenths (50
  hundredths)! That is 10 times more crumb!
]

#divider()

=== 5. Interpolation and Bounding: The Infinite World Between Two Numbers
<interpolation-and-bounding-the-infinite-world-between-two-numbers>
With whole numbers, between 3 and 4, there is #strong[nothing]. No
integer can slip between them. With decimals, the world becomes
infinitely rich!

Look at a ruler between 3 and 4:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
3           3.2         3.4         3.6         3.8          4
3.1         3.3         3.5         3.7         3.9
    ```
  ]
]

Between 3 and 4, we have interpolated 9 numbers at the tenths level:
$3.1\,3.2\,3.3 dots.h$

And what happens between $3.1$ and $3.2$? Write them in hundredths:
$3.10$ and $3.20$. Between $3.10$ and $3.20$, we can slip:
$ 3.11 quad\;quad 3.12 quad\;quad 3.15 quad\;quad 3.19 $ And between
$3.14$ and $3.15$? We can write $3.140$ and $3.150$, and insert
thousandths like $3.145$!

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Between any two decimal numbers, no matter how
close they seem, you can ALWAYS find another decimal number (in fact,
infinitely many!). There is never an empty gap that cannot be filled by
zooming in further.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Bounding and Interpolation
]


- #emph[Bound 4.72 between consecutive whole numbers:] $4 < 4.72 < 5$.

- #emph[Bound 4.72 to the nearest tenth:] $4.7 < 4.72 < 4.8$ (since
  $4.70 < 4.72 < 4.80$).

- #emph[Insert a decimal between 6.3 and 6.4:] Think of $6.30$ and
  $6.40$. We can choose $6.35$ (or $6.31$ or $6.38$).
]

#divider()

=== 6. Column Addition and Subtraction: The Shirt Buttons
<column-addition-and-subtraction-the-shirt-buttons>
To add or subtract decimal numbers, the golden rule is simple and
absolute: #strong[You must vertically align the decimal points directly
under one another.]

Think of the decimal point as the line of buttons on a shirt: if one
button is off by a single hole, the entire shirt sits crooked!

==== Worked Example: Calculate $43.7 + 8.56$
<worked-example-calculate-43.7-8.56>
#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   u . tenths  hundredths
    1   1                 <-- carry digits
    4   3 .    7      0   <-- we pad with a 0 to balance columns
+       8 .    5      6
-----------------------
    5   2 .    2      6
    ```
  ]
]

+ Align the decimal points vertically.

+ Add a trailing zero to $43.7$ so both numbers have two decimal digits:
  $43.70$.

+ Add column by column from right to left:

  - Hundredths: $0 + 6 = 6$.

  - Tenths: $7 + 5 = 12 arrow.r.double.long$ write 2, carry 1 to the
    units column.

  - #strong[Immediately place the decimal point in the answer, perfectly
    aligned!]

  - Units: $1 upright(" (carry)") + 3 + 8 = 12 arrow.r.double.long$
    write 2, carry 1 to the tens.

  - Tens: $1 upright(" (carry)") + 4 = 5$.

+ #strong[Result:] $52.26$.

==== Worked Example: Calculate $35.2 - 7.64$
<worked-example-calculate-35.2---7.64>
#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1.2pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 18pt, y: 6pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 10.5pt, weight: "bold", fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
    t   u . tenths  hundredths
    2  14     11     10   <-- borrowing / regrouping
    3   5 .    2      0   <-- this zero is MANDATORY!

-       7 .    6      4
-----------------------
    2   7 .    5      6
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning --- The Mandatory Zero in Subtraction
]
 In
$35.2 - 7.64$, you cannot simply say “nothing minus 4 is 4”! That is a
grave error. You must write $35.2 upright(bold(0))$. Then calculate: 0
hundredths minus 4 hundredths cannot be done directly
$arrow.r.double.long$ regroup 1 tenth into 10 hundredths, and
$10 - 4 = 6$!
]

#divider()

=== 7. Multiplying by 10, 100, and 1,000: The Great Place-Value Shift
<multiplying-by-10-100-and-1000-the-great-place-value-shift>
What happens when you multiply a decimal number by 10? Remember: each
unit becomes a ten, each tenth becomes a unit, and each hundredth
becomes a tenth! Every digit jumps one place-value column to the left.
On paper, this creates the visual appearance that #strong[the decimal
point shifts to the right].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
× 10    > Decimal point shifts 1 place to the right:    3.45 × 10 = 34.5
× 100   > Decimal point shifts 2 places to the right:   3.45 × 100 = 345
× 1 000 > Decimal point shifts 3 places to the right:   3.45 × 1 000 = 3 450
    ```
  ]
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Mental Fluency
]


- $0.6 times 10 = 6$

- $2.38 times 100 = 238$

- $4.7 times 100 = 470$ (shift one place to make 47, then append a zero
  for the second place!)

- $0.005 times 1 thin 000 = 5$
]

#divider()

=== What You Need to Remember
<what-you-need-to-remember-2>
+ #strong[A decimal number] consists of a whole-number part and a
  decimal part separated by a decimal point.

+ #strong[Place value:]

  - 1st digit after the point = #strong[tenths] ($1 / 10 = 0.1$);

  - 2nd digit = #strong[hundredths] ($1 / 100 = 0.01$);

  - 3rd digit = #strong[thousandths] ($frac(1, 1 thin 000) = 0.001$).

+ #strong[Comparing decimals:] Always look at the whole-number part
  first. If equal, compare tenths, then hundredths. Pad with trailing
  zeros to level columns ($3.2 = 3.20 > 3.18$).

+ #strong[Column addition/subtraction:] Line up decimal points
  vertically without fail. Remember to pad top numbers with zeros in
  subtraction when needed ($5 - 2.3 = 5.0 - 2.3$).

+ #strong[Multiplying by 10, 100, 1,000:] Shift the decimal point 1, 2,
  or 3 places to the right, adding zeros as necessary.

#divider()

