=== The Question that Opens What Lies Ahead
<the-question-that-opens-what-lies-ahead-2>
Thanks to Simon Stevin's decimals, humanity gained a flawless system for
measuring continuous magnitudes. Yet until the end of the 18th century,
every city and province in France used entirely different units: a
league in Paris was not a league in Lyon, a pint varied from town to
town, and the King's Foot was not the size of anyone else's shoe!

In 1789, the French Revolution broke out. Citizens and scholars resolved
to unite the nation---and soon the entire world---around a single
universal standard of measure drawn directly from the Earth itself:
#strong[the Meter]. And for all physical magnitudes, from length and
mass to liquid volume, they embraced the elegance of the decimal system!

How can we measure the Earth, calculate the perimeter of a field, or
find the area of a pasture without ever stumbling? Join us in
#strong[Chapter 12: The Meter of the Revolution]!

#divider()

