== CHAPTER 12: The Meter of the Revolution
<chapter-12-the-meter-of-the-revolution>
#quote_box[
#emph[“Nothing greater or more sublime has ever come from the hands of
human beings than this metric system, which gives one and the same
standard to all nations and for all time.”
]
 --- Decree of the National
Convention (1795).
]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-11>
- The daring expedition of astronomers #strong[Delambre and Méchain]
  during the French Revolution to measure planet Earth.
- The secret of universal prefixes: Greek to multiply (kilo-, hecto-,
  deca-) and Latin to divide (deci-, centi-, milli-).
- The three great families of measurement: #strong[lengths] ($m$),
  #strong[masses] ($g\,k g\,t$), and #strong[capacities] ($L$).
- The fundamental distinction between #strong[perimeter] (the boundary,
  in 1 dimension) and #strong[area] (the covered surface, in 2
  dimensions).
- How to compute the perimeter and area of rectangles and squares
  without ever confusing the two formulas.
- How to recognize a #strong[right angle], an #strong[acute angle], and
  an #strong[obtuse angle] using a set square.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Tower of Babel of Old Regime Measurements
<the-problem-the-tower-of-babel-of-old-regime-measurements>
Before 1789, France was not united in its measurements: it was a
bewildering labyrinth! Every province, every town, and often every
feudal estate had its own measuring units:

- In Paris, cloth was measured using the #strong[Paris ell]
  (#emph[aune], about 1.18 meters). But in Rouen, the ell was shorter,
  and in Lyon, it was longer!
- To measure land, surveyors used the #strong[King's Foot] (about 32.5
  cm), but the foot in Bordeaux was 35.7 cm.
- To weigh grain, people used the #strong[pound] (#emph[livre]), but
  more than 50 different kinds of pounds existed across France!
- For liquids, the Saint-Denis #strong[pint] was twice as large as the
  Paris pint.

Historians estimate that France possessed over #strong[800 different
names for measures] and nearly #strong[250,000 different ways of
calculating them]!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Before 1789:
In Paris: 1 foot = 32.5 cm
In Bordeaux: 1 foot = 35.7 cm
In Strasbourg: 1 foot = 28.9 cm
Honest trade between cities was practically impossible!
    ```
  ]
]

Dishonest merchants took advantage of this confusion to cheat customers;
peasants were ruined by feudal lords who secretly enlarged grain bushels
to collect heavier taxes; nobody could agree.

In 1789, the French Revolution erupted. In their registers of grievances
(#emph[cahiers de doléances]), citizens demanded with one voice:
#emph[“One king, one law, one weight, and one measure!”]

#divider()

=== 2. Measuring the Earth: The Adventure of the Universal Meter
<measuring-the-earth-the-adventure-of-the-universal-meter>
The scientists of the French Academy of Sciences (Lavoisier, Condorcet,
Lagrange, Laplace) refused to base the new unit on the length of a
king's foot or an emperor's arm: kings die, and human limbs vary from
person to person. They sought a standard belonging to all humankind,
drawn from nature itself: #strong[planet Earth]!

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History --- The Epic Journey of Delambre and Méchain
(1792--1798)
]
 In 1792, in the midst of revolutionary turmoil and the
threat of war, two French astronomers set out to measure the Earth:

- #strong[Jean-Baptiste Delambre] traveled south from Dunkirk.
- #strong[Pierre Méchain] traveled north from Barcelona. For six arduous
  years, across rugged mountains, violent storms, and arrests by
  suspicious revolutionary guards who mistook their surveying
  theodolites for spy equipment, they measured the distance between
  Dunkirk and Barcelona using triangulation.

From these measurements, scientists determined the exact total distance
along the Earth's meridian arc from the North Pole to the Equator. They
divided this immense distance into #strong[ten million equal parts].
This ten-millionth part of the Earth's meridian quadrant became the new
standard of length for humanity: #strong[the Meter] (from the Greek
#emph[metron], meaning “measure”).
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
NORTH POLE
D  |  <-- Dunkirk
B  |  <-- Barcelona
EQUATOR
Distance North Pole to Equator divided by 10,000,000 = EXACTLY 1 METER!
    ```
  ]
]

The motto of this new universal standard was engraved upon the first
official commemorative medals: #strong[“For all times, for all
peoples.”]

#divider()

=== 3. The Universal Chart: The Magic of Greek and Latin Prefixes
<the-universal-chart-the-magic-of-greek-and-latin-prefixes>
The scholars had a second stroke of genius: apply Simon Stevin's decimal
place-value system to every physical measurement. Gone were the clumsy
systems where 1 foot was 12 inches and 1 inch was 12 lines! From now on,
#strong[everything scales by tens].

To name the units, they devised a brilliant linguistic convention:

- For units #strong[larger] than the base unit, they borrowed words from
  #strong[Greek] (the language of giants):
  - #strong[Deca-] (from Greek #emph[deka]) = $times 10$

  - #strong[Hecto-] (from Greek #emph[hekaton]) = $times 100$

  - #strong[Kilo-] (from Greek #emph[khilioi]) = $times 1 thin 000$
- For units #strong[smaller] than the base unit, they borrowed words
  from #strong[Latin] (the language of division):
  - #strong[Deci-] (from Latin #emph[decimus]) = $div 10$ ($1 / 10$)

  - #strong[Centi-] (from Latin #emph[centesimus]) = $div 100$
    ($1 / 100$)

  - #strong[Milli-] (from Latin #emph[millesimus]) = $div 1 thin 000$
    ($frac(1, 1 thin 000)$)

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
GREEK PREFIXES (× 10, × 100, × 1 000)
kilo-    hecto-    deca-
BASE UNIT (m, g, L)
LATIN PREFIXES (÷ 10, ÷ 100, ÷ 1 000)
deci-    centi-    milli-
    ```
  ]
]

This exact same chart structure works #strong[identically] for lengths,
masses, and capacities!

