=== 7. Angles: Acute, Right, or Obtuse with a Set Square
<angles-acute-right-or-obtuse-with-a-set-square>
When two straight lines meet at a point (the vertex), they form an
opening called an #strong[angle]. To inspect this opening, the
fundamental reference tool of students and carpenters is the #strong[set
square] (or drafting triangle).

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
ACUTE ANGLE                RIGHT ANGLE                 OBTUSE ANGLE
Sharper than set square)  (Matches set square: 90°)   (Wider than set square)
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[Right angle ($90^compose$):] the angle of a perfect sheet of
  paper, the corner of a square room, or the corner of a set square. Its
  two rays are perpendicular.
+ #strong[Acute angle:] a sharp, narrow angle tighter than a right angle
  (opening strictly between $0^compose$ and $90^compose$).
+ #strong[Obtuse angle:] an open, wide angle broader than a right angle
  (opening strictly between $90^compose$ and $180^compose$).

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Checking Angles with a Set Square
]
 1. Place the
vertex of the set square's right angle directly onto the angle's vertex.
\2. Align one straight edge of the set square along one arm of the
angle. 3. Observe the other arm:

- If it hides beneath the body of the set square: the angle is
  #strong[acute] (smaller than $90^compose$).

- If it traces exactly along the second edge: the angle is
  #strong[right] ($90^compose$).

- If it spreads outward beyond the set square: the angle is
  #strong[obtuse] (greater than $90^compose$).
]

#divider()

=== What You Need to Remember
<what-you-need-to-remember-3>
+ #strong[The metric system] is anchored on the #strong[meter] ($m$),
  defined during the French Revolution as a decimal fraction of planet
  Earth.

+ #strong[Prefixes:]

  - Kilo ($1 thin 000$), Hecto ($100$), Deca ($10$) to multiply;
  - Deci ($1 / 10$), Centi ($1 / 100$), Milli ($frac(1, 1 thin 000)$) to
    divide.

+ #strong[Masses:] $1 upright(" kg") = 1 thin 000 upright(" g")$ and
  $1 upright(" metric ton (t)") = 1 thin 000 upright(" kg")$.

+ #strong[Perimeter (1D):] Total boundary length.

  - Rectangle: $cal(P) = 2 times\(L + w\)$.
  - Square: $cal(P) = 4 times s$.

+ #strong[Area (2D):] Total enclosed surface.

  - Rectangle: $cal(A) = L times w$.
  - Square: $cal(A) = s times s$.

+ #strong[Angles:]

  - Acute ($< 90^compose$);

  - Right ($= 90^compose$, corner of set square);

  - Obtuse ($> 90^compose$).

#divider()

