=== 1. The Problem: The Birthday Feast and the Cake for 4
<the-problem-the-birthday-feast-and-the-cake-for-4>
Thomas's birthday is just around the corner. To treat his friends, his
grandmother pulls out her treasured recipe notebook, stained with cocoa
and dog-eared from years of love. She turns to her legendary pear
chocolate fudge cake:

#figure(image("figures/vol_2/fig_13_regle_trois.svg", alt: "Le tableau de proportionnalité et la règle de trois"),
  caption: [
    Le tableau de proportionnalité et la règle de trois
  ]
)

Thomas jumps with delight: #emph[“Grandma, this is wonderful! But there
will be #strong[6 people] at the table for my birthday: my parents, you,
my two cousins, and me! And tomorrow, we're having a second party with
#strong[10 classmates]!”]

Grandmother smiles: #emph[“If we simply toss in 6 eggs at random and 300
grams of sugar, our cake will turn out cloyingly sweet or terribly dry.
We must respect #strong[the right proportions]!”]

How can we calculate the exact amount of chocolate for 6 people without
ruining Grandma's recipe?

Thomas takes a sheet of scrap paper and thinks it through:

- For 4 people, we need 200 g of chocolate.
- Therefore, for half as many (#strong[2 people]), we need half as much:
  $200 div 2 = 100 upright(" g")$.
- And #strong[6 people] is simply 4 people plus 2 people!

We can just add the corresponding amounts together:
$ 200 upright(" g") + 100 upright(" g") = 300 upright(" g of chocolate!") $

Thomas has just used, without realizing it, one of the most powerful
tools in everyday mathematics: #strong[proportionality].

#divider()

=== 2. What Is Proportionality? The Doubling Test
<what-is-proportionality-the-doubling-test>
In nature and daily life, many quantities are linked together. But they
are not all linked in the same way!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Two Proportional Quantities
]
 Two quantities
are #strong[proportional] when the values of one are obtained by
#strong[always multiplying the values of the other by the exact same
constant number]. This implies that:

- If one quantity becomes #strong[2 times larger], the other becomes
  #strong[2 times larger].

- If one quantity becomes #strong[3 times larger], the other becomes
  #strong[3 times larger].

- If one quantity becomes #strong[2 times smaller] (halved), the other
  becomes #strong[2 times smaller].
]

==== The Pitfall: Not Everything in Life Is Proportional!
<the-pitfall-not-everything-in-life-is-proportional>
Let us put three everyday situations to the #strong[“doubling test”]:

+ #strong[The Price of Apples:] $1 upright(" kg")$ of apples costs
  $\$3$. $2 upright(" kg")$ of apples cost $\$6$ ($2 times 3$).
  $4 upright(" kg")$ of apples cost $\$12$ ($4 times 3$). $arrow.r$
  #strong[This is a true proportional relationship!]

+ #strong[A Child's Age and Height:] At age 5, Tom was
  $1.10 upright(" m")$ tall. At age 10 (his age has doubled:
  $5 times 2 = 10$), Tom is about $1.40 upright(" m")$ tall. Is he twice
  as tall as $1.10 upright(" m")$, which would be $2.20 upright(" m")$?
  Absolutely not! And at age 20, will he stand $4.40 upright(" m")$
  tall? That would be a terrifying giant! $arrow.r$ #strong[Height is
  NOT proportional to age!] Human bodies grow quickly in early
  childhood, slow down, and stop growing altogether in adulthood.

+ #strong[A Cinema Membership:] An annual membership card costs $\$10$,
  and then each movie ticket costs $\$5$.

  - For 1 movie: $\$10 +\$5 =\$15$.
  - For 2 movies: $\$10 +\(2 times\$5\)=\$20$. The number of movies
    doubled (from 1 to 2), but the total cost did not double
    ($\$20 eq.not 2 times\$15 =\$30$). $arrow.r$ Because of the fixed
    starting fee, this is #strong[NOT direct proportionality].

#divider()

=== 3. The Proportionality Table: Three Calculation Methods
<the-proportionality-table-three-calculation-methods>
To solve problems cleanly without getting tangled up, we arrange our
numbers into a two-row table:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Quantity 1 (e.g., number of notebooks)    2    4    6   10
Quantity 2 (e.g., cost in dollars)        5   10   15   25
    ```
  ]
]

There are #strong[three reliable methods] for filling in missing entries
in this table. Always pick the one that makes the numbers simplest to
compute!

==== Method 1: Operating Across Columns (Multiplication and Addition)
<method-1-operating-across-columns-multiplication-and-addition>
In a proportionality table, you are allowed to:

- Multiply or divide an entire column by any number.
- Add (or subtract) two columns together!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
× 2                  +
Notebooks    2      4     6     10  (4 + 6 = 10)
Cost ($)     5     10    15     25  (10 + 15 = 25)
× 2                  +
    ```
  ]
]

==== Method 2: Finding the Unit Rate (How Much for 1?)
<method-2-finding-the-unit-rate-how-much-for-1>
This is the common-sense method favored by generations of shoppers:
#emph[“First find the value for #strong[1 single item], then multiply by
the desired number of items!”]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- The Unit Rate Method
]
 #emph[Problem:] 3
identical pens cost $\$4.50$. How much do 7 of these pens cost?

- #strong[Step 1: Find the price of 1 pen.] $\$4.50 div 3 =\$1.50$. (One
  pen costs $\$1.50$).

- #strong[Step 2: Calculate the cost for 7 pens.]
  $7 times\$1.50 =\$10.50$. 7 pens cost #strong[$\$10.50$].
]

==== Method 3: The Constant of Proportionality (The Vertical Bridge)
<method-3-the-constant-of-proportionality-the-vertical-bridge>
This is the multiplier that takes you directly from the top row to the
bottom row in one step.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Number of cakes          3      5      8
× 4  (Constant)
Number of strawberries  12     20     32
    ```
  ]
]

To find this multiplier, divide any number in the second row by its
corresponding partner in the first row:
$ upright("Constant") = 12 / 3 = 4 $ Every cake requires 4 strawberries.
For any order, simply #strong[multiply by 4]! And to work backwards from
strawberries to cakes, divide by 4.

#divider()

=== 4. The Legendary “Rule of Three” Explained
<the-legendary-rule-of-three-explained>
For centuries across European schools, pupils were taught #strong[“the
rule of three”]. Why that name? Because you are given #strong[three
known numbers], and your goal is to uncover the #strong[fourth]!

Consider the setup: $ a & arrow.r & b\
c & arrow.r & ? $

#emph[Example:] If $4$ meters of fabric cost $\$28$, how much do $9$
meters cost? - The three known numbers are: $4$, $28$, and $9$. - The
unknown number is labeled $?$.

How did ancient merchants solve this in a single concise line? They
combined finding the unit rate and multiplying: 1. The price of 1 meter
is: $28 / 4 =\$7$. 2. The price of 9 meters is: $9 times 7 =\$63$.

In a single expression: $ ? = frac(28 times 9, 4) = 252 / 4 =\$63 $

Multiply the two numbers located along the diagonal pair, then divide by
the third remaining number! This is also known as
#strong[cross-multiplication].

#divider()

=== 5. Two Classic Applications: Speed and Map Scales
<two-classic-applications-speed-and-map-scales>
==== 1. Traveling at Constant Speed: Kilometers per Hour ($k m\/h$)
<traveling-at-constant-speed-kilometers-per-hour-kmh>
When a train or car travels at a steady speed of
#strong[$80 upright(" km/h")$] (80 kilometers per hour), it covers
$80 upright(" km")$ in exactly $1 upright(" hour")$. Distance covered is
directly proportional to elapsed travel time!

Let us build the table:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (16.67%, 16.67%, 16.67%, 16.67%, 16.67%, 16.67%),
    align: (center,center,center,center,center,center,),
    table.header([Time
      ($h$)], [$1 upright(" h")$], [$2 upright(" h")$], [$3 upright(" h")$], [$30 upright(" min")$
      ($1 / 2 upright(" h")$)], [$15 upright(" min")$
      ($1 / 4 upright(" h")$)],),
    table.hline(),
    [Distance
    ($k m$)], [#strong[$80$]], [$160$], [$240$], [$40$], [$20$],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

- In $2 upright(" h")$: twice as much time
  $arrow.r 2 times 80 = 160 upright(" km")$.
- In $30 upright(" min")$ (half an hour): half the distance
  $arrow.r 80 div 2 = 40 upright(" km")$.
- In $15 upright(" min")$ (a quarter of an hour):
  $80 div 4 = 20 upright(" km")$.

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Road Trip
]
 A car drives at an average speed
of $90 upright(" km/h")$. What distance does it cover in
$2 upright(" h ") 30 upright(" min")$?

- In $2 upright(" h")$: $2 times 90 = 180 upright(" km")$.

- In $30 upright(" min")$ (half-hour): $90 div 2 = 45 upright(" km")$.

- Total distance: $180 + 45 = upright(bold(225 upright(" km")))$.
]

==== 2. Geographic Maps and Architectural Models: Scales
<geographic-maps-and-architectural-models-scales>
On a road map or city floorplan, the real world is scaled down
proportionally. If the scale says:
#strong[$1 upright(" cm on the map") = 100 upright(" m in the real world")$]
This means all real-world distances are divided by $10 thin 000$ (since
$100 upright(" m") = 10 thin 000 upright(" cm")$).

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (20%, 20%, 20%, 20%, 20%),
    align: (center,center,center,center,center,),
    table.header([On the map
      ($c m$)], [$1 upright(" cm")$], [$2 upright(" cm")$], [$5 upright(" cm")$], [$10 upright(" cm")$],),
    table.hline(),
    [In
    reality], [$100 upright(" m")$], [$200 upright(" m")$], [$500 upright(" m")$], [$1 thin 000 upright(" m")$
    ($1 upright(" km")$)],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Forest Hike
]
 On a hiking trail map where
$1 upright(" cm")$ represents $250 upright(" m")$, two mountain villages
are linked by a trail measuring $8 upright(" cm")$ on the map. What is
the actual distance between the two villages?

- $8 times 250 upright(" m") = 2 thin 000 upright(" m") = upright(bold(2 upright(" km")))$.
]

#divider()

=== What You Need to Remember
<what-you-need-to-remember-4>
+ #strong[Two quantities are proportional] if you can move from one to
  the other by multiplying by a fixed constant value (the
  #strong[constant of proportionality]).

+ #strong[Properties of proportionality tables:]

  - You can multiply or divide any column by a constant.
  - You can add or subtract columns together.

+ #strong[The unit rate method:] Find the value for 1 unit (by
  division), then multiply to find the desired target quantity.

+ #strong[The rule of three:] To solve $a / b = c / ?$, calculate
  $? = frac(b times c, a)$.

+ #strong[Constant speed:] Distance is proportional to elapsed time
  ($upright("distance") = upright("speed") times upright("time")$).

+ #strong[Map scale:] Distances drawn on paper are proportional to
  physical distances on the ground.

#divider()

