=== 1. The Problem: The Woolen Blanket and the Black Slate
<the-problem-the-woolen-blanket-and-the-black-slate>
Autumn draws to a close across the grand central square of the trading
city. Northern winds bring the first white frosts, and camel caravans
hasten to unload their bundles before the heavy snows set in.

A young river fisherman pauses before the stall of a cloth merchant from
the high plateaus. The fisherman shivers in his worn linen tunic. He
admires a magnificent alpine wool blanket---thick, soft, and warm.

The merchant announces the price: #emph[“This blanket costs #strong[5
silver coins]!”]

The fisherman unties his leather pouch and counts his coins onto the
wooden table: #emph[One… two…] and that is all! He has only #strong[2
silver coins] in his pouch. He is short by 3 coins. With downcast eyes,
he packs away his two coins and prepares to head back out into the
freezing wind.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Price of the blanket:        5 silver coins
Fisherman's pouch:           2 silver coins
What is missing:             3 silver coins!
    ```
  ]
]

Seeing the fisherman's honest character, the merchant places a
comforting hand on his shoulder: #emph[“Take the blanket today so you do
not freeze tonight. Tomorrow, after your net fishing, bring me the 3
coins that are missing!”]

The fisherman gives heartfelt thanks and walks away wrapped in the snug
warmth of the blanket. The merchant then takes a black slate and a stick
of white chalk to record the transaction in his ledger.

What should he write beside the fisherman's name?

- If he writes “$0$”, that is false! Zero would mean the fisherman owes
  him nothing, that their account is settled.
- The fisherman does not have zero coins: #strong[he holds a debt of 3
  coins].
- To return to a neutral purse of zero ($0$), he must first earn 3
  silver coins and surrender them to the merchant without keeping any
  for himself.

On his slate, the merchant writes the digit 3 preceded by a small
horizontal dash:

$ upright(bold(- 3)) $

You have just met #strong[negative numbers].

#divider()

=== 2. The Heap and the Pit: Zero as Equilibrium
<the-heap-and-the-pit-zero-as-equilibrium>
To understand clearly what a negative number means in the physical
world, let us leave the bustling market and walk down to the river's
sandy beach.

Look at the beach: the wind has smoothed the sand. The ground is level
and flat. This is our starting baseline, our reference level:
#strong[Level Zero ($0$)].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Level 0 (Flat Ground)
    ```
  ]
]

Take a small shovel: 1. Plunge the blade into the sand and lift out one
shovelful. 2. Deposit this shovelful right next to the hole.

Look closely at what your single action created in the landscape:

- You built a #strong[Heap] rising above the ground: a positive
  quantity, written #strong[$+ 1$].
- At the exact same instant, unavoidably, you hollowed out a
  #strong[Pit] sinking below the surface: an absence of sand, a debt of
  matter, written #strong[$- 1$].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
1: The Heap above ground)
Level 0 (Flat Ground)
1: The Pit below ground)
    ```
  ]
]

Now, what happens if you kick the heap $+ 1$ back into the pit $- 1$?
The sand falls back into the cavity. The pit is filled completely. The
beach becomes perfectly flat once more! The heap has vanished, the pit
has vanished: only the zero level of the ground remains.

$ \(+ 1\)+\(- 1\)= 0 $

#quote_box[
#strong[💡 An Illuminating Truth --- The Meaning of Zero
]
 Zero is not an
absolute void. It is #strong[the mutual cancellation of two equal and
opposite forces]: A positive quantity ($+ 1$) and a negative quantity
($- 1$) possess the exact same magnitude. When they meet, they balance
out and yield zero.
]

#divider()

=== 3. Negative Numbers All Around Us
<negative-numbers-all-around-us>
In our daily lives, we encounter negative numbers continuously:

==== 1. Thermometers and the Freezing of Water
<thermometers-and-the-freezing-of-water>
On a Celsius thermometer ($""^compose upright("C")$), zero
($0^compose upright("C")$) is not the absence of heat: it is the precise
temperature at which liquid water turns to solid ice.

- Above zero: water is liquid
  ($+ 5^compose upright("C")\,+ 20^compose upright("C")$).
- Below zero: water freezes, frost forms, snow falls
  ($- 2^compose upright("C")\,- 10^compose upright("C")$).

