=== 6. Simple Additions with Negatives: Filling the Pits
<simple-additions-with-negatives-filling-the-pits>
How do we add signed numbers intuitively? Think of shovelfuls of sand or
monetary balances!

==== Case 1: Two Gains or Two Debts with the Same Sign
<case-1-two-gains-or-two-debts-with-the-same-sign>
- If you gain 3 coins, then gain 4 more: $\(+ 3\)+\(+ 4\)= + 7$.
- If you dig a hole 3 scoops deep, then dig 2 scoops deeper from the
  bottom: you have a hole 5 scoops deep! $ \(- 3\)+\(- 2\)= - 5 $
  #emph[Adding two debts together results in an even larger debt.]

==== Case 2: A Pit and a Heap (Opposite Signs)
<case-2-a-pit-and-a-heap-opposite-signs>
Imagine you have a hole 3 scoops deep ($- 3$) and you arrive with a heap
of 5 scoops ($+ 5$). You pour the sand into the hole:

- 3 scoops completely fill the hole back to level zero ($0$).
- The 2 leftover scoops form a small heap above the ground ($+ 2$)!

$ \(- 3\)+\(+ 5\)= + 2 $

What if you have a hole 6 scoops deep ($- 6$) and only 2 scoops of sand
($+ 2$)? You pour in your 2 scoops: the hole is partly filled, but 4
scoops are still missing! $ \(- 6\)+\(+ 2\)= - 4 $

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Elevator Movements
]
 An elevator is on the
2nd basement level ($- 2$). 1. It rides up 4 floors:
$\(- 2\)+ 4 = upright(bold(+ 2))$ (it arrives on the 2nd floor). 2. From
the 2nd floor ($+ 2$), it travels down 5 floors:
$2 - 5 = upright(bold(- 3))$ (it arrives on the 3rd basement level).
]

#divider()

=== 7. Intervals and Differences: Measuring Temperature Swings
<intervals-and-differences-measuring-temperature-swings>
What is the temperature difference between a freezing morning at
$- 3^compose upright("C")$ and an afternoon at
$+ 5^compose upright("C")$?

To find the gap, count the intervals on the thermometer:

- Rising from $- 3^compose upright("C")$ up to $0^compose upright("C")$:
  you must climb #strong[$3$ degrees].
- Rising from $0^compose upright("C")$ up to $+ 5^compose upright("C")$:
  you must climb another #strong[$5$ degrees].
- Total temperature difference:
  $3 + 5 = upright(bold(8^compose upright("C")))$!

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Elevation Difference
]
 A seaside harbor sits
at $+ 10 upright(" m")$ elevation, and an underwater sea cave sits at
$- 45 upright(" m")$. What is the vertical distance between the harbor
pier and the cave floor? Distance =
$10 upright(" m (above water)") + 45 upright(" m (underwater)") = upright(bold(55 upright(" meters")))$.
]

