=== What You Need to Remember
<what-you-need-to-remember-5>
+ #strong[Negative numbers] carry a minus sign ($-$) and describe values
  beneath a reference baseline of zero (debts, temperatures below
  freezing, basement levels, ocean depths).

+ #strong[Zero] is the origin and balance point where opposites
  neutralize one another: $\(+ a\)+\(- a\)= 0$.

+ #strong[On the number line:] Positive numbers sit to the right of 0;
  negative numbers sit to the left.

+ #strong[Comparison:]

  - Every negative number is less than 0 and less than any positive
    number.
  - Between two negative numbers, the greater one is #strong[closest to
    zero] (furthest right on the axis): $- 2 > - 5$.

+ #strong[Addition:]

  - Two negatives combine to form a larger negative:
    $\(- 3\)+\(- 4\)= - 7$.
  - A positive and a negative partially cancel: $\(- 4\)+ 6 = + 2$ and
    $\(- 7\)+ 3 = - 4$.

+ #strong[Temperature differences:] To measure the difference between a
  negative and a positive temperature, sum their distances from zero
  ($\|- a\|+ b$).

