=== 1. The Problem: The Mud of the Nile and the Wrath of the Farmers
<the-problem-the-mud-of-the-nile-and-the-wrath-of-the-farmers>
You are now ten or eleven years old. Let us leave our small village
behind and journey to the banks of a mighty river: the #strong[Nile], in
Egypt, more than three thousand years ago.

Every year, in the heart of summer, an extraordinary event occurs. Far
to the south, in the highlands of Ethiopia, torrential rains pour down.
The waters of the Nile swell, burst from their banks, and submerge the
entire valley. For weeks on end, the dry earth vanishes beneath a vast
sea of brown water.

For the Egyptians, this #strong[annual flood] (#emph[akhet]) is a
supreme blessing: the receding river leaves behind a layer of intensely
fertile black mud, called #strong[silt]. Thanks to this mineral-rich
mud, wheat and barley flourish in abundance. The ancient Egyptians
affectionately named their homeland #emph[Kemet], “the Black Land.”

Yet when the waters recede in autumn, a nightmare begins for the
villagers: #strong[every single stone marker, every furrow and boundary
peg separating the farm plots has been swept away, displaced, or buried
beneath feet of thick mud.] The immense plain has become a uniform grey
expanse devoid of the slightest landmark.

#emph[“My boundary reached all the way to the tall date palm!”] shouts
Ameni. #emph[“Liar! It ended ten cubits further south!”] bellows Nakht,
raising his hoe in fury.

To prevent bloody land disputes and restore order, the Pharaoh
dispatches his most elite civil servants: the #strong[royal surveyors].
Centuries later, Greek travelers would marvel at them and call them the
#strong[harpedonaptai], which literally translates to “the
rope-stretchers.”

Their mission is formidable: they must lay out #strong[rectangular]
fields once more. And to construct a rectangle, one must produce
#strong[right angles]. In your notebook, you simply reach for your
plastic set square. But across a muddy field two hundred meters wide, a
small hand square is completely useless: if your angle is off by just a
single fraction of a degree at the start, two hundred meters down the
field your line will have drifted by more than three meters… cutting
directly into your neighbor's property!

How can one produce a flawless right angle directly upon the open
ground, without a metal drafting square, without a telescope, and
without a protractor? Before answering this question, we must understand
what an angle truly is.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 The word #strong[geometry] originates from the
ancient Greek #emph[gê] (“the earth”) and #emph[metron] (“measure”).
Geometry was born, quite literally, as “the measurement of the earth.”
The Greek historian Herodotus recorded that geometry was invented in
Egypt out of the urgent practical necessity of re-measuring agricultural
parcels after each Nile inundation.
]

