=== 3. Measuring an Angle: The Degree and the Protractor
<measuring-an-angle-the-degree-and-the-protractor>
==== 3.1 Why 360 Degrees?
<why-360-degrees>
To quantify rotation precisely, we require a standard #strong[unit of
measurement]. Approximately four thousand years ago, the astronomers and
mathematicians of Babylon divided a complete circular turn into
#strong[360 equal slices]. Each elementary slice is called a
#strong[degree], symbolized by a small raised circle:

$ 1^compose quad upright("reads “one degree”") $

Why did the ancients choose 360 instead of a round decimal number like
100? For several compelling reasons:

- The Babylonians computed in #strong[sexagesimal (base 60)]\; our 60
  minutes in an hour and 60 seconds in a minute stem directly from their
  system, and $360 = 6 times 60$.
- The solar year lasts roughly 365 days; to the ancient sky-watchers,
  the Sun appeared to advance across the zodiac by approximately one
  degree each day.
- Above all, #strong[360 is exceptionally divisible]. It can be cleanly
  split into equal integer parts by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, 18,
  20, 24, 30, 36, 40, 45, 60, 72, 90, 120, and 180. A third of a turn,
  an eighth of a turn, a fifth of a turn---all yield whole integer
  degrees!

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Rotation], [Fractional Turn], [Exact Degree Measure],),
    table.hline(),
    [Complete turn], [$1$ full turn], [$360^compose$],
    [Half-turn], [$1 / 2$ turn], [$180^compose$],
    [Quarter-turn], [$1 / 4$ turn], [$90^compose$],
    [Eighth-turn], [$1 / 8$ turn], [$45^compose$],
    [Twelfth-turn (one hour on a clock face)], [$1 / 12$
    turn], [$30^compose$],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

==== 3.2 Families of Angles
<families-of-angles>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Angle Classification
]
 According to its measure
$theta$, an angle is classified as:

- #strong[Zero angle:] $theta = 0^compose$ (the two rays completely
  overlap).

- #strong[Acute angle:] $0^compose < theta < 90^compose$ (sharp,
  pointed, narrower than a quarter-turn).

- #strong[Right angle:] $theta = 90^compose$ (the perpendicular corner).

- #strong[Obtuse angle:] $90^compose < theta < 180^compose$ (wide,
  blunt, opened wider than a quarter-turn).

- #strong[Straight angle:] $theta = 180^compose$ (the two rays form a
  continuous straight line).

- #strong[Reflex angle:] $180^compose < theta < 360^compose$ (an angle
  opening beyond a straight line).

- #strong[Full (perigon) angle:] $theta = 360^compose$ (one complete
  rotation).
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Acute            Right             Obtuse               Straight
□)         ___\_____       _____________._____________
    ```
  ]
]

In geometric drawings, a right angle is always highlighted by a
#strong[small square] nestled in the corner: $corner.l.b$.

#emph[Mnemonic tip:] #strong[Acute] shares its Latin root with
#emph[needle] (sharp, piercing, narrow); #strong[obtuse] shares its root
with #emph[blunt] (dull, wide open).

==== 3.3 Mastering the Protractor
<mastering-the-protractor>
A standard protractor is a transparent semicircular tool graduated from
$0^compose$ to $180^compose$. It features #strong[two concentric
scales]: one reads from left to right, and the other reads from right to
left. This dual scale is the source of nearly all beginner measurement
blunders!

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Measuring an Angle $accent(A B C, ̂)$ with a
Protractor
]
 1. Place the #strong[center point] of the protractor (the
crosshair or small hole) directly over the #strong[vertex] $B$. 2. Align
the #strong[baseline zero-mark] of the protractor perfectly along one of
the rays, say $\[B A\)$. 3. Locate the scale #strong[that starts at
$0^compose$ on that chosen ray], follow it around the rim, and read the
number where the second ray $\[B C\)$ crosses the curved graduation. 4.
#strong[Perform a visual sanity check:] Look at the angle with your
naked eye! Is it acute or obtuse? Your numerical reading must agree with
physical reality.
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Warning: Double Scale Trap!
]
 If an angle is visibly acute but
your reading says “$130^compose$”, you have read from the wrong
concentric ring! The correct reading is
$180^compose - 130^compose = 50^compose$. Asking yourself #emph[“Is it
sharp or blunt?”] prevents 99% of mistakes.
]

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Constructing an Angle of Given Measure (e.g.,
$accent(x O y, ̂) = 65^compose$)
]
 1. Draw a straight ray $\[O x\)$ with a
ruler. 2. Place the protractor center on point $O$, aligning the
zero-line with $\[O x\)$. 3. Starting from $0^compose$ along $\[O x\)$,
count up to $65^compose$ and make a crisp pencil tick on the paper. 4.
Remove the protractor and use your ruler to draw ray $\[O y\)$ from
vertex $O$ through your tick mark. 5. Mark the angle with a small arc
and label it $65^compose$.
]

==== Solved Examples
<solved-examples>
#strong[Solved Example 1 --- Classifying Angles.] Classify each of the
following angular measures: $17^compose$\; $90^compose$\; $91^compose$\;
$179^compose$\; $180^compose$\; $45^compose$.

#emph[Solution:] $17^compose$ and $45^compose$ are #strong[acute]
(strictly between $0^compose$ and $90^compose$). $90^compose$ is a
#strong[right] angle. $91^compose$ and $179^compose$ are #strong[obtuse]
(strictly between $90^compose$ and $180^compose$). $180^compose$ is a
#strong[straight] angle. #emph[Remark:] Even though $91^compose$ looks
almost identical to a right angle to the untrained eye, in geometry a
single degree makes all the difference: it is strictly obtuse!

#strong[Solved Example 2 --- Clock Hands.] What angle is formed by the
hands of an analog clock at 2:00? At 5:00?

#emph[Solution:] A clock face represents a complete turn of
$360^compose$ partitioned into 12 hourly increments. Between any two
consecutive numbers, the angle is: $ 360^compose div 12 = 30^compose $
At 2:00, the minute hand points at 12 and the hour hand at 2. There are
2 hourly intervals:
$ 2 times 30^compose = 60^compose quad upright("(acute angle)") $ At
5:00, there are 5 intervals:
$ 5 times 30^compose = 150^compose quad upright("(obtuse angle)") $

#strong[Solved Example 3 --- Reading a Protractor Without Confusion.]
Leah places her protractor over angle $accent(D E F, ̂)$ (vertex $E$,
with ray $\[E D\)$ aligned with the zero mark on the right). Ray
$\[E F\)$ passes through the marks $40$ and $140$. The angle is clearly
wide open. What is its measure?

#emph[Solution:] Ray $\[E D\)$ lies on the #strong[right] zero; we must
follow the scale starting at $0$ on the right. Alternatively, using our
visual check: the angle is visibly obtuse (wider than $90^compose$), so
it cannot possibly be $40^compose$. Therefore,
$accent(D E F, ̂) = 140^compose$.

