=== 4. Perpendicular and Parallel Lines
<perpendicular-and-parallel-lines>
==== 4.1 Two Intersecting Lines
<two-intersecting-lines>
When two straight lines cross at a point, they form #strong[four
angles]:

#figure(image("figures/vol_3/fig_15_angles_opposes.svg", alt: "Angles opposés par le sommet et égalité géométrique"),
  caption: [
    Angles opposés par le sommet et égalité géométrique
  ]
)

- Angles ① and ③ are called #strong[vertically opposite angles] (or
  vertical angles): they sit face-to-face across the intersection, like
  the two blades of an open pair of scissors. They have
  #strong[identical measures].
- Angles ① and ② lie side-by-side along a straight line: their measures
  add up to $180^compose$. They are called #strong[supplementary
  angles].

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Vocabulary --- Angle Pairs
]


- Two angles are #strong[supplementary] if their sum equals
  $180^compose$.

- Two angles are #strong[complementary] if their sum equals
  $90^compose$.

- Two angles are #strong[adjacent] if they share the same vertex, have a
  common side, and lie on opposite sides of that common side.
]

#strong[Why are vertically opposite angles strictly equal?] Because ① +
② $= 180^compose$ and ② + ③ $= 180^compose$. Both ① and ③ complete the
exact same angle ② to reach a straight line ($180^compose$). Therefore:
$ upright("Angle ①") = 180^compose - upright("Angle ②") = upright("Angle ③") $
This is our very first formal #strong[geometric proof]: we did not rely
on physical measurements, but solely on deductive logical reasoning!

==== 4.2 Perpendicular Lines
<perpendicular-lines>
If just one of the four angles at an intersection is a right angle
($90^compose$), then all four angles are automatically right angles
($180^compose - 90^compose = 90^compose$).

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Perpendicular Lines
]
 Two lines are
#strong[perpendicular] if they intersect at a right angle
($90^compose$). We write: $ \(d_1\)perp\(d_2\) $ and read “line $d_1$ is
perpendicular to line $d_2$\.” The symbol $perp$ is itself a graphic
depiction of two perpendicular lines!
]

==== 4.3 Parallel Lines
<parallel-lines>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Parallel Lines
]
 Two lines lying in the same
plane are #strong[parallel] if they never intersect---no matter how far
they are extended---or if they are identical (coincident). We write:
$ \(d_1\)parallel\(d_2\)quad upright("or") quad\(d_1\)thin\/\/thin\(d_2\) $
and read “$d_1$ is parallel to $d_2$\.” The symbol depicts two
side-by-side lines that never meet, like straight railway tracks.
]

Three fundamental properties govern these relationships in a Euclidean
plane:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Euclidean Properties ---
]


- #strong[P1.] If two lines are perpendicular to the same third line,
  then they are parallel to each other.

- #strong[P2.] If two lines are parallel, any third line perpendicular
  to one is also perpendicular to the other.

- #strong[P3.] If two lines are parallel to the same third line, they
  are parallel to each other.
]

#figure(image("figures/vol_3/fig_15_paralleles_perpendiculaires.svg", alt: "Droites parallèles et perpendiculaires fondamentales"),
  caption: [
    Droites parallèles et perpendiculaires fondamentales
  ]
)

The logical symbol $arrow.r.double$ reads “therefore” or “implies”: the
truth of the statement on the left guarantees the truth of the statement
on the right.

==== 4.4 Construction with Tools
<construction-with-tools>
#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Drawing the Perpendicular to $\(d\)$ Through Point
$A$ (Set Square)
]
 Place one side of the set square's right angle firmly
along line $\(d\)$. Slide the set square along $\(d\)$ until its other
perpendicular side meets point $A$. Draw the line through $A$.
]

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Drawing the Parallel to $\(d\)$ Through Point $A$
(Ruler + Set Square)
]
 First, draw the line $\(p\)$ perpendicular to
$\(d\)$ passing through $A$. Then construct the line perpendicular to
$\(p\)$ passing through $A$. By property #strong[P1], this second line
is strictly parallel to $\(d\)$.
]

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- The Compass-Only Perpendicular (The Master
Builder's Method)
]
 1. Place the compass point at $A$ and strike an arc
that cuts line $\(d\)$ at two points, $M$ and $N$. 2. With a wider
opening, place the compass point at $M$, then at $N$, and strike two
intersecting arcs at a point $P$ (on the other side of $\(d\)$). 3. Draw
line $\(A P\)$: it is guaranteed to be perpendicular to $\(d\)$.
#emph[Why?] Both $A$ and $P$ are equidistant from $M$ and $N$, placing
them on the #strong[perpendicular bisector] of segment $\[M N\]$.
]

This compass method is precisely what Egyptian and medieval master
builders performed on vast building sites using #strong[two pegs and a
piece of cord] functioning as a giant compass!

==== 4.5 Distance from a Point to a Line
<distance-from-a-point-to-a-line>
Imagine you are standing at point $A$ in the middle of a field and wish
to reach a straight paved road $\(d\)$ as quickly as possible.
Instinctively, you walk straight towards the road at a
#strong[perpendicular angle].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Distance from a Point to a Line
]
 The
#strong[distance from point $A$ to line $\(d\)$] is the length of
segment $\[A H\]$, where $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular dropped
from $A$ onto $\(d\)$. It represents the shortest possible path
connecting $A$ to $\(d\)$.
]

==== Solved Example 4 --- Angles Around an Intersection
<solved-example-4-angles-around-an-intersection>
Two straight lines intersect at $O$. One of the angles measures
$38^compose$. Find the measures of the remaining three angles.

#emph[Solution:] The vertically opposite angle also measures
$38^compose$. The two adjacent angles are supplementary to $38^compose$:
$ 180^compose - 38^compose = 142^compose $ The four angles measure
$38^compose$, $142^compose$, $38^compose$, and $142^compose$.
#emph[Verification:]
$38^compose + 142^compose + 38^compose + 142^compose = 360^compose$ (one
full rotation). ✔

#divider()

=== 5. The Sum of the Angles in Any Triangle: Always $180^compose$
<the-sum-of-the-angles-in-any-triangle-always-180circ>
Here stands one of the most stunning and universal truths of all
geometry: take any triangle in the universe---whether microscopic or
spanning light-years, razor-sharp or flat and obtuse---and measure its
three internal angles. Add them together. #strong[The sum will always be
exactly $180^compose$!]

$ hat(A) + hat(B) + hat(C) = 180^compose $

Let us explore three distinct ways to convince ourselves of this eternal
truth.

==== 5.1 Proof 1: The Paper-Tear Experiment (Physical Intuition)
<proof-1-the-paper-tear-experiment-physical-intuition>
+ Take a sheet of blank paper and draw any triangle you like with a
  ruler.
+ Color each of the three corners with a different colored pencil: Blue
  at vertex $A$, Red at vertex $B$, and Amber at vertex $C$.
+ Carefully cut out the triangle, then tear off the three colored
  corners.
+ Arrange the three points together on a flat tabletop so their tips
  meet at a single common point, placing the colored edges snugly
  side-by-side:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Paper Triangle                        Torn Corners Reassembled
A (Blue)                                      A
Blue    /  \   Red
Red) B /______\ C (Amber)
Amber           Line = 180°
    ```
  ]
]

The three angles fit together seamlessly to form a perfect straight
edge! Since a straight edge represents a straight angle of
$180^compose$, their sum is demonstrably $180^compose$.

==== 5.2 Proof 2: The Euclidean Proof (Deductive Rigor)
<proof-2-the-euclidean-proof-deductive-rigor>
To transform this sensory experiment into a watertight mathematical
proof, we follow Euclid's luminous reasoning using parallel lines:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
D  A  E       Line (DE) // (BC)
Angle B' /   \ Angle C'
BC
    ```
  ]
]

+ Given any triangle $A B C$, construct line $\(D E\)$ passing through
  vertex $A$ parallel to the opposite base $\(B C\)$.

+ The three angles meeting at point $A$ along the straight line
  $\(D E\)$ form a straight angle:
  $ accent(D A B, ̂) + accent(B A C, ̂) + accent(C A E, ̂) = 180^compose $

+ Because $\(D E\)$ is parallel to $\(B C\)$, line $\(A B\)$ acts as a
  transversal line cutting two parallel lines:

  - Angle $accent(D A B, ̂)$ and angle $accent(A B C, ̂)$ are
    #strong[alternate interior angles], so they are equal:
    $accent(D A B, ̂) = hat(B)$.
  - Similarly, angle $accent(C A E, ̂)$ and angle $accent(A C B, ̂)$ are
    alternate interior angles, so: $accent(C A E, ̂) = hat(C)$.

+ Substituting these equalities into our sum yields:
  $ hat(B) + hat(A) + hat(C) = 180^compose $ The proof is complete!

==== 5.3 Proof 3: The Walker's Turn (Algorithmic / Robotic View)
<proof-3-the-walkers-turn-algorithmic-robotic-view>
Imagine a tiny explorer (or a robot) walking along the perimeter of
triangle $A B C$:

- The walker starts at $A$, walks toward $B$, and at vertex $B$ turns
  left by exterior angle $theta_1 = 180^compose - hat(B)$.
- She walks to $C$ and turns left by $theta_2 = 180^compose - hat(C)$.
- She walks back to $A$ and turns left to face her initial heading:
  $theta_3 = 180^compose - hat(A)$.

Upon returning to $A$, the walker has made exactly #strong[one full
rotation] ($360^compose$) in the plane:
$ \(180^compose - hat(A)\)+\(180^compose - hat(B)\)+\(180^compose - hat(C)\)= 360^compose $
$ 540^compose -\(hat(A) + hat(B) + hat(C)\)= 360^compose $
$ hat(A) + hat(B) + hat(C) = 540^compose - 360^compose = 180^compose $

#divider()

=== 6. Special Triangles and the Secret of the Thirteen-Knot Rope
<special-triangles-and-the-secret-of-the-thirteen-knot-rope>
==== 6.1 The Equilateral Triangle
<the-equilateral-triangle>
An #strong[equilateral triangle] has three sides of equal length. By
symmetry, its three internal angles are also strictly equal:
$ hat(A) = hat(B) = hat(C) = 180^compose / 3 = 60^compose $ Every
equilateral triangle in the universe has three $60^compose$ angles.

==== 6.2 The Isosceles Triangle
<the-isosceles-triangle>
An #strong[isosceles triangle] has two sides of equal length. The angles
sitting opposite those equal sides (called the #strong[base angles]) are
strictly equal:

- If the apex angle is $40^compose$, the two base angles share the
  remaining $140^compose$ equally:
  $ frac(180^compose - 40^compose, 2) = 70^compose quad upright("each") $

==== 6.3 The Right-Angled Triangle
<the-right-angled-triangle>
A #strong[right-angled triangle] has one right angle ($90^compose$). The
other two acute angles must therefore absorb the remaining $90^compose$:
$ hat(A) + hat(B) = 90^compose $ The two acute angles of a right
triangle are always #strong[complementary]. If one measures
$35^compose$, the other must measure
$90^compose - 35^compose = 55^compose$.

==== 6.4 The Thirteen-Knot Rope: The Harpedonaptai's Secret Weapon
<the-thirteen-knot-rope-the-harpedonaptais-secret-weapon>
We can now resolve the mystery posed in our opening problem: how did the
Egyptian rope-stretchers lay out a flawless right angle on the ground?

They used a single length of rope tied with #strong[thirteen knots
spaced at identical intervals], and joined its ends to form a continuous
closed loop:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Knot 1  Knot 2  Knot 3  Knot 4  ...  Knot 13 (tied to Knot 1)
12 equal intervals between the 13 knots
    ```
  ]
]

Because knot 13 joins knot 1, the loop contains exactly #strong[12 equal
segments]. The surveyors drove three wooden stakes into the ground and
pulled the rope tight around them, distributing the segments as follows:

- #strong[3 segments] along the first side (from knot 1 to knot 4);
- #strong[4 segments] along the second side (from knot 4 to knot 8);
- #strong[5 segments] along the third side (from knot 8 back to knot
  13/1).

$ 3 + 4 + 5 = 12 upright(" segments") $

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Knot 4 (Stake)
3 units  |                                |     \  5 units
Knot 8 (Stake)
Knot 1 & 13       4 units
Stake)
    ```
  ]
]

When pulled taut, the angle formed between the side of 3 units and the
side of 4 units is #strong[guaranteed to be an absolute, mathematical
right angle ($90^compose$)]! #emph[Why does this work?] Because
$3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2$. This is the famous
#strong[$\(3\,4\,5\)$ Pythagorean triple], an algebraic harmony
discovered empirically by Egyptian builders more than a millennium
before Pythagoras was born!

#divider()

=== 7. 🛠️ The Boundary Case Laboratory
<the-boundary-case-laboratory>
To sharpen our mathematical thinking, let us test the boundaries of our
rules where everyday intuition falters:

==== Case 1: Can a Triangle Have Two Right Angles?
<case-1-can-a-triangle-have-two-right-angles>
Suppose a triangle had two right angles: $hat(A) = 90^compose$ and
$hat(B) = 90^compose$. The sum of all three angles must equal
$180^compose$:
$ hat(C) = 180^compose -\(90^compose + 90^compose\)= 0^compose $ An
angle of $0^compose$ means the two sides are parallel and never meet! In
a Euclidean flat plane, #strong[it is strictly impossible for a triangle
to possess two right angles].

#quote_box[
#strong[🌍 Beyond Flat Space --- Spherical Geometry:
]
 However, on the
curved surface of a sphere---such as planet Earth---lines of longitude
meet the equator at $90^compose$ right angles, and they also meet at the
North Pole! A triangle drawn from the North Pole down to the equator at
$0^compose$ longitude, along the equator to $90^compose$ East longitude,
and back up to the pole has #strong[three right angles]
($90^compose + 90^compose + 90^compose = 270^compose$). The
$180^compose$ theorem is true only in #strong[flat Euclidean space]!
]

==== Case 2: The Degenerate (Flat) Triangle
<case-2-the-degenerate-flat-triangle>
What happens if the vertex $C$ is pushed down onto the base segment
$\[A B\]$? The angle $hat(C)$ opens all the way to $180^compose$, while
$hat(A)$ and $hat(B)$ collapse to $0^compose$:
$ 0^compose + 180^compose + 0^compose = 180^compose $ The triangle
flattens into a single straight line segment. It is called a
#strong[degenerate triangle].

==== Case 3: Angles in Non-Coplanar Space
<case-3-angles-in-non-coplanar-space>
In three-dimensional space, two lines that never intersect are not
necessarily parallel! Think of a bridge crossing high above a highway:
the road below and the bridge above never meet, yet they point in
different directions. Such lines are called #strong[skew lines] (or
non-coplanar lines). Parallelism requires the lines to lie within the
#strong[same flat plane].

#divider()

=== What You Must Remember
<what-you-must-remember>
+ #strong[Nature of an Angle:] An angle measures an opening of rotation
  between two rays sharing a common vertex. It does not depend on the
  length of the drawn strokes.

+ #strong[Standard Units:] A full turn is $360^compose$\; a straight
  angle is $180^compose$\; a right angle is $90^compose$.

+ #strong[Protractor Sanity Check:] Always verify whether the angle is
  acute ($< 90^compose$) or obtuse ($> 90^compose$) before recording
  your measurement.

+ #strong[Opposite and Supplementary Angles:] Vertically opposite angles
  are equal. Adjacent angles along a straight line are supplementary
  (sum $= 180^compose$).

+ #strong[Sum of Angles in a Triangle:] For any flat triangle,
  $hat(A) + hat(B) + hat(C) = 180^compose$.

+ #strong[Special Triangles:]

  - Equilateral: three equal sides, three $60^compose$ angles.

  - Isosceles: two equal sides, two equal base angles.

  - Right: one $90^compose$ angle, acute angles sum to $90^compose$.

+ #strong[The Thirteen-Knot Rope:] A closed loop with 12 equal intervals
  formed into a $3 - 4 - 5$ triangle guarantees a perfect $90^compose$
  corner.

#divider()

