=== 2. From the Magic Instant to Universal Proportions
<from-the-magic-instant-to-universal-proportions>
==== 2.1 The First Insight: The Magic Hour
<the-first-insight-the-magic-hour>
Thales sits in the warm sand and carefully observes the path of the sun.

- Early in the morning, the sun sits low on the horizon: shadows stretch
  out enormously across the plain.
- At noon, the sun reaches its zenith overhead: shadows shrink to tiny
  pools huddled under one's feet.
- Through the afternoon, the sun descends again, and the shadows begin
  to lengthen once more.

Thales drives his wooden staff vertically into the sand. The staff
stands exactly #strong[2 meters tall]. He marks a tick mark in the sand
and tracks the tip of the staff's shadow:

- At 2:00 PM, the shadow measures $1.20 upright(" m")$ (shorter than the
  staff).
- At 3:00 PM, it measures $1.60 upright(" m")$.
- Suddenly, at #strong[3:45 PM], Thales measures the shadow again:
  #strong[it measures precisely 2 meters!]

At that singular instant, a geometric miracle occurs:
$ upright("Length of Staff’s Shadow") = upright("Actual Height of Staff") = 2 upright(" meters") $

The sunlight strikes the ground at an angle of exactly $45^compose$,
forming an #strong[isosceles right-angled triangle] with the upright
staff and the horizontal ground. Thales shouts to the servants:
#emph[“Measure the shadow of the pyramid right now!”] At that exact
second, the length of the pyramid's shadow (measured from the vertical
line below its apex to the far tip of its shadow) is #strong[strictly
equal to its true vertical height]!

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 According to the Greek historian Plutarch,
Pharaoh Amasis was so utterly astonished to see Thales determine the
monument's height in just a few paces that he showered the philosopher
with gifts and revered him as the wisest living mortal.
]

==== 2.2 What If the Sun Won't Wait? The Power of Proportion
<what-if-the-sun-wont-wait-the-power-of-proportion>
The “magic hour” technique is brilliant, but it has one glaring
limitation: one must wait for the exact moment when the solar angle
reaches $45^compose$. What if a storm cloud hides the sun at 3:45 PM?
What if you must measure a tall tree at 10:00 AM?

Thales realized a far deeper truth: #strong[sunlight rays, arriving from
an astronomical body 150 million kilometers away, reach the surface of
the Earth along lines that are virtually parallel to one another.]

Therefore, at #strong[any hour of the day], the pyramid and its shadow
form a triangle that has the #strong[exact same geometric shape] as the
triangle formed by the staff and its shadow! One triangle is simply an
#strong[enlargement] of the other.

