=== 4. Powers of 10 and Negative Exponents
<powers-of-10-and-negative-exponents>
The number $10$ is the sovereign ruler of our decimal system (inherited
from our ten human fingers). Powers of 10 are the supreme engine of
modern scientific calculation.

==== 4.1 Positive Powers of 10: The March of Zeros
<positive-powers-of-10-the-march-of-zeros>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Rule --- Positive Powers of 10
]
 For any positive integer $n$:
$ 10^n = 1 underbrace(000 dots.h 0, n upright(" zeros")) $ The exponent
indicates #strong[the exact number of zeros] following the leading digit
1!
]

- $10^1 = 10$ (ten)
- $10^2 = 100$ (one hundred)
- $10^3 = 1\,000$ (one thousand, metric prefix #emph[kilo-])
- $10^6 = 1\,000\,000$ (one million, metric prefix #emph[mega-])
- $10^9 = 1\,000\,000\,000$ (one billion, metric prefix #emph[giga-])
- $10^12 = 1\,000\,000\,000\,000$ (one trillion, metric prefix
  #emph[tera-])

==== 4.2 Negative Exponents: Journey into the Infinitesimal
<negative-exponents-journey-into-the-infinitesimal>
Let us continue stepping down our arithmetic ladder by dividing by 10 at
each successive step:

$ 10^3 & = 1\,000\
10^2 & = 100\
10^1 & = 10\
10^0 & = 1\
10^(- 1) & = 1 / 10 = 0.1\
10^(- 2) & = 1 / 10^2 = 1 / 100 = 0.01\
10^(- 3) & = 1 / 10^3 = frac(1, 1\,000) = 0.001 $

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Negative Exponents
]
 For any non-zero base
$a eq.not 0$ and any integer $n$: $ a^(- n) = 1 / a^n $ Specifically for
powers of 10:
$ 10^(- n) = 1 / 10^n = underbrace(0.00 dots.h 0, n upright(" total zeros")) 1 $
A negative exponent designates the #strong[reciprocal] of the power. In
$10^(- n)$, there are #strong[$n$ zeros in total], including the leading
zero before the decimal point!
]

- $10^(- 1) = 0.1$ (one tenth, prefix #emph[deci-])
- $10^(- 2) = 0.01$ (one hundredth, prefix #emph[centi-])
- $10^(- 3) = 0.001$ (one thousandth, prefix #emph[milli-])
- $10^(- 6) = 0.000001$ (one millionth, prefix #emph[micro-])
- $10^(- 9) = 0.000000001$ (one billionth, prefix #emph[nano-])

#divider()

=== 5. The Golden Rules of Exponent Arithmetic
<the-golden-rules-of-exponent-arithmetic>
When manipulating powers with the same base, computation transforms
magically: #strong[multiplication of powers becomes simple addition of
exponents!]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Exponent Laws
]
 For all integers $m$ and $n$, and
non-zero bases $a$ and $b$:

+ #strong[Product of Powers with Same Base:]
  $ a^m times a^n = a^(m + n) $ #emph[\(Example:
  $10^3 times 10^4 = 10^(3 + 4) = 10^7$)]

+ #strong[Quotient of Powers with Same Base:] $ a^m / a^n = a^(m - n) $
  #emph[\(Example: $10^7 / 10^2 = 10^(7 - 2) = 10^5$\;
  $10^3 / 10^8 = 10^(3 - 8) = 10^(- 5)$)]

+ #strong[Power of a Power:] $ \(a^m\)^n = a^(m times n) $
  #emph[\(Example: $\(10^3\)^4 = 10^(3 times 4) = 10^12$)]

+ #strong[Power of a Product:] $ \(a times b\)^n = a^n times b^n $
  #emph[\(Example:
  $\(2 times 5\)^3 = 2^3 times 5^3 = 8 times 125 = 1\,000 = 10^3$)]
]

#divider()

=== 6. Scientific Notation: Writing the Universe
<scientific-notation-writing-the-universe>
How can one compare the mass of a single hydrogen atom
($0.00000000000000000000000000167 upright(" kg")$) to the mass of planet
Earth ($5\,972\,000\,000\,000\,000\,000\,000\,000 upright(" kg")$)? With
standard digits, human eyes lose count of the zeros instantly!
Scientists therefore established an international standard format:
#strong[scientific notation].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Scientific Notation
]
 A number is written in
#strong[scientific notation] when formatted strictly as:
$ a times 10^n $ where:

- $a$ is a decimal number with #strong[exactly one non-zero digit before
  the decimal point] ($1 lt.eq a < 10$ or $- 10 < a lt.eq - 1$).
- $n$ is an integer (positive, zero, or negative).
]

==== Rapid Conversion Technique
<rapid-conversion-technique>
- For large numbers ($> 10$): shift the decimal point $n$ places to the
  #strong[left] $arrow.r.double.long 10^(+ n)$.
  $ 149\,600\,000 upright(" km") = 1.496 times 10^8 upright(" km") quad\(upright("Earth-Sun distance")\) $

- For small numbers ($< 1$): shift the decimal point $n$ places to the
  #strong[right] $arrow.r.double.long 10^(- n)$.
  $ 0.00000012 upright(" m") = 1.2 times 10^(- 7) upright(" m") quad\(upright("size of a virus")\) $

