=== 1. The Problem: The White Brotherhood of Croton
<the-problem-the-white-brotherhood-of-croton>
Twenty-five hundred years ago, in the sun-drenched maritime city of
Croton in southern Italy, flourished a cloistered community that both
fascinated and unsettled the ancient Mediterranean: #strong[the
Pythagorean school].

Its initiates dressed in flowing white linen robes. They ate no meat,
held no private property, and observed up to five years of absolute
silence to master their unruly thoughts. They recognized one another
through a secret emblem drawn in the palm of the hand: the five-pointed
star pentagram.

Engraved upon the portal of their academy was their supreme doctrine:
$ upright(bold(upright("“ALL IS NUMBER”"))) $

To master #strong[Pythagoras] and his disciples, this was no poetic
figure of speech. They firmly believed that #strong[the entire
cosmos---the orbital dance of planets, the changing seasons, the justice
of the laws, and the music of the lyre---was built strictly upon whole
integers ($1\,2\,3\,4 dots.h$) and their rational fractions
($1 / 2\,2 / 3\,3 / 4 dots.h$).]

When you pinch a vibrating lyre string at its exact midpoint ($1 / 2$),
it rings with the same pitch one octave higher. When you pinch it at
two-thirds of its length ($2 / 3$), it sings a pure fifth. To them, all
of creation was harmony, light, and integer proportion: every physical
length across the earth could be measured by a ratio of whole numbers!

Until the fateful day when a young disciple named #strong[Hippasus of
Metapontum] took a wooden stylus and drew a seemingly innocent figure in
the dust. He did not sketch an elaborate polygon. He drew the simplest,
humblest square imaginable: #strong[a square whose sides measure exactly
1 meter.]

Then, he connected two opposite corners with a straight line: he drew
#strong[the diagonal].

