    ```text
Leg of the      |       \  Hypotenuse (the longest side)
right angle     |         \
A    | C
Leg of the right angle
    ```
  ]
]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Right Triangle and Hypotenuse
]


- A #strong[right-angled triangle] (or right triangle) is a triangle
  possessing one #strong[right angle] ($90^compose$).

- The side directly facing the right angle is called the
  #strong[hypotenuse].

- The hypotenuse is #strong[always the strictly longest] of the three
  sides in any right triangle.

- The two shorter sides that meet to form the right angle are called the
  #strong[legs] (or sides of the right angle).
]

#quote_box[
#strong[💡 Where Does This Strange Word Come From?
]
 The word
#emph[hypotenuse] originates from the ancient Greek #emph[hupoteinousa],
formed from #emph[hupo] (“under”) and #emph[teinein] (“to stretch”). The
hypotenuse is literally the string “stretched beneath” the right angle,
just like the taut bowstring of an archer or the staysail stay of a
ship.
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Pythagorean Theorem: The Dance of Three Squares
<the-pythagorean-theorem-the-dance-of-three-squares>
The theorem is not originally an abstract algebraic formula, but an
astonishing truth about the #strong[geometric areas of squares] resting
against the triangle's flanks.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 The Pythagorean Theorem (Geometric Statement)
]
 In any
right-angled triangle, the area of the square constructed upon the
hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the areas of the two squares
constructed upon the two legs.
]

#figure(image("figures/vol_3/fig_19_pythagore_puzzle.svg", alt: "Démonstration géométrique du Théorème de Pythagore"),
  caption: [
    Démonstration géométrique du Théorème de Pythagore
  ]
)

Denoting $a$ and $b$ as the lengths of the two legs, and $c$ as the
length of the hypotenuse:

- The area of the first leg square is $a^2$ ($a times a$).
- The area of the second leg square is $b^2$ ($b times b$).
- The area of the hypotenuse square is $c^2$ ($c times c$).

The foundational equality is written: $ upright(bold(a^2 + b^2 = c^2)) $

If the triangle is labeled $A B C$ with its right angle at vertex $A$,
the hypotenuse is $\[B C\]$, and the formula is expressed via its
vertices: $ B C^2 = A B^2 + A C^2 $

==== The Three Historic Visual Demonstrations
<the-three-historic-visual-demonstrations>
Why does this equality hold true for #strong[every single right triangle
in the universe]? Here are three visual proofs you can understand
without writing a single algebraic equation, relying purely on your own
eyes.

==== Proof 1: The Café-Tile Floor Puzzle
<proof-1-the-café-tile-floor-puzzle>
This is the simplest demonstration in existence, applying to an
isosceles right triangle (half of a square):

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Small Square 1     Small Square 2           Large Hypotenuse Square
2 triangles        2 triangles                  4 triangles united!
    ```
  ]
]

Each small square resting on a leg contains 2 half-square right
triangles, totaling $2 + 2 = 4$ triangles. The tilted square resting
upon the hypotenuse contains #strong[precisely 4 identical half-square
triangles]! The combined area is visibly identical.

==== Proof 2: The Master Square in Two Arrangements
<proof-2-the-master-square-in-two-arrangements>
Draw a large square of side length $\(a + b\)$. We can arrange 4
identical copies of our right triangle (with sides $a\,b\,c$) inside
this boundary in two distinct ways:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Arrangement 1                           Arrangement 2
a        b                      \                /
Square                       \  c     c     /
a²                                 \            /
Square                       \    /
4 right triangles                        Square   /
nestled in corners                    c   c²    c
b²
Remaining Area = a² + b²                Remaining Area = c²
    ```
  ]
]

In both identical enclosing squares:

- On the left, we remove the 4 triangles: the remaining uncovered area
  is $a^2 + b^2$.
- On the right, we remove the exact same 4 triangles: the remaining
  uncovered area is $c^2$.

Because we subtracted the exact same total area from identical large
squares, what remains must be strictly equal: $ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $

==== Proof 3: Bhāskara's “Behold!”
<proof-3-bhāskaras-behold>
In 12th-century India, mathematician Bhāskara arranged 4 identical right
triangles of sides $a\,b\,c$ into an outer square of side $c$, leaving
an interior empty square of side $\(b - a\)$:
$ upright("Total Area") = c^2 = 4 times (frac(a times b, 2)) +\(b - a\)^2 = 2 a b +\(b^2 - 2 a b + a^2\)= a^2 + b^2 $
Beneath his diagram, Bhāskara wrote not a single sentence of
explanation, but a single command: #emph[“Behold!”]

==== Proof 4: Oliver Byrne's Color Semiotic Proof (1847)
<proof-4-oliver-byrnes-color-semiotic-proof-1847>
In 1847, the civil engineer and mathematician Oliver Byrne published an
extraordinary edition of #emph[Euclid's Elements] in London, banishing
alphabetical lettering in favor of vibrant primary colors: brilliant
blues, bold reds, and warm ambers. The squares constructed upon the legs
decompose into colored triangles of matching area, rendering
$a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ self-evident at a single glance. It is this
masterpiece of visual semiotics that inspired the chromatic charter of
the present treatise.

