    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
50 m North
50 m West   [ Standing Stone ]   50 m East
50 m South
    ```
  ]
]

As we proved in Chapter 16, all the points located at 50 meters from the
central stone form an #strong[immense circle surrounding the marker]! If
you must excavate every trench along that circular perimeter, you will
have to dig a ditch over 314 meters long. By the time you finish, you
will be an old hermit with a long white beard!

A single number is insufficient to pinpoint a unique position across a
two-dimensional surface. To locate any object without hesitation, one
requires #strong[two independent pieces of information].

#divider()

=== 2. The Fly on the Ceiling of René Descartes
<the-fly-on-the-ceiling-of-rené-descartes>
To solve this riddle, let us leave our tropical island and travel
through time to the bitter winter of #strong[1637] in France.

Inside a bedroom of a venerable stone house, a forty-year-old gentleman
rests beneath heavy feather quilts well past mid-morning. His name is
#strong[René Descartes]. Descartes had suffered from frail health since
boyhood, and his Jesuit tutors had granted him the lifetime habit of
lingering in bed each morning to let his mind meditate in quiet
stillness.

As he gazes upward, a small black housefly lands upon the white plaster
ceiling. The fly crawls a few inches, pauses, buzzes into the air, and
settles on a new spot. Descartes asks himself a deceptively simple
question that no human mind had ever formulated with such precision:
#emph[“How could I describe the exact, instantaneous position of that
fly to a friend across the room without needing to climb a ladder and
point my finger at it?”]

