=== 1. The Problem: The Medieval Trade Fair and the Quarrel of Merchants
<the-problem-the-medieval-trade-fair-and-the-quarrel-of-merchants>
We are in the 13th century, in the heart of Champagne, during the great
Trade Fair of Troyes. Merchants from Venice, Flanders, and Castile have
raised hundreds of striped canvas stalls overflowing with fragrant
spices, silk bolts, fine wools, and embossed leathers.

At the end of the week, the #strong[Count's chief tax collector] sits
behind a heavy oak table with his iron-bound chest and leather ledger.
Two fabric merchants present themselves before his quill to settle the
tax on their earnings.

- Merchant #strong[Geoffroy] achieved a modest profit of #strong[150
  silver ecus]. The collector levies a tax of #strong[30 ecus].
- Merchant #strong[Barthélemy], a wealthy Flemish draper, amassed an
  opulent profit of #strong[400 silver ecus]. The collector levies a tax
  of #strong[72 ecus].

Barthélemy slams his fist onto the oak table in indignation: #emph[“This
is an outrageous injustice! You take 72 ecus from me while demanding a
mere 30 from Geoffroy! I am paying more than double what he pays!”]

Geoffroy shoots back indignantly: #emph[“Come now, Barthélemy! You
earned an enormous fortune compared to my humble shop! My 30 ecus
represent a painful butchery of my meager earnings, while your 72 ecus
are a mere drop in your golden vaults!”]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Découverte & Histoire --- La querelle des marchands
médiévaux
]


- Geoffroy (Small merchant) Barthélemy (Wealthy merchant)
- 150 ecus) (400 ecus)
- Tax: 30 ecus Tax: 72 ecus
- Part = 30 / 150 Part = 72 / 400
]

The provost of the fair is summoned to judge the dispute. Who is truly
taxed more heavily?

To answer with true mathematical justice, one cannot merely compare the
raw number of coins surrendered (72 versus 30). One must compare the
#strong[proportional share] surrendered relative to total profits
earned:

- For Geoffroy: $30 / 150 = 3 / 15 = 1 / 5 = 0.20$.
- For Barthélemy: $72 / 400 = 18 / 100 = 0.18$.

Imagine that each merchant had earned a standardized benchmark purse of
#strong[100 ecus]:

- Geoffroy would have surrendered #strong[20 ecus per hundred ecus
  earned].
- Barthélemy would have surrendered only #strong[18 ecus per hundred
  ecus earned]!

It is Geoffroy who bears the heavier burden! To compare parts
originating from groups of completely different sizes, humanity invented
a universal standard benchmark denominator: #strong[the number one
hundred]. This is the birth of the #strong[percentage].

