=== 5. The Three Guardians of Data: Mean, Median, and Range
<the-three-guardians-of-data-mean-median-and-range>
To summarize an entire statistical distribution in a few representative
numbers, statisticians rely on three major indicators:

==== 5.1 The Arithmetic Mean ($macron(x)$)
<the-arithmetic-mean-barx>
The #strong[mean] (commonly called the average) is the fair-share value
each individual would receive if all values were pooled together and
redistributed equally.

- #strong[Simple Mean:]
  $ macron(x) = frac(upright("sum of all data values"), upright("total number of values ") N) $

- #strong[Weighted Mean (with frequencies or course credits):]
  $ macron(x) = frac(\(n_1 times x_1\)+\(n_2 times x_2\)+ dots.h +\(n_k times x_k\), N) $

==== 5.2 The Median ($M e$): The Justice of the Midpoint
<the-median-me-the-justice-of-the-midpoint>
The #strong[median] is the exact central value that partitions an
ordered data set into two groups of identical size:

- At least $50 %$ of individuals have a value less than or equal to the
  median.
- At least $50 %$ of individuals have a value greater than or equal to
  the median.

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Finding the Median of a Data Set
]
 1. You
#strong[must first sort] all data values in ascending order (from
smallest to largest). 2. Determine half the sample size: $N / 2$.

- #strong[Case 1: $N$ is odd ($N = 2 k + 1$):] The median is the unique
  value located at rank $k + 1$. #emph[\(Example:
  $N = 7 arrow.r.double.long 7 / 2 = 3.5$. The median is the 4th
  value).]

- #strong[Case 2: $N$ is even ($N = 2 k$):] The median is the average of
  the $k$-th and $\(k + 1\)$-th values. #emph[\(Example:
  $N = 8 arrow.r.double.long 8 / 2 = 4$. Median
  $= frac(upright("4th value") + upright("5th value"), 2)$).]
]

==== 5.3 The Range ($R$): Measuring Dispersion
<the-range-r-measuring-dispersion>
The #strong[range] measures the overall spread or dispersion of the data
set: $ R = upright("Maximum Value") - upright("Minimum Value") $ A small
range indicates that values are clustered tightly together (homogeneous
group). A large range indicates substantial disparity.

#divider()

=== 6. Beware of Pitfalls!
<beware-of-pitfalls-5>
#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Warning: Statistical Illusions!
]
 1. #strong[The Consecutive
Increase and Decrease Trap:] #emph[“If an item rises in price by $20 %$
and subsequently drops by $20 %$, does it return to its original
price?”] #strong[ABSOLUTELY NOT, NEVER!] Test with an initial price of
$\$100$:

- $20 %$ increase $arrow.r.double.long 100 times 1.20 =\$120$.
- $20 %$ decrease on
  $\$120 arrow.r.double.long 120 times\(1 - 0.20\)= 120 times 0.80 = upright(bold(\$96))$!
  You lost $\$4$, because the $20 %$ drop was calculated on a larger
  base amount!

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Adding Percentages from Different Groups:] If Class A has 20
  students with $50 %$ girls, and Class B has 30 students with $40 %$
  girls, the combined proportion of girls is NOT $50 % + 40 % = 90 %$,
  nor is it $frac(50 + 40, 2) = 45 %$! You must count actual human
  beings: $10 + 12 = 22$ girls out of $50$ students total, which is
  $22 / 50 = 44 %$.

+ #strong[Confusing Mean and Median:] In a small workshop of 5 people, 4
  workers earn $\$1\,500$ per month, while the owner earns $\$21\,000$.

  - Mean salary:
    $frac(4 times 1\,500 + 21\,000, 5) = frac(27\,000, 5) = upright(bold(\$5\,400))$!
  - Median salary: the 3rd person earns #strong[$\$1\,500$]. The mean
    gives the deceptive impression that everyone is well-off. The median
    reflects the true economic reality of the majority!
]
]

#divider()

=== 7. Solved Examples
<solved-examples-6>
==== Solved Example 1 --- Applying a Percentage in a Bakery Recipe
<solved-example-1-applying-a-percentage-in-a-bakery-recipe>
To make $1.2 upright(" kg")$ of brioche dough, a baker uses $55 %$
flour, $20 %$ butter, $15 %$ eggs, and $10 %$ sugar and yeast. What mass
of butter (in grams) must the baker weigh?

#emph[Solution:] - Convert total mass to grams:
$1.2 upright(" kg") = 1\,200 upright(" g")$. - Butter share: $20 %$. -
Calculation:
$ upright("Mass of butter") = 1\,200 times 20 / 100 = 1\,200 times 0.20 = 240 upright(" grams") $
The baker must weigh $240 upright(" g")$ of butter. ✔

==== Solved Example 2 --- Calculating an Exam Pass Rate
<solved-example-2-calculating-an-exam-pass-rate>
In a middle school, 138 eighth-grade students sat for the national
diploma exam. 124 of them passed successfully. What was the pass rate
for this school? Round to the nearest tenth of a percent.

#emph[Solution:] - Successful candidates (part): $A = 124$. - Total
cohort (whole): $T = 138$. - Rate:
$ t = A / T times 100 = 124 / 138 times 100 approx 0.89855 times 100 approx 89.9 % $
The diploma pass rate was $89.9 %$. ✔

==== Solved Example 3 --- Winter Sales and Multiplier Coefficients
<solved-example-3-winter-sales-and-multiplier-coefficients>
A sporting goods store applies a $40 %$ discount to running shoes
originally priced at $\$115$. What is the discounted price?

#emph[Solution:] - Multiplier coefficient for a $40 %$ decrease:
$ C = 1 - 40 / 100 = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60 $

- Discounted price: $ upright("Sale Price") = 115 times 0.60 =\$69 $ The
  shoes now cost $\$69$. ✔

==== Solved Example 4 --- Full Statistical Analysis of an Exam
<solved-example-4-full-statistical-analysis-of-an-exam>
Here are the scores out of 20 earned by 25 students on a mathematics
exam:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
