=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-21>
- Why we suddenly begin to #strong[calculate with letters], and what
  “$3 x$”, “$x^2$”, or “$a\(b + c\)$” truly mean.
- How to #strong[simplify and combine like terms] (sorting apples with
  apples) and how to #strong[expand] using the #strong[distributive
  property], proved with a simple rectangle.
- How to #strong[test an equality]: true for one value, true for all, or
  false---and the power of a single #strong[counterexample].
- How to solve any #strong[first-degree equation] using the two
  fundamental gestures of the balance scale.
- How to #strong[set up an equation from a word problem]: ages,
  perimeters, computational algorithms, and even the riddle of
  Diophantus's epitaph.
- The classic #strong[pitfalls]: $2 x + 3 eq.not 5 x$,
  $x times x eq.not 2 x$, the minus sign “$-$” before parentheses, and
  equations that have… no solution, or infinitely many.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Grand Bazaar of Baghdad and the Bag of Gold
<the-problem-the-grand-bazaar-of-baghdad-and-the-bag-of-gold>
You are now twelve or thirteen years old. Let us leave the plains of
Egypt behind, cross the desert sands, and enter the most fabulous
metropolis of the medieval world: #strong[Baghdad], the “City of Peace,”
around the year #strong[820].

At the heart of the city stands the #emph[House of Wisdom] (#emph[Bayt
al-Hikma]), an immense library where scholars from Persia, India, and
Greece translate the world's manuscripts into Arabic: Euclid, Ptolemy,
Indian astronomers… But today, a lively crowd gathers in the great
goldsmiths' bazaar.

At the center of a circle of curious merchants, a man draped in a green
brocade cloak has set upon a table a magnificent #strong[brass balance
scale with two pans].

- On the left pan, he places #strong[a brown canvas bag, tightly
  stitched], sealed with a stamp of red wax. No one knows what is
  inside! Beside the bag, he places #strong[3 copper ingots], each
  weighing one kilogram.
- On the right pan, he places #strong[11 copper ingots] of one kilogram
  each.

The beam of the scale rocks slowly… and comes to rest, #strong[perfectly
horizontal].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Bag ] + 3 kg                     11 kg
oo                        oo
Scale Fulcrum
    ```
  ]
]

#emph[“This bag contains pure gold dust. Whoever tells me its exact
weight without breaking the seal will leave with all the gold! But
whoever is off by a single gram must surrender his mount to me!”]

No one dares risk their horse. Then, a Persian astronomer steps forward
with a calm stride: #strong[Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi].

#emph[“The truth is already before your eyes. The two pans are in
balance. If I remove the exact same amount from both sides at the same
time, will the scale remain level?”] --- #emph[“Of course!”] replies the
crowd.

With his left hand, he removes the #strong[3 ingots] from the left pan:
the pan lightens and rises. But instantly, with his right hand, he
removes #strong[3 ingots] from the right pan. The beam settles back down
and rests once more, #strong[level and still].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Bag ]                          8 kg
oo                        oo
    ```
  ]
]

On the left: the bag, all alone. On the right: $11 - 3 = 8$ ingots.
#emph[“The bag weighs eight kilograms.”] The merchant pales, breaks the
seal, and weighs the bag alone: #strong[precisely eight kilograms].

So far, nothing you could not already do in your head. But the next day,
the merchant returns, stung in his pride, with increasingly cunning
riddles: two identical bags and 5 kg balancing 17 kg; three bags and 2
kg balancing one bag and 10 kg; then bags from which a portion of the
contents has been removed… After a few puzzles, mental arithmetic is no
longer enough. We need #strong[a language] to write down these balance
states, and #strong[rules] to manipulate them without error. That
language is #strong[algebraic notation]. Those rules are the laws of
#strong[algebra].

#divider()

=== 2. Calculating with Letters: Why and How
<calculating-with-letters-why-and-how>
==== 2.1 The Letter Is a Hidden Number
<the-letter-is-a-hidden-number>
In mathematics, a letter is #strong[not] a word or an abbreviation for a
unit. It is #strong[a number] whose value is not (yet) known, or whose
value can vary.

- When the letter represents a specific but #strong[unknown] number (the
  weight of the bag), we call it an #strong[unknown].
- When the letter can take on #strong[any value whatsoever] (the side
  length of an arbitrary square), we call it a #strong[variable].

You already know formulas with letters: the perimeter of a square of
side length $c$ is $4 times c$\; the area of a rectangle is
$L times ell$. These formulas say something that is true #strong[for all
squares], for #strong[all rectangles], all at once. That is the great
power of letters: #strong[a single statement for an infinity of cases].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Algebraic Expression.
]
 An #emph[algebraic
expression] is a sequence of operations in which certain numbers are
replaced by letters. Examples: $3 x + 5$\; $thick 2\(a - 4\)$\;
$thick x^2 - 7 x + 1$.
]

==== 2.2 Notational Conventions (and How to Read Them)
<notational-conventions-and-how-to-read-them>
To work faster, mathematicians have agreed on a few shorthand
conventions. Each has a spoken form:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Written Form], [Meaning], [Read Aloud],),
    table.hline(),
    [$3 x$], [$3 times x$], [“three $x$”],
    [$a b$], [$a times b$], [“$a$ times $b$” or “$a$ $b$”],
    [$4\(x + 1\)$], [$4 times\(x + 1\)$], [“four times the quantity $x$
    plus one”],
    [$x^2$], [$x times x$], [“$x$ squared”],
    [$x^3$], [$x times x times x$], [“$x$ cubed”],
    [$1 x$], [written simply as $x$], [“$x$”],
    [$- 1 x$], [written simply as $- x$], [“negative $x$” or “minus
    $x$”],
    [$0 x$], [equals $0$], [---],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Safety rules:

- #strong[Never omit] the $times$ sign between #strong[two numbers]:
  $3 times 5$ must never be written as $35$!
- By convention, we write the #strong[number before the letter]:
  $x times 3$ is written $3 x$ (and not $x 3$).
- The order of operations from Chapter 18 still holds: exponents first,
  then multiplications and divisions, then additions and subtractions.
  Thus $3 x^2$ means $3 times\(x times x\)$ and #strong[not]
  $\(3 x\)times\(3 x\)$.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History --- Who Invented Letters in Math?
]
 Al-Khwarizmi
himself wrote #strong[no symbols at all]! Everything was written out in
words: “a square and ten roots equal thirty-nine dirhams” (which we
write $x^2 + 10 x = 39$). He called the unknown #emph[shay'] (“the
thing”). Around 250 AD, the Greek mathematician #strong[Diophantus] was
already using abbreviations. In 1591, the Frenchman #strong[François
Viète], advisor to King Henry IV and a master codebreaker, had the
decisive insight: designate unknowns by vowels and known numbers by
consonants. Finally, in 1637, #strong[René Descartes], in #emph[La
Géométrie], took the last letters of the alphabet $x\,y\,z$ for unknowns
and the first letters $a\,b\,c$ for known quantities: this is our modern
convention. A popular tale claims that $x$ comes from the Arabic word
#emph[shay'] transcribed into Spanish as #emph[xei]\; it is a charming
story, but no historical document confirms it.
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 The symbols $+$ and $-$ first appeared in
print in 1489 in a commercial arithmetic book by the German Johannes
Widmann. The equals sign $=$ was invented in 1557 by the Welsh
mathematician #strong[Robert Recorde], who grew tired of writing “is
equal to”: he chose two parallel line segments of identical length,
“bicause noe 2 thynges can be moare equalle.”
]

==== 2.3 Evaluating an Expression (Substitution)
<evaluating-an-expression-substitution>
#emph[Substitution] means replacing the letter with a specific number.
The process is straightforward, but it requires two safeguards:
#strong[reinsert the multiplication signs $times$] and #strong[put
negative numbers in parentheses].

#quote_box[
#strong[✏️ Worked Example 1 --- Evaluating an expression for several
values.
]
 Let $A = 3 x^2 - 5 x + 2$. Evaluate $A$ for $x = 4$, then for
$x = - 2$.

For $x = 4$:
$A = 3 times 4^2 - 5 times 4 + 2 = 3 times 16 - 20 + 2 = 48 - 20 + 2 = 30$.

For $x = - 2$:
$A = 3 times\(- 2\)^2 - 5 times\(- 2\)+ 2 = 3 times 4 + 10 + 2 = 24$.

Note: $\(- 2\)^2 =\(- 2\)times\(- 2\)= 4$ (the rule of signs from
Chapter 20), and $- 5 times\(- 2\)= + 10$.
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Common Pitfall --- Squaring and the Minus Sign.
]


- $- x^2$ means “the opposite of $x^2$”. For $x = 3$: $- x^2 = - 9$.

- $\(- x\)^2$ means “the square of the opposite of $x$”. For $x = 3$:
  $\(- 3\)^2 = 9$.

- For $x = - 2$, $3 x^2 = 3 times 4 = 12$, but
  $\(3 x\)^2 =\(- 6\)^2 = 36$. The exponent applies #strong[only to what
  immediately precedes it].

- And $x times x = x^2$, #strong[not] $2 x$! For $x = 5$: $x^2 = 25$
  whereas $2 x = 10$.
]

#divider()

=== 3. Simplifying: Sorting Apples with Apples
<simplifying-sorting-apples-with-apples>
Imagine a bazaar stall: “5 melons, 3 dates, 2 melons, 7 dates”. Any
merchant will immediately say: “7 melons and 10 dates”. He
#strong[groups items of the same kind together]. But he would never say
“17 melon-dates”!

In algebraic manipulation, it is the exact same. $5 x + 3 + 2 x + 7$ is
grouped into $7 x + 10$. The “$x$” terms go with the “$x$” terms, and
the standalone constants with standalone constants. But $7 x + 10$
cannot become $17 x$: they are not objects of the same nature.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Like Terms, Combining Like Terms.
]


- In a sum, each piece separated by $+$ or $-$ is a #strong[term]. In
  $5 x^2 - 3 x + 8$, the terms are $5 x^2$, $- 3 x$, and $8$.

- The number placed in front of the variable part is called the
  #strong[coefficient]: in $- 3 x$, the coefficient is $- 3$.

- Two terms are #strong[like terms] if they have #strong[the exact same
  variable part]: $4 x$ and $- 7 x$ are like terms; $3 x^2$ and $x^2$
  are like terms; but $3 x$ and $3 x^2$ #strong[are not like terms].

- #strong[Simplifying (combining like terms)] an expression means
  grouping like terms by adding their coefficients, until the expression
  is written in its shortest possible form.
]

Why are we allowed to write $5 x + 2 x = 7 x$? Because $5 x + 2 x$ is “5
bags plus 2 bags,” hence 7 bags. We will prove this rigorously in a
moment using the distributive property.

#quote_box[
#strong[✏️ Worked Example 2 --- Combining like terms.
]
 a)
$B = 5 x + 3 - 2 x + 7$. We group: $\(5 x - 2 x\)+\(3 + 7\)= 3 x + 10$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 2)
+ $C = 4 x^2 - 3 x + x^2 + 7 x - 1$. The $x^2$ terms:
  $4 x^2 + x^2 = 5 x^2$. The $x$ terms: $- 3 x + 7 x = 4 x$. The
  constants: $- 1$. Thus $C = 5 x^2 + 4 x - 1$.

+ $D = 2 a times 3 a$. Here it is a #strong[product]: we multiply the
  numbers together and the letters together:
  $2 times 3 times a times a = 6 a^2$.

+ $E = 2 a + 3 a = 5 a$ (sum), whereas $2 a times 3 a = 6 a^2$
  (product). Never confuse the two!
]
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Common Pitfall --- What Cannot Be Combined.
]


- $2 x + 3$ #strong[does not equal] $5 x$. Test: for $x = 10$,
  $2 x + 3 = 23$, but $5 x = 50$.

- $x^2 + x$ #strong[does not equal] $x^3$ nor $2 x^2$. Test: for
  $x = 2$, $x^2 + x = 6$, $x^3 = 8$, $2 x^2 = 8$.

- $3 x^2 + 2 x^2 = 5 x^2$, #strong[and not] $5 x^4$: we are adding
  “packets of $x^2$”, we are not multiplying.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Distributivity: A Rectangle That Does Not Lie
<distributivity-a-rectangle-that-does-not-lie>
==== 4.1 The Intuition
<the-intuition>
A jeweler sells necklaces. Each necklace contains $a$ gold beads and $b$
silver beads. A customer orders 3 necklaces. How many beads are there in
total? We can count necklace by necklace: $3 times\(a + b\)$. Or we can
count all the gold beads, then all the silver beads: $3 a + 3 b$. The
total number of beads is identical! Therefore, $3\(a + b\)= 3 a + 3 b$.

==== 4.2 Proof by the Area of a Rectangle
<proof-by-the-area-of-a-rectangle>
Draw a rectangle of height $k$ and width $a + b$, partitioned into two
parts:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
a              b
k     area k×a    area k×b
a + b
    ```
  ]
]

The area of the large rectangle is $k times\(a + b\)$. But it is also
the sum of the areas of the two smaller rectangles:
$k times a + k times b$. #strong[One and the same surface, two ways to
calculate its area]: they must be equal.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Property --- The Distributive Property.
]
 For any real numbers
$k$, $a$, $b$:
$ k\(a + b\)= k a + k b #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) k\(a - b\)= k a - k b . $
Read from left to right, we #strong[expand] (converting a product into a
sum). Read from right to left, we #strong[factor] (converting a sum into
a product, factoring out the #strong[greatest common factor] $k$).
]

The word “distributivity” comes from the image of the multiplier $k$
being #strong[distributed] to each term inside the parentheses, just as
a mail carrier delivers a letter to every mailbox on the street.

And here is the promised proof of $5 x + 2 x = 7 x$: it is simply the
distributive property read in reverse: $5 x + 2 x =\(5 + 2\)x = 7 x$.

#quote_box[
#strong[✏️ Worked Example 3 --- Expanding and simplifying.
]
 a)
$3\(2 x - 5\)= 3 times 2 x - 3 times 5 = 6 x - 15$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 2)
+ $- 2\(x - 4\)=\(- 2\)times x -\(- 2\)times 4 = - 2 x + 8$. (Watch the
  signs carefully: $- 2 times\(- 4\)= + 8$\.)

+ $x\(x + 3\)= x times x + x times 3 = x^2 + 3 x$.

+ $F = 4\(x + 2\)- 3\(2 x - 1\)= 4 x + 8 - 6 x + 3 = - 2 x + 11$.
]
]

==== 4.3 The Minus Sign in Front of Parentheses
<the-minus-sign-in-front-of-parentheses>
Parentheses preceded by a minus sign secretly conceal a factor of $- 1$:
$-\(a + b\)=\(- 1\)times\(a + b\)= - a - b$. Consequently:

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Rule --- Removing Parentheses.
]


- Preceded by $+$: remove the parentheses #strong[without changing
  anything]. $thick 7 +\(3 x - 2\)= 7 + 3 x - 2$.
- Preceded by $-$: remove the parentheses #strong[by reversing the sign
  of every term] inside. $thick 7 -\(3 x - 2\)= 7 - 3 x + 2 = 9 - 3 x$.
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Common Pitfall.
]
 The most frequent error in middle
school: writing $7 -\(3 x - 2\)= 7 - 3 x - 2$. The student forgot to
flip the sign of the #strong[second] term! Test with $x = 0$:
$7 -\(0 - 2\)= 9$, but $7 - 0 - 2 = 5$. The test exposes the blunder in
two seconds.
]

==== 4.4 Factoring: The Return Journey
<factoring-the-return-journey>
#quote_box[
#strong[✏️ Worked Example 4 --- Factoring out a common factor.
]
 a)
$6 x + 9$: $6 x = 3 times 2 x$ and $9 = 3 times 3$. Common factor $3$:
$6 x + 9 = 3\(2 x + 3\)$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 2)
+ $5 x^2 - 10 x$: $5 x^2 = 5 x times x$ and $10 x = 5 x times 2$. Common
  factor $5 x$: $5 x^2 - 10 x = 5 x\(x - 2\)$.

+ $7 a + 7 = 7 times a + 7 times 1 = 7\(a + 1\)$. Do not forget the
  “$1$”!
]

#strong[Systematic Verification]: expand back out.
$5 x\(x - 2\)= 5 x^2 - 10 x$ ✓.
]

Why factor? Because a #strong[product] is often far easier to study than
a sum. You will see in the next chapter that factoring allows us to
solve equations that no physical scale could ever untangle.

