$ \(x + 3\)^2 - 9 = 16 arrow.l.r.double\(x + 3\)^2 = 16 + 9 = 25 . $ 3.
Since $25 = 5^2$, we have $x + 3 = 5$ (for the positive solution sought
by Al-Khwarizmi), yielding $x = 5 - 3 = 2$. (The modern second algebraic
solution is $x + 3 = - 5 arrow.r.double.long x = - 8$).

