=== 1. The Problem: The Spice Fair of Venice and the Merchant's Dilemma
<the-problem-the-spice-fair-of-venice-and-the-merchants-dilemma>
The year is #strong[1340], upon the Rialto quayside in #strong[Venice].
The galleys have returned from the Levant, their holds heavy with
cinnamon, black pepper, and saffron. Two Venetian merchants, Giovanni
and Marco, have set up their stalls facing each other in the bustling
marketplace. Both sell the exact same first-press olive oil from Crete.

Yet each offers a different pricing scheme to entice tavern keepers and
ship captains:

- #strong[Merchant Giovanni] announces: #emph[“Free entry! No membership
  fee! I sell every liter of olive oil at the fixed price of 6 ducats
  per liter.”]
- #strong[Merchant Marco], more cunning, proposes: #emph[“Purchase a
  seasonal patron card for 30 ducats, and I will deliver every liter of
  oil to you for only 4 ducats per liter!”]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Giovanni's Tariff:  Total Price = 6 × Number of liters
Marco's Tariff   :  Total Price = 30 + 4 × Number of liters
    ```
  ]
]

A tavern keeper stops, hesitating, clutching the purse at his belt. He
asks his young apprentice: #emph[“From whom should we buy our oil this
season?”]

The apprentice replies with common sense: #emph[“That depends on our
consumption, master! If we only purchase 2 liters for the week, Giovanni
costs us $2 times 6 = 12$ ducats, whereas with Marco we would pay
$30 + 4 times 2 = 38$ ducats: Giovanni is far cheaper. But if we run a
large tavern and purchase 50 liters, Giovanni would cost us
$50 times 6 = 300$ ducats, while Marco would charge us only
$30 + 4 times 50 = 230$ ducats: Marco becomes significantly less
expensive!”]

The master tavern keeper knits his brow: #emph[“Very well. But at
#strong[exactly how many liters] does Marco's price become more
advantageous than Giovanni's?”]

This is no longer an equality to be solved, but an #strong[inequality]:
$ 30 + 4 x < 6 x . $ We seek the region where the total expenditure with
Marco is #emph[strictly less than] that with Giovanni.

And that is not all. The next morning, Giovanni and Marco partner
together to sell a lot of 100 amphorae containing a total of 380 liters
of oil. Some amphorae hold 3 liters, while others hold 5 liters. How
many amphorae of each kind are there? This is a problem with #strong[two
unknowns], where two conditions must be satisfied simultaneously.

To answer the tavern keeper and pierce the secrets of the market, we
must forge two new mathematical tools: #strong[inequalities] and
#strong[linear systems].

#divider()

=== 2. The Language of Inequalities: Comparing Quantities
<the-language-of-inequalities-comparing-quantities>
==== 2.1 Symbols and Reading Direction
<symbols-and-reading-direction>
In mathematics, an equality ($=$) asserts that two quantities have the
exact same value. An #strong[inequality] captures an imbalance: one
quantity is greater or smaller than another.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (25%, 25%, 25%, 25%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Symbol], [Meaning], [Read Aloud], [Example],),
    table.hline(),
    [$<$], [Strictly less than], [“is strictly less than”], [$3 < 7$],
    [$>$], [Strictly greater than], [“is strictly greater
    than”], [$8 > 2$],
    [$lt.eq$ or $lt.eq$], [Less than or equal to], [“is less than or
    equal to” (at most)], [$x lt.eq 10$ ($x$ is 10 or less)],
    [$gt.eq$ or $gt.eq$], [Greater than or equal to], [“is greater than
    or equal to” (at least)], [$x gt.eq 18$ ($x$ is 18 or more)],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#quote_box[
#strong[💡 The Arrowhead Visual Rule ---
]
 The symbol always opens wide
toward the larger number and points its sharp tip toward the smaller
number:
$ upright("BIG") > upright("small") #h(2em) upright("small") < upright("BIG") . $
]

- #strong[Strict inequality ($<$ or $>$) :] Equality is strictly
  excluded. If age $a > 12$, being exactly 12 years old does not
  qualify.
- #strong[Non-strict (weak) inequality ($lt.eq$ or $gt.eq$) :] Equality
  is accepted. If test score $s gt.eq 10$, scoring exactly 10 is
  sufficient to pass.

