=== 3. The Rules of the Tilted Scale
<the-rules-of-the-tilted-scale>
Let us return to Al-Khwarizmi's two-pan scale, but this time,
#strong[the beam tilts]. The left pan sits higher than the right pan:
the left side is lighter than the right side ($A < B$).

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
A (Lighter)
oo
B (Heavier)
oo
    ```
  ]
]

What occurs if we perform identical operations on both pans
simultaneously?

==== 3.1 Rule 1: Adding or Subtracting the Same Number
<rule-1-adding-or-subtracting-the-same-number>
If you place 2 kg of lead on the left pan AND 2 kg of lead on the right
pan, the right pan remains heavier. The direction of the tilt does not
budge by a millimeter. Likewise, if you remove 3 kg from both sides, the
scale preserves its exact tilt.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Rule 1 --- Addition and Subtraction.
]
 The
direction of an inequality does not change if you #strong[add] or
#strong[subtract] the same number from both sides:
$ upright("If ") a < b\,upright(" then ") a + c < b + c quad upright("and") quad a - c < b - c . $
]

#emph[Numerical Example:] We know that $3 < 8$.

- Add $5$: $3 + 5 < 8 + 5 arrow.r.double.long 8 < 13$ (true).
- Subtract $10$: $3 - 10 < 8 - 10 arrow.r.double.long - 7 < - 2$ (true,
  $- 7^compose upright("C")$ is colder than $- 2^compose upright("C")$).

==== 3.2 Rule 2: Multiplying or Dividing by a POSITIVE Number
<rule-2-multiplying-or-dividing-by-a-positive-number>
If you triple the contents of the left pan and triple the contents of
the right pan, the heavier side becomes three times heavier, and the
lighter side three times lighter. The direction of imbalance remains
unchanged.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Rule 2 --- Multiplying and Dividing by a POSITIVE
Number ($c > 0$).
]
 The direction of an inequality does not change if you
#strong[multiply] or #strong[divide] both sides by the same
#strong[strictly positive] number:
$ upright("If ") a < b upright(" and ") c > 0\,upright(" then ") a times c < b times c quad upright("and") quad a / c < b / c . $
]

#emph[Numerical Example:] We have $- 4 < 6$. Multiply by $3$:
$ - 4 times 3 < 6 times 3 arrow.r.double.long - 12 < 18 quad upright("(still true)") . $

==== 3.3 Rule 3: The Great Reversal! Multiplying or Dividing by a NEGATIVE Number
<rule-3-the-great-reversal-multiplying-or-dividing-by-a-negative-number>
Here unfolds the most surprising phenomenon in algebra, the one that
trips up countless students.

Take two self-evident numbers: $ 2 < 5 . $ Multiply both sides by $- 1$.
What do they become?

- On the left: $2 times\(- 1\)= - 2$.
- On the right: $5 times\(- 1\)= - 5$.

Now compare $- 2$ and $- 5$. Which number is greater? On a thermometer,
$- 2^compose upright("C")$ is warmer than $- 5^compose upright("C")$. On
the number line, $- 2$ is located #strong[to the right] of $- 5$:
$ - 2 > - 5 upright(" !") $

#strong[The inequality has flipped!] The sign $<$ became $>$.

#figure(image("figures/vol_4/fig_24_intervalles_droite.svg", alt: "Intervalles et inégalités sur la droite réelle : notation et crochets"),
  caption: [
    Intervalles et inégalités sur la droite réelle : notation et
    crochets
  ]
)

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Rule 3 --- Multiplying and Dividing by a NEGATIVE
Number ($c < 0$).
]
 #strong[Whenever you multiply or divide both sides of
an inequality by a STRICTLY NEGATIVE number, YOU MUST REVERSE THE
DIRECTION OF THE INEQUALITY SYMBOL!]
$ upright("If ") a < b upright(" and ") c < 0\,upright(" then ") a times c upright(bold(>)) b times c quad upright("and") quad a / c upright(bold(>)) b / c . $
]

#strong[Why this reversal? Two concrete insights:] 1. #strong[The Mirror
Reflection:] Multiplying by $- 1$ rotates the number line through a
half-turn around zero. Whatever lay farthest to the right in the
positive realm ($+ 5$) is hurled farthest to the left in the negative
realm ($- 5$). The champion of greatness becomes the champion of
smallness! 2. #strong[The Debt Analogy:] Is having \$200 in your account
better than having \$500? No, $200 < 500$. But having a #strong[debt] of
\$200 ($- 200$) is vastly preferable to having a debt of \$500
($- 500$)! Therefore, $- 200 > - 500$.

