Each column provides the coordinates of a point: $\(- 2\,- 3\)$,
$\(- 1\,- 1\)$, $\(0\,1\)$, $\(1\,3\)$, etc.

==== 3.2 The Graph in a Coordinate Plane
<the-graph-in-a-coordinate-plane>
Plot these coordinate pairs in a Cartesian plane:

- The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) displays the #strong[inputs $x$].
- The vertical axis ($y$-axis) displays the #strong[outputs
  $y = f\(x\)$].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Graph of a Function.
]
 The graph of a function
$f$ (often denoted $cal(C)_f$) is the collection of all points in the
plane having coordinates $\(x\,f\(x\)\)$.
]

