=== 4. The Linear Function: Pure Proportionality $f\(x\)= a x$
<the-linear-function-pure-proportionality-fx-ax>
==== 4.1 Definition and Physical Meaning
<definition-and-physical-meaning>
A #strong[linear function] is the simplest and purest of all functions:
it is the algebraic translation of a #strong[perfectly proportional
relationship] (as studied in Chapter 13).

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Linear Function.
]
 A linear function is a
function defined by a formula of the form: $ f\(x\)= a x $ where $a$ is
a fixed real constant, known as the #strong[constant of proportionality]
or the #strong[slope].
]

#emph[Examples:] - Buying cherries at €4 per kg: $P\(x\)= 4 x$
($a = 4$). - Cycling at a constant speed of 20 km/h: distance
$d\(t\)= 20 t$ ($a = 20$). - A 30% discount: the discounted price is
$N\(x\)=\(1 - 0.30\)x = 0.7 x$ ($a = 0.7$).

==== 4.2 Graphical Representation: The Line Passing Through the Origin
<graphical-representation-the-line-passing-through-the-origin>
If you plot points belonging to a linear function in a coordinate plane,
a geometric wonder appears: #strong[all points align in a straight line
passing through the origin $O\(0\,0\)$]!

Why? Because if you purchase nothing ($x = 0$), you pay nothing
($f\(0\)= a times 0 = 0$).

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Graphical Theorem ---
]
 The graph of a linear function
$f\(x\)= a x$ is #strong[a straight line passing through the origin
$O\(0\,0\)$]. Conversely, any non-vertical line passing through the
origin represents a linear function.
]

To plot the graph of a linear function, it suffices to calculate
#strong[a single additional point] alongside the origin $\(0\,0\)$!

#divider()

=== 5. The Affine Function: The General Tariff Line $f\(x\)= a x + b$
<the-affine-function-the-general-tariff-line-fx-ax-b>
==== 5.1 Definition: Proportionality with a Base Fee
<definition-proportionality-with-a-base-fee>
What happens when a real-world scenario involves an initial fixed charge
(a subscription fee, an opening meter charge, a deposit) to which a
proportional rate is added? It is no longer pure proportionality, but an
#strong[affine function] (commonly called a linear function with
non-zero intercept in English curricula).

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Affine Function.
]
 An affine function is a
function defined for all real numbers $x$ by: $ f\(x\)= a x + b $ where
$a$ and $b$ are fixed real constants.

- The constant $a$ is the #strong[slope] (rate of change / directional
  coefficient).
- The constant $b$ is the #strong[$y$-intercept] (the initial value at
  $x = 0$).
]

==== 5.2 Why This Terminology?
<why-this-terminology>
+ #strong[The $y$-intercept ($b$):] Evaluate the function at $0$:
  $ f\(0\)= a times 0 + b = 0 + b = b . $ The point on the line
  corresponding to $x = 0$ has coordinates $\(0\,b\)$. That is precisely
  where the line #strong[crosses the vertical $y$-axis]!

+ #strong[The slope ($a$):] It governs the tilt and steepness of the
  line:

  - If $a > 0$, the function is #strong[increasing] (the line rises from
    left to right).

  - If $a < 0$, the function is #strong[decreasing] (the line falls from
    left to right).

  - The larger $\|a\|$, the steeper the line. The closer $\|a\|$ is to
    0, the flatter the line.

==== 5.3 Special Cases
<special-cases>
- If $b = 0$, the formula becomes $f\(x\)= a x$: a linear function is
  simply an #strong[affine function that starts at the origin $b = 0$].
- If $a = 0$, the formula becomes $f\(x\)= b$: this is a
  #strong[constant function]. The output is always $b$, regardless of
  $x$. Its graph is a #strong[horizontal straight line].

#divider()

=== 6. The Art of Graphing and Reading Lines
<the-art-of-graphing-and-reading-lines>
==== 6.1 The Magic Staircase: “Run 1, Rise $a$”
<the-magic-staircase-run-1-rise-a>
To draw the line $y = a x + b$ without building a lengthy table of
values, use the staircase rule (rise over run):

+ Plot the initial starting anchor on the vertical $y$-axis: the point
  $\(0\,b\)$.

+ From this point, #strong[step horizontally 1 unit to the right] (Run
  $+ 1$).

+ Then:

  - If $a$ is positive: #strong[climb vertically by $a$ units] (Rise
    $+ a$).
  - If $a$ is negative: #strong[drop vertically by $\|a\|$ units] (Rise
    $-\|a\|$).

+ Mark this second point. Place your straightedge through both points:
  your line is complete!

==== 6.2 Computing the Slope $a$ from Two Given Points
<computing-the-slope-a-from-two-given-points>
If a line passes through two known points $A\(x_A\,y_A\)$ and
$B\(x_B\,y_B\)$ (with $x_A eq.not x_B$), its slope is calculated using
the fundamental ratio:

$ a = upright("Vertical change") / upright("Horizontal change") = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(Delta y, Delta x) = upright("Rise") / upright("Run") . $

The symbol $Delta$ (the uppercase Greek letter Delta) signifies “change”
or “difference.” This formula expresses that the slope measures how many
vertical units the line climbs for each unit traveled toward the right.

#divider()

=== 7. True History: The Thermometer of Fahrenheit and Celsius
<true-history-the-thermometer-of-fahrenheit-and-celsius>
In the 18th century, European scientists sought reliable means to
measure temperature. In 1724, German physicist Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit
constructed the first dependable mercury thermometer. He established:

- $0^compose upright("F")$: the coldest temperature he could achieve in
  his laboratory using a mixture of water, ice, and ammonium chloride.
- $96^compose upright("F")$: the body temperature of a healthy human
  being (later calibrated to $98.6^compose upright("F")$).

On his scale, pure water freezes at $32^compose upright("F")$ and boils
at $212^compose upright("F")$.

In 1742, Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius devised a decimal scale
grounded in the fundamental states of pure water:

- $0^compose upright("C")$ for the freezing point of water.
- $100^compose upright("C")$ for the boiling point of water.

How do we convert Celsius temperature ($x$) into Fahrenheit temperature
($f\(x\)$)? The mapping is precisely an #strong[affine function]!

- The freezing point yields point $A\(0\,32\)$.
- The boiling point yields point $B\(100\,212\)$.

Let us compute the slope $a$ and the $y$-intercept $b$: 1. Since $x = 0$
yields $y = 32$, the $y$-intercept is immediately: $ b = 32 . $ 2. The
slope is:
$ a = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(212 - 32, 100 - 0) = 180 / 100 = 9 / 5 = 1.8 . $

The universal formula connecting both worlds is:
$ f\(x\)= 1.8 x + 32 quad upright("or") quad T_(upright("Fahrenheit")) = 9 / 5 T_(upright("Celsius")) + 32 . $

When your bedroom temperature reads $20^compose upright("C")$, an
American reading a Fahrenheit thermometer sees:
$ f\(20\)= 1.8 times 20 + 32 = 36 + 32 = 68^compose upright("F") ! $

#divider()

=== 8. Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-2>
==== Example 1: Evaluating images and preimages
<example-1-evaluating-images-and-preimages>
Consider the affine function $f$ defined by: $ f\(x\)= - 3 x + 7 . $ 1.
Calculate the image of $4$ and the image of $- 2$. 2. Determine the
preimage of $- 11$. 3. Determine the preimage of $0$.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. To calculate an image, substitute the
given value for $x$:

- $f\(4\)= - 3 times 4 + 7 = - 12 + 7 = - 5$. The image of $4$ is $- 5$.
- $f\(- 2\)= - 3 times\(- 2\)+ 7 = + 6 + 7 = 13$. The image of $- 2$ is
  $13$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Finding the preimage of $- 11$ means solving $f\(x\)= - 11$:
  $ - 3 x + 7 = - 11 $ $ - 3 x = - 11 - 7 = - 18 $
  $ x = frac(- 18, - 3) = 6 . $ The preimage of $- 11$ under $f$ is $6$.
+ Finding the preimage of $0$:
  $ - 3 x + 7 = 0 arrow.r.double.long - 3 x = - 7 arrow.r.double.long x = frac(- 7, - 3) = 7 / 3 . $
  The preimage of $0$ is the irreducible fraction $7 / 3$.
]

#divider()

==== Example 2: Determining an affine function from two points
<example-2-determining-an-affine-function-from-two-points>
A straight line passes through points $A\(2\,5\)$ and $B\(6\,17\)$.
Determine the formula of the affine function $f\(x\)= a x + b$ whose
graph is this line.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. #strong[Compute the slope $a$:]
$ a = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(17 - 5, 6 - 2) = 12 / 4 = 3 . $
The function is now known to be of the form $f\(x\)= 3 x + b$. 2.
#strong[Compute the $y$-intercept $b$:] Since $A\(2\,5\)$ lies on the
line, $f\(2\)= 5$: $ 3 times 2 + b = 5 $
$ 6 + b = 5 arrow.r.double.long b = 5 - 6 = - 1 . $ 3.
#strong[Conclusion:] The desired affine function is:
$ f\(x\)= 3 x - 1 . $ #emph[\(Quick check with point $B$:
$f\(6\)= 3 times 6 - 1 = 18 - 1 = 17$. Exact!)]

#divider()

==== Example 3: Finding $a$ and $b$ by graphical inspection
<example-3-finding-a-and-b-by-graphical-inspection>
On a given coordinate grid, a straight line $\(d\)$ crosses the vertical
axis at $- 3$. Stepping $2$ grid units to the right requires climbing
$5$ units vertically to return to the line. 1. State the $y$-intercept
$b$. 2. Calculate the slope $a$. 3. Deduce the formula of the
corresponding function.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. The line intersects the vertical axis at
$- 3$, so: $ b = - 3 . $ 2. Running 2 units right and rising 5 units
means: $ Delta x = + 2 quad upright("and") quad Delta y = + 5 . $ The
slope is: $ a = frac(Delta y, Delta x) = 5 / 2 = 2.5 . $ 3. The function
formula is:
$ f\(x\)= 2.5 x - 3 quad (upright("or ") f \( x \) = 5 / 2 x - 3) . $

#divider()

==== Example 4: Mobile phone plan comparison
<example-4-mobile-phone-plan-comparison>
A telecommunications provider offers two mobile subscriptions:

- #strong[Eco Plan:] €0.10 per call minute, with no monthly base fee.
- #strong[Serenity Plan:] A fixed monthly fee of €12, plus €0.02 per
  call minute.

+ Write formulas for $E\(x\)$ and $S\(x\)$ giving the monthly cost for
  $x$ call minutes.
+ Identify the mathematical type of each function.
+ Compute the monthly bill for 60 minutes of calls under each plan.
+ Beyond how many minutes per month does the Serenity Plan become
  cheaper than the Eco Plan?

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. For $x$ minutes:

- $E\(x\)= 0.10 x$.
- $S\(x\)= 0.02 x + 12$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ $E$ is a #strong[linear function] (pure proportionality, passing
  through the origin). $S$ is an #strong[affine function] (with non-zero
  base intercept $b = 12$).

+ For 60 minutes:

  - $E\(60\)= 0.10 times 60 = 6$ €.
  - $S\(60\)= 0.02 times 60 + 12 = 1.20 + 12 = 13.20$ €.

+ Serenity is cheaper when its cost is strictly less than Eco:
  $ S\(x\)< E\(x\)arrow.l.r.double 0.02 x + 12 < 0.10 x $
  $ 12 < 0.10 x - 0.02 x arrow.r.double.long 12 < 0.08 x arrow.r.double.long x > 12 / 0.08 = 150 . $
  Conclusion: The Serenity Plan becomes more economical whenever calling
  exceeds #strong[150 minutes (2 hours 30 minutes) per month].
]

#divider()

==== Example 5: Speed, distance, and affine functions
<example-5-speed-distance-and-affine-functions>
A water cistern contains 800 liters. At 8:00 AM, a drain valve is
opened: water empties at a steady, constant rate of 25 liters per
minute. 1. Express the remaining volume $V\(t\)$ (in liters) in terms of
elapsed time $t$ (in minutes). 2. What is the nature of function $V$?
What is the sign of its slope? 3. How much water remains after 12
minutes? 4. How long until the cistern is completely dry?

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. At time $t = 0$, there are 800 L. Every
minute, 25 L drain out: $ V\(t\)= 800 - 25 t = - 25 t + 800 . $ 2. $V$
is an #strong[affine function] with slope $a = - 25$ and $y$-intercept
$b = 800$. Its slope is #strong[negative] ($a = - 25 < 0$), so the
function is #strong[decreasing] (volume decreases over time). 3. At
$t = 12$ min:
$ V\(12\)= - 25 times 12 + 800 = - 300 + 800 = 500 upright(" liters") . $
\4. The cistern is empty when $V\(t\)= 0$:
$ V\(t\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double - 25 t + 800 = 0 arrow.l.r.double 25 t = 800 arrow.l.r.double t = 800 / 25 = 32 upright(" minutes") . $
The tank will be completely empty at 8:32 AM.

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-9>
+ #strong[Core Vocabulary:]

  - $x$ is the #strong[input / preimage] (the starting value).

  - $f\(x\)$ is the #strong[output / image] of $x$ under $f$ (the
    computed result).

  - The mapping notation $f : x mapsto f\(x\)$ reads: “the function $f$
    that maps $x$ to $f\(x\)$\.”

+ #strong[Linear Function: $f\(x\)= a x$]

  - Represents pure #strong[proportionality].
  - Its graph is #strong[a straight line strictly passing through the
    origin $O\(0\,0\)$].

+ #strong[Affine Function: $f\(x\)= a x + b$]

  - Its graph is #strong[a straight line] intersecting the vertical
    $y$-axis at $\(0\,b\)$.

  - $b$ is the #strong[$y$-intercept]: $f\(0\)= b$.

  - $a$ is the #strong[slope (rate of change)]:
    $ a = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(Delta y, Delta x) . $ If
    $a > 0$, the line rises. If $a < 0$, the line falls. If $a = 0$, the
    line is horizontal (constant function).

+ #strong[Graphical Reading:]

  - To find an output given an input: start at $x$ on the horizontal
    axis, move vertically to meet the line, then read $y$ on the
    vertical axis.
  - To find an input given an output: start at $y$ on the vertical axis,
    move horizontally to meet the line, then read $x$ on the horizontal
    axis.

