=== 2. The Two Mirrors: Axial Reflection and Point Reflection
<the-two-mirrors-axial-reflection-and-point-reflection>
A #strong[geometric transformation] is a rule that maps each point in
the plane to a new position according to a strict geometric
prescription. The original point is called the preimage $M$, and the
resulting point is called its #strong[image] $M'$.

==== 2.1 Axial Reflection: Paper Folding Along a Line
<axial-reflection-paper-folding-along-a-line>
Take a sheet of paper, place a drop of wet ink on the left side, fold
the sheet in half along a straight crease $\(d\)$, and press down
firmly. When unfolded, the ink blot has imprinted itself on the other
side.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Axial Reflection (across line $\(d\)$).
]


- The image of a point $M$ lying off $\(d\)$ is the point $M'$ such that
  the line $\(d\)$ is the #strong[perpendicular bisector] of the segment
  $\[M M'\]$. This means that $\(d\)$ is perpendicular to $\[M M'\]$ and
  passes through its midpoint.

- If a point $M$ lies directly on the axis $\(d\)$, its image is itself:
  $M' = M$ (such a point is called an #strong[invariant point] or fixed
  point).
]

