    ```
  ]
]

#emph[The Mirror Effect:] Axial reflection reverses orientation! Your
right hand reflects as a left hand in a mirror. If the vertices of a
triangle proceed clockwise ($A arrow.r B arrow.r C$), their reflected
images will proceed counterclockwise.

==== 2.2 Point Reflection (Central Symmetry): The Half-Turn Around a Pivot
<point-reflection-central-symmetry-the-half-turn-around-a-pivot>
Now imagine placing a pushpin into your paper at point $O$, and rotating
the tracing paper through an #strong[exact half-turn ($180^compose$)].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Point Reflection (Central Inversion across
center $O$).
]
 The image of a point $M$ under point reflection centered
at $O$ is the point $M'$ such that #strong[point $O$ is the midpoint of
the segment $\[M M'\]$].
]

#figure(image("figures/vol_4/fig_26_symetrie_centrale.svg", alt: "La symétrie centrale : demi-tour parfait de 180° autour du centre O"),
  caption: [
    La symétrie centrale : demi-tour parfait de 180° autour du centre O
  ]
)

#emph[Essential Property:] A point reflection transforms any line
$\(d\)$ into a line $\(d'\)$ that is #strong[strictly parallel] to it!
It preserves orientation (a clockwise loop remains clockwise after a
$180^compose$ rotation).

#divider()

=== 3. The Two Rigid Motions: Translation and Rotation
<the-two-rigid-motions-translation-and-rotation>
==== 3.1 Translation: The Pure Slide
<translation-the-pure-slide>
Picture opening a desk drawer, or a train speeding along straight
tracks. Every point of the object glides by the exact same distance,
along the same direction, and in the same sense.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Translation.
]
 A #strong[translation] slides a
geometric figure from point $A$ to point $B$. The image of any point $M$
is the point $M'$ such that: 1. The lines $\(A B\)$ and $\(M M'\)$ are
#strong[parallel] (same direction). 2. Motion from $M$ to $M'$ has the
#strong[same sense] (orientation) as motion from $A$ to $B$. 3. The
length $M M' = A B$ (same distance).

#emph[In summary:] The quadrilateral $A B M' M$ is a
#strong[parallelogram] (possibly degenerate).
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
A > B
Displacement Vector)
M > M'
    ```
  ]
]

In a translation, the figure glides without rotating by a hair. It
models footprints left in sequence across beach sand.

==== 3.2 Rotation: The Controlled Swirl
<rotation-the-controlled-swirl>
Think of the minute hand of a clock, the blades of a windmill, or the
cabins on a Ferris wheel.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Rotation.
]
 A #strong[rotation] is determined
by three inseparable components: 1. A fixed point called the
#strong[center of rotation] $O$. 2. An #strong[angle of rotation]
$alpha$ (in degrees). 3. A #strong[direction of rotation]:

- either #strong[clockwise]\;
- or #strong[counterclockwise] (standard mathematical / counterclockwise
  sense).

The image of a point $M$ under rotation centered at $O$ by angle $alpha$
is the point $M'$ such that:

- $O M = O M'$ (the point remains on a concentric circle of radius
  $O M$);
- The angle $accent(M O M', ̂) = alpha$.
]

#emph[Remarkable Insight:] A rotation of $180^compose$ is identical to a
#strong[point reflection]!

#divider()

=== 4. The Optical Zoom: Dilation (Homothety)
<the-optical-zoom-dilation-homothety>
All previous transformations (reflections, translations, rotations)
moved shapes across the plane without ever changing their dimensions.
They are called #strong[isometries] (from Greek #emph[isos], equal, and
#emph[metron], measure).

A #strong[dilation] (homothety, from Greek #emph[homoios], similar, and
#emph[thesis], arrangement) is fundamentally different: it is an
#strong[optical zoom]. It expands or shrinks a shape proportionally from
a central projection point.

==== 4.1 Definition and Scale Factor $k$
<definition-and-scale-factor-k>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Dilation (Homothety).
]
 A dilation is defined
by: 1. A fixed point called the #strong[center] $O$. 2. A non-zero real
number called the #strong[scale factor] $k$.

The image of a point $M$ is the point $M'$ on the line $\(O M\)$ such
that:

- The distance $O M' =\|k\|times O M$.

- #strong[If $k > 0$:] point $M'$ lies on the ray $\[O M\)$ (on the same
  side of $O$ as $M$).

- #strong[If $k < 0$:] point $M'$ lies on the opposite ray from $O$.
  This combines an expansion/contraction with a half-turn!
]

#figure(image("figures/vol_4/fig_26_homothetie.svg", alt: "L’homothétie : zoom vectoriel depuis le centre d’alignement"),
  caption: [
    L'homothétie : zoom vectoriel depuis le centre d'alignement
  ]
)

==== 4.2 Scaling Effects of Factor $k$
<scaling-effects-of-factor-k>
- If $\|k\|> 1$: the transformation is an #strong[enlargement].
- If $0 <\|k\|< 1$: the transformation is a #strong[reduction].
- If $k = 1$: the shape does not move (identity transformation).
- If $k = - 1$: the dilation is identical to #strong[point reflection
  across center $O$]!

==== 4.3 The Dramatic Effect on Areas and Volumes
<the-dramatic-effect-on-areas-and-volumes>
This is one of the most classic pitfalls in middle and high school
exams:

- Under a dilation of scale factor $k$, #strong[all lengths are
  multiplied by $\|k\|$].
- #strong[The area of any surface is multiplied by $k^2$!]
- #strong[The volume of any solid is multiplied by $\|k\|^3$!]

#emph[Vivid Example:] If you scale up an architectural model by $k = 3$:
\- Its height is multiplied by 3. - The paint required for its facade is
multiplied by $3^2 = 9$. - Its total weight and volume are multiplied by
$3^3 = 27$!

#divider()

=== 5. What Transformations Preserve: The Genetic Code of Shapes
<what-transformations-preserve-the-genetic-code-of-shapes>
