=== 6. Congruent Triangles and Similar Triangles
<congruent-triangles-and-similar-triangles>
==== 6.1 Congruent Triangles (Isometries): Perfect Clones
<congruent-triangles-isometries-perfect-clones>
Two triangles are #strong[congruent] when they can be made to
#strong[coincide perfectly] through a rigid motion (translation,
rotation, or reflection). Their corresponding sides have identical
lengths and their corresponding angles have identical measures.

To prove that two triangles are congruent, you do not need to measure
all 6 components (3 sides and 3 angles)! Three suffice, according to the
#strong[three congruence criteria]:

+ #strong[SSS (Side-Side-Side):] The three sides of one triangle are
  respectively equal in length to the three sides of the other.
+ #strong[SAS (Side-Angle-Side):] Two sides of one triangle are equal to
  two sides of the other, and #strong[the angle enclosed between these
  two sides] has the same measure.
+ #strong[ASA (Angle-Side-Angle):] One side of one triangle is equal to
  one side of the other, and #strong[the two angles adjacent to that
  side] have the same measures.

