=== 7. The Lines of Equity: Perpendicular Bisector and Angle Bisector
<the-lines-of-equity-perpendicular-bisector-and-angle-bisector>
These two remarkable lines are rooted in axial symmetry:

==== 7.1 The Perpendicular Bisector of a Segment
<the-perpendicular-bisector-of-a-segment>
- #strong[Definition:] The line perpendicular to a line segment at its
  midpoint.

- #strong[Equidistance Property:] A point lies on the perpendicular
  bisector of segment $\[A B\]$ if and only if it is equidistant from
  both endpoints:
  $ M in upright("Perpendicular Bisector of ")\[A B\]arrow.l.r.double M A = M B . $

- #strong[The Circumscribed Circle (Circumcircle):] The perpendicular
  bisectors of the three sides of a triangle intersect at a single
  point: the #strong[circumcenter], which is the center of the unique
  circle passing through all three vertices of the triangle.

==== 7.2 The Angle Bisector
<the-angle-bisector>
- #strong[Definition:] The ray that divides an angle into two adjacent
  angles of equal measure. It is the #strong[axis of symmetry] of the
  angle.
- #strong[Equidistance Property:] Every point on an angle bisector is
  equidistant from the two arms (sides) of the angle.
- #strong[The Inscribed Circle (Incircle):] The angle bisectors of the
  three interior angles of a triangle meet at the #strong[incenter],
  which is the center of the circle tangent to all three sides inside
  the triangle.

#divider()

=== 8. The Art of Friezes, Tessellations, and the Genius of Escher
<the-art-of-friezes-tessellations-and-the-genius-of-escher>
When the geometry of transformations meets artistic imagination,
mathematics touches the sublime.

==== 8.1 What Is a Tessellation?
<what-is-a-tessellation>
A #strong[tessellation (tiling)] is a complete covering of the plane
using interlocking geometric shapes, leaving no empty gaps and with no
shapes overlapping.

- A #strong[frieze (band ornament)] is a one-dimensional tessellation (a
  decorative border along a wall). It relies on periodic horizontal
  translations, sometimes combined with vertical reflections or
  half-turns.
- A #strong[planar tessellation] extends infinitely across both
  dimensions.

==== 8.2 The Mosaics of the Alhambra in Granada
<the-mosaics-of-the-alhambra-in-granada>
In the 14th century, Moorish artists decorating the Alhambra palace,
bound by cultural restrictions against depicting living forms, explored
the mathematics of symmetry with staggering brilliance. Mathematicians
proved centuries later that there are strictly #strong[17 possible
periodic planar symmetry groups] (the 17 wallpaper groups). The master
craftsmen of Granada had #strong[empirically discovered every single one
of them] in the ceramic tilework (#emph[azulejos]) of their palace!

==== 8.3 The Metamorphoses of M. C. Escher
<the-metamorphoses-of-m.-c.-escher>
In 1936, Dutch graphic artist M. C. Escher visited the Alhambra and was
deeply captivated by these repeating patterns. He devoted his artistic
life to replacing abstract polygons with interlocking living creatures:
birds, fish, lizards, angels, and demons.

How did he design a lizard that interlocks seamlessly with identical
copies of itself? 1. He began with a basic grid shape (such as a square
or parallelogram) that naturally tiles the plane. 2. He sliced out a
curved contour along the left boundary and #strong[translated] it to the
right boundary. 3. He sliced a contour from the bottom boundary and
performed a #strong[$90^compose$ rotation] to form the upper paw.
Because every piece of area removed from one side is perfectly restored
to another, the total area remains invariant, allowing the motifs to
interlock into infinity!

