equidistant from $A$ and $B$, so $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector
of chord $\[A B\]$. 2. The perpendicular bisector is the unique line
perpendicular to $\[A B\]$ through its midpoint. Since $O$ lies on it,
any line through $O$ perpendicular to $\[A B\]$ is the perpendicular
bisector, necessarily bisecting $\[A B\]$.

#strong[Solution 26.11] 1. Total length
$1.20 upright(" m") = 120 upright(" cm")$. Tile count: $120 / 4 = 30$
