== CHAPTER 27: Land Partitioning and the Potter's Box
<chapter-27-land-partitioning-and-the-potters-box>
#quote_box[
#emph[“A formula memorized by heart without ever being seen is like a
sterile seed. But take a pair of scissors, cut out a triangle, paste the
pieces on the other side: the rectangle appears, and the formula becomes
self-evident.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-26>
- Why #strong[every triangle is half of a rectangle], and how a pair of
  scissors suffices to rediscover the formulas for the area of a
  parallelogram, a trapezoid, and even a circle.
- How to transition from the #strong[flat] (areas, in $upright("m")^2$)
  to the #strong[solid] (volumes, in $upright("m")^3$), and why we
  multiply by 100 in one case and by 1,000 in the other.
- The mystery of the #strong[“divided by 3”] in pyramids and cones, and
  the crowning achievement Archimedes was most proud of: the volume of
  the #strong[sphere].
- What appears when you #strong[slice] a solid with a flat blade:
  geometric #strong[cross sections].
- The classic pitfalls that cost points on exams: radius or diameter?
  vertical height or slanted side? $upright("cm")^3$ or liters?

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Feud of Two Farmers and the Potter's Order
<the-problem-the-feud-of-two-farmers-and-the-potters-order>
In the Meuse valley during the 13th century, the count's chief steward
compiles the land registry to levy the fair share of harvested wheat.
Two neighboring farmers step forward before him.

- Farmer #strong[Martin] owns a #strong[rectangular] field measuring 20
  meters wide by 30 meters long. The steward computes without
  hesitation: $ 20 times 30 = 600 upright(" square meters.") $ It is a
  grid of 600 small one-meter-by-one-meter plots of land (remember the
  brick wall from Chapter 5?).
- But farmer #strong[Thomas] owns an #strong[elongated, sharply pointed
  triangular] field. The side running along the stream measures 40
  meters, and the perpendicular distance from the stream to the farthest
  tip of the meadow is 30 meters.

The surveyors argue: #emph[“How can we count whole squares in a pointed
field? The slanted boundaries slice the grid squares into irregular
fragments impossible to measure!”]

A young journeyman mason steps up holding a sheet of parchment and a
pair of scissors: #emph[“Put away your measuring cords, gentlemen. There
is no need to chop blades of grass into crumbs: we merely need to invite
a twin brother to the table.”]

That very afternoon, at the opposite end of the village, the
#strong[potter] receives a curious commission: the miller requests three
vessels holding #strong[the exact same capacity of grain]---a
cylindrical jar, a conical funnel, and a hemispherical bowl. The potter
knows how to measure length with a cord and area with tiles… but how do
we measure #strong[what fits inside]?

These two challenges---measuring an irregular surface and measuring
interior capacity---will guide us through this chapter. You will
discover that both rest upon one and the same foundational principle:
#strong[cut, rearrange, and reassemble], without losing or adding a
single grain.

#divider()

=== 2. What Is an Area? Units and Conversions
<what-is-an-area-units-and-conversions>
==== 2.1 The Intuitive Idea
<the-intuitive-idea>
The #strong[area] of a planar shape is the quantity of “paint” required
to coat it, or the number of unit squares needed to tile it completely.
Two shapes with completely different outlines can have the exact same
area: it suffices that one can be dissected and reassembled to form the
other.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 We choose a unit of area: for example, the
#strong[square of side length 1 cm]. The #strong[area] of a figure is
the number of these unit squares (and fractions of squares) required to
tile it exactly, without gaps or overlaps.
]

#strong[The notation $upright("cm")^2$.] We write $1 upright(" cm")^2$
and pronounce it “one #strong[square centimeter]\.” The small
superscript 2 is not decorative: it records that we multiplied
#strong[two] lengths (a width in cm and a length in cm). It is the exact
same exponent seen in $5^2 = 5 times 5$ (Chapter 18). Indeed, the area
of a square of side length $c$ is $c^2$: that is precisely why we say
“$c$ #strong[squared]”!

#strong[Perimeter versus Area:] The #strong[perimeter] is the length of
the fence (walking around the boundary, in cm); the #strong[area] is the
surface of the lawn (filling the interior, in $upright("cm")^2$). A
rectangle of $1 times 9$ and a square of $3 times 3$ share the same area
($9 upright(" cm")^2$) but have very different perimeters
($20 upright(" cm")$ versus $12 upright(" cm")$).

==== 2.2 Area Conversions: The Pitfall of “Times 100”
<area-conversions-the-pitfall-of-times-100>
One meter is 10 decimeters. But how many square decimeters are in one
#strong[square meter]? Sketch it: a square of side length 1 m is a
square of side length 10 dm, so it contains $10 times 10 = 100$ small
squares of 1 dm on each side.

#figure(image("figures/vol_2/fig_12_perimetre_vs_aire.svg", alt: "Périmètre linéaire 1D vs Aire surfacique 2D"),
  caption: [
    Périmètre linéaire 1D vs Aire surfacique 2D
  ]
)

$ 1 upright(" m")^2 = 100 upright(" dm")^2 = 10\,000 upright(" cm")^2 = 1\,000\,000 upright(" mm")^2 . $

In an area conversion table, #strong[every metric unit spans two
columns]:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%, 7.14%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$upright("km")^2$], [], [$upright("hm")^2$
      (ha)], [], [$upright("dam")^2$
      (a)], [], [$upright("m")^2$], [], [$upright("dm")^2$], [], [$upright("cm")^2$], [], [$upright("mm")^2$], [],),
    table.hline(),
    [], [], [], [], [], [], [], [3], [5], [0], [0], [0], [], [],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Here we read:
$3.5 upright(" m")^2 = 350 upright(" dm")^2 = 35\,000 upright(" cm")^2$.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Farmers traditionally use the #strong[are]
(a), which equals $1 upright(" dam")^2 = 100 upright(" m")^2$ (a square
of 10 m by 10 m), and the #strong[hectare] (ha), which equals
$1 upright(" hm")^2 = 10\,000 upright(" m")^2$ (a square of 100 m by 100
m, roughly the size of one and a half soccer fields). The word “are”
comes from the Latin #emph[area], meaning “open space,” which also gave
rise to the English word “area”!
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]

$1 upright(" m")^2 eq.not 100 upright(" cm")^2$. Because
$1 upright(" m") = 100 upright(" cm")$, we have
$1 upright(" m")^2 = 100 upright(" cm") times 100 upright(" cm") = 10\,000 upright(" cm")^2$.
When converting units of area, you multiply or divide by 100 #strong[at
each step], not by 10.
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Scissors Stroke: Every Triangle Is Half a Rectangle
<the-scissors-stroke-every-triangle-is-half-a-rectangle>
==== 3.1 Base and Height: Precise Definitions
<base-and-height-precise-definitions>
Before cutting, we must establish exact terms.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 In any triangle, we choose one side to call
the #strong[base]. The #strong[altitude (height) relative to that base]
is the line segment dropped from the opposite vertex
#strong[perpendicular] to the line containing the base. Its length is
also termed the “height.”
]

The symbol $perp$ reads “is perpendicular to.” The small square drawn at
the foot $H$ of the altitude indicates the right angle.

A triangle has #strong[three] sides, hence #strong[three]
altitudes---one for each possible base. You may select whichever is most
convenient, provided you pair a base #strong[with its own corresponding
height].

==== 3.2 The Twin Brother Proof
<the-twin-brother-proof>
Let us return to Thomas's pointed field: base $b = 40$ m, height
$h = 30$ m.

+ Cut out a #strong[second identical twin triangle] in another color.
+ Rotate this twin through a #strong[half-turn ($180^compose$)] (a point
  reflection through the midpoint of a side, as in Chapter 26).
+ Glue it alongside the first: together, they form a
  #strong[parallelogram] of base $b$ and height $h$.
+ And as we will prove momentarily, a parallelogram has the exact same
  area as a #strong[rectangle] of dimensions $b times h$.

Even simpler, when the foot $H$ of the altitude falls inside the base:
the altitude splits the triangle into two right triangles. Each is half
of a small rectangle (halved along its diagonal). The two small
rectangles together form the large rectangle $b times h$, and the
triangle occupies precisely half of it.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
1')  (2')         Pieces (1) and (2) form the triangle.
Pieces (1') and (2') are their twin mirrors.
1)        (2)       (1) = (1') and (2) = (2')
therefore triangle = half the rectangle.
A         H         B
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Formula --- Area of a Triangle
]

$ cal(A)_(upright("triangle")) = frac(upright("base") times upright("height"), 2) = frac(b times h, 2) $
]

We frequently write $cal(A)$ in calligraphy script to avoid confusion
with vertex point $A$.

For Thomas's field:
$cal(A) = frac(40 times 30, 2) = 1200 / 2 = 600 upright(" m")^2$. Both
farmers, despite their drastically different field shapes, hold
#strong[the exact same surface area] of farmland: they will owe the
identical tax, down to the single grain of wheat.

==== 3.3 Boundary Cases: When the Altitude Falls Outside
<boundary-cases-when-the-altitude-falls-outside>
#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]
 In an #strong[obtuse] triangle (having
an angle greater than $90^compose$), the altitude to certain sides falls
#strong[outside] the triangle, landing on the #strong[extended line] of
the base. The formula remains unconditionally true, but the proof
requires care!
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof ---
]
 Foot $H$ lies on the line extension of $\[A B\]$,
beyond vertex $B$. The large right triangle $A H C$ contains our target
triangle $A B C$ #strong[plus] the small right triangle $B H C$.
Therefore:
$ cal(A)_(A B C) = cal(A)_(A H C) - cal(A)_(B H C) = frac(A H times h, 2) - frac(B H times h, 2) = frac(\(A H - B H\)times h, 2) = frac(A B times h, 2) . $
The formula “$upright("base") times upright("height") div 2$” is
universally valid for #strong[all] triangles.
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Any triangle sharing base $\[A B\]$ whose
third vertex $C$ slides along a line parallel to $\(A B\)$ possesses
#strong[the exact same area], because its perpendicular height never
varies! Even if the triangle becomes extremely flattened and skewed.
This is Euclid's famous “shearing triangle” theorem (Book I of the
#emph[Elements], Proposition 37).
]

#divider()

=== 4. Parallelograms and Trapezoids Unveiled
<parallelograms-and-trapezoids-unveiled>
==== 4.1 The Parallelogram: The Tilted Field
<the-parallelogram-the-tilted-field>
Take a cardboard rectangle and push its top edge sideways: it tilts into
a parallelogram. Has it lost any material? Cut off the small protruding
triangle on one side and glue it into the recess on the opposite side:
#strong[the rectangle reappears], with identical base and height.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
h          /                  |              |
b ----->                    <------ b ----->
Cut the left triangle and paste it onto the right side.
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Formula --- Area of a Parallelogram
]

$ cal(A)_(upright("parallelogram")) = b times h $ where $h$ is the
distance between the two parallel sides of length $b$ (measured
#strong[perpendicularly]).
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]
 #strong[Never] multiply the base by the
slanted side! A parallelogram with sides 10 cm and 6 cm can have an area
of $60 upright(" cm")^2$ (if upright, forming a rectangle),
$30 upright(" cm")^2$ (if tilted), or nearly 0 if squashed flat. The
slanted edge length does not change, but the height certainly does.
]

==== 4.2 The Trapezoid: The Flared Skirt
<the-trapezoid-the-flared-skirt>
A #strong[trapezoid (trapezium)] is a quadrilateral with two parallel
sides called #strong[bases]: the major base $B$ and the minor base $b$.
The #strong[height] $h$ is the perpendicular distance between them.

Take two twin trapezoids. Invert one upside down and place it next to
the other: together they form a #strong[parallelogram] whose base equals
$B + b$ and whose height is $h$.

#figure(image("figures/vol_4/fig_27_corde_treize_noeuds.svg", alt: "La corde égyptienne à treize nœuds et le triangle rectangle 3-4-5"),
  caption: [
    La corde égyptienne à treize nœuds et le triangle rectangle 3-4-5
  ]
)

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Formula --- Area of a Trapezoid
]

$ cal(A)_(upright("trapezoid")) = frac(\(B + b\)times h, 2) $
]

This can also be understood as $frac(B + b, 2) times h$: it equals the
area of a rectangle whose width is the #strong[average] of the two
bases.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Checking extreme boundary cases is reassuring.
If $b = B$, the trapezoid becomes a parallelogram, recovering
$frac(2 B times h, 2) = B times h$. If $b = 0$, the minor base collapses
to a point, turning the trapezoid into a triangle, recovering
$frac(B times h, 2)$. A sound mathematical formula always holds true at
its extreme limits!
]

#divider()

=== 5. Archimedes' Pizza: The Area of a Disk
<archimedes-pizza-the-area-of-a-disk>
You met the number $pi$ in Chapter 16: it is the ratio of a circle's
circumference to its diameter, $pi approx 3.14159$. The circumference of
a circle of radius $R$ is $2 pi R$. But what about the #strong[surface
area] of the interior disk?

In the 3rd century BC, #strong[Archimedes of Syracuse] envisioned
slicing the disk like a pizza into a large number of slender triangular
wedges radiating from the center. Each wedge has length equal to radius
$R$. He arranged these slices #strong[head-to-tail], alternating one
pointing up and one pointing down:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
height ≈ R
‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾  ‾‾‾‾
half-circumference = π R --------->|
    ```
  ]
]

The resulting shape resembles an #strong[undulating rectangle]: \* its
height is radius $R$\; \* its length is #strong[half the circumference]
of the pizza (the other half of the crust forms the bottom edge), which
is $frac(2 pi R, 2) = pi R$.

As the slices become infinitely slender, the ripples vanish: in the
limit, it becomes a true rectangle.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Formula --- Area of a Disk of Radius $R$
]

$ cal(A)_(upright("disk")) = pi R times R = pi R^2 $ Pronounced “pi R
squared.” Note: this is $pi times\(R times R\)$\; the exponent applies
only to $R$.
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]
 Two classical mistakes: 1. Using the
#strong[diameter] instead of the radius. For a disk of diameter 10 cm,
$R = 5$ cm and $cal(A) = 25 pi approx 78.5 upright(" cm")^2$, and
definitely not $100 pi$! 2. Confusing $2 pi R$ (the
#strong[circumference], a 1D length in cm) with $pi R^2$ (the
#strong[area], a 2D surface in $upright("cm")^2$). Memory aid: area
contains a square exponent, matching its unit $upright("cm")^2$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History ---
]
 Archimedes did not possess the decimal
digits of $pi$ (no one can write them all down, as they are endless and
non-repeating!). He proved that the area of a circle equals that of a
right triangle whose legs are the radius and the circle's circumference:
$frac(2 pi R times R, 2) = pi R^2$. Furthermore, by inscribing and
circumscribing 96-sided regular polygons around a circle, he bounded
$pi$ strictly between $3 10 / 71$ and $3 1 / 7$.
]

#divider()

=== 6. Worked Examples on Area
<worked-examples-on-area>
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- The Trapezoidal Garden.] A garden has the
shape of a trapezoid: the back fence measures 16 m, the front fence
measures 10 m, and the two parallel fences are separated by 8 m. What is
its area? How many bags of grass seed are needed if one bag covers
$25 upright(" m")^2$?

#emph[Solution.]
$cal(A) = frac(\(16 + 10\)times 8, 2) = frac(26 times 8, 2) = 208 / 2 = 104 upright(" m")^2$.
$104 div 25 = 4.16$: 4 bags are not enough, so #strong[5 bags] are
required. (Always round #strong[up] when purchasing coverage materials!)

#strong[Worked Example 2 --- The Hidden Altitude of a Right Triangle.] A
triangle $A B C$ is right-angled at $A$ with $A B = 6$ cm, $A C = 8$ cm,
and hypotenuse $B C = 10$ cm. Calculate the length of the altitude drawn
from vertex $A$ to hypotenuse $\[B C\]$.

#emph[Solution.] In a right triangle, the two perpendicular legs act as
#strong[base and altitude to each other]. Using base $\[A B\]$ and
altitude $\[A C\]$:
$ cal(A) = frac(6 times 8, 2) = 24 upright(" cm")^2 . $ Alternatively,
we can express the area using hypotenuse $\[B C\]$ as the base and
unknown altitude $h$ dropped from $A$:
$cal(A) = frac(10 times h, 2) = 5 h$. Both calculations describe the
exact same area, so
$5 h = 24 arrow.r.double.long h = 4.8 upright(" cm")$. We found an
unknown length purely through area equivalence!

#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Composite Figure.] A church window has the
shape of a rectangle 120 cm wide and 150 cm tall, topped by a semicircle
of diameter 120 cm. What is the area of the stained glass window,
rounded to the nearest $upright("cm")^2$?

#emph[Solution.] Dissect the figure into basic components: \* Rectangle:
$120 times 150 = 18\,000 upright(" cm")^2$. \* Semicircle: radius
$R = 60$ cm, area
$frac(pi times 60^2, 2) = frac(3600 pi, 2) = 1800 pi approx 5654.87 upright(" cm")^2$.
\* Total: $18\,000 + 1800 pi approx 23\,655 upright(" cm")^2$, or
approximately $2.37 upright(" m")^2$.

#strong[Worked Example 4 --- The Annulus (Circular Ring).] A circular
pond of radius 6 m is surrounded by a walking path 2 m wide. What is the
area of the path?

#emph[Solution.] The path is an #strong[annulus]: the large disk (radius
$6 + 2 = 8$ m) minus the small inner pond (radius 6 m).
$ cal(A) = pi times 8^2 - pi times 6^2 = 64 pi - 36 pi = 28 pi approx 87.96 upright(" m")^2 . $

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]
 You cannot subtract radii before
squaring: $pi times\(8 - 6\)^2 = 4 pi$ is completely wrong! One
subtracts the #strong[areas], not the linear radii.
]

#strong[Worked Example 5 --- Notary Land Conversions.] A rectangular
parcel of farmland measures 85 m by 64 m. Express its area in
$upright("m")^2$, in ares, and in hectares.

#emph[Solution.] $85 times 64 = 5\,440 upright(" m")^2$. Since
$1 upright(" a") = 100 upright(" m")^2$: $5\,440 upright(" m")^2 = 54.4$
a. Since $1 upright(" ha") = 100$ a: $54.4 upright(" a") = 0.544$ ha.

#divider()

