== CHAPTER 28: Eratosthenes and the Circumference of the Earth
<chapter-28-eratosthenes-and-the-circumference-of-the-earth>
#quote_box[
#emph[“Do not say that the Earth is too vast to be measured by a mortal.
Grant me a simple staff planted upright in the soil, a ray of sunlight,
and a right triangle: I will tell you the circumference of the world
before the sun sets.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-27>
- How #strong[Eratosthenes], more than 2,200 years ago, calculated the
  circumference of the Earth using a staff, a well, and a camel caravan.
- How a right triangle constructs three indestructible bridges between
  #strong[angles] and #strong[lengths]: the #strong[cosine], the
  #strong[sine], and the #strong[tangent].
- The universal mnemonic #strong[SOH-CAH-TOA] (or #strong[CAH-SOH-TOA])
  so you never hesitate again.
- Why the cosine and sine are #strong[strictly bounded between 0 and 1]
  inside any right triangle.
- How to determine the #strong[inaccessible height of a sea cliff], the
  #strong[width of a roaring river], or the path of a ship without
  taking a single step across.
- Pythagoras's magical identity in disguise:
  $cos^2\(x\)+ sin^2\(x\)= 1$.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: Midday on the Summer Solstice in Alexandria
<the-problem-midday-on-the-summer-solstice-in-alexandria>
The date is #strong[June 21], around the year #strong[240 BC], beneath
the luminous skies of Egypt. In the coastal city of Alexandria, the
polymath #strong[Eratosthenes] presides over the most prestigious
library of the ancient world: the Great Library. Hundreds of thousands
of papyrus scrolls line cedar shelves.

Eratosthenes reads an astonishing dispatch arriving from the southern
frontier town of #strong[Syene] (modern-day Aswan), roughly 800
kilometers up the Nile River: \> #emph[“On the day of the summer
solstice, at the stroke of noon, the sun shines straight down to
illuminate the water at the bottom of the deepest vertical wells without
casting the faintest shadow on their walls. The stone columns of temples
cast no shadow on the pavement. The solar rays strike the earth
perpendicularly.”]

Eratosthenes waits for that exact day, at that exact hour, in
#strong[Alexandria]. He plants a #strong[gnomon]---a perfectly vertical
wooden staff---into level ground, and watches closely. To his wonder: in
Alexandria, at the exact same moment, #strong[the staff casts a clear
shadow]!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Solar rays (parallel)
Alexandria:                           Syene:
Vertical staff                        Vertical staff
Cast shadow!                         No shadow!
The sun strikes at an angle)         (The sun is at the zenith)
    ```
  ]
]

How is this possible? If the Earth were a flat disk, as ancient poets
once imagined, the Sun---situated at a tremendous cosmic distance and
showering down parallel light rays---would strike Alexandria at the
exact same angle as Syene. There would either be no shadows anywhere, or
identical shadows everywhere!

If the staff in Alexandria casts a shadow while the staff in Syene casts
none, it can mean only one thing: #strong[the surface of the Earth is
curved]!

Eratosthenes measures the height of his staff and the length of its
shadow upon the ground. These two segments form a #strong[right
triangle]. Comparing these two lengths, he determines that the solar
rays deviate from the vertical by an angle of approximately
#strong[$7.2^compose$].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Tip of the staff
Vertical staff (height h)
Sunbeam      /
P (Base of staff)
O      Right angle
l ->
Shadow on ground
    ```
  ]
]

Now, by the geometric theorem of alternate interior angles (studied in
Chapter 15), this $7.2^compose$ angle at the tip of the staff is
#strong[strictly equal to the subtended central angle at the center of
the Earth] separating Alexandria from Syene!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Alexandria
Angle α = 7.2°
Syene
Center of the Earth
    ```
  ]
]

Eratosthenes then sets up a luminous proportion: \* A complete circle
spans $360^compose$. \* But $7.2^compose$ is exactly
$7.2 / 360 = 1 / 50$ of a full circle! \* Therefore, the overland
distance between Alexandria and Syene represents #strong[one-fiftieth of
the entire circumference of the Earth].

Royal surveyors and camel caravans estimate this travel distance at
approximately #strong[5,000 stadia] (about 800 km). Eratosthenes
multiplies:
$ upright("Earth’s Circumference") = 50 times 5\,000 = upright(bold(250\,000 upright(" stadia"))) approx upright(bold(40\,000 upright(" kilometers"))) ! $

An estimate of staggering precision for antiquity: modern satellite
geodesy measures the equatorial circumference at 40,075 km!

Admire the core genius of this feat: to uncover the celestial angle of
$7.2^compose$, Eratosthenes did not ascend into the heavens with a giant
protractor. He measured #strong[two linear lengths on the ground] (the
height of the staff and the length of its shadow) and deduced #strong[an
angle].

This science connecting the side lengths and angles of a triangle is
called #strong[trigonometry] (from Greek #emph[trigōnon], triangle, and
#emph[metron], measure).

#divider()

=== 2. The Right Triangle: Naming the Three Sides
<the-right-triangle-naming-the-three-sides>
To practice trigonometry without confusion, one must learn to view a
right triangle through the eyes of a #strong[specific acute angle].

Consider triangle $A B C$, right-angled at $B$. Its two acute angles are
$hat(A)$ and $hat(C)$. Let us choose to view the triangle from acute
angle $hat(A)$.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
OPPOSITE side     \   HYPOTENUSE
to angle A         \  (the longest side,
facing the right angle)
B   ^
ADJACENT side to angle A
    ```
  ]
]

Observe the three sides from vertex $A$: 1. #strong[The hypotenuse:] the
side directly opposite the $90^compose$ right angle. It is always the
longest side of the right triangle. Here, it is segment $\[A C\]$. Its
identity never depends on which acute angle you select: it is the master
side of the right triangle. 2. #strong[The side adjacent to angle
$hat(A)$:] the word #emph[adjacent] comes from Latin #emph[ad-jacere],
meaning “lying next to.” It is the leg of the right angle that touches
angle $hat(A)$. Here, it is segment $\[A B\]$. 3. #strong[The side
opposite to angle $hat(A)$:] it is the leg of the right angle facing
directly across from angle $hat(A)$. Here, it is segment $\[B C\]$.

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall --- Roles Reverse When You Switch Angles!
]

If you shift your perspective to vertex $C$:

- The hypotenuse remains $\[A C\]$.

- The side #strong[adjacent] to $hat(C)$ becomes $\[B C\]$!

- The side #strong[opposite] to $hat(C)$ becomes $\[A B\]$! A side is
  never “opposite” or “adjacent” in the absolute: it is so
  #strong[strictly relative to the chosen acute angle].
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Three Ratios: Cosine, Sine, Tangent
<the-three-ratios-cosine-sine-tangent>
Imagine drawing a right triangle with an acute angle of $30^compose$.
Whether you draw it in your notebook (hypotenuse 10 cm), on the
classroom blackboard (hypotenuse 1 meter), or across the courtyard
pavement (hypotenuse 10 meters), these three triangles are exact
#strong[dilations] of one another (similar triangles, Chapter 17).

Because their shapes are geometrically identical, #strong[the ratios of
their side lengths depend solely on the angle], and not at all on the
physical size of the drawing!

For any given acute angle $x$, these ratios carry three universal names:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Definitions ---
]
 In any right triangle:

+ The #strong[cosine] of an acute angle is the ratio of the adjacent
  side to the hypotenuse:
  $ cos\(x\)= upright("length of adjacent side") / upright("length of hypotenuse") $

+ The #strong[sine] of an acute angle is the ratio of the opposite side
  to the hypotenuse:
  $ sin\(x\)= upright("length of opposite side") / upright("length of hypotenuse") $

+ The #strong[tangent] of an acute angle is the ratio of the opposite
  side to the adjacent side:
  $ tan\(x\)= upright("length of opposite side") / upright("length of adjacent side") $
]

==== How to Pronounce and Write These Notations
<how-to-pronounce-and-write-these-notations>
- $cos\(x\)$ is written “cos” and read aloud as #strong[“cosine of
  $x$\.”]
- $sin\(x\)$ is written “sin” and read aloud as #strong[“sine of $x$\.”]
- $tan\(x\)$ is written “tan” and read aloud as #strong[“tangent of
  $x$\.”]

==== The Universal Mnemonic: SOH-CAH-TOA
<the-universal-mnemonic-soh-cah-toa>
To recall these definitions instantaneously, students across the world
chant this phrase like a warrior's banner:

$ upright(bold(S)) upright("O") upright("H") quad - quad upright(bold(C)) upright("A") upright("H") quad - quad upright(bold(T)) upright("O") upright("A") $

- #strong[S]ine = #strong[O]pposite / #strong[H]ypotenuse
- #strong[C]osine = #strong[A]djacent / #strong[H]ypotenuse
- #strong[T]angent = #strong[O]pposite / #strong[A]djacent

Some European curricula memorize it in the order #strong[CAH-SOH-TOA].
Both are identical; choose whichever resonates best in your memory!

#divider()

=== 4. Two Essential Properties to Master
<two-essential-properties-to-master>
==== 4.1 Why Cosine and Sine Always Lie Between 0 and 1
<why-cosine-and-sine-always-lie-between-0-and-1>
In any right triangle, the hypotenuse is strictly the #strong[longest
side]. Consequently, the adjacent leg is strictly shorter than the
hypotenuse, and the opposite leg is likewise strictly shorter than the
hypotenuse.

Since the numerator is strictly smaller than the denominator (and all
lengths are positive):
$ upright("adjacent") / upright("hypotenuse") < 1 #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) upright("opposite") / upright("hypotenuse") < 1 $

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Property --- Bounds on Cosine and Sine.
]
 For any acute angle
$x$ in a right triangle ($0^compose < x < 90^compose$):
$ 0 < cos\(x\)< 1 #h(2em) upright("and") #h(2em) 0 < sin\(x\)< 1 $
]

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Watch Out! Pitfall.
]
 If on a test you calculate
$cos\(x\)= 1.45$ or $sin\(x\)= 2.3$, #strong[stop immediately]: that is
mathematically impossible! You almost certainly inverted the numerator
and denominator. By contrast, the #strong[tangent]
$tan\(x\)= upright("opposite") / upright("adjacent")$ can equal any
positive real number: it can be $0.5$, $1$, $10$, or even $1\,000$ if
the triangle is extremely tall and narrow!
]

==== 4.2 The Fundamental Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity
<the-fundamental-pythagorean-trigonometric-identity>
What happens if we sum the squares of the cosine and sine of the same
angle $x$? Consider our right triangle with adjacent side $a$, opposite
side $b$, and hypotenuse $c$. By the Pythagorean theorem (Chapter 19):
$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $

Now compute:
$ cos^2\(x\)+ sin^2\(x\)= (a / c)^2 + (b / c)^2 = a^2 / c^2 + b^2 / c^2 = frac(a^2 + b^2, c^2) $
Since $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, we have:
$ frac(a^2 + b^2, c^2) = c^2 / c^2 = 1 $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem of Trigonometry ---
]
 For any angle $x$:
$ cos^2\(x\)+ sin^2\(x\)= 1 $ #emph[\(Note on notation: $cos^2\(x\)$
denotes $\(cos\(x\)\)^2$, meaning the cosine multiplied by itself).]
]

Furthermore, dividing sine by cosine yields:
$ frac(sin\(x\), cos\(x\)) = upright("opposite") / upright("hypotenuse") / upright("adjacent") / upright("hypotenuse") = upright("opposite") / upright("adjacent") = tan\(x\) $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Quotient Identity ---
]
 $ tan\(x\)= frac(sin\(x\), cos\(x\)) $
]

These two identities allow you to determine the sine and tangent of an
angle the instant its cosine is known, without even determining the
angle itself!

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? Where Do These Strange Words Come From?
]
 The
word #strong[sine] traveled through one of the most famous linguistic
misadventures in history. Fifth-century Indian astronomers termed this
half-chord #emph[jya-ardha], shortened to #emph[jiva]. When Arab
scholars translated these texts in the 8th century, they transliterated
the sound as #emph[jiba], written #emph[jb] without vowels. In
12th-century Toledo, Spain, the translator Gerard of Cremona mistook
this word for the Arabic word #emph[jayb], which means “crease of a
robe, pocket, or bay.” He translated it into Latin as #strong[sinus],
meaning “fold, curve, or gulf” (which also gives us the word “sinuous”)!
As for #strong[cosine], it is an abbreviation of #emph[complementi
sinus]: the sine of the complementary angle!
]

#divider()

=== 5. Using the Calculator: Two Essential Modes
<using-the-calculator-two-essential-modes>
To carry out trigonometric calculations, you will use the scientific
calculator keys: `cos`, `sin`, `tan`.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Critical Warning! Calculator Angle Mode!
]
 A scientific
calculator can measure angles in Degrees (DEG), Radians (RAD), or
Gradians (GRA). In middle school, #strong[all angles are given in
degrees]. Always ensure that a small letter #strong[D] or #strong[DEG]
appears at the top of your display screen. If you see an #strong[R] or
#strong[RAD], all your calculations will be wrong! To switch to degrees,
navigate to the `Setup` or `Mode` menu.
]

==== Case 1: The Angle Is Known, Find the Ratio
<case-1-the-angle-is-known-find-the-ratio>
Press the key directly:

- $cos\(60^compose\)= 0.5$ exactly.
- $sin\(30^compose\)= 0.5$ exactly.
- $tan\(45^compose\)= 1$ exactly (because in a half-square, opposite leg
  \= adjacent leg!).
- $cos\(48^compose\)approx 0.6691 . . .$

==== Case 2: The Ratio Is Known, Find the Angle (Inverse Keys)
<case-2-the-ratio-is-known-find-the-angle-inverse-keys>
If you know that $cos\(x\)= 0.8$, what is the angle $x$? Use the inverse
key, labeled #strong[$arccos$] or #strong[$cos^(- 1)$] (typically
accessed by pressing `2nd` or `Shift` followed by `cos`):

- If $cos\(x\)= 0.8$, then $x = arccos\(0.8\)approx 36.87^compose$.
- If $sin\(x\)= 0.5$, then $x = arcsin\(0.5\)= 30^compose$.
- If $tan\(x\)= 1$, then $x = arctan\(1\)= 45^compose$.

