=== The Question Opening the Next Chapter
<the-question-opening-the-next-chapter-4>
Through trigonometry, we learned to survey continuous space, celestial
angles, and vast terrestrial distances. But what happens if we step away
from smoothly stretching lengths and return to the realm of
#strong[discrete, indivisible integers]?

How can we tile a rectangular hall with square marble slabs without
cutting a single tile? How can treasures be divided into strictly equal
shares without breaking a single coin? In Alexandria, a century before
Eratosthenes, another intellectual giant established the eternal laws of
integer division. Prepare to discover the secrets of divisors and the
immortal Euclidean algorithm in Chapter 29: #strong[The Partition of
Euclid].

