=== 1. The Problem: The Pharaoh's Mosaic and the Scribe's Baskets
<the-problem-the-pharaohs-mosaic-and-the-scribes-baskets>
Alexandria, around the year #strong[300 BC]. King Ptolemy I commissions
a ceremonial hall in his royal palace. Two master craftsmen appear
before the chief royal architect, their brows furrowed with concern.

- The first is the #strong[master mosaicist]. The floor of the hall is a
  vast rectangle measuring #strong[420 centimeters long by 132
  centimeters wide]. The king has decreed: #emph[“I want this floor
  paved entirely with square marble slabs, all strictly identical. I
  forbid cutting a single tile along the edges: every slab must be laid
  whole! And so that the floor appears magnificent, I demand that these
  slabs be #strong[as large as possible]!”] The mosaicist groans: 1 cm
  tiles would work, of course, but it would take 55,440 of them and they
  would look tiny! Slabs of 2 cm would also work. But what is the
  largest possible square dimension?

- The second artisan is the #strong[steward scribe] in charge of royal
  festivities. To honor visiting foreign dignitaries, he opens two
  chests of prized delicacies:

  - One chest holding #strong[315 Egyptian pomegranates]\;

  - One chest holding #strong[180 honey confections]. The king demands
    identical ceremonial gift baskets for the guests: #emph[“All baskets
    must be strictly identical: exactly the same count of pomegranates
    and the same count of honey confections in each basket. And I want
    nothing left over in the chests! Finally, I wish to host the
    #strong[greatest possible number] of dignitaries!”]

