=== 3. Prime Numbers: The Atoms of the Mathematical Universe
<prime-numbers-the-atoms-of-the-mathematical-universe>
Take the number 12. You can split it: $12 = 3 times 4$. Then split 4
further: $4 = 2 times 2$. Thus $12 = 2 times 2 times 3$. Can you split 2
or 3 any further? No! Two and three are indivisible. They can only be
divided by 1 and by themselves.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Formal Definition --- Prime Number.
]
 A natural number is
called #strong[prime] if it possesses #strong[exactly two distinct
divisors]: 1 and itself.
]

Observe the direct consequences of this definition:

- #strong[The number 0 is not prime]: it has infinitely many divisors
  (every non-zero number divides 0).
- #strong[The number 1 is NOT prime]: it has only #strong[one] divisor
  (itself). The definition strictly requires #emph[two] distinct
  divisors. If 1 were classified as prime, the uniqueness of prime
  factorizations would break down
  ($6 = 2 times 3 = 1 times 2 times 3 = 1 times 1 times 2 times 3 dots.h$).
- #strong[The number 2 is prime]: it is the #strong[only even prime
  number] in the universe! Every other even number is divisible by 2,
  and therefore composite.

==== List of Prime Numbers Less Than 30
<list-of-prime-numbers-less-than-30>
Memorize these as fundamental building blocks:
$ upright(bold(2\,quad 3\,quad 5\,quad 7\,quad 11\,quad 13\,quad 17\,quad 19\,quad 23\,quad 29)) $
