=== 4. Prime Factorization
<prime-factorization>
Here stands the single most important theorem in all of number theory.
It is so foundational that it is termed the #strong[Fundamental Theorem
of Arithmetic].

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic ---
]
 Every integer greater
than or equal to 2 can be written as a #strong[product of prime
numbers]. This factorization is #strong[unique], up to the order of the
factors.
]

This is the “genetic fingerprint” of an integer. Two different numbers
can never share the exact same prime factorization.

==== Two Practical Methods for Factoring Numbers
<two-practical-methods-for-factoring-numbers>
Let us factor the number #strong[$360$].

===== Method 1: The Factor Tree (Ideal for Mental Arithmetic)
<method-1-the-factor-tree-ideal-for-mental-arithmetic>
Split the number into two convenient factors, then branch downward until
every terminal leaf is a prime number:

Collect all prime leaves at the ends of the branches:
$ 360 = 2 times 2 times 2 times 3 times 3 times 5 = upright(bold(2^3 times 3^2 times 5)) $

===== Method 2: The Division Ladder (The Algorithmic Method)
<method-2-the-division-ladder-the-algorithmic-method>
Draw a vertical bar. On the left, write the quotient; on the right,
divide by successive primes in ascending order
($2\,3\,5\,7\,11 dots.h$):

The column on the right lists the prime factors directly:
$ 360 = 2 times 2 times 2 times 3 times 3 times 5 = upright(bold(2^3 times 3^2 times 5)) . $

