=== 5. The GCD: Greatest Common Divisor
<the-gcd-greatest-common-divisor>
==== 5.1 Definition
<definition>
When comparing two positive integers $a$ and $b$, certain integers
divide both $a$ and $b$ simultaneously: these are their #strong[common
divisors]. Because 1 divides every integer, there is always at least one
common divisor: the number 1.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- GCD (Greatest Common Divisor).
]
 Among all
common divisors of two positive integers $a$ and $b$, the greatest of
them is called the #strong[Greatest Common Divisor], denoted
#strong[$gcd\(a\,b\)$] or #strong[$upright("GCD")\(a\,b\)$].
]

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Relatively Prime (Coprime) Numbers.
]
 Two
positive integers are said to be #strong[relatively prime (coprime)]
when their only common divisor is 1: $ gcd\(a\,b\)= 1 $ #emph[\(Example:
8 and 15 are not prime numbers, but they are coprime because the
divisors of 8 are ${ 1\,2\,4\,8 }$ and those of 15 are
${ 1\,3\,5\,15 }$\; their sole common divisor is 1).]
]

==== 5.2 First Method: Using Prime Factorization
<first-method-using-prime-factorization>
To find the GCD of two numbers from their prime factorizations: 1.
Identify the #strong[common prime factors] shared by both
factorizations. 2. For each shared prime factor, select the
#strong[smallest exponent] with which it appears. 3. Multiply these
shared powers together.

#emph[Example:] Compute $gcd\(180\,315\)$ (the banquet foods from our
opening problem!): - $180 = 2^2 times 3^2 times 5$ -
$315 = 3^2 times 5 times 7$

Shared prime factors: 3 and 5.

- Lowest power of 3: $3^2$.

- Lowest power of 5: $5^1$.
  $ gcd\(180\,315\)= 3^2 times 5 = 9 times 5 = upright(bold(45)) . $ The
  royal scribe can prepare at most #strong[45 identical gift baskets]!
  Each basket will contain:

- $315 div 45 = 7$ pomegranates;

- $180 div 45 = 4$ honey confections.

Zero leftovers in the royal pantry!

#divider()

=== 6. The Two Historical Algorithms: Subtraction and Division
<the-two-historical-algorithms-subtraction-and-division>
When dealing with large numbers, listing all divisors or finding
complete prime factorizations can be labor-intensive. Ancient
mathematicians devised fast, mechanical algorithms.

==== 6.1 The Repeated Subtraction Algorithm
<the-repeated-subtraction-algorithm>
This method rests on a straightforward arithmetic property: \>
#strong[📐 Property of Subtractions ---] \> If an integer $d$ divides
both $a$ and $b$ (with $a gt.eq b$), then $d$ also divides their
difference $a - b$. \> Consequently: $gcd\(a\,b\)= gcd\(b\,a - b\)$.

#strong[The Process:] Replace the larger number with the difference of
the two numbers, and repeat until both numbers become equal. That shared
final value is the GCD!

#emph[Example:] Compute $gcd\(75\,45\)$: -
$75 - 45 = 30 arrow.r.double.long upright("keep ") 45 upright(" and ") 30$
\-
$45 - 30 = 15 arrow.r.double.long upright("keep ") 30 upright(" and ") 15$
\-
$30 - 15 = 15 arrow.r.double.long upright("keep ") 15 upright(" and ") 15$
\- $15 - 15 = 0$

The matching numbers reached are 15, so
$gcd\(75\,45\)= upright(bold(15))$.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ The Limitation of Subtraction:
]
 If you needed to compute
$gcd\(1\,000\,000\,2\)$, you would have to subtract 2 five hundred
thousand times! That is impractically slow. Fortunately, Euclid
conceived a stroke of brilliance.
]

==== 6.2 The Euclidean Algorithm (Successive Divisions)
<the-euclidean-algorithm-successive-divisions>
Instead of subtracting $b$ one step at a time, why not subtract all
possible multiples of $b$ in a single stroke? That is precisely the
principle of #strong[Euclidean division] (integer division with
remainder): $ a = b times q + r quad\(0 lt.eq r < b\) $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Euclid's Theorem ---
]
 If integer division of $a$ by $b$
yields remainder $r$, then: $ gcd\(a\,b\)= gcd\(b\,r\) $
]

#strong[The Euclidean Algorithm Procedure:] 1. Perform integer division
of the larger number by the smaller number. 2. If the remainder is
non-zero, divide the previous divisor by the remainder. 3. Repeat until
the remainder becomes zero ($r = 0$). 4. #strong[The GCD is the last
NON-ZERO remainder.]

#emph[Example: Solve the Alexandria mosaic tiling problem:
$gcd\(420\,132\)$.]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Step 1:  420 = 132 × 3 + 24    (remainder r₁ = 24 ≠ 0)
Step 2:  132 =  24 × 5 + 12    (remainder r₂ = 12 ≠ 0)
Step 3:   24 =  12 × 2 +  0    (remainder r₃ = 0)
    ```
  ]
]

The final remainder before 0 is #strong[12]. Therefore:
$ gcd\(420\,132\)= upright(bold(12)) . $

The master mosaicist has his solution:

- The largest possible uncut square slabs measure
  #strong[$12 upright(" cm") times 12 upright(" cm")$].
- Along the length, he lays $420 div 12 = 35$ slabs.
- Along the width, he lays $132 div 12 = 11$ slabs.
- In total, he will install
  $35 times 11 = upright(bold(385 upright(" slabs")))$, fitting
  seamlessly without cutting a millimeter of marble!

#divider()

=== 7. The Crown Application: Simplifying Fractions to Lowest Terms
<the-crown-application-simplifying-fractions-to-lowest-terms>
In middle school, you learned to simplify fractions by dividing
numerator and denominator by common factors. But often students stop
prematurely: dividing by 2, then by 3, uncertain whether further
reduction is possible.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Irreducible Fraction (Lowest Terms).
]
 A
fraction $a / b$ is said to be #strong[irreducible] (in lowest terms) if
its numerator $a$ and denominator $b$ are #strong[relatively prime],
meaning $gcd\(a\,b\)= 1$. It cannot be simplified any further.
]

Using the GCD, any fraction can be simplified to lowest terms #strong[in
a single step]:

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- One-Step Simplification.
]
 To reduce $a / b$ to
lowest terms, divide both numerator and denominator by their
#strong[GCD]: $ a / b = frac(a div gcd\(a\,b\), b div gcd\(a\,b\)) $
]

#emph[Example:] Simplify the fraction $132 / 420$ to lowest terms. We
previously calculated $gcd\(420\,132\)= 12$:
$ 132 / 420 = frac(132 div 12, 420 div 12) = upright(bold(11 / 35)) . $
Since 11 and 35 share no common factors other than 1, the fraction is
guaranteed to be in lowest terms!

#divider()

=== 8. Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-5>
==== Example 1: Factorization and divisibility
<example-1-factorization-and-divisibility>
+ Factor the integer $540$ into a product of primes using the division
  ladder.
+ Write the result using exponent notation.
+ Is 540 divisible by 18? By 24? Justify without performing long
  division.

#emph[Detailed Solution:] 1. Division ladder:

- $540 div 2 = 270$

- $270 div 2 = 135$

- $135 div 3 = 45$ (since $1 + 3 + 5 = 9$)

- $45 div 3 = 15$

- $15 div 3 = 5$

- $5 div 5 = 1$

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Prime factorization:
  $540 = 2 times 2 times 3 times 3 times 3 times 5 = upright(bold(2^2 times 3^3 times 5))$.

+ - For 18: $18 = 2 times 3^2$. The prime factorization of 540 contains
    at least one factor of 2 (it has two) and at least two factors of 3
    (it has three). Thus #strong[540 is divisible by 18].

  - For 24: $24 = 2^3 times 3$. The factorization of 540 contains only
    $2^2$ (only two factors of 2). It lacks a third 2, so #strong[540 is
    not divisible by 24].
]

#divider()

==== Example 2: The Euclidean algorithm on large numbers
<example-2-the-euclidean-algorithm-on-large-numbers>
Find the GCD of $1\,575$ and $420$ using the Euclidean algorithm.

#emph[Detailed Solution:] Execute successive Euclidean divisions:

- $1\,575 = 420 times 3 + 315$ (remainder $315 eq.not 0$)
- $420 = 315 times 1 + 105$ (remainder $105 eq.not 0$)
- $315 = 105 times 3 + 0$ (remainder $0$)

The last non-zero remainder is #strong[105]. Conclusion:
$upright(bold(gcd\(1\,575\,420\)= 105))$.

#divider()

==== Example 3: Comparing subtractions versus divisions
<example-3-comparing-subtractions-versus-divisions>
Compute $gcd\(252\,105\)$: 1. Using repeated subtractions. 2. Using the
Euclidean algorithm.

#emph[Detailed Solution:] 1. #strong[Repeated subtractions:]

- $252 - 105 = 147$

- $147 - 105 = 42$

- $105 - 42 = 63$

- $63 - 42 = 21$

- $42 - 21 = 21$

- $21 - 21 = 0 arrow.r.double.long upright(bold(gcd = 21))$ (5
  subtraction steps).

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Euclidean algorithm:]

  - $252 = 105 times 2 + 42$

  - $105 = 42 times 2 + 21$

  - $42 = 21 times 2 + 0 arrow.r.double.long upright(bold(gcd = 21))$ (2
    division steps).
]

Euclid's division approach is distinctly faster and cleaner!

#divider()

==== Example 4: School fair party bags
<example-4-school-fair-party-bags>
For the school carnival, organizers have 280 glow bracelets and 448
collector stickers. They wish to make identical surprise gift bags
containing the exact same number of bracelets and stickers, using all
supplies with no leftovers. 1. What is the maximum number of gift bags
they can prepare? 2. What will be the contents of each gift bag?

#emph[Detailed Solution:] 1. Let $N$ be the number of gift bags. For
supplies to be distributed evenly without leftovers, $N$ must divide
both 280 and 448. For $N$ to be maximal, $N = gcd\(448\,280\)$.
Euclidean algorithm:

- $448 = 280 times 1 + 168$

- $280 = 168 times 1 + 112$

- $168 = 112 times 1 + 56$

- $112 = 56 times 2 + 0$ The last non-zero remainder is 56, so
  $gcd\(448\,280\)= 56$. They can prepare at most #strong[56 identical
  gift bags].

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Contents per bag:

  - Glow bracelets:
    $280 div 56 = upright(bold(5 upright(" bracelets")))$.
  - Collector stickers:
    $448 div 56 = upright(bold(8 upright(" stickers")))$.
]

#divider()

==== Example 5: Lowest terms and addition of fractions
<example-5-lowest-terms-and-addition-of-fractions>
Consider the fraction $A = frac(1\,575, 420)$. 1. Reduce fraction $A$ to
lowest terms. 2. Compute the sum $S = frac(1\,575, 420) + 1 / 4$ as an
irreducible fraction.

#emph[Detailed Solution:] 1. From Example 2, $gcd\(1\,575\,420\)= 105$.
Divide numerator and denominator by 105:
$ A = frac(1\,575 div 105, 420 div 105) = upright(bold(15 / 4)) . $ 2.
Substitute the simplified fraction into the addition:
$ S = 15 / 4 + 1 / 4 = frac(15 + 1, 4) = 16 / 4 = upright(bold(4)) . $
The calculation becomes instantaneous!

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-12>
+ #strong[Foundational Vocabulary:]

  - $a = b times k arrow.l.r.double a$ is a multiple of
    $b arrow.l.r.double b$ is a divisor of $a$.
  - #strong[Prime Number]: an integer with exactly 2 distinct divisors
    (1 and itself). 0 and 1 are NOT prime. The only even prime is 2.

+ #strong[Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic:]

  - Every integer $gt.eq 2$ factors uniquely into a product of prime
    powers ($N = p_1^(alpha_1) times p_2^(alpha_2) times dots.h$).

+ #strong[GCD (Greatest Common Divisor):]

  - The largest integer that divides both numbers evenly.

  - Two numbers are #strong[coprime] if $gcd\(a\,b\)= 1$.

  - #strong[Prime Factorization Method]: product of all shared prime
    factors raised to their lowest powers.

  - #strong[Euclidean Algorithm]: chain successive divisions
    $a = b times q + r$. The GCD is the #strong[last non-zero
    remainder].

+ #strong[Applications:]

  - #strong[Fractions in Lowest Terms]: divide numerator and denominator
    by their GCD.
  - #strong[Tiling and Equal Batches]: the GCD gives the maximal side
    length of unbroken square tiles or the maximal number of identical
    batches without leftovers.

#divider()

