=== 2. The Formal Vocabulary of Probability
<the-formal-vocabulary-of-probability>
To speak of chance with the rigor of a geometer, mathematicians forged a
very precise vocabulary. Each word carries a strict definition that must
be mastered.

==== 2.1 Random Experiment and Outcome
<random-experiment-and-outcome>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Random Experiment.
]
 An experiment is said to
be #strong[random] if: 1. All possible results are known in advance; 2.
The final result depends solely on chance and is #strong[impossible to
predict with certainty].
]

#emph[Examples:] - Tossing a balanced coin and observing the upper face
is a random experiment. - Dropping an apple to see if it falls toward
the ground is #strong[not] a random experiment (gravity guarantees that
it will always fall).

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Outcome.
]
 An #strong[outcome] (or elementary
event) is one of the possible results of a random experiment. The set of
all possible outcomes is called the #strong[sample space] (often denoted
by the Greek letter $Omega$, “omega”).
]

#emph[Example with a 6-sided die:] The possible outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4,
5, and 6. The sample space is ${ 1\,2\,3\,4\,5\,6 }$.

==== 2.2 Events: Impossible, Certain, Complementary
<events-impossible-certain-complementary>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Event.
]
 An #strong[event] is a set consisting
of zero, one, or several outcomes. An event is said to #strong[occur]
(or be realized) if the outcome obtained during the experiment belongs
to that event.
]

Consider the roll of an ordinary six-sided die:

- Event $A$: “Roll an even number” consists of the outcomes
  ${ 2\,4\,6 }$. If the die lands on 4, event $A$ has occurred.

- #strong[Impossible event:] an event that contains no outcomes and can
  never happen. Its probability is equal to #strong[0]. #emph[Example:]
  “Roll a 7” with a 6-sided die.

- #strong[Certain event:] an event that contains all possible outcomes
  and always happens. Its probability is equal to #strong[1] (or
  $100 %$)! #emph[Example:] “Roll a number less than or equal to 6”.

- #strong[Complementary event:] the complementary event of an event $A$,
  denoted #strong[$macron(A)$] (read #strong[“A bar”] or “not $A$”), is
  the event that occurs #strong[precisely when $A$ does not occur].

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Complete Sample Space Ω (Total=1)
Event A         Complement Ā
Success)       (Failures)
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Property of Complementary Events ---
]
 For any
event $A$:
$ P\(macron(A)\)= 1 - P\(A\)#h(2em) upright("or equivalently") #h(2em) P\(A\)+ P\(macron(A)\)= 1 $
]

#emph[Example:] If the probability of rain tomorrow is
$P\(upright("Rain")\)= 0.3$ (or $30 %$), then the probability of no rain
is:
$ P\(upright("No Rain")\)= 1 - 0.3 = 0.7 quad\(upright("or ") 70 %\). $

#divider()

=== 3. Calculating Probability under Equiprobability
<calculating-probability-under-equiprobability>
==== 3.1 What is Equiprobability?
<what-is-equiprobability>
A situation is one of #strong[equiprobability] (equally likely outcomes)
when #strong[every possible outcome has exactly the same chance of
occurring]. In problems, this condition is indicated by standard key
phrases:

- “A #strong[fair] (or balanced, unbiased) six-sided die”;
- “A #strong[fair] coin”;
- “Marbles #strong[indistinguishable by touch]” in an urn;
- “A card drawn #strong[at random] from a well-shuffled deck”.

==== 3.2 Laplace's Formula
<laplaces-formula>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Laplace's Rule --- Probability under Equiprobability.
]
 In a
situation with equally likely outcomes, the probability of an event $E$
is equal to the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number
of possible outcomes:
$ P\(E\)= frac(upright("Number of outcomes favorable to ") E, upright("Total number of possible outcomes")) $
]

==== 3.3 The Three Guises of a Probability
<the-three-guises-of-a-probability>
A probability is always a real number between 0 and 1:
$ 0 lt.eq P\(E\)lt.eq 1 $ It can be written in three equivalent forms:
\1. As a #strong[fraction] (exact value): $1 / 4$ 2. As a
#strong[decimal number]: $0.25$ 3. As a #strong[percentage]: $25 %$

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Warning, Trap!
]
 A probability can #strong[NEVER] be negative,
nor greater than 1 (or $100 %$)! If you calculate $P = 1.25$ or
$P = - 0.3$, there is inevitably an error in your work. Furthermore, the
sum of the probabilities of all elementary outcomes is #strong[always
equal to 1].
]

Returning to our caravan:

- For the Marsh Trail:
  $P\(upright("Peril")\)= 10 / 100 = 1 / 10 = 0.10 = upright(bold(10 %))$.
- For the Gorge Trail:
  $P\(upright("Peril")\)= 40 / 100 = 4 / 10 = 0.40 = upright(bold(40 %))$.

The danger in the gorge is #strong[four times higher]! The wise master
chooses the marsh, and the caravan reaches its destination unharmed.

#divider()

=== 4. Two-Stage Experiments: Trees and Tables
<two-stage-experiments-trees-and-tables>
When a random experiment unfolds across several successive stages (for
instance: tossing two coins in a row, rolling two dice, or drawing two
marbles from a bag), keeping track of all outcomes mentally becomes
treacherous. Two visual tools make everything transparent.

==== 4.1 Tool \#1: The Two-Way Table (The Classic Two-Dice Example)
<tool-1-the-two-way-table-the-classic-two-dice-example>
If you roll two 6-sided dice and compute the sum of the two numbers, how
many possible outcomes are there? The sums range from $1 + 1 = 2$ up to
$6 + 6 = 12$. That makes 11 different possible sums. Some students leap
to the conclusion: #emph[“The probability of getting a sum of 7 is 1 in
11!”] #strong[This is a monumental blunder!] Because the sums are not
equally likely!

Let us construct the table of all possible pairs
($6 times 6 = upright(bold(36 upright(" equally likely outcomes")))$):

