  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Look at the long diagonal of #strong[7s]:

- To obtain a sum of #strong[2], there is only a single cell:
  $\(1\;1\)arrow.r.double.long P\(2\)= 1 / 36$.
- To obtain a sum of #strong[12], only one cell:
  $\(6\;6\)arrow.r.double.long P\(12\)= 1 / 36$.
- But to obtain a sum of #strong[7], there are #strong[6 different
  cells]: $\(1\;6\)\,\(2\;5\)\,\(3\;4\)\,\(4\;3\)\,\(5\;2\)\,\(6\;1\)$!
  $ P\(upright("Sum") = 7\)= 6 / 36 = 1 / 6 approx upright(bold(16.7 %)) $
  A sum of 7 is #strong[six times more likely] than a sum of 2! It is
  the king of the dice table.

