=== 5. Sampling With Replacement vs.~Sampling Without Replacement
<sampling-with-replacement-vs.-sampling-without-replacement>
This is one of the most vital distinctions in introductory probability.
Imagine a box containing #strong[3 red marbles (R)] and #strong[2 green
marbles (G)] (making 5 marbles in total). We draw two marbles
successively at random.

==== Case 1: Sampling WITH Replacement
<case-1-sampling-with-replacement>
After recording the color of the first marble, we #strong[place it back
into the box] and mix before drawing the second marble.

- On the first draw: $P\(R\)= 3 / 5$ and $P\(G\)= 2 / 5$.

- On the second draw: the box is once again #strong[strictly identical]!
  The probabilities do not change: $P\(R\)= 3 / 5$ and $P\(G\)= 2 / 5$.

- The probability of drawing two reds $\(R\;R\)$ is:
  $ P\(R\;R\)= 3 / 5 times 3 / 5 = 9 / 25 = 0.36 = upright(bold(36 %)) . $

==== Case 2: Sampling WITHOUT Replacement
<case-2-sampling-without-replacement>
After the first draw, we keep the marble in hand: #strong[we do not
return it]!

- On the first draw: $P\(R\)= 3 / 5$.
- For the second draw, only #strong[4 marbles in total remain] in the
  box!
  - If the first marble was red, only 2 red marbles remain out of 4:
    $P\(upright("2nd ") R\)= 2 / 4$.

  - The probability of drawing two reds $\(R\;R\)$ is:
    $ P\(R\;R\)= 3 / 5 times 2 / 4 = 6 / 20 = 3 / 10 = 0.30 = upright(bold(30 %)) . $

#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Warning, Trap!
]
 In sampling without replacement, #strong[the
denominator decreases by 1] on the second draw, and the numerator
corresponding to the drawn color also decreases by 1!
]

#divider()

=== 6. From Theoretical Chance to the Real World: The Law of Large Numbers
<from-theoretical-chance-to-the-real-world-the-law-of-large-numbers>
When you flip a fair coin, the theoretical probability of landing Heads
is $p = 0.5$. Does this mean that if you flip the coin 10 times, you
will obtain #strong[exactly] 5 Heads and 5 Tails? No! You could very
easily get 7 Heads and 3 Tails (an observed relative frequency of $70 %$
Heads).

What happens, however, if you flip the coin 100 times, 1,000 times,
100,000 times? Let us calculate the #strong[observed relative
frequency]:
$ f = frac(upright("Number of observed Heads"), upright("Total number of flips ") N) $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Phenomenon of Frequency Stabilization (Law of Large Numbers)
---
]
 When a random experiment is repeated an #strong[independently large
number of times], the observed relative frequency of an event approaches
and stabilizes around its #strong[theoretical probability].
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Relative Frequency f
1.0 o
0.7 o
0.5 \ooo Theoretical Probability = 0.5
0.3 o   \  /   o   \
o          \
0.0
10    100    1,000   10,000   Number of trials N
    ```
  ]
]

This very principle allows insurance companies to set policy premiums
fairly, meteorologists to predict storm patterns, and casinos to
guarantee they never operate at a net loss over the course of a full
year!

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History --- The Letters of Pascal and Fermat (1654)
]
 In
1654, a Parisian gambler, the #strong[Chevalier de Méré], presented
#strong[Blaise Pascal] with an enigma that set tavern arguments ablaze:
“the problem of points” (#emph[le problème des partis]). Two players are
tossing a coin for a prize purse of 64 gold pistoles. The first player
to reach 3 wins takes everything. But the game is abruptly interrupted
by a fire when Player A is leading 2 to 1! How should the purse be
divided in fairness? Pascal began exchanging letters with the brilliant
magistrate of Toulouse, #strong[Pierre de Fermat]. Rather than dwelling
on the past, they mapped the tree of all possible futures: across the 4
possible game completions, Player A would win in 3 of them, and Player B
in only 1. Natural justice therefore demanded giving $3 / 4$ of the pot
to Player A (48 pistoles) and $1 / 4$ to Player B (16 pistoles). Modern
probability theory was born!
]

#divider()

=== 7. Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-6>
==== Worked Example 1 --- Drawing from a Deck of Cards (Equiprobability and Complementary Events)
<worked-example-1-drawing-from-a-deck-of-cards-equiprobability-and-complementary-events>
A card is drawn at random from a well-shuffled 32-card deck (consisting
of 4 suits: Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs, Spades, each with 8 cards: 7, 8, 9,
10, Jack, Queen, King, Ace). 1. What is the probability of drawing an
Ace? 2. What is the probability of drawing a red card (Heart or
Diamond)? 3. What is the probability of drawing a card that is
#strong[not] a Club?

#emph[Step-by-Step Solution:] Because the deck is thoroughly shuffled,
every card is equally likely to be drawn. 1. The deck contains 4 Aces
(one of each suit).
$ P\(upright("Ace")\)= 4 / 32 = 1 / 8 = upright(bold(0.125)) quad\(12.5 %\). $
\2. There are 8 hearts and 8 diamonds, totaling $8 + 8 = 16$ red cards
out of 32.
$ P\(upright("Red")\)= 16 / 32 = 1 / 2 = upright(bold(0.5)) quad\(50 %\). $
\3. #emph[Method using the complementary event:] Let event $C$ be
“Drawing a Club”. There are 8 clubs, so $P\(C\)= 8 / 32 = 1 / 4$. The
event “Not drawing a Club” is the complementary event $macron(C)$:
$ P\(macron(C)\)= 1 - P\(C\)= 1 - 1 / 4 = upright(bold(3 / 4)) = upright(bold(0.75)) quad\(75 %\). $

#divider()

==== Worked Example 2 --- Two-Stage Sequential Experiment (The Keychain)
<worked-example-2-two-stage-sequential-experiment-the-keychain>
A keychain holds 4 keys that look identical, but #strong[only one] opens
the garage door. A hurried driver tries the keys at random in the dark.
Whenever a key does not work, he sets it aside so as not to retest it
(sampling without replacement). What is the probability that he opens
the door on exactly his second attempt?

#emph[Step-by-Step Solution:] To succeed on the second attempt, two
consecutive events must occur:

- Failure on the 1st attempt ($F_1$);
- Success on the 2nd attempt ($S_2$).

Let us examine the corresponding branches: 1. On the 1st attempt: there
are 3 wrong keys out of 4 keys in total: $ P\(F_1\)= 3 / 4 $ 2. On the
2nd attempt: the wrong key has been removed. Only 3 keys remain, of
which exactly 1 is the right key:
$ P\(S_2 upright(" given ") F_1\)= 1 / 3 $ 3. By the multiplication rule
along a path:
$ P\(upright("Success on 2nd attempt")\)= P\(F_1\)times P\(S_2\)= 3 / 4 times 1 / 3 = 3 / 12 = upright(bold(1 / 4)) = upright(bold(0.25)) . $
Each attempt has strictly a $1 / 4$ chance of being the successful one!

#divider()

==== Worked Example 3 --- Drawing Marbles With and Without Replacement
<worked-example-3-drawing-marbles-with-and-without-replacement>
A bag contains 2 blue marbles and 3 yellow marbles. Two marbles are
drawn successively at random. What is the probability of drawing
#strong[two marbles of different colors]: 1. When sampling #strong[with
replacement]? 2. When sampling #strong[without replacement]?

#emph[Step-by-Step Solution:] Drawing two marbles of different colors
corresponds to two disjoint paths: $\(B\;Y\)$ or $\(Y\;B\)$. 1.
#strong[With replacement:]

- $P\(B\;Y\)= 2 / 5 times 3 / 5 = 6 / 25$.
- $P\(Y\;B\)= 3 / 5 times 2 / 5 = 6 / 25$. Adding the two mutually
  exclusive paths:
  $ P\(upright("Different colors")\)= 6 / 25 + 6 / 25 = 12 / 25 = upright(bold(0.48)) quad\(48 %\). $

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Without replacement:]

  - For $\(B\;Y\)$: first blue ($2\/5$), then 4 marbles remain including
    3 yellow ($3\/4$):
    $ P\(B\;Y\)= 2 / 5 times 3 / 4 = 6 / 20 = 3 / 10 . $

  - For $\(Y\;B\)$: first yellow ($3\/5$), then 4 marbles remain
    including 2 blue ($2\/4$):
    $ P\(Y\;B\)= 3 / 5 times 2 / 4 = 6 / 20 = 3 / 10 . $ Adding the two
    paths:
    $ P\(upright("Different colors")\)= 3 / 10 + 3 / 10 = 6 / 10 = upright(bold(0.60)) quad\(60 %\). $
    #emph[Notice: the probability shifts significantly depending on
    whether the item is replaced or not!]
]

#divider()

==== Worked Example 4 --- The Magic Trick of “At Least One”
<worked-example-4-the-magic-trick-of-at-least-one>
A fair coin is tossed three times in succession. What is the probability
of getting #strong[at least one Head]?

#emph[Step-by-Step Solution:] “Getting at least one Head” means
obtaining 1 Head, 2 Heads, or 3 Heads. Calculating all these cases
individually would be tedious. Let us examine the #strong[complementary
event]: The opposite of “getting at least one Head” is #strong[“getting
NO Heads at all”], which means obtaining three consecutive Tails
$\(T\;T\;T\)$.

- The probability of Tails on any single toss is $1 / 2$.

- Since the three tosses are independent:
  $ P\(upright("No Heads")\)= P\(T\;T\;T\)= 1 / 2 times 1 / 2 times 1 / 2 = 1 / 8 . $

- By the complementary event formula:
  $ P\(upright("At least one Head")\)= 1 - P\(upright("No Heads")\)= 1 - 1 / 8 = upright(bold(7 / 8)) = upright(bold(0.875)) quad\(87.5 %\). $
  By passing through the complement, the calculation took a single line!

#divider()

==== Worked Example 5 --- Two-Color Die and the Fairground Game
<worked-example-5-two-color-die-and-the-fairground-game>
A carnival booth invites players to roll a special 6-sided die: 4 faces
are painted Green and 2 faces are painted Red. To play, the participant
pays \$2.

- If the die lands on Red, the player wins \$5 (a net profit of +\$3).
- If the die lands on Green, the player wins nothing (a net loss of
  \-\$2).

The booth owner claims: #emph[“Step right up and test your luck! You can
win more than double your stake!”] Is this game favorable to the player?

#emph[Step-by-Step Solution:] Let us calculate the probabilities:

- $P\(upright("Red")\)= 2 / 6 = 1 / 3 approx 33.3 %$.
- $P\(upright("Green")\)= 4 / 6 = 2 / 3 approx 66.7 %$.

Imagine playing 300 rounds:

- The die lands on Red approximately $300 times 1 / 3 = 100$ times,
  yielding $100 times\(+\$3\)= +\$300$.
- The die lands on Green approximately $300 times 2 / 3 = 200$ times,
  costing $200 times\(-\$2\)= -\$400$.

Overall balance for 300 rounds: $300 - 400 = -\$100$! On average per
round, the player loses approximately $frac(- 100, 300) approx -\$0.33$.
#strong[The game is strictly unfavorable to the player] (and reliably
profitable for the booth operator).

#divider()

=== What You Must Remember
<what-you-must-remember-7>
+ #strong[Vocabulary:]

  - #strong[Random experiment]: an outcome driven by chance, not
    predictable with certainty.

  - #strong[Outcomes]: individual elementary results.

  - #strong[Impossible event] ($P = 0$); #strong[Certain event]
    ($P = 1$).

  - #strong[Complementary event $macron(A)$]:
    $P\(macron(A)\)= 1 - P\(A\)$. The magic shortcut for “at least one”:
    $P\(upright("at least one")\)= 1 - P\(upright("none")\)$.

+ #strong[Equiprobability:]
  $ P\(E\)= upright("Number of favorable outcomes") / upright("Total number of possible outcomes") $
  Always verify: $0 lt.eq P\(E\)lt.eq 1$. The sum of probabilities over
  all elementary outcomes equals $1$.

+ #strong[Two-Stage Experiments:]

  - #strong[Two-way table]: ideal for 2 dice, 2 spinners (total cells =
    product of outcomes).

  - #strong[Probability tree diagram]:

    - Sum of branches issuing from a single node = 1.

    - #strong[Multiply] probabilities along a path.

    - #strong[Add] probabilities of disjoint paths that achieve the
      event.

  - #strong[With replacement]: probabilities remain constant.

  - #strong[Without replacement]: sample size decreases by 1 on each
    draw.

+ #strong[Law of Large Numbers:]

  - The observed #strong[relative frequency] over a large number of
    repetitions converges and stabilizes around the #strong[theoretical
    probability].

