== CHAPTER 31: The Court of Logic
<chapter-31-the-court-of-logic>
#quote_box[
#emph[“In the courtroom of mathematics, no eloquence can save the
accused. Verdicts are not reached on the sincere expression of the
witness, nor on the number of those who believe him: judgement is passed
strictly on proof. A single counterexample suffices to condemn a
statement, but a thousand examples will never suffice to clear it.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-30>
- The great #strong[number families] ($bb(N)$, $bb(Z)$, $bb(D)$,
  $bb(Q)$, $bb(R)$), nested like Russian dolls, and why each of them had
  to be invented.
- The language of #strong[sets] and #strong[intervals]: $in$, $subset$,
  $union$, $inter$, $\[a thin\;b\[$, $+ oo$… and how to read them aloud.
- The #strong[absolute value], which measures distance without caring
  about direction.
- The rules of the #strong[court]: implication, converse,
  contrapositive, equivalence, and the quantifiers “for all” and “there
  exists.”
- The four great #strong[weapons of proof]: direct proof,
  counterexample, contraposition, and proof by contradiction---with the
  core demonstrations required in Grade 10 ($1 / 3$ is not decimal,
  $sqrt(2)$ is irrational, the square of an odd integer is odd…).

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Formula That Lied Forty Times in a Row
<the-problem-the-formula-that-lied-forty-times-in-a-row>
Imagine a courtroom. In the dock sits not a man, but a
#strong[sentence]. It has been brought forward by an enthusiastic
student, who defends it with fiery passion:

#quote_box[
“For every natural number $n$, the number $n^2 + n + 41$ is prime.”
]


The defense attorney calls witnesses to the stand, one by one.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$n$], [$n^2 + n + 41$], [Prime?],),
    table.hline(),
    [0], [41], [yes],
    [1], [43], [yes],
    [2], [47], [yes],
    [3], [53], [yes],
    [4], [61], [yes],
    [5], [71], [yes],
    [10], [151], [yes],
    [20], [461], [yes],
    [30], [971], [yes],
    [39], [1601], [yes],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Forty witnesses ($n = 0\,1\,2\,dots.h\,39$) step up to the stand, and
#strong[every single one] confirms: the resulting number is prime. The
courtroom audience is convinced. The jury prepares to acquit.

Then the prosecutor stands up, completely calm, and calls a single
witness: $n = 40$.

$ 40^2 + 40 + 41 = 1600 + 40 + 41 = 1681 = 41 times 41 . $

$1681$ is #strong[not] prime. The statement is #strong[false]. The gavel
strikes; the verdict is final and unappealable.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 This polynomial was observed by
#strong[Leonhard Euler] in 1772. It produces prime numbers for $n$ from
$0$ up to $39$---an astounding streak---but it fails at $n = 40$. One
can even see this without calculating: for $n = 41$,
$41^2 + 41 + 41 = 41 times 43$, which is obviously divisible by $41$.
]

This story encapsulates the entire spirit of this chapter:

+ #strong[Checking cases, even dozens of them, proves nothing] for a
  statement that speaks of #emph[all] integers.
+ #strong[A single counterexample is enough] to shatter a universal
  assertion.
+ To #emph[establish] a general truth, an entirely different tool is
  required: #strong[proof].

In middle school, you often “saw” that a property worked on examples.
Starting in tenth grade (Seconde), you step into the court of law. You
need precise language (which numbers are we talking about? what does
“if… then” mean?) and reliable methods of proof. This is what we will
construct together.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 The True Story ---
]
 Around 350 BCE, #strong[Aristotle] laid
the foundations of logic by studying the #emph[syllogism]: “All men are
mortal; Socrates is a man; therefore Socrates is mortal.” More than two
thousand years later, in 1847 and 1854, the Englishman #strong[George
Boole], a cobbler's son and largely self-taught, transformed logic into
an #emph[algebra]: truth became $1$, falsehood $0$, and “and” became
multiplication. Without knowing it, he had just penned the grammar for
all twentieth-century computers.
]

#divider()

=== 2. The Number Families: A History of Crises
<the-number-families-a-history-of-crises>
Every family of numbers was born out of an #strong[impossible
operation]. When existing tools no longer sufficed, humanity had to
expand the universe of numbers.

==== 2.1 The Natural Numbers $bb(N)$: Counting Pebbles
<the-natural-numbers-mathbbn-counting-pebbles>
The very first numbers are those of the shepherd counting his sheep:
$0\,1\,2\,3\,dots.h$

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 The set of #strong[natural numbers] is
$ bb(N) = { 0 thin\;1 thin\;2 thin\;3 thin\;dots.h } . $
]

#strong[Notation Explained.] - The #strong[curly braces] ${ med }$ are
used to write a set by listing its elements. We read this as “the set of
elements $0\,1\,2 dots.h$”. In French and continental European
tradition, elements are often separated by semicolons to avoid confusion
with the decimal comma. - The letter $bb(N)$ comes from #emph[naturale]
(Italian): it was introduced by #strong[Giuseppe Peano] in 1889. The
“double-struck” style (known as #emph[blackboard bold]) was born at the
blackboard: chalk cannot easily produce boldface type, so mathematicians
doubled a stroke. It became standard in printed textbooks during the
mid-twentieth century. - We simply read “$bb(N)$” as “the set of natural
numbers” or “N”.

In $bb(N)$, we can always add and multiply. But #strong[subtraction]
causes trouble: $3 - 5$ has no answer in $bb(N)$.

==== 2.2 The Integers $bb(Z)$: The Merchant's Debts
<the-integers-mathbbz-the-merchants-debts>
Recall the merchant's slate (Chapter 14): to record a debt, negative
numbers become indispensable.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 The set of #strong[integers] (or relative
integers) is
$ bb(Z) = { dots.h thin\;- 3 thin\;- 2 thin\;- 1 thin\;0 thin\;1 thin\;2 thin\;3 thin\;dots.h } . $
]

The letter $bb(Z)$ comes from the German #strong[#emph[Zahlen]]
(“numbers”), widely adopted by Richard Dedekind and German
mathematicians in the nineteenth century. In $bb(Z)$, subtraction is
always possible. But #strong[division] is not: $7 div 2$ is not an
integer.

==== 2.3 The Decimals $bb(D)$: Stevin's Tenths
<the-decimals-mathbbd-stevins-tenths>
A merchant weighing spices needs $2.35 upright(" kg")$. Simon Stevin
(Chapter 11) popularized decimal numbers.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 A number is #strong[decimal] if it can be
written in the form
$ a / 10^n quad upright("with ") a in bb(Z) upright(" and ") n in bb(N) . $
The set of decimal numbers is denoted $bb(D)$.
]

In other words: a decimal number is one that can be written with a
#strong[finite] number of digits after the decimal point. Examples:
$2.35 = 235 / 10^2$\; $- 0.007 = frac(- 7, 10^3)$\; $12 = 12 / 10^0$
(every integer is a decimal number).

The symbol $in$ is read “#strong[belongs to]” or “#strong[is an element
of]”. “$a in bb(Z)$” means “$a$ is an element of $bb(Z)$”, that is, “$a$
is an integer.” This symbol is a stylized version of the Greek letter
epsilon $epsilon$, the initial of ἐστί (#emph[esti], “is”): introduced
once again by #strong[Peano] in 1889. Its negation, $in.not$, is read
“does not belong to.”

Everything seems in order… until the day we divide a loaf of bread into
#strong[three].

==== 2.4 The Rational Numbers $bb(Q)$: Dividing into Three
<the-rational-numbers-mathbbq-dividing-into-three>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 A number is #strong[rational] if it can be
written in the form
$ p / q quad upright("with ") p in bb(Z) upright(" and ") q in bb(N)\,med q eq.not 0 . $
The set of rational numbers is denoted $bb(Q)$ (from the initial of
#emph[quotient], proposed by Peano in 1895).
]

The word “rational” comes from the Latin #emph[ratio], meaning “ratio”
or “proportion”: a rational number is a #strong[ratio] of two integers.
It has nothing to do with being “sensible” or “reasonable”… even though
mathematical history often plays on this double meaning.

The natural question arises: $1 / 3$ is rational, but is it decimal?
Your calculator displays $0.333333333$, which #emph[looks] like a
decimal. But its screen is too small to tell the whole truth. Here is
the first trial of the chapter.

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Core Curriculum) --- $1 / 3$ is not a decimal number.
]


Let us reason #strong[by contradiction] (#emph[reductio ad absurdum]):
suppose that $1 / 3$ were a decimal number. There would then exist an
integer $a$ and a natural number $n$ such that $ 1 / 3 = a / 10^n . $
Multiplying both sides by $3 times 10^n$, we obtain: $ 10^n = 3 a . $
Thus $10^n$ would be a multiple of $3$. However, the sum of the digits
of $10^n$ (a single “1” followed by $n$ zeros) is $1$, which is not
divisible by $3$\; by the standard divisibility rule for $3$, $10^n$ is
#strong[not] a multiple of $3$.

\(Alternative argument: $10^n = 2^n times 5^n$\; its prime factorization
contains only $2$s and $5$s, and never any factor $3$\.)

We have reached a contradiction. The initial assumption must therefore
be false: $1 / 3 in.not bb(D)$. $square.filled.medium$
]

The small filled black square $square.filled.medium$ (or Halmos symbol)
marks the end of a proof. The Ancients used to write “#strong[QED]”:
#emph[quod erat demonstrandum] (“which was to be demonstrated”).

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 An irreducible fraction $p / q$ is decimal if
and only if its denominator $q$ contains only the prime factors $2$ and
$5$. Thus $7 / 40 = frac(7, 2^3 times 5) = 0.175$ is decimal, but
$1 / 6 = frac(1, 2 times 3)$ is not. Every rational number has a decimal
representation that is either #strong[terminating] (finite) or
#strong[eventually periodic]: for example,
$1 / 7 = 0 . overline(142857)$ (where the overline indicates the
repeating period).
]

==== 2.5 The Real Numbers $bb(R)$: The Diagonal That Broke the Brotherhood
<the-real-numbers-mathbbr-the-diagonal-that-broke-the-brotherhood>
You have already encountered this drama (Chapter 19, #emph[The Forbidden
Secret of the Pythagoreans]). The diagonal of a square with unit side
length measures $sqrt(2)$, according to the Pythagorean theorem:
$d^2 = 1^2 + 1^2 = 2$. The Pythagoreans believed that “all is number,”
meaning every length in the cosmos is a ratio of integers. The diagonal
betrayed them. We will now write out the proof with full high-school
rigor. First, we need a small lemma (a #emph[lemma] is an auxiliary
stepping stone towards a main theorem).

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Core Curriculum) --- The square of an odd integer is
odd.
]


Let $n$ be an odd integer. By definition, there exists an integer $k$
such that $n = 2 k + 1$. Then:
$ n^2 =\(2 k + 1\)^2 = 4 k^2 + 4 k + 1 = 2\(2 k^2 + 2 k\)+ 1 . $ Since
$2 k^2 + 2 k$ is an integer (let us call it $k'$), we have
$n^2 = 2 k' + 1$: therefore $n^2$ is odd. $square.filled.medium$
]

Similarly, the square of an even integer $n = 2 k$ is
$n^2 = 4 k^2 = 2\(2 k^2\)$, which is even.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Consequence (by Contraposition) ---
]
 If $n^2$ is even, then
$n$ is even.

Indeed, if $n$ were not even, it would be odd, and its square would be
odd by the lemma above. We will revisit this mode of reasoning
(contraposition) in detail in Section 6.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Core Curriculum) --- $sqrt(2)$ is irrational.
]


Let us reason by contradiction. Suppose that $sqrt(2)$ were rational.
Then it could be written as an #strong[irreducible fraction]:
$ sqrt(2) = p / q\,quad p in bb(N)\,med q in bb(N)^(*)\,med upright("where ") p upright(" and ") q upright(" share no common factor other than 1.") $
Squaring both sides: $2 = p^2 / q^2$, hence: $ p^2 = 2 q^2 . $ 1.
Therefore $p^2$ is even. By the consequence above, $p$ is even: there
exists an integer $k$ such that $p = 2 k$. 2. Substituting this back:
$\(2 k\)^2 = 2 q^2$, which gives $4 k^2 = 2 q^2$, or $q^2 = 2 k^2$.
Hence $q^2$ is even, which implies that $q$ is also even. 3.
Consequently, $p$ and $q$ are both even: they share $2$ as a common
factor. This #strong[contradicts] the fact that the fraction $p / q$ is
in lowest terms (irreducible).

The assumption “$sqrt(2)$ is rational” leads to an absurdity: it must be
false. Therefore $sqrt(2) in.not bb(Q)$. $square.filled.medium$
]

The notation $bb(N)^(*)$ denotes $bb(N)$ excluding $0$. Likewise,
$bb(R)^(*)$ denotes the non-zero real numbers.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 The set of #strong[real numbers], denoted
$bb(R)$, is the set of all coordinates of points along a graduated
number line. A real number that is not rational is called
#strong[irrational].
]

Examples of irrationals: $sqrt(2)$, $sqrt(3)$, $pi$ (proved irrational
by Lambert in 1761), $sqrt(2) + 1$ (see Exercise 11).

==== 2.6 The Russian Dolls: Inclusion $subset$
<the-russian-dolls-inclusion-subset>
Every natural number is an integer; every integer is a decimal; every
decimal is rational ($a / 10^n$ is a quotient of integers); every
rational is real.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 A set $A$ is said to be #strong[included] in
(or a #strong[subset] of) a set $B$, written $A subset B$, if every
element of $A$ is also an element of $B$. We read this as “$A$ is
included in $B$” or “$A$ is a subset of $B$\.”
]

$ bb(N) subset bb(Z) subset bb(D) subset bb(Q) subset bb(R) $

#figure(image("figures/vol_5/fig_31_ensembles_emboites.svg", alt: "Les ensembles de nombres emboîtés N ⊂ Z ⊂ D ⊂ Q ⊂ R"),
  caption: [
    Les ensembles de nombres emboîtés N ⊂ Z ⊂ D ⊂ Q ⊂ R
  ]
)

Each inclusion is #strong[strict]: $- 1 in bb(Z)$ but
$- 1 in.not bb(N)$\; $0.5 in bb(D)$ but $0.5 in.not bb(Z)$\;
$1 / 3 in bb(Q)$ but $1 / 3 in.not bb(D)$\; $sqrt(2) in bb(R)$ but
$sqrt(2) in.not bb(Q)$.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning!
]
 Do not confuse $in$ and $subset$.

- $in$ connects an #strong[element] to a #strong[set]: $3 in bb(N)$.
- $subset$ connects #strong[two sets]: ${ 3 } subset bb(N)$,
  $bb(N) subset bb(Z)$.

Writing “$3 subset bb(N)$” or “$bb(N) in bb(Z)$” is a violation of
mathematical grammar.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning!
]
 A number can wear many disguises. $12 / 4$
looks like a fraction, but it simplifies to $3$, a natural number.
$sqrt(9) = 3 in bb(N)$, $sqrt(4 / 9) = 2 / 3 in bb(Q)$,
$21 / 75 = 7 / 25 = 0.28 in bb(D)$. Always #strong[simplify] before
passing judgement.
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Classifying Numbers.
]
 For each number,
determine the smallest set among $bb(N)\,bb(Z)\,bb(D)\,bb(Q)\,bb(R)$
that contains it:
$ a = - 18 / 6\,quad b = 3 / 8\,quad c = 5 / 12\,quad d = sqrt(16) - sqrt(2)\,quad e =\(sqrt(5)\)^2\,quad f = frac(pi, 2 pi) . $

- $a = - 3$: $a in bb(Z)$, and $a in.not bb(N)$. Smallest set: $bb(Z)$.

- $b = 3 / 8 = 3 / 2^3$: denominator contains only factors of 2, hence
  decimal: $b = 375 / 1000 = 0.375$. Smallest set: $bb(D)$.

- $c = 5 / 12 = frac(5, 2^2 times 3)$, irreducible with a factor of 3 in
  the denominator: $c in bb(Q)$, $c in.not bb(D)$. (Verification:
  $c = 0.41666 dots.h$)

- $d = 4 - sqrt(2)$. If $d$ were rational, then $sqrt(2) = 4 - d$ would
  be rational (the difference of two rationals), which is false. Thus
  $d in bb(R)$, irrational.

- $e = 5$: $bb(N)$.

- $f = 1 / 2 = 0.5$: $bb(D)$. The $pi$ cancels out: do not let outward
  appearances mislead you!
]

