=== 3. The Language of Sets and Intervals
<the-language-of-sets-and-intervals>
==== 3.1 Describing a Set
<describing-a-set>
There are two primary ways to describe a set:

- #strong[By extension (roster method)]: listing all its elements,
  $A = { 1 thin\;3 thin\;5 thin\;7 }$\;
- #strong[By comprehension (set-builder notation)]: stating a
  characteristic property,
  $A = { x in bb(N) divides x upright(" is odd and ") x < 8 }$.

The vertical bar $divides$ (or colon “:”) is read “#strong[such that]”.
The statement is read: “the set of all $x$ belonging to $bb(N)$ such
that $x$ is odd and strictly less than $8$\.”

The #strong[empty set], which contains no elements at all, is denoted
$diameter$. This symbol was introduced by #strong[André Weil] (of the
Bourbaki group) around 1939, inspired by the Scandinavian letter Ø.
Warning: $diameter$ is not the same as ${ 0 }$! The set ${ 0 }$ contains
one element, namely zero; $diameter$ contains nothing. An empty box is
not a box containing a zero.

==== 3.2 Union, Intersection, and Complement
<union-intersection-and-complement>
Let $A$ and $B$ be two subsets of a universal set $E$.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definitions ---
]


- The #strong[intersection] $A inter B$ (read “$A$ intersect $B$”) is
  the set of elements belonging to both $A$ #strong[and] $B$.

- The #strong[union] $A union B$ (read “$A$ union $B$”) is the set of
  elements belonging to $A$ #strong[or] $B$ (or both).

- The #strong[complement] of $A$ in $E$, denoted $overline(A)$ or
  $E\\A$, is the set of elements in $E$ that #strong[do not belong] to
  $A$. The symbol $\\$ is read “minus” or “excluding.”

- $A$ and $B$ are #strong[disjoint] if $A inter B = diameter$.
]

A mnemonic trick: $inter$ resembles a narrow tunnel or arch through
which only those holding both tickets may pass; $union$ resembles a wide
cup or bowl, a #strong[U] for #strong[U]nion, gathering everything
together.

