    ```
  ]
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 2 --- Operations on Finite Sets.
]
 Let
$E = { 1 thin\;2 thin\;dots.h thin\;12 }$, $A$ = the set of multiples of
2 in $E$, and $B$ = the set of multiples of 3 in $E$.

- $A = { 2 thin\;4 thin\;6 thin\;8 thin\;10 thin\;12 }$,
  $B = { 3 thin\;6 thin\;9 thin\;12 }$.

- $A inter B = { 6 thin\;12 }$: these are the multiples of $6$.

- $A union B = { 2 thin\;3 thin\;4 thin\;6 thin\;8 thin\;9 thin\;10 thin\;12 }$
  (6 and 12 are not duplicated).

- $overline(A union B) = { 1 thin\;5 thin\;7 thin\;11 }$: numbers in $E$
  divisible by neither 2 nor 3.

- Checking cardinalities:
  $upright("card")\(A union B\)= upright("card")\(A\)+ upright("card")\(B\)- upright("card")\(A inter B\)= 6 + 4 - 2 = 8$.
  (The #emph[cardinality], written $upright("card")$, denotes the number
  of elements.)
]

==== 3.3 Intervals of $bb(R)$
<intervals-of-mathbbr>
On the real number line, the most useful subsets are #strong[unbroken
line segments without holes]: intervals.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 Let $a$ and $b$ be two real numbers with
$a < b$.

