We also write $bb(R) =\]- oo thin\;+ oo\[=\(- oo\,+ oo\)$. The real
numbers $a$ and $b$ are the #strong[endpoints] (or bounds) of the
interval; $b - a$ is its #strong[length] (or width).
]

#strong[Why these brackets?] The French and ISO convention is visual: an
#strong[inward-facing bracket] $\[$ or $\]$ “captures” the endpoint (it
is included); an #strong[outward-facing bracket] $\]$ or $\[$ “pushes it
away” (it is excluded). In many English-speaking traditions, parentheses
$\(a\,b\)$ are used for open bounds. A #strong[semicolon] is used
between endpoints to avoid confusion with decimal commas or periods:
$\[1.5 thin\;2\]$.

#strong[The symbol $oo$] (“infinity”) was introduced by the Englishman
#strong[John Wallis] in 1655. It is #strong[not a number]: it is a way
to express “without bound in this direction.” This is why brackets are
#strong[always open] adjacent to $+ oo$ or $- oo$: one can never “reach”
infinity, and so it can never be included.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning!
]


- $\[3 thin\;+ oo\]$ is #strong[incorrect]: the bracket must be open at
  $+ oo$.

- $\[5 thin\;2\]$ makes no sense: the smaller endpoint must always be
  written on the left.

- $\]2 thin\;2\[= diameter$, but $\[2 thin\;2\]= { 2 }$ (a single
  point).

- The interval $\[1 thin\;3\]$ contains an #strong[infinite] number of
  real values ($1.5$, $sqrt(2)$, $2.999 dots.h$), not just the integers
  $1\,2\,3$! Do not confuse it with the discrete set of integers
  ${ 1 thin\;2 thin\;3 }$.
]

#strong[Useful Notations:] $bb(R)_(+) =\[0 thin\;+ oo\[$ (non-negative
real numbers), $bb(R)_(-) =\]- oo thin\;0\]$ (non-positive reals),
$bb(R)_(+)^(*) =\]0 thin\;+ oo\[$ (strictly positive reals),
$bb(R)^(*) = bb(R)\\{ 0 } =\]- oo thin\;0\[union\]0 thin\;+ oo\[$.

