== CHAPTER 32: The Sailboat in the Storm and the Free Arrow
<chapter-32-the-sailboat-in-the-storm-and-the-free-arrow>
#quote_box[
#emph[“A point is an anchor holding a ship to the seabed. A Cartesian
coordinate states where you are. But the sailor confronting the swell
has little use for an anchor: he needs to know where the wind is driving
him, at what speed, and how two competing currents merge into a single
momentum. To liberate motion from the earth, humanity had to invent the
flying arrow: the Vector.”
]

]

#divider()

=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-31>
- Why the Cartesian coordinate plane was no longer enough for
  physicists, and how #strong[Hermann Grassmann] freed displacement from
  its fixed tether in 1844.
- The fundamental triad defining a #strong[vector]: direction, sense
  (orientation), and magnitude (norm)---along with the arrow notation
  $arrow(u)$ or $accent(A B, ⃗)$.
- #strong[Chasles' relation]
  ($accent(A B, ⃗) + accent(B C, ⃗) = accent(A C, ⃗)$) understood as a
  relay of navigators or dispatch couriers, and the parallelogram rule.
- Multiplying a vector by a real number (scalar) and the concept of
  #strong[collinear vectors].
- How to translate geometry into algebraic calculations within a
  coordinate frame $\(O thin\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\)$: vector coordinates,
  midpoint formula, and distance formula.
- The decisive arbiter of alignment and parallelism: the
  #strong[determinant] $x y' - y x' = 0$.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Rowboat in the River Current
<the-problem-the-rowboat-in-the-river-current>
You stand at the oars of a fishing boat on the waters of a river five
hundred meters wide. On the opposite bank, straight ahead of you to the
North, stands the dock where your companions await you.

You grip both oars. You haul against the timber with all your muscle:
your bow points straight #strong[North], and your effort propels you
through the water at a steady speed of #strong[$3 upright(" km/h")$].
You mentally calculate that in ten minutes, you will tie up at the
pontoon.

Yet the river is no tranquil pond. A strong current rushes from West to
#strong[East] at a speed of #strong[$4 upright(" km/h")$].

Look at the scene from the riverbank:

#figure(image("figures/vol_5/fig_32_chasles_addition.svg", alt: "Vecteurs dans le plan : relation de Chasles et coordonnées"),
  caption: [
    Vecteurs dans le plan : relation de Chasles et coordonnées
  ]
)

What forces act upon your vessel? 1. Your arms drive you North: in one
hour, without current, you would travel $3 upright(" km")$ North. 2. The
water beneath your hull sweeps you East: in one hour, without rowing,
you would drift $4 upright(" km")$ East.

Both actions take place #strong[simultaneously]. At the end of an
imaginary hour in which both movements combine, you would be neither
purely North nor purely East, but at point $C$. What distance have you
covered relative to the riverbed?

The triangle formed by the eastward displacement ($4 upright(" km")$),
the northward displacement ($3 upright(" km")$), and the actual
trajectory is a right-angled triangle. The Pythagorean theorem gives the
distance traveled:
$ d = sqrt(4^2 + 3^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 upright(" km") . $

Your actual speed relative to the banks is
#strong[$5 upright(" km/h")$], directed diagonally toward the Northeast!

This scenario reveals a fundamental limitation of ordinary numbers:

- A single number ($5 upright(" km/h")$) states #strong[how much], but
  it does not state #strong[where to].
- The coordinate of a point $A\(x_A\,y_A\)$ states #strong[where one
  is], but it is nailed to the grid drawn on the map. If the boat rows
  in the exact same manner a hundred kilometers downstream, the physical
  phenomenon is identical, even though the starting and ending
  coordinates are entirely different.

A new mathematical object was needed: an #strong[autonomous arrow],
transportable anywhere without distortion, encoding tilt, orientation,
and magnitude all at once.

#divider()

=== 2. Liberating the Arrow: The Birth of the Vector
<liberating-the-arrow-the-birth-of-the-vector>
==== 2.1 Grassmann's Breakthrough (1844)
<grassmanns-breakthrough-1844>
For two centuries following René Descartes (1637), analytic geometry
operated by chaining every point to a fixed origin $O\(0\,0\)$. To study
the flight of a bird or the blast of a squall, one had to manipulate the
coordinates of the starting point $A$, then those of the destination
$B$, and compute differences.

In 1844, a high school teacher in Germany unknown to his contemporaries,
#strong[Hermann Günther Grassmann], published a visionary treatise:
#emph[Die Lineale Ausdehnungslehre] (The Theory of Linear Extension).
Grassmann introduced a revolutionary insight: \> “The point is a prison.
What matters in physics is not the place where the action begins, but
the action itself: pure transport.”

This geometric entity was given the name #strong[vector], from the Latin
#emph[vehere] (“to carry”, “to convey”---the root of #emph[vehicle]).

==== 2.2 The Three Defining Characteristics of a Vector
<the-three-defining-characteristics-of-a-vector>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Vector.
]
 A non-zero vector $arrow(u)$ is
defined by three inseparable pieces of data: 1. #strong[Its direction:]
the line along which it lies (or any line parallel to it). This
indicates the line of action of the movement (e.g., the North-South
line, the horizontal axis, a $30^compose$ incline). 2. #strong[Its sense
(or orientation):] the heading along that line, indicated by the
arrowhead (e.g., Northward rather than Southward, left to right). 3.
#strong[Its magnitude (or norm):] the length of the directed line
segment, denoted $parallel arrow(u) parallel$. It represents physical
intensity (a speed in $upright("km/h")$, a force in newtons, a
displacement in meters).
]

#strong[Notation Explained.] - We place a rightward arrow above the
letter: $arrow(u)$, $arrow(v)$, $arrow(w)$. We read this aloud as:
“vector u”, “vector v”. - When the vector represents displacement
explicitly from a point $A$ to a point $B$, we write $accent(A B, ⃗)$,
read “vector AB”. Here, $A$ is the #strong[initial point] (or
origin/tail) of the representative, and $B$ is its #strong[terminal
point] (or tip/head). - The #strong[norm] is written with double
vertical bars: $parallel arrow(u) parallel$ or
$parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel$. We read “norm of u” or “magnitude of
AB”. We always have $parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel = A B$ (the
standard geometric length). Double bars are used to avoid confusion with
the absolute value $\|x\|$ of a real number.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning! Never confuse direction and sense!
]
 In
everyday English, people often say “walk in the direction of Paris.” In
mathematics, this is imprecise:

- The #strong[direction] is the railway line connecting Paris to Lyon.
- The #strong[sense] (or heading) is traveling from Paris toward Lyon,
  or from Lyon toward Paris. Two cars driving past each other head-on
  along the same straight highway share the #strong[same direction], but
  have #strong[opposite senses].
]

==== 2.3 The Zero Vector and Representatives
<the-zero-vector-and-representatives>
What happens if a traveler does not move a single millimeter? His
starting point $A$ and destination $A$ coincide. The corresponding
displacement is the #strong[zero vector], denoted $arrow(0)$ (a zero
topped by an arrow): $ accent(A A, ⃗) = accent(B B, ⃗) = arrow(0) . $ The
zero vector has a magnitude equal to zero:
$parallel arrow(0) parallel = 0$. It possesses neither a distinct
direction nor a specific sense (it is said to have every possible
direction).

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? A vector is an infinity of arrows at once!
]
 A
vector $arrow(u)$ is not “glued” to one specific spot on paper. It is an
equivalence class of displacements. If ten birds fly in tight formation,
all heading South at $40 upright(" km/h")$, each bird traces its own
arrow across the sky. All these arrows have the same direction, the same
sense, and the same length. They are called #strong[representatives] (or
bound arrows) of one and the same free vector $arrow(u)$.
]

