=== 5. Collinearity and Determinants: The Art of Aiming True
<collinearity-and-determinants-the-art-of-aiming-true>
==== 5.1 Definition of Collinearity
<definition-of-collinearity>
Two arrows may have different lengths and opposite orientations: if they
lie on parallel lines, they have the #strong[same direction]. In
mathematics, they are termed #strong[collinear] (from the Latin
#emph[cum], together, and #emph[linea], line: “on the same line”).

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Collinear Vectors.
]
 Two vectors $arrow(u)$ and
$arrow(v)$ are said to be #strong[collinear] if one is the scalar
multiple of the other:
$ upright("there exists ") k in bb(R) upright(" such that ") arrow(u) = k arrow(v) quad upright("or") quad arrow(v) = k arrow(u) . $
By convention, the zero vector $arrow(0)$ is collinear to #strong[all]
vectors in the plane (since $arrow(0) = 0 times arrow(u)$).
]

==== 5.2 The Determinant of Two Vectors
<the-determinant-of-two-vectors>
How can one instantly test whether two vectors $arrow(u) vec(x, y)$ and
$arrow(v) vec(x', y')$ are collinear, without guessing $k$?

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition and Theorem --- Determinant in the Plane.
]
 The
#strong[determinant] of vectors $arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$ in an
orthonormal basis is the real number denoted
$det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(v)\)$, calculated by the cross-product rule:
$ det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(v)\)= mat(delim: "|", x, x'; y, y') = x y' - y x' . $
#strong[Fundamental Collinearity Criterion:]
$ arrow(u) upright(" and ") arrow(v) upright(" are collinear") arrow.l.r.double det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(v)\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double x y' - y x' = 0 . $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof --- Collinearity Condition.
]


- #strong[Forward direction ($arrow.r.double.long$):] Assume $arrow(u)$
  and $arrow(v)$ are collinear. If $arrow(v) = arrow(0)$, then
  $x' = y' = 0$, so $x y' - y x' = x\(0\)- y\(0\)= 0$. If
  $arrow(v) eq.not arrow(0)$, there exists $k in bb(R)$ such that
  $arrow(u) = k arrow(v)$, which means $x = k x'$ and $y = k y'$.
  Calculating the cross difference:
  $ x y' - y x' =\(k x'\)y' -\(k y'\)x' = k x' y' - k y' x' = 0 . $

- #strong[Reverse direction ($arrow.l.double.long$):] Assume
  $x y' - y x' = 0$. If $arrow(v) = arrow(0)$, then $arrow(u)$ and
  $arrow(v)$ are collinear by definition. Assume
  $arrow(v) eq.not arrow(0)$. At least one of its components is
  non-zero. Suppose, without loss of generality, that $x' eq.not 0$. Let
  $k = x / x'$, so that $x = k x'$. The identity $x y' - y x' = 0$ then
  becomes:
  $ \(k x'\)y' - y x' = 0 arrow.l.r.double x'\(k y' - y\)= 0 . $ Since
  $x' eq.not 0$, it follows that $k y' - y = 0$, hence $y = k y'$. We
  therefore have both $x = k x'$ and $y = k y'$, which is concisely
  written $arrow(u) = k arrow(v)$. The vectors are collinear.
  $square.filled.medium$
]

#quote_box[
#strong[💡 Geometric Interpretation: Area of the Parallelogram.
]
 The
absolute value of the determinant $\|det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(v)\)\|$ is
exactly equal to the #strong[area of the parallelogram] spanned by the
two vectors $arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$! If the vectors are collinear, the
parallelogram collapses flat: its area is zero, which yields
$det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(v)\)= 0$.
]

==== 5.3 Two Major Geometric Applications
<two-major-geometric-applications>
Thanks to the determinant, geometric problems of alignment and
parallelism transform into direct algebraic tests:

+ #strong[Parallelism of lines:] Two lines $\(A B\)$ and $\(C D\)$ are
  parallel if and only if their direction vectors $accent(A B, ⃗)$ and
  $accent(C D, ⃗)$ are collinear:
  $ \(A B\)parallel\(C D\)arrow.l.r.double det\(accent(A B, ⃗)\,accent(C D, ⃗)\)= 0 . $

+ #strong[Collinearity of three points:] Three distinct points $A\,B\,C$
  are collinear if and only if the vectors $accent(A B, ⃗)$ and
  $accent(A C, ⃗)$ share the same direction:
  $ A\,B\,C upright(" are collinear") arrow.l.r.double accent(A B, ⃗) upright(" and ") accent(A C, ⃗) upright(" are collinear") arrow.l.r.double det\(accent(A B, ⃗)\,accent(A C, ⃗)\)= 0 . $

