=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-32>
- How a line is born from an anchor point and a #strong[direction
  vector] that sets its heading.
- The general #strong[Cartesian equation] $a x + b y + c = 0$, and why
  the vector $arrow(u) vec(- b, a)$ serves as its compass needle.
- The #strong[slope-intercept form] (or reduced equation) $y = m x + p$:
  the physical meaning of the #strong[slope] $m$ (gradient) and the
  #strong[$y$-intercept] $p$.
- The special case of vertical lines with equation $x = k$.
- The three possible fates of two meeting lines: intersecting at a
  unique point, strictly parallel, or coincident.
- Solving #strong[systems of two linear equations in two variables]
  algebraically and graphically via substitution and linear combination
  (elimination).

