=== 1. The Problem: The Watchtower and the Ship in the Fog
<the-problem-the-watchtower-and-the-ship-in-the-fog>
A solitary watchtower stands atop a craggy cliff overlooking the ocean.
Night has fallen, thick fog shrouds the bay, and two ships navigate
blindly.

The lighthouse keeper monitors a Cartesian radar screen:

- The merchant vessel #emph[L'Espérance] is presently located at
  coordinates $A\(2 thin\;3\)$ and maintains a steady heading guided by
  velocity vector $arrow(u) vec(2, 1)$.
- A rescue frigate #emph[La Vigilante] is reported at point
  $B\(- 1 thin\;8\)$, steaming in a straight path along vector
  $arrow(v) vec(1, - 1)$.

#figure(image("figures/vol_5/fig_33_pente_ordonnee.svg", alt: "Droite affine y = mx + p : coefficient directeur et ordonnée à l’origine"),
  caption: [
    Droite affine y = mx + p : coefficient directeur et ordonnée à
    l'origine
  ]
)

The port commander poses two urgent questions: 1. #emph[Do these two
vessels risk collision---meaning, do their paths cross at a specific
point on the water?] 2. #emph[If so, what are the exact coordinates of
the intersection point?]

To answer, drawing on graph paper is unreliable: a blunt pencil lead or
a slightly warped scale could be off by hundreds of meters. We need an
infallible algebraic method capable of translating an infinite line into
an equation solvable with absolute precision.

