=== 2. Heading and Path: The Direction Vector
<heading-and-path-the-direction-vector>
==== 2.1 Setting the Direction of a Line
<setting-the-direction-of-a-line>
A line in the plane is entirely determined by:

- either #strong[two distinct points] $A$ and $B$ (through two points
  passes a unique straight line);
- or #strong[one anchor point] $A$ and #strong[one non-zero vector]
  prescribing its direction.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Direction Vector.
]
 Let $cal(D)$ be a line in
the plane. Any non-zero vector $arrow(u)$ having the same direction as
line $cal(D)$ is called a #strong[direction vector] of $cal(D)$. If $A$
and $B$ are two distinct points on $cal(D)$, then the vector
$accent(A B, ⃗)$ is a direction vector of $cal(D)$.
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
u (direction vector)
o A
    ```
  ]
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? A line possesses infinitely many direction
vectors!
]
 If $arrow(u)$ is a direction vector of line $cal(D)$, then for
any non-zero real number $k$, the vector $k arrow(u)$ shares the same
direction: it is therefore also a direction vector of $cal(D)$. Twice as
long, one third the length, or pointing in reverse, it still lies along
the very same line.
]

==== 2.2 Vector Characterization of a Line
<vector-characterization-of-a-line>
A point $M\(x thin\;y\)$ belongs to the line $cal(D)$ passing through
$A\(x_A thin\;y_A\)$ with direction vector $arrow(u)$ if and only if the
displacement from $A$ to $M$ follows this exact same heading:
$ M in cal(D) arrow.l.r.double accent(A M, ⃗) upright(" and ") arrow(u) upright(" are collinear") . $

From the preceding chapter, two vectors are collinear if and only if
their determinant vanishes:
$ M in cal(D) arrow.l.r.double det\(accent(A M, ⃗)\,arrow(u)\)= 0 . $

This single, elegant condition generates all linear equations!

#divider()

=== 3. The Cartesian Equation of a Line
<the-cartesian-equation-of-a-line>
==== 3.1 The General Form $a x + b y + c = 0$
<the-general-form-ax-by-c-0>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem --- Cartesian Equation.
]
 1. Every
straight line $cal(D)$ in the plane admits an equation of the form:
$ a x + b y + c = 0 $ where $a\,b\,c$ are real numbers with
$\(a\,b\)eq.not\(0\,0\)$ (that is, $a$ and $b$ are not both zero). 2.
Conversely, every equation of the form $a x + b y + c = 0$ with
$\(a\,b\)eq.not\(0\,0\)$ represents a straight line. 3. The vector
$arrow(u) vec(- b, a)$ is a #strong[direction vector] of this line.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Core Curriculum) --- From Collinearity to the
Cartesian Equation.
]
 Let $cal(D)$ be the line passing through
$A\(x_A thin\;y_A\)$ with non-zero direction vector
$arrow(u) vec(u_1, u_2)$. Let $M\(x thin\;y\)$ be an arbitrary point in
the plane. Vector $accent(A M, ⃗)$ has coordinates:
$ accent(A M, ⃗) vec(x - x_A, y - y_A) . $ Point $M$ belongs to $cal(D)$
if and only if $accent(A M, ⃗)$ and $arrow(u)$ are collinear:
$ det\(accent(A M, ⃗)\,arrow(u)\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double\(x - x_A\)u_2 -\(y - y_A\)u_1 = 0 . $
Expanding: $ u_2 x - u_1 y +\(- u_2 x_A + u_1 y_A\)= 0 . $ Let us set:
$ a = u_2\,quad b = - u_1\,quad c = - u_2 x_A + u_1 y_A . $ Since
$arrow(u) eq.not arrow(0)$, the pair $\(u_1\,u_2\)eq.not\(0\,0\)$, hence
$\(a\,b\)eq.not\(0\,0\)$. The identity becomes: $ a x + b y + c = 0 . $
Furthermore, vector $arrow(u)$ has coordinates
$vec(u_1, u_2) = vec(- b, a)$. This is indeed a direction vector of the
line. $square.filled.medium$
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Warning! Cartesian equations are not unique.
]
 If
$2 x - 3 y + 5 = 0$ is the equation of a line, then multiplying each
term by $2$ gives $4 x - 6 y + 10 = 0$, which describes #strong[the
exact same line]! Two equations $a x + b y + c = 0$ and
$a' x + b' y + c' = 0$ represent the same line if and only if their
coefficients are proportional: there exists a non-zero real scalar $k$
such that $a' = k a$, $b' = k b$, and $c' = k c$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Slope-Intercept Form: The Gradient and the $y$-Intercept
<slope-intercept-form-the-gradient-and-the-y-intercept>
While the general form $a x + b y + c = 0$ is universally applicable,
for graphing and physical intuition mathematicians often prefer
expressing $y$ explicitly in terms of $x$.

==== 4.1 Two Categories of Lines
<two-categories-of-lines>
Isolate the $y$-term in the Cartesian equation: $ b y = - a x - c . $

Two cases arise:

===== Case 1: $b eq.not 0$ (the line is non-vertical)
<case-1-b-neq-0-the-line-is-non-vertical>
We can divide through by $b$: $ y = - a / b x - c / b . $ Setting
$m = - a / b$ and $p = - c / b$, the equation takes the canonical form:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ y = m x + p $]) $ This is the
#strong[slope-intercept form] (or reduced equation) of the line.

- #strong[$m$ is the slope] (gradient, or #emph[coefficient directeur]).
- #strong[$p$ is the $y$-intercept] (ordonnée à l'origine): it is the
  vertical coordinate where the line crosses the $y$-axis $\(O y\)$
  (setting $x = 0$ yields $y = m\(0\)+ p = p$). The point
  $\(0 thin\;p\)$ lies on the line.

===== Case 2: $b = 0$ (the line is vertical)
<case-2-b-0-the-line-is-vertical>
The equation reduces to $a x + c = 0$. Since $\(a\,b\)eq.not\(0\,0\)$
and $b = 0$, we must have $a eq.not 0$. We deduce: $ x = - c / a = k . $
Every point on the line shares the #strong[same $x$-coordinate] $k$. The
line is parallel to the $y$-axis $\(O y\)$. Its slope is undefined
(“infinite”), and it cannot be written in the form $y = m x + p$. Its
direction vector is strictly vertical, such as $arrow(j) vec(0, 1)$.

==== 4.2 The Geometric Meaning of Slope $m$
<the-geometric-meaning-of-slope-m>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Property --- Calculating the Slope.
]
 Let
$A\(x_A thin\;y_A\)$ and $B\(x_B thin\;y_B\)$ be two distinct points on
a non-vertical line ($x_A eq.not x_B$). The slope $m$ is given by:
$ m = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(Delta y, Delta x) = upright("rise") / upright("run") . $
]

#strong[Physical Intuition:] $Delta y$ represents vertical elevation
change and $Delta x$ represents horizontal advance.

- If $m > 0$: the line “climbs” from left to right. The associated
  affine function is strictly increasing.
- If $m < 0$: the line “descends” from left to right. The associated
  affine function is strictly decreasing.
- If $m = 0$: the equation becomes $y = p$. The line is perfectly
  #strong[horizontal], parallel to the $x$-axis.
- An immediate direction vector for a line with equation $y = m x + p$
  is $arrow(u) vec(1, m)$. Advancing by $1$ unit horizontally produces a
  vertical change of $m$ units.

