=== 5. Standard Techniques: Construction and Identification
<standard-techniques-construction-and-identification>
==== 5.1 Graphing a Line Accurately
<graphing-a-line-accurately>
Two reliable methods: 1. #strong[The Two-Point Method:] Choose two
simple values for $x$ (frequently $x = 0$ and $x = 1$ or $x = 2$),
compute the corresponding $y$-values, plot both points, and connect them
with a straightedge. 2. #strong[The Slope-Intercept Method:] Plot the
intercept $\(0 thin\;p\)$ on the vertical axis. From there, move $1$
unit to the right, then move vertically by $m$ units (upward if $m > 0$,
downward if $m < 0$). Mark the second point and draw the line.

==== 5.2 Finding the Equation of a Line
<finding-the-equation-of-a-line>
- #strong[Case A: Given two points $A\(x_A thin\;y_A\)$ and
  $B\(x_B thin\;y_B\)$ with $x_A eq.not x_B$.]
  + Compute the slope: $m = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A)$.
  + The equation is $y = m x + p$. To find $p$, use the condition that
    $A in cal(D)$:
    $ y_A = m x_A + p arrow.r.double.long p = y_A - m x_A . $
- #strong[Case B: Given a point $A\(x_A thin\;y_A\)$ and a direction
  vector $arrow(u) vec(- b, a)$.]
  + Write down the general Cartesian template: $a x + b y + c = 0$.
  + Substitute the coordinates of $A$ into the equation to determine
    constant $c$:
    $ a x_A + b y_A + c = 0 arrow.r.double.long c = - a x_A - b y_A . $

