=== 6. Relative Positions of Two Lines and Linear Systems
<relative-positions-of-two-lines-and-linear-systems>
Consider two lines in the plane: $cal(D) : a x + b y + c = 0$ and
$cal(D)' : a' x + b' y + c' = 0$, with direction vectors
$arrow(u) vec(- b, a)$ and $arrow(u)' vec(- b', a')$ respectively.

==== 6.1 The Three Configurations
<the-three-configurations>
#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Intersecting Lines          Strictly Parallel Lines            Coincident Lines
X  (one point)               /     /   (no point)           /////// (infinitely many)
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[Intersecting Lines:] Their direction vectors are not
  collinear:
  $ det\(arrow(u)\,arrow(u)'\)eq.not 0 arrow.l.r.double\(- b\)\(a'\)-\(a\)\(- b'\)eq.not 0 arrow.l.r.double a b' - a' b eq.not 0 . $
  For non-vertical lines: $m eq.not m'$. They meet at a #strong[single,
  unique point].

+ #strong[Strictly Parallel Lines:] Their direction vectors are
  collinear ($a b' - a' b = 0$, so $m = m'$), but their intercepts
  differ ($p eq.not p'$). Their intersection is empty:
  $cal(D) inter cal(D)' = diameter$.

+ #strong[Coincident (Identical) Lines:] Their direction vectors are
  collinear and they share at least one point (hence $m = m'$ and
  $p = p'$). Every point is shared: $cal(D) = cal(D)'$.

==== 6.2 Solving a System of Two Linear Equations in Two Variables
<solving-a-system-of-two-linear-equations-in-two-variables>
Finding where two lines intersect is equivalent to finding the pair
$\(x thin\;y\)$ satisfying both equations simultaneously:
$ {a x + b y = e\
c x + d y = f $

Two primary algebraic techniques exist:

===== Method 1: Substitution
<method-1-substitution>
Isolate one unknown (the one with the simplest coefficient, preferably
$1$ or $- 1$) in one equation, then substitute its expression into the
second equation. This reduces the problem to a single linear equation in
one unknown.

===== Method 2: Elimination (Linear Combination)
<method-2-elimination-linear-combination>
Multiply one or both equations by suitable non-zero constants so that
the coefficients of one variable (say $x$) become exact opposites.
Adding the two resulting equations cancels $x$ completely!

