=== 1. The Problem: The Silk Weavers of Lyon and Ada Lovelace's Cards
<the-problem-the-silk-weavers-of-lyon-and-ada-lovelaces-cards>
At the very opening of the nineteenth century, on the steep hillsides of
the Croix-Rousse in Lyon, France, the silk workshops of the
#emph[canuts] (silk weavers) echoed with the deafening clatter of wooden
shuttles.

To weave a rich damask brocade embroidered with crimson roses and silver
leaves, two workers were required at every loom: the master weaver
seated before the reed, and a young child huddled in the dust below (the
draw-boy or “draw-girl”) who pulled drawstrings by hand to raise warp
threads one by one. The labor was grueling, ruinous to eyesight and
posture, and a single moment of distraction by the child created an
irreversible knot across the luxurious fabric.

In #strong[1801], the son of a Lyon weaver, #strong[Joseph Marie
Jacquard], unveiled an automated mechanism that astonished Europe: He
replaced the draw-boy with an endless loop of #strong[punched cardboard
cards] linked together in a chain.

- A hole in the card? A needle passes through freely: the hook engages,
  lifting the silk thread.
- No hole? The solid card pushes the needle back: the silk thread
  remains lowered.

The Jacquard loom was the first programmable machine in human history!

