  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Punched Card ]    [ Patterned Silk ]
Input x)              The Jacquard Loom              (Output f(x))
Mechanical Rule)
    ```
  ]
]

In #strong[1843], in London, a young mathematician of extraordinary
vision, #strong[Ada Lovelace] (the only legitimate child of the poet
Lord Byron), studied the mechanical blueprints of Charles Babbage's
“Analytical Engine.” She immediately saw the profound parallel with the
Lyon silk mills and penned this famous observation: \> #emph[“We may say
most aptly that the Analytical Engine weaves algebraical patterns just
as the Jacquard-loom weaves flowers and leaves.”]

Ada Lovelace then drafted an algorithm for computing Bernoulli numbers:
she had just composed the #strong[world's very first computer program].

What Jacquard realized in cardboard and Ada Lovelace formalized in
thought is the very soul of what mathematicians define as a
#strong[Function].

