==== 5.2 The Reciprocal Function: $f\(x\)= 1 / x$
<the-reciprocal-function-fx-frac1x>
- #strong[Domain of definition:]
  $cal(D)_f = bb(R)^(*) = bb(R)\\{ 0 } =\]- oo thin\;0\[union\]0 thin\;+ oo\[$.
- #strong[Parity:] For all $x eq.not 0$, $frac(1, - x) = - 1 / x$. The
  reciprocal function is #strong[odd].
- #strong[Variations:]
  - Strictly decreasing on $\]- oo thin\;0\[$.

  - Strictly decreasing on $\]0 thin\;+ oo\[$.
- #strong[Graph:] A #strong[hyperbola] in two distinct branches,
  point-symmetric about $O$. The coordinate axes serve as its
  #strong[asymptotes].

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Classic Pitfall Warning!
]
 Never say that the reciprocal
function is “decreasing on $bb(R)^(*)$\.” Take $a = - 1$ and $b = 1$.
Clearly $a < b$, but $f\(a\)= frac(1, - 1) = - 1$ and $f\(b\)= 1$. The
value increased from $- 1$ to $+ 1$! The function is strictly decreasing
#strong[on each of the two intervals taken separately], but not on their
disconnected union.
]

