=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-35>
- The fundamental difference between a continuous quantity and a
  #strong[numerical sequence] indexed by natural numbers $n in bb(N)$.
- The two primary ways of generating a sequence: the #strong[explicit
  formula] $u_n = f\(n\)$ and the #strong[recurrence relation]
  $u_(n + 1) = g\(u_n\)$.
- #strong[Arithmetic sequences]: the steady staircase where one always
  adds the same common difference $r$, and the legendary trick of young
  #strong[Carl Friedrich Gauss] to sum consecutive integers.
- #strong[Geometric sequences]: chained multiplication by the common
  ratio $q$, the dizzying growth of compound interest, and the trap of
  Sissa's chessboard.
- How to determine the #strong[direction of variation] (monotonicity) of
  a sequence and intuitively grasp its #strong[limit] at infinity.
- Programming a #strong[threshold algorithm] in Python to determine when
  a quantity surpasses a given ceiling.

