=== 1. The Problem: The Two Bankers of Florence
<the-problem-the-two-bankers-of-florence>
In Renaissance Florence, two cloth merchants, Matteo and Cosimo, seek
financing to charter a caravel bound for the Levant. Each needs to
borrow $1 thin 000$ gold florins for a term of 10 years.

They approach two moneylenders on the Ponte Vecchio:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
MATTEO'S CONTRACT (Arithmetic Loan)          COSIMO'S CONTRACT (Geometric Loan)
Each year, Matteo repays                     Each year, Cosimo's debt
a FIXED penalty of 100 florins.              increases by 10% (compound rate).
Year 0 : 1,000 florins                       Year 0 : 1,000 florins
Year 1 : 1,100 florins                       Year 1 : 1,100 florins
Year 2 : 1,200 florins                       Year 2 : 1,210 florins
Year 3 : 1,300 florins                       Year 3 : 1,331 florins
Year 10: 2,000 florins                       Year 10: 2,593.74 florins!
    ```
  ]
]

Look closely at what occurred:

- For Matteo, each year adds #strong[the same fixed amount]: $+ 100$
  florins. It is an even staircase. Over 10 years, he pays
  $1 thin 000 + 10 times 100 = 2 thin 000$ florins.
- For Cosimo, the first year appears identical ($+ 100$ florins). But in
  the second year, the $10 %$ applies to the $1 thin 100$ florins
  already owed: the debt expands by $110$ florins! In the third year,
  the $10 %$ applies to $1 thin 210$ florins ($+ 121$ florins). Interest
  begets interest, which in turn breeds more interest: this is the
  inexorable engine of #strong[compound interest]. In 10 years, Cosimo
  owes nearly $2 thin 594$ florins---almost $60 %$ more than Matteo!

These two contracts embody the two royal families of discrete sequences:

- #strong[Arithmetic sequences], governed by #strong[repeated addition].
- #strong[Geometric sequences], propelled by #strong[repeated
  multiplication].

