=== 4. Geometric Sequences: The Vertigo of Multiplication
<geometric-sequences-the-vertigo-of-multiplication>
==== 4.1 Definition and General Term
<definition-and-general-term-1>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Geometric Sequence.
]
 A sequence $\(u_n\)$ is
called #strong[geometric] if there exists a fixed real number $q$,
called the #strong[common ratio], such that for every natural number
$n$: $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ u_(n + 1) = q times u_n $]) $
]

To move from one term to the next, we always multiply by the same number
$q$:

- $u_1 = q times u_0$
- $u_2 = q times u_1 = q times\(q u_0\)= u_0 times q^2$
- $u_3 = q times u_2 = u_0 times q^3$

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Explicit Formula for the General Term.
]
 If
$\(u_n\)$ is a geometric sequence with initial term $u_0$ and common
ratio $q$, then for all $n in bb(N)$:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ u_n = u_0 times q^n $]) $ If the
initial term is given at index $p$ as $u_p$, the formula becomes:
$ u_n = u_p times q^(n - p) . $
]

==== 4.2 Sissa's Chessboard and Exponential Explosion
<sissas-chessboard-and-exponential-explosion>
The Indian sage Sissa invented the game of chess to entertain his king.
Delighted, the monarch offered him any reward he desired. Sissa replied
modestly: \> “Sire, place a single grain of wheat on the first square,
two on the second, four on the third, eight on the fourth, doubling on
each subsequent square up to the 64th square of the board.”

The king smiled at what seemed a trifling request. But his treasurers
returned as pale as death:

- Square 1: $u_0 = 1 = 2^0$
- Square 2: $u_1 = 2 = 2^1$
- Square 3: $u_2 = 4 = 2^2$
- Square 64: $u_63 = 2^63 approx 9.22 times 10^18$ grains!

The total number of grains across the entire board equals:
$ S = 1 + 2 + 4 + dots.h + 2^63 = 2^64 - 1 approx 18.4 times 10^18 upright(" grains") . $
This represents more than $1 thin 200$ billion metric tons of
wheat---equivalent to the modern world's wheat harvest for more than two
millennia! The king could never honor his promise.

==== 4.3 Sum of Consecutive Powers of a Number $q$
<sum-of-consecutive-powers-of-a-number-q>
#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Required knowledge) --- Sum of Powers of $q$.
]
 For any
real $q eq.not 1$ and any integer $n gt.eq 0$:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ 1 + q + q^2 + q^3 + dots.h + q^n = frac(1 - q^(n + 1), 1 - q) $]) $

#emph[Proof (by the telescoping method):] Let
$S_n = 1 + q + q^2 + dots.h + q^n$. Multiply both sides by $q$:
$ q S_n = q\(1 + q + q^2 + dots.h + q^n\)= q + q^2 + q^3 + dots.h + q^(n + 1) . $
Now subtract this equation from the original equation ($S_n - q S_n$):
$ S_n - q S_n =\(1 + q + q^2 + dots.h + q^n\)-\(q + q^2 + q^3 + dots.h + q^(n + 1)\). $
Observe the striking cancellation: all intermediate terms eliminate each
other diagonally in pairs! Only the very first term ($1$) and the very
last term ($- q^(n + 1)$) survive: $ S_n\(1 - q\)= 1 - q^(n + 1) . $
Since $q eq.not 1$, we can divide by $1 - q$:
$ S_n = frac(1 - q^(n + 1), 1 - q) . quad square.filled.medium $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 General Theorem --- Geometric Sum.
]
 For any geometric
sequence with common ratio $q eq.not 1$:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ S =\(upright("first term")\)times frac(1 - q^(upright("number of terms")), 1 - q) $]) $
]

#divider()

=== 5. Variations and Intuition of the Limit
<variations-and-intuition-of-the-limit>
==== 5.1 The Three Methods for Studying the Monotonicity of a Sequence
<the-three-methods-for-studying-the-monotonicity-of-a-sequence>
To determine whether a sequence climbs ($u_(n + 1) gt.eq u_n$) or
descends ($u_(n + 1) lt.eq u_n$):

+ #strong[Method 1: Study the sign of the difference $u_(n + 1) - u_n$
  (Universal method).]

  - If for all $n$, $u_(n + 1) - u_n > 0$, the sequence $\(u_n\)$ is
    strictly increasing.
  - If for all $n$, $u_(n + 1) - u_n < 0$, the sequence $\(u_n\)$ is
    strictly decreasing.

+ #strong[Method 2: Compare the quotient $u_(n + 1) / u_n$ with $1$
  (valid ONLY when all terms are strictly positive!).]

  - If $u_(n + 1) / u_n > 1$, the sequence is strictly increasing.
  - If $u_(n + 1) / u_n < 1$, the sequence is strictly decreasing.

+ #strong[Method 3: Leverage the associated function if $u_n = f\(n\)$.]
  If the function $f$ is increasing on $\[0\,+ oo\[$, then the sequence
  $u_n = f\(n\)$ is increasing.

==== 5.2 Limit of a Geometric Sequence According to Ratio $q$
<limit-of-a-geometric-sequence-according-to-ratio-q>
What happens to $q^n$ as $n$ grows indefinitely large
($n arrow.r + oo$)?

- #strong[If $q > 1$:] The sequence explodes toward infinity:
  $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) q^n = + oo$. (Example: $2^n$).
- #strong[If $q = 1$:] The sequence is constant and equal to 1.
- #strong[If $- 1 < q < 1$:] The sequence shrinks toward zero:
  $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) q^n = 0$. (Example: $(1 / 2)^n arrow.r 0$).
- #strong[If $q lt.eq - 1$:] The sequence oscillates without admitting
  any limit (Example: $\(- 1\)^n = 1\,- 1\,1\,- 1 dots.h$).

