=== 1. The Problem: The Fairground Gondola Turning Endlessly
<the-problem-the-fairground-gondola-turning-endlessly>
You have boarded the giant Ferris wheel at the fairground. It has a
radius of $20 upright(" m")$, its central axle is $22 upright(" m")$
above the ground, and your gondola departs from the lowest point, just
$2 upright(" m")$ off the ground. The wheel turns slowly, steadily, in a
counterclockwise direction when viewed from the parking lot.

Your little brother, watching from below, asks you a simple question:

#emph[“When your gondola has traveled 50 meters along the circle, how
high will you be? And after 300 meters? And after 1 kilometer?”]

You pull out your middle-school trigonometry (Chapter 28): in a
right-angled triangle,
$cos hat(A) = upright("adjacent") / upright("hypotenuse")$. But very
quickly, three major obstacles arise.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
peak (42 m)
'   |   '-.
'      |      '.
O---------|   axle at 22 m
20 m  /
'.      |      .'
'-.   |   .-'
'-*-'   ← gondola boarding (2 m)
ground
    ```
  ]
]

#strong[Obstacle 1 --- Middle-school angles stop at 90°.] A right-angled
triangle cannot have an acute angle of $120^compose$, much less
$400^compose$. Yet your gondola keeps turning: after a turn and a half,
it has “turned through $540^compose$”.

#strong[Obstacle 2 --- The degree has no natural connection to distance
traveled.] Your brother speaks in #strong[meters traveled along the
circle]. To convert meters to degrees, you must each time perform a rule
of three with the circumference $2 pi times 20$. This is clumsy: the
unit “degree” is foreign to the circle.

#strong[Obstacle 3 --- Direction matters.] If the wheel turns backward
(to let passengers off), we must account for #strong[negative] angles.

We therefore need: 1. a #strong[natural] unit of angle, directly tied to
arc length; 2. a definition of cosine and sine valid for #strong[any
real number], positive or negative, small or vast.

This is precisely what mathematicians constructed between the
seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, and what you will now reconstruct
with a simple piece of string.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 The True History --- The Sine Is a Pocket!
]
 Around 150 BC,
the Greek astronomer #strong[Hipparchus] compiled the first table of
“chords” to calculate the positions of celestial bodies;
#strong[Ptolemy] refined it in the #emph[Almagest] (2nd century AD). In
India, around 499, #strong[Aryabhata] preferred to work with the
#strong[half-chord], which he termed #emph[ardha-jyā]
(“half-bowstring”), abbreviated to #emph[jyā] and then #emph[jīva]. Arab
scholars transcribed this word phonetically as #emph[jība], which,
written without vowels, resembled #emph[jayb], meaning “pocket” or “fold
of a garment”. In the twelfth century, while translating these texts in
Toledo, #strong[Gerard of Cremona] rendered it literally into Latin as
#emph[sinus], meaning “fold, pocket, bay”. That is why the most famous
wave function in mathematics bears the name of a fold in a tunic!
]

