=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-37>
- Why ordinary multiplication between two numbers is not enough when we
  want to multiply #strong[two arrows] (two vectors).
- The physical meaning of the #strong[work of a force] and the discovery
  of the #strong[scalar product] (dot product).
- The geometry of the #strong[orthogonal projection]: how to let light
  fall from above to measure the effective shadow.
- The four faces of the dot product: by #strong[cosine], by
  #strong[projection], by #strong[Cartesian coordinates]
  ($x x' + y y'$), and by #strong[lengths alone] (polarization
  identities).
- The absolute criterion of #strong[orthogonality] and its crowning
  application: finding the normal vector to a line and its Cartesian
  equation $a x + b y + c = 0$.
- The powerful metric theorems of the triangle: #strong[Al-Kashi's
  theorem] (generalized Pythagorean theorem / Law of Cosines) and the
  #strong[median theorem].
- Determining #strong[geometric loci]: characterizing a circle by the
  dot product $accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) = 0$.

