=== 1. The Problem: The Towhorse and the Coal Barge
<the-problem-the-towhorse-and-the-coal-barge>
At the dawn of the nineteenth century, in the furnace of the Industrial
Revolution, coal is the lifeblood powering the steam engines. To
transport thousands of tons of coal from northern collieries to the
factories of Paris or London, the inland waterway network is the most
economical route. Heavy, broad-beamed barges weighing over a hundred
tons glide silently along the canals.

These barges do not yet have engines. They are towed from dry land by a
powerful draft horse treading heavily along the dirt path running beside
the canal: the #strong[towpath].

Observe the scene from a footbridge above the water:

#figure(image("figures/vol_5/fig_38_produit_scalaire_travail.svg", alt: "Produit scalaire et travail d’une force : composante utile et signe géométrique"),
  caption: [
    Produit scalaire et travail d'une force : composante utile et signe
    géométrique
  ]
)

The canal is straight. The barge glides forward along a displacement
vector $arrow(d)$. But the horse cannot walk on water! It is forced to
march along the bank. The long hemp rope therefore makes an
#strong[oblique angle $theta$] relative to the direction of the canal.

The horse strains its haunches, its hooves dig into the clay, its chest
presses into the leather harness collar: it exerts a considerable force
$arrow(F)$. Yet a young barge apprentice sitting by the tiller notices
something puzzling: #emph[“The horse is exhausting itself, yet the barge
moves forward only very slowly. Why?”]

The seasoned boatman replies: #emph[“Because part of its force pulls the
boat sideways toward the bank! That part contributes nothing to moving
forward: it tries only to ground the barge in the reeds. I have to fight
against it constantly by leaning on the tiller. Only the part of the
force pointing straight ahead, parallel to the water, produces useful
motion.”]

