=== 2. The Work of a Force and the Geometric Definition
<the-work-of-a-force-and-the-geometric-definition>
==== 2.1 From Physical Action to the Scalar Product
<from-physical-action-to-the-scalar-product>
In physics, when an object undergoes a constant force $arrow(F)$ over a
rectilinear displacement $arrow(d) = accent(A B, ⃗)$, the mechanical
energy transferred is called the #strong[work of the force], denoted $W$
(for #emph[Work]).

If the force were perfectly aligned with the motion ($theta = 0$), the
entire force would contribute:
$W = parallel arrow(F) parallel times parallel arrow(d) parallel$. When
the rope is tilted at an angle $theta$, the effective shadow of the
force along the path is $parallel arrow(F) parallel cos theta$. The work
is then written:

$ W = parallel arrow(F) parallel times parallel arrow(d) parallel times cos theta . $

To formalize this mathematical operation combining two vectors
$arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$ to produce #strong[a single real number] (a
scalar, as opposed to a vector), mathematicians forged a universal
concept.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Scalar Product (by Norm and Cosine)
]
 Let
$arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$ be two vectors in the plane.

- If either vector is the zero vector ($arrow(u) = arrow(0)$ or
  $arrow(v) = arrow(0)$), their scalar product is zero:
  $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 0$.
- If $arrow(u) eq.not arrow(0)$ and $arrow(v) eq.not arrow(0)$, set
  $arrow(u) = accent(O A, ⃗)$ and $arrow(v) = accent(O B, ⃗)$. The
  #strong[scalar product] (or #strong[dot product]) of $arrow(u)$ and
  $arrow(v)$, denoted $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v)$, is the real number
  defined by:

$ arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = parallel arrow(u) parallel times parallel arrow(v) parallel times cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\). $
]

#strong[Reading and Pronunciation:] $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v)$ is read
aloud as “#strong[$arrow(u)$ dot $arrow(v)$]”. The centered dot
“$dot.op$” is reserved for this operation.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 The True History --- Where Does the Word “Scalar” Come From?
]

The word comes from the Latin #emph[scala], meaning “ladder” or “scale”.
The Irish mathematician #strong[William Rowan Hamilton] introduced it in
1843 when inventing quaternions: he called the pure numerical part,
which is marked on a standard numerical scale, the “scalar part”,
distinguishing it from the directional “vector part”. The modern dot
product with a dot symbol was popularized by the American physicist
#strong[Josiah Willard Gibbs] in the late nineteenth century.
]

==== 2.2 The Three Regimes of the Rope
<the-three-regimes-of-the-rope>
The value of the dot product depends on the angle $theta$ between the
two vectors:

+ #strong[Acute angle ($0 lt.eq theta < pi / 2$): strictly positive dot
  product.] $cos theta > 0$. The force assists the displacement:
  physicists speak of #strong[motive work]. The shadow falls in the
  direction of motion.
+ #strong[Right angle ($theta = pi / 2$, or $90^compose$): zero dot
  product.] $cos (pi / 2) = 0$, hence $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 0$. If
  the horse pulls perpendicularly to the canal, the barge does not
  advance a millimeter along the waterway. All energy is wasted trying
  to collapse the bank.
+ #strong[Obtuse angle ($pi / 2 < theta lt.eq pi$): strictly negative
  dot product.] $cos theta < 0$. The force opposes the displacement:
  this is the role of brakes or hydrodynamic friction, which physicists
  call #strong[resistive work].

