=== 3. The Orthogonal Projection: Measuring the Cast Shadow
<the-orthogonal-projection-measuring-the-cast-shadow>
==== 3.1 Definition by Projection
<definition-by-projection>
Instead of calculating a cosine, it is often much faster to drop a
perpendicular.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Property --- Definition by Orthogonal Projection
]
 Let
$arrow(u) = accent(O A, ⃗)$ and $arrow(v) = accent(O B, ⃗)$ be two
non-zero vectors. Let $H$ be the orthogonal projection of point $B$ onto
the line $\(O A\)$.

$ arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O B, ⃗) = accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O H, ⃗) . $

In terms of directed lengths along the axis $\(O A\)$:

- If $accent(O H, ⃗)$ and $accent(O A, ⃗)$ point in the #strong[same
  direction] (acute angle): $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = O A times O H$.
- If $accent(O H, ⃗)$ and $accent(O A, ⃗)$ point in #strong[opposite
  directions] (obtuse angle):
  $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = - O A times O H$.
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Case 1: Same direction                 Case 2: Opposite directions
B                                            B
o                               o
O   H         A                                  H   O        A
u · v = + OA × OH                                u · v = - OA × OH
    ```
  ]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof
]
 In the triangle $O H B$, right-angled at $H$:

- If the angle $accent(A O B, ̂)$ is acute,
  $cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\)= frac(O H, O B)$, so
  $O H = O B cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\)$. Thus,
  $accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O B, ⃗) = O A times O B cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\)= O A times O H$.

- If the angle $accent(A O B, ̂)$ is obtuse, the projected point $H$ lies
  on the opposite side of $O$. The adjacent angle inside the
  right-angled triangle is $pi - accent(A O B, ̂)$. Since
  $cos\(pi - theta\)= - cos theta$, we have
  $O H = O B cos\(pi - accent(A O B, ̂)\)= - O B cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\)$.
  Consequently, $O A times O B cos\(accent(A O B, ̂)\)= - O A times O H$.
  In both cases, the result exactly matches the stated formula.
  $square.filled.medium$
]

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know? --- The Symmetry of the Dot Product
]
 Projecting
$B$ onto $\(O A\)$ gives the exact same result as projecting $A$ onto
$\(O B\)$! Indeed,
$arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = parallel arrow(u) parallel parallel arrow(v) parallel cos theta = parallel arrow(v) parallel parallel arrow(u) parallel cos theta = arrow(v) dot.op arrow(u)$.
The dot product is #strong[commutative].
]

==== 3.2 The Scalar Square
<the-scalar-square>
What happens when we take the dot product of a vector with itself? The
angle between $arrow(u)$ and $arrow(u)$ is zero ($theta = 0$), and
$cos\(0\)= 1$.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Scalar Square
]
 For any vector $arrow(u)$, we
write $arrow(u)^2 = arrow(u) dot.op arrow(u)$. We have:

$ arrow(u)^2 = parallel arrow(u) parallel^2 . $

The scalar square of a vector is equal to the square of its norm. It is
a #strong[non-negative real number], and
$arrow(u)^2 = 0 arrow.l.r.double arrow(u) = arrow(0)$. For a geometric
segment $\[A B\]$, we have $accent(A B, ⃗)^2 = A B^2$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. Analytical Expression in an Orthonormal Coordinate System
<analytical-expression-in-an-orthonormal-coordinate-system>
==== 4.1 The Formula $x x' + y y'$
<the-formula-xx-yy>
When the plane is equipped with an orthonormal coordinate system
$\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\)$, the unit basis vectors satisfy:

- $parallel arrow(i) parallel = 1$ and $parallel arrow(j) parallel = 1$,
  so $arrow(i)^2 = 1$ and $arrow(j)^2 = 1$.
- $arrow(i)$ and $arrow(j)$ are perpendicular, so
  $arrow(i) dot.op arrow(j) = 0$.

Here the beauty of algebra dramatically simplifies geometric
computations:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Analytical Formula for the Dot Product
]
 In an
#strong[orthonormal] coordinate system $\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\)$, if
$arrow(u) vec(x, y)$ and $arrow(v) vec(x', y')$, then:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = x x' + y y' $]) $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof
]
 By the bilinearity of the scalar product (expanding
just like an ordinary product):
$ arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) & =\(x arrow(i) + y arrow(j)\)dot.op\(x' arrow(i) + y' arrow(j)\)\
 & = x x'\(arrow(i) dot.op arrow(i)\)+ x y'\(arrow(i) dot.op arrow(j)\)+ y x'\(arrow(j) dot.op arrow(i)\)+ y y'\(arrow(j) dot.op arrow(j)\). $
Since $arrow(i) dot.op arrow(i) = 1$, $arrow(j) dot.op arrow(j) = 1$,
and $arrow(i) dot.op arrow(j) = arrow(j) dot.op arrow(i) = 0$, the two
cross-terms vanish entirely! We are left with:
$ arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = x x'\(1\)+ 0 + 0 + y y'\(1\)= x x' + y y' . quad square.filled.medium $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Beware: Common Pitfall! --- Coordinate System Must Be
Orthonormal
]
 The formula $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = x x' + y y'$ is
#strong[false] if the axes are not perpendicular or if the units on the
two axes differ! It holds true only in an #strong[orthonormal]
coordinate system.
]

#divider()

=== 5. Polarization Identities: Computing with Lengths Alone
<polarization-identities-computing-with-lengths-alone>
Can we find the dot product of two vectors without knowing their
coordinates or the angle between them, using only the lengths of the
segments that compose them? Yes, thanks to #strong[vector binomial
identities].

For any vectors $arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$:
$ parallel arrow(u) + arrow(v) parallel^2 =\(arrow(u) + arrow(v)\)^2 = arrow(u)^2 + 2 arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) + arrow(v)^2 = parallel arrow(u) parallel^2 + 2 arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) + parallel arrow(v) parallel^2 . $

Isolating the dot product $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v)$ yields the famous
polarization formulas:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Polarization Identities
]
 For all vectors
$arrow(u)$ and $arrow(v)$:

+ $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 1 / 2 (parallel arrow(u) + arrow(v) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(u) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(v) parallel^2)$
+ $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 1 / 2 (parallel arrow(u) parallel^2 + parallel arrow(v) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(u) - arrow(v) parallel^2)$
+ $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 1 / 4 (parallel arrow(u) + arrow(v) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(u) - arrow(v) parallel^2)$
]

In a triangle $A B C$ with $arrow(u) = accent(A B, ⃗)$ and
$arrow(v) = accent(A C, ⃗)$, notice that
$arrow(u) - arrow(v) = accent(A B, ⃗) - accent(A C, ⃗) = accent(C B, ⃗)$.
The second identity becomes:

$ accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = 1 / 2 (A B^2 + A C^2 - B C^2) . $

This provides a direct bridge between the lengths of the three sides of
a triangle and the dot product!

#divider()

=== 6. Orthogonality, Normal Vectors, and Equations of Lines
<orthogonality-normal-vectors-and-equations-of-lines>
==== 6.1 The Orthogonality Criterion
<the-orthogonality-criterion>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem of Orthogonality
]
 Two vectors $arrow(u)$
and $arrow(v)$ are orthogonal if and only if:

$ arrow(u) perp arrow(v) arrow.l.r.double arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 0 . $

In an orthonormal coordinate system with $arrow(u)\(x\;y\)$ and
$arrow(v)\(x'\;y'\)$:

$ arrow(u) perp arrow(v) arrow.l.r.double x x' + y y' = 0 . $
]

==== 6.2 Normal Vector to a Line
<normal-vector-to-a-line>
In Grade 10, a line was characterized by a point and a #emph[direction
vector] $arrow(u)$ parallel to the line. In Grade 11, the dot product
allows a much more convenient approach: characterizing the line by an
arrow that is #strong[perpendicular] to it.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Normal Vector
]
 A non-zero vector $arrow(n)$ is
said to be #strong[normal] to a line $\(d\)$ if it is orthogonal to
every direction vector of $\(d\)$.
]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
n (normal vector)
M Line (d)
A      AM ⟂ n
    ```
  ]
]

A point $M\(x\;y\)$ belongs to the line $\(d\)$ passing through
$A\(x_A\;y_A\)$ with normal vector $arrow(n)\(a\;b\)$ if and only if:

$ accent(A M, ⃗) dot.op arrow(n) = 0 arrow.l.r.double a\(x - x_A\)+ b\(y - y_A\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double a x + b y +\(- a x_A - b y_A\)= 0 . $

Setting $c = - a x_A - b y_A$, we retrieve the standard Cartesian
equation!

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Cartesian Equation and Normal Vector
]


- In an orthonormal coordinate system, every line with normal vector
  $arrow(n) vec(a, b)$ has a Cartesian equation of the form:

$ a x + b y + c = 0 quad\(upright("with ")\(a\;b\)eq.not\(0\;0\)\). $

- Conversely, any equation of the form $a x + b y + c = 0$ with
  $\(a\;b\)eq.not\(0\;0\)$ is the equation of a straight line having
  normal vector $arrow(n) vec(a, b)$.
]

#divider()

=== 7. The Two Great Metric Theorems of the Triangle
<the-two-great-metric-theorems-of-the-triangle>
==== 7.1 Al-Kashi's Theorem (Law of Cosines)
<al-kashis-theorem-law-of-cosines>
In an arbitrary triangle $A B C$, when the angle $hat(A)$ is not right,
what becomes of the Pythagorean theorem?

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Al-Kashi's Theorem (Generalized Pythagorean Theorem / Law of
Cosines)
]
 Let $A B C$ be any triangle. Setting $a = B C$, $b = A C$,
$c = A B$, and $hat(A) = accent(B A C, ̂)$:

$ a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2 b c cos hat(A) . $

Likewise for the other two vertices:
$ b^2 = a^2 + c^2 - 2 a c cos hat(B)\,#h(2em) c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2 a b cos hat(C) . $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Geometric Proof via the Dot Product
]
 Write Chasles' relation
for the vector $accent(B C, ⃗)$:
$accent(B C, ⃗) = accent(B A, ⃗) + accent(A C, ⃗) = accent(A C, ⃗) - accent(A B, ⃗)$.
Take the scalar square of both sides:
$ B C^2 = parallel accent(B C, ⃗) parallel^2 = parallel accent(A C, ⃗) - accent(A B, ⃗) parallel^2 = parallel accent(A C, ⃗) parallel^2 - 2 accent(A C, ⃗) dot.op accent(A B, ⃗) + parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel^2 . $
Since $parallel accent(A C, ⃗) parallel = b$,
$parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel = c$, and
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = A B times A C cos hat(A) = b c cos hat(A)$,
substituting gives:
$ a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2 b c cos hat(A) . quad square.filled.medium $
]

Notice the beauty of the formula: if $hat(A) = 90^compose$, then
$cos\(90^compose\)= 0$, and the corrective term $- 2 b c cos hat(A)$
vanishes: we recover exactly the Pythagorean theorem $a^2 = b^2 + c^2$!
Al-Kashi contains Pythagoras as a special case.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 The True History --- Ghiyath al-Din Jamshid al-Kashi
]
 A
fifteenth-century Persian astronomer and mathematician, Al-Kashi worked
at the Samarkand observatory under the patronage of Prince Ulugh Beg. In
his masterpiece, #emph[The Key to Arithmetic] (1427), he calculated $pi$
to 16 correct decimal places (a world record that held for nearly two
centuries) and compiled trigonometric tables of staggering precision.
The formula that today bears his name in France enabled astronomers to
solve any oblique triangle without dividing it into right-angled
triangles.
]

==== 7.2 The Median Theorem (Apollonius's Theorem)
<the-median-theorem-apolloniuss-theorem>
Consider a triangle $A B C$ and let $I$ be the #strong[midpoint] of side
$\[B C\]$. How can we relate the length of the median $A I$ to the
lengths of the three sides?

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Median Theorem (Apollonius's Theorem)
]
 In every triangle
$A B C$, if $I$ is the midpoint of segment $\[B C\]$, then:

$ A B^2 + A C^2 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2 B C^2 . $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof
]
 Express the vectors $accent(A B, ⃗)$ and
$accent(A C, ⃗)$ passing through the midpoint $I$:
$accent(A B, ⃗) = accent(A I, ⃗) + accent(I B, ⃗)$ and
$accent(A C, ⃗) = accent(A I, ⃗) + accent(I C, ⃗)$. Take their scalar
squares:
$ A B^2 = parallel accent(A I, ⃗) + accent(I B, ⃗) parallel^2 = A I^2 + 2 accent(A I, ⃗) dot.op accent(I B, ⃗) + I B^2\, $
$ A C^2 = parallel accent(A I, ⃗) + accent(I C, ⃗) parallel^2 = A I^2 + 2 accent(A I, ⃗) dot.op accent(I C, ⃗) + I C^2 . $
Add the two equations side by side:
$ A B^2 + A C^2 = 2 A I^2 + 2 accent(A I, ⃗) dot.op\(accent(I B, ⃗) + accent(I C, ⃗)\)+ I B^2 + I C^2 . $
Since $I$ is the midpoint of $\[B C\]$, we have
$accent(I B, ⃗) + accent(I C, ⃗) = arrow(0)$! The cross-term cancels
completely. Moreover, $I B = I C = frac(B C, 2)$, so
$I B^2 + I C^2 = (frac(B C, 2))^2 + (frac(B C, 2))^2 = 2 frac(B C^2, 4) = 1 / 2 B C^2$.
We arrive directly at:
$ A B^2 + A C^2 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2 B C^2 . quad square.filled.medium $
]

#divider()

=== 8. Loci of Points and Equations of Circles
<loci-of-points-and-equations-of-circles>
The dot product provides a method of unmatched elegance for finding the
algebraic formula of a circle.

==== 8.1 Circle Defined by Center and Radius
<circle-defined-by-center-and-radius>
A point $M\(x\;y\)$ lies on the circle $cal(C)$ with center
$Omega\(x_Omega\;y_Omega\)$ and radius $R$ if and only if the distance
$Omega M$ equals $R$:

$ Omega M^2 = R^2 arrow.l.r.double\(x - x_Omega\)^2 +\(y - y_Omega\)^2 = R^2 . $

==== 8.2 Circle Defined by Diameter $\[A B\]$
<circle-defined-by-diameter-ab>
Recall from middle school: any triangle inscribed in a circle with a
diameter as its hypotenuse is a #strong[right-angled triangle]. In other
words: a point $M$ (distinct from $A$ and $B$) lies on the circle with
diameter $\[A B\]$ if and only if the angle $accent(A M B, ̂)$ is a right
angle, which vectorially translates to:

$ accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) = 0 . $

\(Note: if $M = A$ or $M = B$, one of the vectors is zero, so the dot
product is still zero; the relation holds for all points on the circle.)

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Equation of a Circle with Diameter $\[A B\
]
$] The
circle with diameter $\[A B\]$, where $A\(x_A\;y_A\)$ and
$B\(x_B\;y_B\)$, is the set of points $M\(x\;y\)$ satisfying:

$ accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) = 0 arrow.l.r.double\(x_A - x\)\(x_B - x\)+\(y_A - y\)\(y_B - y\)= 0\, $
or equivalently:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(x - x_A\)\(x - x_B\)+\(y - y_A\)\(y - y_B\)= 0 $]) $
]

#divider()

=== Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-7>
#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 1 --- Computing the Dot Product in Various
Ways
]
 Consider a square $A B C D$ of side length 4 and center $O$.
Compute the following dot products: 1.
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗)$ 2.
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A D, ⃗)$ 3.
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(C D, ⃗)$ 4.
$accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O B, ⃗)$

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. By orthogonal projection: The orthogonal
projection of $C$ onto the line $\(A B\)$ is point $B$. Therefore,
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A B, ⃗) = A B^2 = 4^2 = 16$.
\2. By orthogonality: In a square, $\(A B\)perp\(A D\)$, so the angle is
a right angle: $accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A D, ⃗) = 0$. 3. Collinear
vectors in opposite directions: $accent(C D, ⃗) = - accent(A B, ⃗)$. Hence
$accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(C D, ⃗) = - A B^2 = - 16$. 4. In a square,
the diagonals intersect perpendicularly at their midpoint $O$, so
$\(O A\)perp\(O B\)$. Thus, $accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O B, ⃗) = 0$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 2 --- Computing an Angle Using Coordinates
]
 In
an orthonormal coordinate system, three points are given: $A\(1\;2\)$,
$B\(4\;6\)$, and $C\(- 2\;6\)$. Compute the angle $accent(B A C, ̂)$
rounded to the nearest tenth of a degree.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] Determine the coordinates of vectors
$accent(A B, ⃗)$ and $accent(A C, ⃗)$:

- $accent(A B, ⃗) vec(4 - 1, 6 - 2) = vec(3, 4)$\;
- $accent(A C, ⃗) vec(- 2 - 1, 6 - 2) = vec(- 3, 4)$.

+ Analytical dot product:
  $accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = x x' + y y' = 3 times\(- 3\)+ 4 times 4 = - 9 + 16 = 7$.
+ Compute norms:
  $parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5$.
  $parallel accent(A C, ⃗) parallel = sqrt(\(- 3\)^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5$.
+ Extract the cosine:
  $accent(A B, ⃗) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel times parallel accent(A C, ⃗) parallel times cos accent(B A C, ̂)$,
  so $7 = 5 times 5 times cos accent(B A C, ̂) = 25 cos accent(B A C, ̂)$,
  giving $cos accent(B A C, ̂) = 7 / 25 = 0.28$.
+ Using a calculator:
  $accent(B A C, ̂) = arccos\(0.28\)approx 73.7^compose$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 3 --- Cartesian Equation Using a Normal
Vector
]
 Find a Cartesian equation of the line $\(d\)$ passing through
the point $A\(3\;- 1\)$ and perpendicular to the line $\(Delta\)$ with
equation $2 x - 5 y + 7 = 0$.

#strong[Detailed Solution:]

- The line $\(Delta\)$ has normal vector $arrow(n_Delta) vec(2, - 5)$.

- Since $\(d\)$ is perpendicular to $\(Delta\)$, a normal vector to
  $\(Delta\)$ serves as a #strong[direction vector] for $\(d\)$. Hence
  $arrow(u)_d = vec(2, - 5)$.

- A normal vector to $\(d\)$ must be orthogonal to $arrow(u)_d$. The
  vector $arrow(n)_d vec(5, 2)$ works since
  $2 times 5 +\(- 5\)times 2 = 10 - 10 = 0$.

- The equation of $\(d\)$ is therefore of the form $5 x + 2 y + c = 0$.

- Since $A\(3\;- 1\)in\(d\)$, its coordinates satisfy the equation:
  $5\(3\)+ 2\(- 1\)+ c = 0 arrow.l.r.double 15 - 2 + c = 0 arrow.l.r.double 13 + c = 0 arrow.l.r.double c = - 13$.

- The Cartesian equation of $\(d\)$ is therefore:
  $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ 5 x + 2 y - 13 = 0 $])$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 4 --- Length and Angle Calculation via
Al-Kashi
]
 A surveyor inspects a triangular parcel $A B C$. He measures
$A B = 60 upright(" m")$, $A C = 100 upright(" m")$, and the angle
$accent(B A C, ̂) = 60^compose$. 1. Compute the distance $B C$. 2.
Compute the angle $accent(A B C, ̂)$ to the nearest degree.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. By Al-Kashi's theorem:
$B C^2 = A B^2 + A C^2 - 2 times A B times A C times cos hat(A)$.
$B C^2 = 60^2 + 100^2 - 2 times 60 times 100 times cos\(60^compose\)$.
Since $cos\(60^compose\)= 1 / 2$:
$B C^2 = 3600 + 10000 - 12000 times 0.5 = 13600 - 6000 = 7600$. Hence
$B C = sqrt(7600) approx 87.18 upright(" m")$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ For angle $hat(B)$, apply Al-Kashi isolating $cos hat(B)$:
  $A C^2 = A B^2 + B C^2 - 2 times A B times B C times cos hat(B)$,
  $100^2 = 60^2 + 7600 - 2 times 60 times sqrt(7600) times cos hat(B)$,
  $10000 = 3600 + 7600 - 120 sqrt(7600) cos hat(B) = 11200 - 120 sqrt(7600) cos hat(B)$.
  $120 sqrt(7600) cos hat(B) = 11200 - 10000 = 1200$.
  $cos hat(B) = frac(1200, 120 sqrt(7600)) = 10 / sqrt(7600) approx 0.1147$.
  Whence $hat(B) = arccos\(0.1147\)approx 83^compose$.
]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 5 --- Equation of a Circle Defined by a
Diameter
]
 Given points $A\(- 1\;3\)$ and $B\(5\;1\)$. 1. Determine a
Cartesian equation of the circle $cal(C)$ having diameter $\[A B\]$. 2.
Find the coordinates of its center $Omega$ and radius $R$, and verify
the form $\(x - x_Omega\)^2 +\(y - y_Omega\)^2 = R^2$.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1.
$M\(x\;y\)in cal(C) arrow.l.r.double accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) = 0$.
$accent(M A, ⃗) vec(- 1 - x, 3 - y)$ and
$accent(M B, ⃗) vec(5 - x, 1 - y)$.
$accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) =\(- 1 - x\)\(5 - x\)+\(3 - y\)\(1 - y\)= 0$.
Expand:
$\(- 5 + x - 5 x + x^2\)+\(3 - 3 y - y + y^2\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double x^2 - 4 x + y^2 - 4 y - 2 = 0$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Center $Omega$, midpoint of $\[A B\]$:
  $x_Omega = frac(- 1 + 5, 2) = 2$, and $y_Omega = frac(3 + 1, 2) = 2$,
  so $Omega\(2\;2\)$. Radius:
  $A B = sqrt(\(5 -\(- 1\)\)^2 +\(1 - 3\)^2) = sqrt(6^2 +\(- 2\)^2) = sqrt(36 + 4) = sqrt(40) = 2 sqrt(10)$.
  The radius is $R = frac(A B, 2) = sqrt(10)$, hence $R^2 = 10$. The
  standard equation is $\(x - 2\)^2 +\(y - 2\)^2 = 10$. Expanding:
  $x^2 - 4 x + 4 + y^2 - 4 y + 4 = 10 arrow.l.r.double x^2 - 4 x + y^2 - 4 y - 2 = 0$.
  We obtain the exact same equation!
]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 6 --- Using the Median Theorem
]
 Let $A B C$ be
a triangle such that $A B = 7$, $A C = 9$, and $B C = 8$. Compute the
exact length of the median through $A$.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] Let $I$ be the midpoint of $\[B C\]$. The
median through $A$ is the segment $\[A I\]$. By the median theorem:
$ A B^2 + A C^2 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2 B C^2 . $ Substitute the known values:
$ 7^2 + 9^2 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2\(8^2\)\, $
$ 49 + 81 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2\(64\)\, $ $ 130 = 2 A I^2 + 32 . $ Isolate
$A I^2$:
$ 2 A I^2 = 130 - 32 = 98 arrow.r.double.long A I^2 = 49 arrow.r.double.long A I = sqrt(49) = 7 . $
The median measures exactly 7.
]

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-18>
+ #strong[Four Equivalent Definitions of the Dot Product:]

  - Geometric:
    $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = parallel arrow(u) parallel parallel arrow(v) parallel cos theta$.

  - By projection:
    $accent(O A, ⃗) dot.op accent(O B, ⃗) = plus.minus O A times O H$ ($H$
    is the orthogonal projection of $B$ onto line $\(O A\)$).

  - Analytical (orthonormal frame):
    $arrow(u) vec(x, y) dot.op arrow(v) vec(x', y') = x x' + y y'$.

  - Polarization:
    $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 1 / 2 (parallel arrow(u) + arrow(v) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(u) parallel^2 - parallel arrow(v) parallel^2)$.

+ #strong[Orthogonality and Vector Geometry:]

  - $arrow(u) perp arrow(v) arrow.l.r.double arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 0 arrow.l.r.double x x' + y y' = 0$.
  - A line with normal vector $arrow(n)\(a\;b\)$ has Cartesian equation
    $a x + b y + c = 0$.

+ #strong[Fundamental Triangle Theorems:]

  - Al-Kashi (Law of Cosines): $a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2 b c cos hat(A)$.
  - Median: $A B^2 + A C^2 = 2 A I^2 + 1 / 2 B C^2$ (where $I$ is the
    midpoint of $\[B C\]$).

+ #strong[Circles:]

  - Center $Omega\(x_Omega\;y_Omega\)$ and radius $R$:
    $\(x - x_Omega\)^2 +\(y - y_Omega\)^2 = R^2$.
  - Diameter $\[A B\]$:
    $accent(M A, ⃗) dot.op accent(M B, ⃗) = 0 arrow.l.r.double\(x - x_A\)\(x - x_B\)+\(y - y_A\)\(y - y_B\)= 0$.

