=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-38>
- The skier's paradox: how to measure the #strong[slope at a single
  point], when calculating a slope inherently requires two separate
  points.
- The #strong[difference quotient] (rate of change) and the stroke of
  genius of #strong[passing to the limit] as the gap $h$ approaches 0.
- Legendary notations: #strong[Lagrange's] “$f'\(a\)$” and
  #strong[Leibniz's] “$frac(d y, d x)$”.
- The equation of the #strong[tangent line]: the rigid line that grazes
  the curve without cutting through it.
- Geometric proofs of standard derivatives: why $\(x^2\)' = 2 x$ thanks
  to the #strong[growing flowerbed], and the derivatives of $x^n$,
  $1 / x$, $sqrt(x)$.
- Operations on functions: $\(u v\)' = u' v + u v'$ (proved via the
  expanding rectangle) and the quotient rule.
- The fundamental link between the #strong[sign of the derivative] and
  #strong[intervals of increase/decrease], and hunting for #strong[local
  extrema].
- The boundary cases where differential calculus breaks down: the
  #strong[corner point] (sharp turn) of the absolute value and the
  #strong[vertical tangent] of the square root.
- Concrete problems in #strong[optimization]: constructing the most
  spacious box or the most economical tin can.

#divider()

=== 1. The Problem: The Vertigo of the Descent
<the-problem-the-vertigo-of-the-descent>
You are sixteen years old. You stand atop a high, snow-covered mountain
peak, skis clipped to your boots, poles planted firmly in the fresh
powder. Ahead unfolds a daunting Olympic run: this is no straight, even
ramp; it is an unbroken succession of mogulled hills, sheer dips, and
deceptive flats. The vertical profile of the mountain is a sinuous curve
described by an elevation function $y = f\(x\)$.

You push off. The wind roars against your helmet, snow billowing in icy
plumes beneath your steel edges. With every hundredth of a second, the
incline under your boots shifts: gentle here, plunging precipitously
into the void there.

In the hollow of the run, at the precise horizontal position $a$, a
surge of centrifugal force crushes your thighs into the snow. Ask
yourself this fundamental question of physics:

#emph[“At this exact instant, at this very millisecond when my edges
bite into the snowpack, what is the slope of the mountain beneath my
boots?”]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Elevation y
Profile of the hill y = f(x)
'""'--_   (Peak: Zero slope!)
"          "-_
' (Steep uphill slope)   \__
'                                "---_ (Fast descent)
Position x
    ```
  ]
]

Finding your altitude at this point is trivial: it is simply the value
$f\(a\)$. But what about #strong[the slope]? Ever since middle school,
you have known that a slope (or gradient) is always calculated by
dividing a vertical rise by a horizontal run between #strong[two
distinct points]:

$ upright("Slope") = frac(y_B - y_A, x_B - x_A) = frac(Delta y, Delta x) . $

Yet, at this exact split second, you are at #strong[only one single
point]! If you attempt to apply the formula taking the exact same point
twice, $A\(a\;f\(a\)\)$, you obtain:

$ upright("Slope") = frac(f\(a\)- f\(a\), a - a) = 0 / 0 . $

Division by zero! A mathematical absurdity. How can the mountain have a
perfectly tangible, physical slope under your feet, while the raw slope
formula collapses into $0 / 0$?

#divider()

=== 2. The Board under the Boot: From Secant to Tangent
<the-board-under-the-boot-from-secant-to-tangent>
==== 2.1 The Rigidity of the Ski
<the-rigidity-of-the-ski>
To untie this knot, listen to your body and inspect your equipment.
Under your boots, you have strapped two long boards of laminated wood,
fiberglass, and hardened steel edges: skis. Even though the snow surface
is humped and continuously curved, #strong[your skis are straight, rigid
objects].

At every point where your weight presses down, the flat sole of your ski
embraces the mountain's curve without sinking through it. It grazes the
curve at a single instantaneous point of contact. This straight line
that caresses the curve at a point without locally crossing it is the
#strong[tangent line] to the curve. And the slope beneath your boots is
quite simply #strong[the slope of your ski]!

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Curve of the mountain
The rigid ski (the Tangent!)
Δy   /  |  Vertical rise
Δx    Horizontal run
    ```
  ]
]

==== 2.2 The Difference Quotient: The Secant Line
<the-difference-quotient-the-secant-line>
To compute this slope without cheating with zero, imagine that the tail
of your ski touches the snow at $A\(a\;f\(a\)\)$, and the tip touches
the snow slightly ahead, at $M\(a + h\;f\(a + h\)\)$, where $h$ is a
small, non-zero separation.

#figure(image("figures/vol_5/fig_39_secante_tangente.svg", alt: "Nombre dérivé et limite des sécantes quand le pas h tend vers zéro"),
  caption: [
    Nombre dérivé et limite des sécantes quand le pas h tend vers zéro
  ]
)

The line $\(A M\)$ passing through two distinct points of the curve is
called a #strong[secant line]. Its slope is given by the ratio:

$ tau\(h\)= frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h) . $

This fundamental ratio is called the #strong[difference quotient] (or
average rate of change) of the function $f$ between $a$ and $a + h$.
Because $h eq.not 0$, this fraction is perfectly well-defined and
computable!

==== 2.3 Passing to the Limit: The Zoom into the Infinitesimal
<passing-to-the-limit-the-zoom-into-the-infinitesimal>
Now, imagine shortening your ski! Bring the tip closer and closer to the
heel by letting $h$ shrink ever closer to zero: $h = 0.1$, then
$h = 0.01$, then $h = 0.000001$… The point $M$ glides down along the
hill, merging into point $A$. The secant line $\(A M\)$ pivots smoothly
and stabilizes onto a definite limiting position: #strong[the tangent
line]!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Derivative at a Point
]
 Let $f$ be a function
defined on an open interval containing $a$. We say that $f$ is
#strong[differentiable at $a$] if the difference quotient
$frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h)$ approaches a finite limit as $h$ tends to
\0. This limit is called the #strong[derivative of $f$ at $a$], denoted
$f'\(a\)$:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ f'\(a\)= lim_(h arrow.r 0) frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h) $]) $

Geometrically, $f'\(a\)$ is the #strong[slope (gradient) of the tangent
line] to the curve of $f$ at the point with abscissa $a$.
]

#divider()

=== 3. The Notations of History: Lagrange vs.~Leibniz
<the-notations-of-history-lagrange-vs.-leibniz>
Two giants of mathematics bequeathed to humanity two complementary ways
of writing this single profound idea:

==== 3.1 Joseph-Louis Lagrange's “Prime” Notation (1797)
<joseph-louis-lagranges-prime-notation-1797>
The Franco-Italian mathematician #strong[Joseph-Louis Lagrange]
introduced the compact notation: $ f'\(a\) $ read aloud as: “#strong[f
prime of a]”. The prime mark signals that this new function is “derived”
from the original function $f$. Differentiating a second time gives
$f''\(a\)$ (“f double prime”). This is the fastest, cleanest notation
for algebraic manipulation in secondary school.

==== 3.2 Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz's Differential Notation (1684)
<gottfried-wilhelm-leibnizs-differential-notation-1684>
A century earlier, the German polymath #strong[Gottfried Wilhelm
Leibniz] invented a magnificent notation directly reflecting the slope
calculation $frac(Delta y, Delta x)$:
$ frac(d f, d x)\(a\)quad upright("or") quad frac(d y, d x) $ read as:
“#strong[d f over d x]” or “#strong[the derivative of y with respect to
x]”. The Latin letter $d$ replaces the Greek letter $Delta$ to signify
an #strong[infinitesimal] (infinitely tiny) variation. This notation
continually reminds us that a derivative is the ratio of an
infinitesimal change in height $d y$ to an infinitesimal change in
horizontal position $d x$. Physicists cherish it because it immediately
displays physical dimensions and units (e.g., meters per second:
$frac(d x, d t) = upright("velocity")$).

#divider()

=== 4. The Equation of the Tangent Line to the Curve
<the-equation-of-the-tangent-line-to-the-curve>
Because the tangent line passes through the contact point
$A\(a\;f\(a\)\)$ and has slope $f'\(a\)$, its point-slope equation
follows directly from coordinate geometry:

$ y - y_A = m\(x - x_A\)arrow.l.r.double y - f\(a\)= f'\(a\)\(x - a\). $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Equation of the Tangent Line
]
 If $f$ is
differentiable at $a$, the curve $cal(C)_f$ admits at the point
$A\(a\;f\(a\)\)$ a tangent line with equation:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ y = f'\(a\)\(x - a\)+ f\(a\) $]) $
]

This line represents the #strong[best local linear approximation] of the
function near $a$. For any $x$ very close to $a$:
$ f\(x\)approx f\(a\)+ f'\(a\)\(x - a\). $

#divider()

=== 5. Standard Derivatives Proved
<standard-derivatives-proved>
Let us examine the foundational toolkit of functions. For each one, we
compute the limit of the difference quotient with complete algebraic
rigor.

==== 5.1 The Constant Function: $f\(x\)= k$
<the-constant-function-fx-k>
A perfectly level track has zero slope everywhere. For any $h eq.not 0$:
$ frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h) = frac(k - k, h) = 0 / h = 0 . $ The limit
as $h arrow.r 0$ is 0. Therefore:
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(k\)' = 0 $]) $

==== 5.2 The Linear Function: $f\(x\)= x$
<the-linear-function-fx-x>
A straight ramp inclined at 45° has a constant slope of 1.
$ frac(\(a + h\)- a, h) = h / h = 1 arrow.r_(h arrow.r 0)^() 1 . $
Therefore: $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(x\)' = 1 $]) $

==== 5.3 The Square Function: Why $\(x^2\)' = 2 x$?
<the-square-function-why-x2-2x>
Here enters the luminous demonstration of the expanding garden:

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Geometric Proof --- The Growing Flowerbed
]
 Consider a square
flowerbed with side length $x$. Its area is $S\(x\)= x^2$. In spring,
the gardener expands the bed by adding a thin strip of soil of width $h$
to the East, and a strip of width $h$ to the North.
]

#quote_box[
#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
x                h
x     Original square    B  h  (East strip: x × h)
x²)
h      North strip       C  h  (Corner: h²)
x             h
    ```
  ]
]
]


#quote_box[
The total new area added consists of three pieces: 1. An East strip of
area $x times h$\; 2. A North strip of area $x times h$\; 3. A tiny
square in the corner of area $h^2$.

The total increase in area is:
$ Delta S =\(x times h\)+\(x times h\)+ h^2 = 2 x h + h^2 . $ Divide by
the expansion width $h$ to find the average rate of area growth:
$ frac(Delta S, h) = frac(2 x h + h^2, h) = 2 x + h . $ Now let $h$
shrink to zero! The tiny corner $h^2$ vanishes infinitely faster than
the borders. All that remains is:
$ lim_(h arrow.r 0)\(2 x + h\)= 2 x . $ The number 2 is not magic:
#strong[it represents the 2 open sides of the square expanding outward
into space!
]
 $square.filled.medium$
]

By pure algebra:
$ frac(\(a + h\)^2 - a^2, h) = frac(a^2 + 2 a h + h^2 - a^2, h) = frac(2 a h + h^2, h) = 2 a + h arrow.r_(h arrow.r 0)^() 2 a . $
Therefore: $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(x^2\)' = 2 x $]) $

==== 5.4 The Power Function: $f\(x\)= x^n$ ($n in bb(N)^(*)$)
<the-power-function-fx-xn-n-in-mathbbn>
Extending this insight to a cube ($x^3$), the 3 expanding faces yield
$\(x^3\)' = 3 x^2$. In dimension $n$, using the standard algebraic
identity:
$ A^n - B^n =\(A - B\)\(A^(n - 1) + A^(n - 2) B + dots.h + A B^(n - 2) + B^(n - 1)\)\, $
with $A = a + h$ and $B = a$, the difference quotient becomes:
$ frac(\(a + h\)^n - a^n, h) = frac(h dot.op (\( a + h \)^(n - 1) + \( a + h \)^(n - 2) a + dots.h + a^(n - 1)), h) . $
There are exactly $n$ terms inside the parentheses. As $h arrow.r 0$,
each term converges to $a^(n - 1)$. Their sum is therefore
$n a^(n - 1)$.
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(x^n\)' = n x^(n - 1) $]) $

==== 5.5 The Reciprocal Function: $f\(x\)= 1 / x$ on $bb(R)^(*)$
<the-reciprocal-function-fx-frac1x-on-mathbbr>
For any $a eq.not 0$ and $h$ sufficiently small:
$ frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h) = frac(frac(1, a + h) - 1 / a, h) = frac(a -\(a + h\), a\(a + h\)) / h = frac(- h, a\(a + h\)) / h = - frac(1, a\(a + h\)) . $
As $h arrow.r 0$, the denominator approaches $a times a = a^2$. The
limit is therefore $- 1 / a^2$.
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ (1 / x)' = - 1 / x^2 $]) $

==== 5.6 The Square Root Function: $f\(x\)= sqrt(x)$ on $\]0\;+ oo\[$
<the-square-root-function-fx-sqrtx-on-0-infty>
For any $a > 0$, let us compute the difference quotient. We face an
indeterminate form $0 / 0$. To resolve it, multiply numerator and
denominator by the #strong[conjugate radical]
$\(sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)\)$:
$ frac(sqrt(a + h) - sqrt(a), h) = frac(\(sqrt(a + h) - sqrt(a)\)\(sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)\), h\(sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)\)) = frac(\(a + h\)- a, h\(sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)\)) = frac(h, h\(sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)\)) = frac(1, sqrt(a + h) + sqrt(a)) . $
As $h arrow.r 0$, the denominator approaches
$sqrt(a) + sqrt(a) = 2 sqrt(a)$.
$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(sqrt(x)\)' = frac(1, 2 sqrt(x)) $]) $

#divider()

=== 6. Operations on Derived Functions
<operations-on-derived-functions>
Let $u$ and $v$ be two differentiable functions on an interval $I$, and
let $k$ be a real constant.

==== 6.1 Sum and Scalar Multiplication
<sum-and-scalar-multiplication>
- $\(u + v\)' = u' + v'$
- $\(k dot.op u\)' = k dot.op u'$

==== 6.2 Derivative of a Product: Why $\(u v\)' eq.not u' v'$?
<derivative-of-a-product-why-uv-neq-uv>
This is the most notorious trap in the history of mathematics. Even
Leibniz fell into it in his initial notes in 1673, before correcting
himself by sketching an expanding rectangle!

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Geometric Proof --- The Expanding Rectangle
]
 Consider a
rectangular field of dimensions $u\(x\)$ by $v\(x\)$. Its area is
$A\(x\)= u\(x\)times v\(x\)$. When $x$ increases by a small amount $h$,
the width becomes $u + Delta u$ and the length becomes $v + Delta v$.
]

#quote_box[
The newly added surface area is composed of three zones: 1. A vertical
strip of area $v\(x\)times Delta u$\; 2. A horizontal strip of area
$u\(x\)times Delta v$\; 3. A negligible corner of area
$Delta u times Delta v$.

Dividing by $h$:
$ frac(Delta A, h) = v\(x\)frac(Delta u, h) + u\(x\)frac(Delta v, h) + frac(Delta u dot.op Delta v, h) . $
As $h arrow.r 0$:

- $frac(Delta u, h) arrow.r u'\(x\)$ and
  $frac(Delta v, h) arrow.r v'\(x\)$\;
- The third term vanishes because $Delta u arrow.r 0$! Only the two main
  strips remain:
  $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ \(u dot.op v\)' = u' v + u v' $
]
) quad square.filled.medium $
]

==== 6.3 Derivative of the Reciprocal and Quotient
<derivative-of-the-reciprocal-and-quotient>
If $v\(x\)eq.not 0$ on $I$:

- Reciprocal Rule:
  $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ (1 / v)' = - v' / v^2 $]) $

- Quotient Rule: Writing $u / v = u times 1 / v$ and applying the
  product rule:
  $ (u / v)' = u' (1 / v) + u (- v' / v^2) = frac(u' v, v^2) - frac(u v', v^2) = #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ frac(u' v - u v', v^2) $]) $

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution: Classic Trap! --- Order Matters in the Quotient
Rule
]
 In the numerator $u' v - u v'$, the minus sign makes the order
crucial! It is always the derivative of the top ($u'$) multiplying the
untouched bottom ($v$) first. Reversing the terms flips the sign of your
entire result.
]

#divider()

=== 7. The Crucial Link: Sign of the Derivative and Monotonicity
<the-crucial-link-sign-of-the-derivative-and-monotonicity>
Here differential calculus reveals its absolute power: it transforms a
hard global geometric question (“is the function climbing or falling
across an interval?”) into a straightforward local algebraic check
(“what is the sign of a number?”).

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem --- Monotonicity and Sign of the
Derivative
]
 Let $f$ be differentiable on an interval $I$.

- If $f'\(x\)> 0$ for all $x in I$, then $f$ is #strong[strictly
  increasing] on $I$.

- If $f'\(x\)< 0$ for all $x in I$, then $f$ is #strong[strictly
  decreasing] on $I$.

- If $f'\(x\)= 0$ for all $x in I$, then $f$ is #strong[constant] on
  $I$.
]

Intuitively: if the tips of your skis point upward ($f'\(x\)> 0$) at
every single millisecond of your glide, you cannot help but gain
altitude overall!

#divider()

=== 8. Finding Local Extrema and Optimization
<finding-local-extrema-and-optimization>
How do we pinpoint the highest crest of a hill or the deepest depression
of a valley? At the summit of a hill, the slope ceases to rise and
prepares to fall: for one infinitesimal heartbeat, the ski levels out to
absolute horizontality!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Règle Fondamentale --- Tangente Horizontale et Extrema Locaux
($f'\(x_0\)= 0$)
]


- Horizontal Tangent (f'(x₀) = 0)
- f' \> 0 f' \< 0
- Uphill) (Downhill)
]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Local Extremum Condition
]
 Let $f$ be
differentiable on an open interval containing $x_0$. If $f$ attains a
local extremum (maximum or minimum) at $x_0$, then: $ f'\(x_0\)= 0 . $

#strong[Converse and Practical Criterion:] If $f'\(x_0\)= 0$ and $f'$
#strong[changes sign] at $x_0$, then $f$ attains a local extremum at
$x_0$:

- From $+$ to $-$: this is a #strong[local maximum] (the curve rises,
  then falls).
- From $-$ to $+$: this is a #strong[local minimum] (the curve falls,
  then rises).
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution: Classic Trap! --- $f'\(x_0\)= 0$ Does Not Guarantee
an Extremum
]
 Look at the cubic function $f\(x\)= x^3$. Its derivative is
$f'\(x\)= 3 x^2$. At $x = 0$, indeed $f'\(0\)= 0$ (the tangent is
perfectly horizontal). But for all $x eq.not 0$, $3 x^2 > 0$: the
derivative never changes sign! The curve climbs before 0 and continues
climbing after 0. The origin $\(0\;0\)$ is a flat bench (an inflection
point), not a summit or basin!
]

#divider()

=== 9. At the Boundaries of Differentiation: When Calculus Breaks Down
<at-the-boundaries-of-differentiation-when-calculus-breaks-down>
Are all functions differentiable everywhere? No! There are two primary
geometric ways for a curve to reject differentiation at a point.

==== 9.1 The Corner Point: The Absolute Value at 0
<the-corner-point-the-absolute-value-at-0>
Consider the absolute value function $f\(x\)=\|x\|$. Its graph forms a
sharp “V” at the origin. Compute the difference quotient at $0$:
$ tau\(h\)= frac(\|0 + h\|-\|0\|, h) = frac(\|h\|, h) . $

- Approaching from the right ($h > 0$): $\|h\|= h$, so
  $tau\(h\)= h / h = + 1$.
- Approaching from the left ($h < 0$): $\|h\|= - h$, so
  $tau\(h\)= frac(- h, h) = - 1$.

The right-hand limit ($+ 1$) does not match the left-hand limit ($- 1$)!
The ski cannot rest stably on a knife-edge apex without wobbling between
two contradictory slopes. We say that the absolute value function
#strong[is not differentiable at 0]. The graph possesses two
half-tangents meeting at a sharp #strong[corner point].

==== 9.2 The Vertical Tangent: The Square Root at 0
<the-vertical-tangent-the-square-root-at-0>
Consider the function $f\(x\)= sqrt(x)$ at $a = 0$. For $h > 0$:
$ tau\(h\)= frac(sqrt(0 + h) - sqrt(0), h) = sqrt(h) / h = 1 / sqrt(h) . $
As $h arrow.r 0^(+)$, $sqrt(h)$ becomes an infinitesimally small
positive quantity, so $1 / sqrt(h)$ blows up toward $+ oo$! The limit is
not a finite real number. The square root function #strong[is not
differentiable at 0]. Geometrically, the curve launches vertically: it
has a #strong[vertical half-tangent] along the $y$-axis ($x = 0$).

#divider()

=== Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-8>
#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 1 --- Computing a Derivative from Definition
]

Let $f\(x\)= 3 x^2 - 5 x + 2$. Compute $f'\(2\)$ directly using the
definition of the difference quotient.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] First find $f\(2\)$:
$f\(2\)= 3\(2^2\)- 5\(2\)+ 2 = 12 - 10 + 2 = 4$. Expand $f\(2 + h\)$ for
$h eq.not 0$: $ f\(2 + h\) & = 3\(2 + h\)^2 - 5\(2 + h\)+ 2\
 & = 3\(4 + 4 h + h^2\)- 10 - 5 h + 2\
 & = 12 + 12 h + 3 h^2 - 10 - 5 h + 2 = 4 + 7 h + 3 h^2 . $ Form the
difference quotient:
$ frac(f\(2 + h\)- f\(2\), h) = frac(\(4 + 7 h + 3 h^2\)- 4, h) = frac(7 h + 3 h^2, h) = frac(h\(7 + 3 h\), h) = 7 + 3 h . $
Take the limit as $h arrow.r 0$: $ lim_(h arrow.r 0)\(7 + 3 h\)= 7 . $
Thus $f$ is differentiable at $2$ and
$#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ f'\(2\)= 7 $])$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 2 --- Equation of a Tangent Line
]
 Let
$f\(x\)= 1 / x$. Determine the equation of the tangent line to the curve
of $f$ at the point with abscissa $a = 2$.

#strong[Detailed Solution:]

- $y$-coordinate of the contact point: $f\(2\)= 1 / 2$.

- Derivative function: $f'\(x\)= - 1 / x^2$, so
  $f'\(2\)= - 1 / 2^2 = - 1 / 4$.

- Tangent line formula: $y = f'\(2\)\(x - 2\)+ f\(2\)$.
  $ y = - 1 / 4\(x - 2\)+ 1 / 2 = - 1 / 4 x + 2 / 4 + 1 / 2 = - 1 / 4 x + 1 . $
  The tangent line equation is
  $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ y = - 1 / 4 x + 1 $])$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 3 --- Product and Quotient Rules
]
 Compute the
derivatives of: 1. $g\(x\)=\(2 x - 3\)\(x^2 + 4 x\)$ 2.
$h\(x\)= frac(3 x - 1, x^2 + 1)$

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. Product form $u dot.op v$ with
$u\(x\)= 2 x - 3 arrow.r.double.long u'\(x\)= 2$, and
$v\(x\)= x^2 + 4 x arrow.r.double.long v'\(x\)= 2 x + 4$.
$ g'\(x\)= u' v + u v' = 2\(x^2 + 4 x\)+\(2 x - 3\)\(2 x + 4\). $
Expanding:
$g'\(x\)=\(2 x^2 + 8 x\)+\(4 x^2 + 8 x - 6 x - 12\)= 6 x^2 + 10 x - 12$.
\2. Quotient form $u / v$ with
$u\(x\)= 3 x - 1 arrow.r.double.long u'\(x\)= 3$, and
$v\(x\)= x^2 + 1 arrow.r.double.long v'\(x\)= 2 x$.
$ h'\(x\)= frac(u' v - u v', v^2) = frac(3\(x^2 + 1\)-\(3 x - 1\)\(2 x\), \(x^2 + 1\)^2) . $
Expanding the numerator:
$3 x^2 + 3 -\(6 x^2 - 2 x\)= 3 x^2 + 3 - 6 x^2 + 2 x = - 3 x^2 + 2 x + 3$.
Hence:
$#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ h'\(x\)= frac(- 3 x^2 + 2 x + 3, \(x^2 + 1\)^2) $])$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 4 --- Complete Analysis of a Cubic Polynomial
]

Let $f\(x\)= 2 x^3 - 9 x^2 + 12 x - 1$ defined on $bb(R)$. Construct its
complete variation table and determine all local extrema.

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. Derivative:
$f'\(x\)= 2\(3 x^2\)- 9\(2 x\)+ 12\(1\)- 0 = 6 x^2 - 18 x + 12$. Factor
out 6: $f'\(x\)= 6\(x^2 - 3 x + 2\)$. 2. Roots of $f'\(x\)$: The
quadratic $x^2 - 3 x + 2$ factors readily as $\(x - 1\)\(x - 2\)$, with
roots $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2$. The leading coefficient is positive
($a = 6 > 0$), so the parabola for $f'$ opens upward:

- $f'\(x\)> 0$ on $\]- oo\;1\[$ and on $\]2\;+ oo\[$\;
- $f'\(x\)< 0$ on $\]1\;2\[$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 3)
+ Key values:
  $f\(1\)= 2\(1\)^3 - 9\(1\)^2 + 12\(1\)- 1 = 2 - 9 + 12 - 1 = 4$.
  $f\(2\)= 2\(8\)- 9\(4\)+ 12\(2\)- 1 = 16 - 36 + 24 - 1 = 3$.
+ Variation table:
]

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 8,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$x$], [$- oo$], [], [$1$], [], [$2$], [], [$+ oo$],),
    table.hline(),
    [$f'\(x\)$], [], [$+$], [$0$], [$-$], [$0$], [$+$], [],
    [$f\(x\)$], [], [$arrow.tr$], [$4$], [$arrow.br$], [$3$], [$arrow.tr$], [],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Conclusion:] $f$ attains a #strong[local maximum] of $4$ at
$x = 1$, and a #strong[local minimum] of $3$ at $x = 2$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 5 --- Concrete Optimization: The Open Box
]

From a square sheet of cardboard measuring 12 cm on each side, small
squares of side length $x$ (with $0 < x < 6$) are cut from each of the
four corners. The sides are then folded up to form an open-top
rectangular box. What value of $x$ maximizes the box's volume?

#strong[Detailed Solution:] 1. Dimensions of the folded box:

- Base: square of side length $12 - 2 x$.
- Height: $x$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Volume function:
  $V\(x\)= upright("Base Area") times upright("Height") =\(12 - 2 x\)^2 times x =\(144 - 48 x + 4 x^2\)x = 4 x^3 - 48 x^2 + 144 x$.
+ Derivative: $V'\(x\)= 12 x^2 - 96 x + 144 = 12\(x^2 - 8 x + 12\)$.
+ Finding critical points: Roots of $x^2 - 8 x + 12 = 0$:
  $Delta =\(- 8\)^2 - 4\(1\)\(12\)= 64 - 48 = 16 = 4^2$.
  $x_1 = frac(8 - 4, 2) = 2$, and $x_2 = frac(8 + 4, 2) = 6$. Since
  $x in\]0\;6\[$, only the root $x = 2$ lies in the feasible domain. For
  $x < 2$, $V'\(x\)> 0$ (volume expands). For $x > 2$, $V'\(x\)< 0$
  (volume shrinks).
+ Conclusion: The maximum volume is achieved when the cutouts measure
  #strong[$x = 2$ cm]. The maximal capacity is
  $V\(2\)=\(12 - 4\)^2 times 2 = 8^2 times 2 = 64 times 2 = 128 upright(" cm")^3$.
]
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✅ Worked Example 6 --- Tangent Parallel to a Given Line
]
 Let
$f\(x\)= x^2 - 4 x + 1$. Are there any points on the parabola where the
tangent line is parallel to the line $y = 2 x - 5$?

#strong[Detailed Solution:]

- Two lines are parallel if and only if they share the exact same slope.

- The given line has slope $m = 2$.

- The tangent line at abscissa $x$ has slope $f'\(x\)$.

- Differentiating: $f'\(x\)= 2 x - 4$.

- Set up the equation $f'\(x\)= 2$:
  $ 2 x - 4 = 2 arrow.l.r.double 2 x = 6 arrow.l.r.double x = 3 . $

- Calculate the corresponding $y$-value:
  $f\(3\)= 3^2 - 4\(3\)+ 1 = 9 - 12 + 1 = - 2$.

- Conclusion: There is exactly one such point on the curve,
  $A\(3\;- 2\)$. Its tangent line equation is
  $y = 2\(x - 3\)- 2 = 2 x - 8$.
]

#divider()

=== What You Must Remember
<what-you-must-remember-8>
+ #strong[The Derivative $f'\(a\)$:]

  - Definition:
    $f'\(a\)= lim_(h arrow.r 0) frac(f\(a + h\)- f\(a\), h)$.

  - Geometric meaning: slope of the ski beneath the boot = slope of the
    tangent line at $A\(a\;f\(a\)\)$.

  - Equation of the tangent line:
    $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ y = f'\(a\)\(x - a\)+ f\(a\) $])$.

+ #strong[Table of Standard Derivatives:] | Function $f\(x\)$ |
  Derivative $f'\(x\)$ | Domain of Validity | |---|---|---| | $k$
  (constant) | $0$ | $bb(R)$ | | $x$ | $1$ | $bb(R)$ | | $x^2$ | $2 x$ |
  $bb(R)$ | | $x^n$ ($n in bb(N)^(*)$) | $n x^(n - 1)$ | $bb(R)$ | |
  $1 / x$ | $- 1 / x^2$ | $bb(R)^(*) =\]- oo\;0\[union\]0\;+ oo\[$ | |
  $sqrt(x)$ | $frac(1, 2 sqrt(x))$ | $\]0\;+ oo\[$ (not differentiable
  at 0) |

+ #strong[Differentiation Rules:]

  - $\(u + v\)' = u' + v'$

  - $\(k u\)' = k u'$

  - $\(u v\)' = u' v + u v'$

  - $(1 / v)' = - v' / v^2$

  - $(u / v)' = frac(u' v - u v', v^2)$

+ #strong[Sign of Derivative and Monotonicity:]

  - $f'\(x\)> 0 arrow.r.double.long f$ is strictly increasing.

  - $f'\(x\)< 0 arrow.r.double.long f$ is strictly decreasing.

  - $f'\(x_0\)= 0$ with a sign change $arrow.r.double.long$ local
    extremum (peak or trough).

+ #strong[Failures of Differentiability:]

  - $f\(x\)=\|x\|$ at 0: corner point (left-hand and right-hand slopes
    differ).
  - $f\(x\)= sqrt(x)$ at 0: vertical half-tangent (difference quotient
    tends to $+ oo$).

