=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-7>
We have seen that the derivative measures the rate at which a function
grows. For a polynomial, differentiation lowers the degree. But imagine
a living population: a bacterial colony in a Petri dish. The larger the
colony, the more individuals divide, and the faster the population
expands! Does there exist anywhere in the universe a magical function
that is #strong[strictly equal to its own derivative]: $f' = f$? A
function whose slope underfoot is at every millisecond equal to its
current elevation? That is the mystery of the #strong[exponential
function], in the next chapter.

