Observe closely what distinguishes this growth from every curve we have
studied until now:

- For a car cruising on the highway at constant speed, distance
  increases by 100 km every hour, regardless of how far it has already
  traveled. The derivative is constant.
- For a stone falling freely under gravity, speed increases by
  $9.8 upright(" m/s")$ each second: acceleration is constant, and
  distance follows a second-degree polynomial ($x^2$).
- But for the bacterial colony, #strong[every newcomer is itself a
  future birth factory]!
  - When the colony counts 10 bacteria, there are 10 potential divisions
    per generation. The slope is modest.

  - When the colony counts one million individuals, there are
    #strong[one million simultaneous cellular divisions]! The speed of
    proliferation becomes titanic.

Letting $N\(t\)$ denote the population size at time $t$, the
instantaneous rate of growth (the derivative $N'\(t\)$) is not constant:
it is #strong[directly proportional to the number of individuals already
present]:

$ N'\(t\)= k dot.op N\(t\). $

By choosing the unit of time so that $k = 1$, and starting from a single
individual at $t = 0$, we face the most fascinating challenge ever
presented to differential calculus:

#quote_box[
#strong[The Fundamental Enigma:
]
 Does there exist a mathematical
function $f$, defined and differentiable on $bb(R)$, which is
#strong[strictly equal to its own derivative] at every moment, and
equals 1 at the origin?

$ {f'\(x\)= f\(x\)quad upright("for all ") x in bb(R)\
f\(0\)= 1 $
]

This is a function whose ski-slope beneath the boot at every point is
equal to its instantaneous altitude! The higher it climbs, the steeper
the slope; the steeper the slope, the faster it ascends into the sky.

