=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-8>
The exponential function allowed us to model populations multiplying
under pure and idealized mathematical laws. Yet when a biologist counts
bacteria under a microscope, or when an opinion pollster interviews a
sample of one thousand citizens on the street, physical measurements
never lie on an immaculate, deterministic curve: they scatter, deviate,
and fluctuate randomly. How do we summarize a scattered cloud of noisy
data with two master numbers: the center of gravity (#strong[the mean])
and the dispersion spread (#strong[the standard deviation])? That is
what we are about to discover in the next chapter with #strong[the
pollster and standard deviation].

