=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-40>
- The fatal trap of the arithmetic mean: why two factories, two classes,
  or two medical treatments sharing the exact same mean can be
  diametrically opposed.
- Position indicators: the #strong[weighted mean] ($macron(x)$) versus
  the #strong[median] ($M_e$), and why the median cannot be fooled by
  billionaires.
- Sentinels of the quarter: the #strong[quartiles] $Q_1$ and $Q_3$, and
  the #strong[interquartile range] ($I Q R$).
- The art of visual summarization: John Tukey's #strong[box-and-whisker
  plot] (#emph[boxplot]).
- The mystery of the square: why the sum of deviations from the mean
  always vanishes ($sum\(x_i - macron(x)\)= 0$), and why we had to
  invent the #strong[variance] and #strong[standard deviation] $sigma$.
- The #strong[König-Huygens formula]: the lightning-fast shortcut for
  computing variance.
- The law of sampling: how a pollster can predict the vote of 45 million
  citizens by interviewing a mere 1,000 individuals, using the
  #strong[sampling fluctuation interval].
