=== 2. Measures of Central Tendency: Mean vs.~Median
<measures-of-central-tendency-mean-vs.-median>
Consider a discrete quantitative dataset where the variable takes sorted
values $x_1 < x_2 < dots.h < x_p$, associated with frequencies (counts)
$n_1\,n_2\,dots.h\,n_p$. The total sample size is
$N = sum_(i = 1)^p n_i$.

==== 2.1 The Weighted Mean ($macron(x)$)
<the-weighted-mean-barx>
The mean is the center of gravity of the distribution: it is the fair
share each individual would receive if all values were pooled and
redistributed equally.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Weighted Mean
]
 The #strong[weighted mean] of a
dataset, denoted $macron(x)$ (read “#strong[x bar]”), is given by:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ macron(x) = frac(sum_(i = 1)^p n_i x_i, N) = sum_(i = 1)^p f_i x_i $]) $

where $f_i = n_i / N$ denotes the relative frequency of the value $x_i$.
]

#strong[Linearity Property:] If every value is multiplied by a constant
$a$ and shifted by a constant $b$ (affine transformation
$y_i = a x_i + b$), the new mean is simply:
$ macron(y) = a macron(x) + b . $

==== 2.2 The Median ($M_e$): The Justice of Rank
<the-median-m_e-the-justice-of-rank>
The median ignores the sum total and focuses entirely on
#strong[position in line].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Median
]
 The #strong[median] of a sorted
statistical dataset, denoted $M_e$, is a value that splits the
population into two halves of equal size:

- At least 50% of the individuals have a value less than or equal to
  $M_e$\;
- At least 50% of the individuals have a value greater than or equal to
  $M_e$.
]

#strong[Practical Computation Method:] 1. If the total count $N$ is
#strong[odd]: $N = 2 k + 1$. The median is the value of the individual
at rank $k + 1$. #emph[\(Example: for $N = 9$, $frac(9 + 1, 2) = 5$: the
median is the 5th value).] 2. If the total count $N$ is #strong[even]:
$N = 2 k$. The median is conventionally taken as the midpoint between
the values at ranks $k$ and $k + 1$. #emph[\(Example: for $N = 10$, take
the average of the 5th and 6th values).]

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True Story --- Bill Gates Walks into a Bar
]
 Imagine a
neighborhood café where ten factory workers and clerks sit at the
counter. Each earns \$2,000 per month. The mean wage is \$2,000, and the
median wage is \$2,000. Suddenly, billionaire #strong[Bill Gates] opens
the door and takes a seat at the bar. His monthly income is around
\$10,000,000. Let us recalculate the statistics of the patrons:

- The #strong[mean income] skyrockets:
  $frac(10 times 2\,000 + 10\,000\,000, 11) approx upright(bold(\$910\,900 upright(" per month")))$!
  A foolish statistician might announce: “In this diner, the average
  customer is nearly a millionaire!” Which is absurd.

- The #strong[median income], however, lines up the 11 patrons in
  ascending order. The 6th person in line still earns exactly
  #strong[\$2,000]! The median did not budge by a single penny: it is
  #strong[robust] against extreme outliers, whereas the mean is
  hyper-sensitive.
]

