=== 1. The Problem: Thomas's Nightmare in the Doctor's Office
<the-problem-thomass-nightmare-in-the-doctors-office>
Thomas is seventeen years old. To compete in high school athletics, his
physician administers a routine blood screening test for a rare but
dangerous congenital heart condition that affects #strong[1 in 1,000
individuals] across the general population.

Three days later, the phone rings. The doctor's tone is heavy:
#emph[“Thomas, your blood test came back positive.”]

Thomas feels the floor give way beneath him. With a trembling voice, he
asks: #emph[“Doctor, how reliable is this test?”] The physician consults
the laboratory specification sheet: #emph[“The test boasts an
exceptional reliability rating of #strong[99%]:] - #emph[If a person has
the condition, the test registers positive 99% of the time
(sensitivity).] - #emph[If a person is healthy, the test registers
negative 99% of the time (specificity).”]

Thomas slumps into his chair, overwhelmed: #emph[“The test is 99%
accurate. My test came back positive. Therefore, I have a 99% chance of
having this heart defect!”]

Is Thomas right to panic? The verdict of mathematics is clear, dramatic,
and will upend your intuition: #strong[NO! Thomas actually faces less
than a 10% chance of having the disease!] He has more than a #strong[90%
chance of being in perfect health]!

How can this be? How can a medical diagnostic tool with 99% laboratory
precision be wrong nine times out of ten when it announces a disease?

#divider()

=== 2. Narrowing the Universe: Conditional Probability
<narrowing-the-universe-conditional-probability>
To see through this paradox without dense symbolism, consider a
concrete, representative cohort of #strong[100,000 individuals]:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
TOTAL POPULATION
100,000 individuals
1 in 1,000 /                  \  999 in 1,000
DISEASED                  HEALTHY
100 individuals          99,900 individuals
99%    /            \ 1%    1%    /            \ 99%
POSITIVE       NEGATIVE    POSITIVE       NEGATIVE
99 diseased    1 diseased  999 healthy  98,901 healthy
    ```
  ]
]

Trace the arithmetic carefully: 1. Among 100,000 people, because the
condition strikes 1 in 1,000, there are #strong[100 diseased
individuals] and #strong[99,900 healthy individuals]. 2. Among the 100
diseased patients: the test detects 99% of them: there are #strong[99
positive tests] (and 1 false negative). 3. Now inspect the
#strong[99,900 healthy individuals]: The test is 99% specific, which
means it misclassifies 1% of them. 1% of 99,900 yields exactly
#strong[999 healthy individuals falsely flagged as positive] (false
positives)!

Now step into Thomas's shoes: The physician informed him that his test
is positive. Thomas's relevant reference frame has suddenly
#strong[shrunk]: Thomas is no longer an arbitrary member of the 100,000
general public; he belongs exclusively to the select club of individuals
who received a #strong[positive test result]!

How many total positive individuals exist in the clinic's waiting room?
$ upright("Total Positives") = 99 upright(" (diseased)") + 999 upright(" (healthy)") = upright(bold(1\,098 upright(" people"))) . $

And among these 1,098 individuals testing positive, how many actually
carry the disease? Only #strong[99 individuals]!

The probability that Thomas is genuinely ill given that his test is
positive is therefore:

$ P\(upright("Diseased") divides upright("Positive")\)= frac(99, 1\,098) approx upright(bold(0.0902)) = upright(bold(9.02 %)) . $

More than #strong[91% of those who test positive are completely
disease-free]! Why? Because the disease is so exceedingly rare that the
tiny 1% false-positive rate applied to the massive ocean of healthy
people creates ten times more false alarms than genuine cases!

