=== 5. Bayes' Theorem: Reasoning Backward from Effects to Causes
<bayes-theorem-reasoning-backward-from-effects-to-causes>
In 1763, the British Presbyterian minister and mathematician
#strong[Thomas Bayes] published a posthumous essay that reshaped the
philosophy of knowledge. He asked: #emph[“Given that an observed effect
has occurred (the test is positive), what is the probability that a
specific underlying cause generated it (the patient is truly
diseased)?”]

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Bayes' Theorem
]
 Let ${ A\,overline(A) }$
partition the sample space and let $B$ have non-zero probability.

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ P\(A divides B\)= frac(P\(A inter B\), P\(B\)) = frac(P\(A\)times P\(B divides A\), P\(A\)times P\(B divides A\)+ P\(overline(A)\)times P\(B divides overline(A)\)) $]) $
]

Applying Bayes' formula to Thomas's scenario:

- Underlying cause $M$: “has the condition”,
  $P\(M\)= 0.001 arrow.r.double.long P\(overline(M)\)= 0.999$.

- Observed effect $T$: “positive test result”.

  - Sensitivity: $P\(T divides M\)= 0.99$.

  - False positive rate: $P\(T divides overline(M)\)= 1 - 0.99 = 0.01$.

- Total probability of testing positive:
  $ P\(T\)= P\(M\)P\(T divides M\)+ P\(overline(M)\)P\(T divides overline(M)\)=\(0.001 times 0.99\)+\(0.999 times 0.01\) $
  $ = 0.00099 + 0.00999 = 0.01098 . $

- Posterior probability of disease given a positive test:
  $ P\(M divides T\)= frac(P\(M inter T\), P\(T\)) = 0.00099 / 0.01098 = 99 / 1098 approx upright(bold(9.02 %)) . $
  The algebraic formula matches our tabular calculation point for point!

#divider()

=== 6. Independence: Randomness Without Memory
<independence-randomness-without-memory>
When two events exert no causal or probabilistic influence on one
another, learning that one has occurred provides zero information about
the likelihood of the other.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Independent Events
]
 Two events $A$ and $B$ are
#strong[independent] if and only if:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ P\(A inter B\)= P\(A\)times P\(B\) $]) $

If $P\(A\)> 0$, this is equivalent to: $ P\(B divides A\)= P\(B\). $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution: The Ultimate Trap! --- Never Conflate “Independent”
and “Disjoint”
]
 This is the single most common conceptual error in
probability!

- Two events are #strong[disjoint] (mutually exclusive) if they cannot
  happen at the same time: $A inter B = diameter$, so
  $P\(A inter B\)= 0$.
- Two events are #strong[independent] if the occurrence of one does not
  alter the likelihood of the other:
  $P\(A inter B\)= P\(A\)times P\(B\)$.

Two non-impossible events that are disjoint are #strong[NEVER
independent]! On the contrary, they are maximally dependent: if $A$
occurs, you know with 100% certainty that $B$ did not occur
($P\(B divides A\)= 0 eq.not P\(B\)$)!
]

#divider()

=== 7. Discrete Random Variables
<discrete-random-variables>
Until now, random outcomes were qualitative categories: “heads”,
“tails”, “sick”, “red card”. A #strong[random variable] bridges
probability to arithmetic: it attaches a #strong[real number] (a
monetary gain, an arrival count, a waiting time) to every elementary
outcome.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Discrete Random Variable
]
 A #strong[discrete
random variable] $X$ defined on a sample space $Omega$ is a function
that maps every elementary outcome $omega in Omega$ to a real number
$x_i in bb(R)$. We write $X\(Omega\)= { x_1\,x_2\,dots.h\,x_k }$ for the
set of values $X$ can take.
]

==== 7.1 Probability Distribution
<probability-distribution>
Specifying the #strong[probability distribution] of $X$ means
determining the probability $P\(X = x_i\)$ for each possible value
$x_i$, presented as a distribution table:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 6,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$x_i$], [$x_1$], [$x_2$], [$dots.h$], [$x_k$], [#strong[Total]],),
    table.hline(),
    [$P\(X = x_i\)$], [$p_1$], [$p_2$], [$dots.h$], [$p_k$], [#strong[1]],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

The sum of all probabilities must strictly equal 1:
$sum_(i = 1)^k p_i = 1$.

==== 7.2 Expected Value: The Fair Price of a Game
<expected-value-the-fair-price-of-a-game>
Imagine a carnival lottery: what can you expect to win on average per
ticket if you play millions of games?

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Expected Value
]
 The #strong[expected value]
(or expectation) of a discrete random variable $X$, denoted $E\(X\)$, is
the weighted average of the values taken by $X$:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ E\(X\)= sum_(i = 1)^k x_i dot.op P\(X = x_i\) $]) $
]

- If $X$ represents a player's net profit (payout minus ticket cost):
  - $E\(X\)> 0$: the game is #strong[in the player's favor].

  - $E\(X\)< 0$: the game is #strong[unfavorable to the player] (the
    mathematical reality of all commercial casinos and state
    lotteries!).

  - $E\(X\)= 0$: the game is #strong[fair].

==== 7.3 Variance and Standard Deviation of a Random Variable
<variance-and-standard-deviation-of-a-random-variable>
Just as in Chapter 41 for descriptive statistics, we measure uncertainty
and dispersion around the mean:

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Variance and Standard Deviation
]


- The #strong[variance] of $X$ is:
  $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ V\(X\)= E #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(]\(X - E\(X\)\)^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = sum_(i = 1)^k\(x_i - E\(X\)\)^2 P\(X = x_i\) $]) $

- Probabilistic König-Huygens identity:
  $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ V\(X\)= E\(X^2\)- #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] E\(X\)#scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^2 $]) $

- The #strong[standard deviation] is:
  $ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ sigma\(X\)= sqrt(V\(X\)) $]) $
]

==== 7.4 Linearity of Expectation
<linearity-of-expectation>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Affine Transformation of a Random Variable
]
 For
all real numbers $a$ and $b$:

+ $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ E\(a X + b\)= a E\(X\)+ b $])$
  (Linearity of Expectation)
+ $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ V\(a X + b\)= a^2 V\(X\) $])$
+ $#box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ sigma\(a X + b\)=\|a\|sigma\(X\) $])$
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof of Linearity
]

$ E\(a X + b\)= sum_(i = 1)^k\(a x_i + b\)p_i = sum_(i = 1)^k a x_i p_i + sum_(i = 1)^k b p_i = a sum_(i = 1)^k x_i p_i + b sum_(i = 1)^k p_i . $
Since $sum x_i p_i = E\(X\)$ and $sum p_i = 1$:
$ E\(a X + b\)= a E\(X\)+ b\(1\)= a E\(X\)+ b . quad square.filled.medium $
]

