<odd-numbers-and-squares>
Take a handful of pebbles and arrange them in nested squares, just as
the Pythagoreans did in the sand:

#figure(image("figures/vol_6/fig_43_dominos_recurrence.svg", alt: "Le principe de récurrence : l’effet domino mathématique"),
  caption: [
    Le principe de récurrence : l'effet domino mathématique
  ]
)

Each new L-shaped angle of pebbles (known as a #strong[gnomon]) adds the
next odd number, and the square grows by one row and one column. We
conjecture:

$ 1 + 3 + 5 + dots.h +\(2 n - 1\)= n^2 . $

We verify for $n = 1\,2\,3\,4\,5$… it always works. But #strong[is it
true for every $n$]? For $n = 10^100$? No human will ever carry out the
full tally. The drawing convinces our eyes, but a visual diagram only
demonstrates a handful of cases.

==== 1.2 Euler's Prime-Generating Polynomial
<eulers-prime-generating-polynomial>
In 1772, Leonhard Euler noted the polynomial $p\(n\)= n^2 + n + 41$. Let
us compute:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 10,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$n$], [0], [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [10], [20], [39],),
    table.hline(),
    [$p\(n\)$], [41], [43], [47], [53], [61], [71], [151], [461], [1601],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Every single one of these numbers is #strong[prime]! We can check all 40
values from $n = 0$ to $n = 39$: not a single exception. Forty successes
in a row… A hasty thinker would conclude: “$n^2 + n + 41$ is always
prime.” And yet:

$ p\(40\)= 40^2 + 40 + 41 = 1600 + 81 = 1681 = 41 times 41 . $

It fails. And $p\(41\)= 41^2 + 41 + 41 = 41 times 43$ is not prime
either.

==== 1.3 Circles and Regions
<circles-and-regions>
Place $n$ points on the boundary of a circle, connect every pair of
points with a straight chord (choosing points in general position so
that no three chords intersect at the same interior point), and count
the number of disjoint regions carved out inside the disk:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 7,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Number of points $n$], [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6],),
    table.hline(),
    [Number of regions], [1], [2], [4], [8], [16], [#strong[31]],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Almost everyone wagers on 32 for $n = 6$: the progression of powers of 2
seems inescapable ($1\,2\,4\,8\,16 dots.h$). Yet drawing it with
scrupulous precision reveals only #strong[31] regions! The true formula
is $binom(n, 4) + binom(n, 2) + 1$, which has nothing to do with
$2^(n - 1)$ for $n gt.eq 6$.

==== 1.4 The Crisis
<the-crisis>
These three examples reveal a profound epistemological problem. In
mathematics, we seek statements that hold #strong[for all natural
numbers $n$]: an infinite collection of cases. However:

- Checking cases, even by millions, proves nothing (Euler, circle
  regions);
- A convincing diagram remains an illustration of a few particular cases
  (the gnomons).

We require an intellectual tool that #strong[captures the entirety of
the infinite sequence of integers in a single stroke]. That tool is
mathematical induction.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 Some conjectures hold out far longer. The
Pólya conjecture (1919), which asserted that “most” integers up to any
bound have an odd number of prime factors, was verified up to hundreds
of millions before a counterexample was found in 1958; the smallest
counterexample is $906 thin 150 thin 257$. No computer search could ever
have “proven” this conjecture by finite verification---and fortunately
so, since it was false.
]

#divider()

=== 2. The Core Idea: Dominoes and the Staircase
<the-core-idea-dominoes-and-the-staircase>
==== 2.1 The Dynamic Gesture
<the-dynamic-gesture>
Imagine an endless row of dominoes stood upright on end. What do you
need to be absolutely certain that they will #strong[all] fall?

+ #strong[That the first one falls]: you knock it over with your
  fingertip.
+ #strong[That every domino, if it falls, causes the next one to fall]:
  they are spaced closely enough together.

Note carefully: condition 2 does #strong[not] assert that the dominoes
are falling. It merely says: “#emph[if] this one falls, #emph[then] the
next one will fall as well.” It is an #strong[implication], a guarantee
of transmission.

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Push!
▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ...      Base step (initialization): the first one falls.
▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ...      Inductive step (heredity): each fall triggers the next.
▌ ▌ ▌ ▌ ...      Conclusion: they all fall.
    ```
  ]
]

If either condition is missing, the entire guarantee collapses:

- Without the initial push, dominoes may stand at perfect intervals
  forever; not one moves.
- If a single gap is too wide (say, between the 40th and the 41st), the
  wave of falling tiles grinds to an abrupt halt.

The endless staircase presents the exact same intuition: to climb
#strong[every] step of an infinite stairway, it suffices to be capable
of stepping onto the very first step, and to know how to step from any
arbitrary step up to the next.

==== 2.2 Mathematical Notation
<mathematical-notation>
Before formally stating the principle, let us sharpen our vocabulary.

- $bb(N)$ (read “the natural numbers”) $= { 0\,1\,2\,3\,dots.h }$. The
  blackboard bold font was popularized in the 20th century by the
  Nicolas Bourbaki collective; the letter N stands for #emph[natural].
- We denote by $cal(P)\(n\)$ (read “P of n”) a #strong[predicate or
  statement] that depends on the integer $n$: it is a mathematical
  #strong[assertion] that is either true or false depending on the value
  of $n$. For example, $cal(P)\(n\)$:
  “$1 + 3 + dots.h +\(2 n - 1\)= n^2$”. Warning: $cal(P)\(n\)$ is
  #strong[not a number]\; it is a logical proposition. You do not add it
  or multiply it: you declare it to be true or false.
- The symbol $arrow.r.double$ (read “implies”): $A arrow.r.double B$
  means “if $A$ is true, then $B$ is necessarily true.”
- $n_0$ denotes the #strong[starting rank (base index)], typically $0$
  or $1$, but occasionally larger.
- The universal quantifier $forall$ (read “for all” or “for every”) is
  an inverted letter A (for the German #emph[alle] or English
  #emph[all]); it was introduced by Gerhard Gentzen in 1935.

==== 2.3 The Principle of Mathematical Induction
<the-principle-of-mathematical-induction>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Principle of Mathematical Induction.
]
 Let
$cal(P)\(n\)$ be a mathematical proposition depending on a natural
number $n$, and let $n_0$ be a fixed integer. If: 1.
#strong[Initialization (Base Step)]: $cal(P)\(n_0\)$ is true; 2.
#strong[Heredity (Inductive Step)]: for every integer $n gt.eq n_0$, if
$cal(P)\(n\)$ is true, then $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true;

then #strong[\(Conclusion)] $cal(P)\(n\)$ is true for all integers
$n gt.eq n_0$.
]

The assumption “$cal(P)\(n\)$ is true”, invoked during the inductive
step, is termed the #strong[induction hypothesis] (frequently
abbreviated as #strong[IH]).

#strong[Why is this principle true?] It can be viewed as a foundational
axiom of the natural numbers: every natural number is generated by
starting from $0$ and repeatedly adding $1$. If a property is inherited
from each integer to the next, and is present at the starting point, no
integer can escape its grasp.

Alternatively, one may reason by contradiction via the
#strong[well-ordering principle] (every non-empty subset of natural
numbers contains a least element): suppose there existed integers
$n gt.eq n_0$ for which $cal(P)\(n\)$ is false. Let $m$ be the
#strong[smallest] such counterexample. By the base step, $m eq.not n_0$,
so $m > n_0$, which means $m - 1 gt.eq n_0$. Because $m$ is the smallest
counterexample, $cal(P)\(m - 1\)$ must be true. But then the inductive
step dictates that
$cal(P) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(]\(m - 1\)+ 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = cal(P)\(m\)$
is true: a contradiction. In 1889, Giuseppe Peano formalized this
principle as one of the foundational #strong[Peano Axioms] defining
arithmetic.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 Historical Note ---
]
 Early manifestations of inductive
reasoning appear in the work of the Persian mathematician
#strong[Al-Karaji] (around 1000 CE), who applied it to the binomial
theorem and sums of cubes, and later #strong[Francesco Maurolico], who
in 1575 proved that the sum of the first $n$ odd integers equals
$n^2$---our exact Pythagorean gnomon problem! However, #strong[Blaise
Pascal], in his #emph[Treatise on the Arithmetical Triangle] (1654), was
the first to give an explicit two-step formulation: proving a property
“for the first base,” and then demonstrating that “if it holds in any
arbitrary base, it will necessarily hold in the following one.” Augustus
De Morgan coined the term “mathematical induction” in 1838.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Pitfall Alert! ---
]
 In the inductive step, you do
#strong[not] assume that $cal(P)\(n\)$ is true for all $n$ (that would
be assuming the very thing you set out to prove!). Rather, you
#strong[fix] an arbitrary integer $n gt.eq n_0$, #strong[assume] that
$cal(P)\(n\)$ holds for that single, specific rank, and #strong[deduce]
that $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ must consequently hold. You are proving an
implication, not asserting unconditional truth.
]

#divider()

=== 3. Method: Writing an Impeccable Induction Proof
<method-writing-an-impeccable-induction-proof>
#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Standard Structure for Written Examinations.
]


#emph[For every integer $n gt.eq n_0$, let $cal(P)\(n\)$ denote the
statement: “\[…\]”.]

#strong[Base Step (Initialization).] For $n = n_0$: evaluate the
left-hand and right-hand sides independently and confirm that
$cal(P)\(n_0\)$ is true.

#strong[Inductive Step (Heredity).] Let $n gt.eq n_0$ be a fixed
integer. Assume that $cal(P)\(n\)$ is true (Induction Hypothesis). We
wish to show that $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true, that is: “\[…\]”
(explicitly write out the target statement at rank $n + 1$: this is
#strong[the goal]). \[Perform algebraic calculations starting from rank
$n + 1$, strategically injecting the Induction Hypothesis and explicitly
stating where it is used.\] Therefore, $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#strong[Conclusion.] The property is true for $n = n_0$ and is inherited
from rank $n$ to $n + 1$ for all $n gt.eq n_0$\; by the principle of
mathematical induction, $cal(P)\(n\)$ is true for every integer
$n gt.eq n_0$.
]

Three golden rules from exam graders: 1. #strong[Always write down the
goal] (the statement at rank $n + 1$) before computing: know exactly
where you are heading. 2. #strong[Start with the expression at rank
$n + 1$] and factor out or isolate the component corresponding to rank
$n$, so you can cleanly apply the induction hypothesis. 3.
#strong[Explicitly cite the induction hypothesis] (“by the induction
hypothesis, we have…”): this is the first thing a grader scans for. An
inductive step that never uses the induction hypothesis is almost
certainly invalid or circular.

#divider()

=== 4. Worked Examples
<worked-examples>
==== Worked Example 1 --- The Pythagorean Gnomons
<worked-example-1-the-pythagorean-gnomons>
#strong[Statement.] Prove that for all integers $n gt.eq 1$:
$1 + 3 + 5 + dots.h +\(2 n - 1\)= n^2$.

#strong[Notation.] We also write this sum as $sum_(k = 1)^n\(2 k - 1\)$.
The symbol $sum$ (capital Greek letter #strong[sigma], corresponding to
S for “sum”) was introduced by Euler in 1755. It reads: “the sum from
$k = 1$ to $n$ of $2 k - 1$”. The index $k$ is a #strong[dummy
variable]: it traverses $1\,2\,dots.h\,n$ and disappears from the final
result.

#strong[Solution.] For $n gt.eq 1$, let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be the
proposition: “$sum_(k = 1)^n\(2 k - 1\)= n^2$”.

#emph[Base Step.] For $n = 1$: the sum contains a single term,
$2\(1\)- 1 = 1$, and the right side gives $1^2 = 1$. Hence $cal(P)\(1\)$
is true.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Let $n gt.eq 1$ be fixed; assume $cal(P)\(n\)$ is
true. #strong[Goal:] Prove that
$sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1)\(2 k - 1\)=\(n + 1\)^2$. Decomposing the sum:
$ sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1)\(2 k - 1\)= underbrace(sum_(k = 1)^n\(2 k - 1\), = n^2 upright(" by IH")) + #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 2\(n + 1\)- 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = n^2 + 2 n + 1 =\(n + 1\)^2 . $
Thus $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] By mathematical induction, $cal(P)\(n\)$ is true for
all integers $n gt.eq 1$. $square.filled.medium$

Remark: The inductive step mirrors #strong[exactly] the geometric
gnomon: adding an L-shaped band of $2 n + 1$ pebbles transforms an
$n times n$ square into an $\(n + 1\)times\(n + 1\)$ square. Induction
is the algebraic rigor of the visual proof.

==== Worked Example 2 --- Gauss's Sum
<worked-example-2-gausss-sum>
#strong[Statement.] Prove that for all $n gt.eq 1$:
$1 + 2 + dots.h + n = frac(n\(n + 1\), 2)$.

#strong[Solution.] Let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be:
“$sum_(k = 1)^n k = frac(n\(n + 1\), 2)$”.

#emph[Base Step.] For $n = 1$: left-hand side $= 1$\; right-hand side
$= frac(1\(2\), 2) = 1$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume $cal(P)\(n\)$ holds for a fixed
$n gt.eq 1$. #strong[Goal:]
$sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1) k = frac(\(n + 1\)\(n + 2\), 2)$.
$ sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1) k = sum_(k = 1)^n k +\(n + 1\)= frac(n\(n + 1\), 2) +\(n + 1\)= frac(n\(n + 1\)+ 2\(n + 1\), 2) = frac(\(n + 1\)\(n + 2\), 2) . $
We factored out $\(n + 1\)$, instantly yielding the required form. Thus
$cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] $cal(P)\(n\)$ holds for all $n gt.eq 1$.
$square.filled.medium$

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did You Know?
]
 The young Carl Friedrich Gauss famously
evaluated this sum for $n = 100$ in school by pairing terms:
$\(1 + 100\)+\(2 + 99\)+ dots.h$, giving 50 pairs of 101, or 5050.
Gauss's insight #strong[discovers] the formula; mathematical induction
#strong[verifies] it. The two approaches are complementary: induction is
formidable at proving a formula once known, but it cannot invent it out
of thin air.
]

==== Worked Example 3 --- Sum of Squares
<worked-example-3-sum-of-squares>
#strong[Statement.] Prove that for all $n gt.eq 1$:
$sum_(k = 1)^n k^2 = frac(n\(n + 1\)\(2 n + 1\), 6)$.

#strong[Solution.] #emph[Base Step.] For $n = 1$: $1^2 = 1$ and
$frac(1\(2\)\(3\), 6) = 1$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume the formula holds at rank $n$.
#strong[Goal:]
$sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1) k^2 = frac(\(n + 1\)\(n + 2\)\(2 n + 3\), 6)$.
$ sum_(k = 1)^(n + 1) k^2 = frac(n\(n + 1\)\(2 n + 1\), 6) +\(n + 1\)^2 = frac(\(n + 1\)#scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] n\(2 n + 1\)+ 6\(n + 1\)#scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]], 6) = frac(\(n + 1\)\(2 n^2 + 7 n + 6\), 6) . $
Expanding the target bracket: $\(n + 2\)\(2 n + 3\)= 2 n^2 + 7 n + 6$.
Hence the sum is indeed $frac(\(n + 1\)\(n + 2\)\(2 n + 3\), 6)$.
Therefore $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] The formula holds for all $n gt.eq 1$.
$square.filled.medium$

#strong[Numerical sanity check]: for $n = 3$, $1 + 4 + 9 = 14$, and
$frac(3\(4\)\(7\), 6) = 14$. ✓

==== Worked Example 4 --- A Sequence Defined by Recurrence
<worked-example-4-a-sequence-defined-by-recurrence>
#strong[Statement.] Let $\(u_n\)$ be the sequence defined by $u_0 = 1$
and, for all $n in bb(N)$, $u_(n + 1) = 2 u_n + 3$. Compute the first
few terms, conjecture an explicit closed formula, and prove it by
induction.

#strong[Notation Reminder:] $\(u_n\)$ denotes the entire sequence, while
$u_n$ is the $n$-th term. The relation $u_(n + 1) = 2 u_n + 3$ is a
#strong[recurrence relation]: each term is generated from the preceding
one.

#strong[Investigation.] $u_0 = 1$, $u_1 = 5$, $u_2 = 13$, $u_3 = 29$,
$u_4 = 61$. Notice that $u_n + 3$ yields $4\,8\,16\,32\,64$, which are
powers of 2: $4 times 2^n$. Conjecture:
$u_n = 4 times 2^n - 3 = 2^(n + 2) - 3$.

#strong[Proof.] Let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be: “$u_n = 2^(n + 2) - 3$”.

#emph[Base Step.] $n = 0$: $2^(0 + 2) - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1 = u_0$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume $u_n = 2^(n + 2) - 3$ for a fixed
$n gt.eq 0$. #strong[Goal:] $u_(n + 1) = 2^(n + 3) - 3$.
$ u_(n + 1) = 2 u_n + 3 = 2 (2^(n + 2) - 3) + 3 = 2^(n + 3) - 6 + 3 = 2^(n + 3) - 3 . $
Thus $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n in bb(N)$, $u_n = 2^(n + 2) - 3$.
$square.filled.medium$

==== Worked Example 5 --- Divisibility
<worked-example-5-divisibility>
#strong[Statement.] Prove that for all $n in bb(N)$, $4^n - 1$ is
divisible by 3.

#strong[Definition Reminder.] “$a$ is divisible by 3” means there exists
an integer $k in bb(Z)$ such that $a = 3 k$.

#strong[Solution.] Let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be:
“$exists k in bb(Z)\,thin 4^n - 1 = 3 k$”.

#emph[Base Step.] For $n = 0$: $4^0 - 1 = 1 - 1 = 0 = 3 times 0$. True
with $k = 0$.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume there exists an integer $k$ such that
$4^n - 1 = 3 k$, so $4^n = 3 k + 1$. #strong[Goal:] Prove that
$4^(n + 1) - 1$ is a multiple of 3.
$ 4^(n + 1) - 1 = 4 times 4^n - 1 = 4\(3 k + 1\)- 1 = 12 k + 3 = 3\(4 k + 1\). $
Since $k$ is an integer, $4 k + 1$ is an integer. Thus $4^(n + 1) - 1$
is divisible by 3. $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n in bb(N)$, $3$ divides $4^n - 1$.
$square.filled.medium$

The key technique is to #strong[rephrase the induction hypothesis as an
equality] ($4^n = 3 k + 1$) so that it can be directly substituted into
the expression at rank $n + 1$.

==== Worked Example 6 --- Bernoulli's Inequality (Core Curriculum Proof)
<worked-example-6-bernoullis-inequality-core-curriculum-proof>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Bernoulli's Inequality.
]
 For every real number
$a > - 1$ (and in particular for all $a > 0$) and for every natural
number $n in bb(N)$: $ \(1 + a\)^n gt.eq 1 + n a . $
]

#strong[Concrete Intuition.] Invest a capital at a compound interest
rate $a$ per year (e.g., $a = 0.05$ for $5 %$): after $n$ years, the
capital has multiplied by $\(1 + a\)^n$. With simple interest, it would
multiply by $1 + n a$. Bernoulli's inequality asserts that
#strong[compound interest always earns at least as much as simple
interest]---which is intuitive, because you earn interest on top of
accumulated interest.

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof.
]
 Let $a > - 1$ be a fixed real number. For
$n in bb(N)$, let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be: “$\(1 + a\)^n gt.eq 1 + n a$”.

#emph[Base Step.] For $n = 0$: $\(1 + a\)^0 = 1$ and
$1 + 0 times a = 1$. Since $1 gt.eq 1$, $cal(P)\(0\)$ is true.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Let $n in bb(N)$ be fixed; assume
$\(1 + a\)^n gt.eq 1 + n a$. #strong[Goal:]
$\(1 + a\)^(n + 1) gt.eq 1 +\(n + 1\)a$. We multiply both sides of the
induction hypothesis by $1 + a$. Since $a > - 1$, we have $1 + a > 0$,
so the inequality direction is #strong[preserved]:
$ \(1 + a\)^(n + 1) =\(1 + a\)^n\(1 + a\)gt.eq\(1 + n a\)\(1 + a\)= 1 + a + n a + n a^2 = 1 +\(n + 1\)a + n a^2 . $
Since $n gt.eq 0$ and $a^2 gt.eq 0$, the quantity $n a^2$ is
non-negative: $n a^2 gt.eq 0$. Therefore,
$1 +\(n + 1\)a + n a^2 gt.eq 1 +\(n + 1\)a$. By transitivity,
$\(1 + a\)^(n + 1) gt.eq 1 +\(n + 1\)a$. Hence $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is
true.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n in bb(N)$, $\(1 + a\)^n gt.eq 1 + n a$.
$square.filled.medium$
]

The little black square $square.filled.medium$ (or Q.E.D., #emph[quod
erat demonstrandum], “which was to be demonstrated”) marks the
conclusion of a proof; it is often called the Halmos tombstone after
Paul Halmos.

#strong[Where did we use the hypothesis $a > - 1$?] Exactly in one
crucial step: multiplying an inequality by $1 + a$ without flipping its
direction. If $1 + a$ were negative, the inequality sign would reverse
and the argument would collapse. For instance, with $a = - 3$ and
$n = 5$: $\(1 - 3\)^5 =\(- 2\)^5 = - 32$, whereas $1 + 5\(- 3\)= - 14$\;
here $- 32 < - 14$, so the inequality is blatantly #strong[false]. In
mathematics, conditions in a theorem are never merely decorative.

#strong[Why is this inequality indispensable?] Because it bounds an
exponential growth $\(1 + a\)^n$ from below by an easily managed
#strong[affine linear] function $1 + n a$. In Chapter 44, it will allow
us to prove that if $q > 1$, then $q^n arrow.r + oo$: setting
$q = 1 + a$ with $a > 0$, we have $q^n gt.eq 1 + n a$, which outgrows
any chosen bound.

==== Worked Example 7 --- An Inequality Starting at a Higher Rank
<worked-example-7-an-inequality-starting-at-a-higher-rank>
#strong[Statement.] Prove that for all integers $n gt.eq 4$:
$2^n gt.eq n^2$.

#strong[Exploration.]

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 8,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([$n$], [0], [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6],),
    table.hline(),
    [$2^n$], [1], [2], [4], [8], [16], [32], [64],
    [$n^2$], [0], [1], [4], [#strong[9]], [16], [25], [36],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

The inequality holds for $n = 0\,1\,2$, but #strong[fails at $n = 3$]
($8 < 9$). It then holds for all $n gt.eq 4$. The starting rank
$n_0 = 4$ is strictly necessary!

#strong[Solution.] #emph[Base Step.] For $n = 4$:
$2^4 = 16 gt.eq 4^2 = 16$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Let $n gt.eq 4$\; assume $2^n gt.eq n^2$.
#strong[Goal:] $2^(n + 1) gt.eq\(n + 1\)^2$. By the induction
hypothesis, $2^(n + 1) = 2 times 2^n gt.eq 2 n^2$. It now suffices to
prove that $2 n^2 gt.eq\(n + 1\)^2$. Let us examine the difference:
$ 2 n^2 -\(n + 1\)^2 = 2 n^2 -\(n^2 + 2 n + 1\)= n^2 - 2 n - 1 =\(n - 1\)^2 - 2 . $
Since $n gt.eq 4$, we have $n - 1 gt.eq 3$, so $\(n - 1\)^2 gt.eq 9$.
Therefore $\(n - 1\)^2 - 2 gt.eq 9 - 2 = 7 > 0$. Hence
$2 n^2 gt.eq\(n + 1\)^2$, which gives
$2^(n + 1) gt.eq 2 n^2 gt.eq\(n + 1\)^2$. Thus $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is
true.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n gt.eq 4$, $2^n gt.eq n^2$.
$square.filled.medium$

==== Worked Example 8 --- A Monotone and Bounded Sequence
<worked-example-8-a-monotone-and-bounded-sequence>
#strong[Statement.] Let $\(u_n\)$ be defined by $u_0 = 1$ and
$u_(n + 1) = sqrt(2 + u_n)$. Prove that for all $n in bb(N)$:
$0 lt.eq u_n lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq 2$.

#strong[Strategy.] Define $f\(x\)= sqrt(2 + x)$ on $\[- 2\,+ oo\[$, so
that $u_(n + 1) = f\(u_n\)$. The function $f$ is strictly
#strong[increasing] (as the composite of two increasing functions:
$x mapsto 2 + x$ and the square root). An increasing function
#strong[preserves inequalities]: if $a lt.eq b$, then
$f\(a\)lt.eq f\(b\)$. This is the decisive tool.

#strong[Solution.] Let $cal(P)\(n\)$ be:
“$0 lt.eq u_n lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq 2$”.

#emph[Base Step.] For $n = 0$: $u_0 = 1$ and
$u_1 = sqrt(2 + 1) = sqrt(3) approx 1.732$. We clearly have
$0 lt.eq 1 lt.eq sqrt(3) lt.eq 2$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume $0 lt.eq u_n lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq 2$.
Since $f$ is increasing on $\[0\,2\]$, applying $f$ across the
inequalities preserves their order:
$ f\(0\)lt.eq f\(u_n\)lt.eq f\(u_(n + 1)\)lt.eq f\(2\)arrow.l.r.double sqrt(2) lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq u_(n + 2) lt.eq sqrt(4) = 2 . $
Since $0 lt.eq sqrt(2)$, we obtain
$0 lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq u_(n + 2) lt.eq 2$, which is precisely
$cal(P)\(n + 1\)$.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n in bb(N)$,
$0 lt.eq u_n lt.eq u_(n + 1) lt.eq 2$. The sequence $\(u_n\)$ is
therefore #strong[increasing] and #strong[bounded above by 2].
$square.filled.medium$

This technique---applying an increasing function to an entire chained
inequality---is one of the most effective and elegant methods tested at
the Grade 12 level.

==== Worked Example 9 --- Higher-Order Derivatives
<worked-example-9-higher-order-derivatives>
#strong[Statement.] Let $f\(x\)= x e^x$ on $bb(R)$. We denote by
$f^(\(n\))$ the #strong[$n$-th derivative] of $f$ ($f^(\(0\)) = f$,
$f^(\(1\)) = f'$, $f^(\(2\)) = f''$). Prove that for all $n in bb(N)$:
$f^(\(n\))\(x\)=\(x + n\)e^x$.

#strong[Notation Note:] Parentheses in $f^(\(n\))$ are vital to
distinguish the $n$-th derivative from the $n$-th algebraic power
$f\(x\)^n$.

#strong[Solution.] #emph[Base Step.] For $n = 0$:
$f^(\(0\))\(x\)= f\(x\)= x e^x =\(x + 0\)e^x$. True.

#emph[Inductive Step.] Assume $f^(\(n\))\(x\)=\(x + n\)e^x$ for all
$x in bb(R)$. The $\(n + 1\)$-th derivative is the derivative of the
$n$-th: $f^(\(n + 1\)) =\(f^(\(n\))\)'$. Applying the product rule
$\(u v\)' = u' v + u v'$ with $u\(x\)= x + n$ and $v\(x\)= e^x$:
$ f^(\(n + 1\))\(x\)= 1 dot.op e^x +\(x + n\)e^x =\(x + n + 1\)e^x . $
Thus $cal(P)\(n + 1\)$ is true.

#emph[Conclusion.] For all $n in bb(N)$, $f^(\(n\))\(x\)=\(x + n\)e^x$.
$square.filled.medium$

