==== Worked Example 1 --- Using the Definition with an Interval
<worked-example-1-using-the-definition-with-an-interval>
#strong[Problem:] Consider the sequence $\(u_n\)_(n gt.eq 1)$ defined by
$u_n = 3 + 2 / n$. 1. What is the conjectured limit $L$ of the sequence?
\2. Determine a rank $N$ from which all terms $u_n$ belong to the open
interval $\]2.99 thin\;thin 3.01\[$.

#strong[Solution:] 1. Since $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) 1 / n = 0$, we have
$lim_(n arrow.r + oo) u_n = 3 + 2 times 0 = 3$. The limit is $L = 3$. 2.
The interval $\]2.99 thin\;thin 3.01\[$ is written
$\]3 - 0.01 thin\;thin 3 + 0.01\[$. We seek the values of $n$ for which
$u_n in\]2.99 thin\;thin 3.01\[$: $ 2.99 < 3 + 2 / n < 3.01 . $ Since
$2 / n > 0$ for all $n gt.eq 1$, the left inequality $2.99 < 3 + 2 / n$
is always true. It remains to satisfy the right inequality:
$ 3 + 2 / n < 3.01 arrow.l.r.double 2 / n < 0.01 arrow.l.r.double 2 / n < 1 / 100 arrow.l.r.double n > 200 . $
Since $n$ is an integer, starting from rank $N = 201$, all terms $u_n$
for $n gt.eq 201$ are guaranteed to lie within the interval
$\]2.99 thin\;thin 3.01\[$.

