==== Worked Example 2 --- Resolving a Rational Indeterminacy $oo / oo$
<worked-example-2-resolving-a-rational-indeterminacy-fracinftyinfty>
#strong[Problem:] Determine the limit of the sequence $\(u_n\)$ defined
for all $n gt.eq 1$ by: $ u_n = frac(5 n^2 - 3 n + 2, 2 n^2 + 7) . $

#strong[Solution:] - Numerator: $lim\(5 n^2 - 3 n + 2\)= + oo$. -
Denominator: $lim\(2 n^2 + 7\)= + oo$.

This is an Indeterminate Form of the type “$oo / oo$”. #emph[Method:
factor out the dominant term ($n^2$) from both numerator and
denominator:]
$ u_n = frac(n^2 (5 - 3 / n + 2 / n^2), n^2 (2 + 7 / n^2)) . $ For
$n gt.eq 1$, cancel out $n^2 eq.not 0$:
$ u_n = frac(5 - 3 / n + 2 / n^2, 2 + 7 / n^2) . $ Now: $lim 3 / n = 0$,
$lim 2 / n^2 = 0$, so $lim (5 - 3 / n + 2 / n^2) = 5$.
$lim 7 / n^2 = 0$, so $lim (2 + 7 / n^2) = 2$. By the quotient rule for
limits, we conclude irrefutably: $ lim_(n arrow.r + oo) u_n = 5 / 2 . $

#divider()

==== Worked Example 3 --- Squeeze Theorem and Trigonometry
<worked-example-3-squeeze-theorem-and-trigonometry>
#strong[Problem:] Determine the limit of the sequence
$\(v_n\)_(n gt.eq 1)$ defined by:
$ v_n = frac(3 +\(- 1\)^n cos\(n\), n + sqrt(n)) . $

#strong[Solution:] The functions $cos\(n\)$ and $\(- 1\)^n$ oscillate
endlessly and have no limit as $n arrow.r + oo$. It is impossible to
apply direct algebraic rules. We immediately turn to the #strong[squeeze
theorem]. For any integer $n$, $- 1 lt.eq cos\(n\)lt.eq 1$ and
$\(- 1\)^n in { - 1\;1 }$. Thus, the product $\(- 1\)^n cos\(n\)$
remains bounded between $- 1$ and $1$:
$ - 1 lt.eq\(- 1\)^n cos\(n\)lt.eq 1 . $ Add 3 to each part:
$ 3 - 1 lt.eq 3 +\(- 1\)^n cos\(n\)lt.eq 3 + 1 arrow.l.r.double 2 lt.eq 3 +\(- 1\)^n cos\(n\)lt.eq 4 . $
Since for all $n gt.eq 1$, the denominator $n + sqrt(n)$ is strictly
positive, we can divide without changing the direction of the
inequalities:
$ frac(2, n + sqrt(n)) lt.eq v_n lt.eq frac(4, n + sqrt(n)) . $ Examine
the limits of both bounding sequences:

- $lim\(n + sqrt(n)\)= + oo$, so
  $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) frac(2, n + sqrt(n)) = 0$.
- Likewise, $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) frac(4, n + sqrt(n)) = 0$.

Both bounding sequences converge to the same limit $0$. By the
#strong[squeeze theorem], the sequence $\(v_n\)$ converges and:
$ lim_(n arrow.r + oo) v_n = 0 . $

