==== Worked Example 4 --- Geometric Sum and Convergence
<worked-example-4-geometric-sum-and-convergence>
#strong[Problem:] Consider the sequence $\(S_n\)$ defined for any
integer $n gt.eq 1$ by:
$ S_n = sum_(k = 1)^n (2 / 3)^k = 2 / 3 + (2 / 3)^2 + dots.h + (2 / 3)^n . $
\1. Express $S_n$ in closed form without summation notation. 2.
Determine the limit of $S_n$ as $n arrow.r + oo$.

#strong[Solution:] 1. $S_n$ is the sum of the first $n$ consecutive
terms of a geometric sequence with first term $a = 2 / 3$ and common
ratio $q = 2 / 3$. The geometric sum formula gives (with $q eq.not 1$):
$ upright("Sum") = upright("First term") times frac(1 - q^(upright("number of terms")), 1 - q) . $
Here:
$ S_n = 2 / 3 times frac(1 - (2 / 3)^n, 1 - 2 / 3) = 2 / 3 times frac(1 - (2 / 3)^n, 1 / 3) = 2 / 3 times 3 times (1 - (2 / 3)^n) = 2 (1 - (2 / 3)^n) . $
\2. Since $- 1 < 2 / 3 < 1$, according to the theorem on geometric
sequences: $ lim_(n arrow.r + oo) (2 / 3)^n = 0 . $ By limit operations:
$ lim_(n arrow.r + oo) S_n = 2\(1 - 0\)= 2 . $

