=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-11>
Until now, our counter $n$ was a natural number taking step-by-step
jumps: $n = 0\,1\,2\,3 . . .$ But what happens if the variable is no
longer a runner with discrete steps, but a bird flying seamlessly
through the sky, or a rocket accelerating at every microsecond? How can
we define the limit of a function $f\(x\)$ when the real variable $x$
glides continuously toward infinity, or worse, when it approaches a
point where the function seems to tear apart or explode?

This is the mystery of function limits and the invisible lines that
guide curves through space: #strong[asymptotes]. See you in Chapter 45.

