=== 1. The Problem: The Optical Lens and the Black Hole
<the-problem-the-optical-lens-and-the-black-hole>
==== 1.1 The Physicist and Focal Length
<the-physicist-and-focal-length>
In an optics laboratory, a light ray passes through a thin converging
lens of focal length $f > 0$. A luminous object is placed at a distance
$p$ in front of the lens. The Descartes-Gauss thin lens formula relates
the object distance $p$ to the position of the sharp image $p'$ formed
on a screen behind the lens:

$ 1 / p + 1 / p' = 1 / f . $

Let us express the image position $p'$ as a function of the object
distance $p$:

$ p'\(p\)= frac(f dot.op p, p - f) . $

What happens as the experimenter moves the object closer to the lens?

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Object                     Lens                     Screen
p | p' |
Light source]               (Focus F)
    ```
  ]
]

+ #strong[If the object is infinitely far away ($p arrow.r + oo$)]:
  $ p'\(p\)= frac(f dot.op p, p\(1 - f\/p\)) = frac(f, 1 - f\/p) arrow.r_(p arrow.r + oo)^() f . $
  The image forms precisely in the focal plane of the lens. The
  horizontal line $y = f$ is a #strong[limiting boundary], a ceiling
  that the image position cannot cross: it is a #strong[horizontal
  asymptote].

+ #strong[If the object approaches the focal point ($p arrow.r f$ with
  $p > f$)]: The denominator $p - f$ becomes an infinitely small
  positive number ($0.001$, $0.000001$…). The quotient $p'\(p\)$
  explodes to astronomical values:
  $ lim_(p arrow.r f^(+)) p'\(p\)= + oo . $ To view the sharp image, one
  would need to push the screen to the end of the room, then outside the
  building, then to the Moon! The emerging rays are parallel: the image
  is cast to infinity. The vertical line $x = f$ acts like an impassable
  cliff: it is a #strong[vertical asymptote].

==== 1.2 From Discrete Steps to Continuous Flux
<from-discrete-steps-to-continuous-flux>
In Chapter 44, the variable $n$ jumped from integer to integer:
$n = 0\,1\,2\,3 dots.h$ It made no sense to ask what happened between
$n = 2$ and $n = 3$, and one could only take the limit of $n$ toward
$+ oo$.

With functions of a real variable $x mapsto f\(x\)$, the world becomes
#strong[fluid and continuous]. The real variable $x$ can:

- flee toward $+ oo$ or toward $- oo$\;
- glide toward a fixed real number $x_0$ (for example $x arrow.r 2$);
- approach a forbidden point from the left ($x < x_0$) or from the right
  ($x > x_0$).

