=== 3. Asymptotes: The Geometric Guide of Curves
<asymptotes-the-geometric-guide-of-curves>
An asymptote is a line that the curve $cal(C)_f$ approaches arbitrarily
closely as points on the curve recede toward infinity.

#figure(image("figures/vol_6/fig_45_asymptotes_complete.svg", alt: "Comportement asymptotique aux bornes : asymptotes verticales et horizontales"),
  caption: [
    Comportement asymptotique aux bornes : asymptotes verticales et
    horizontales
  ]
)

==== 3.1 Horizontal Asymptote
<horizontal-asymptote>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Horizontal asymptote.
]
 If
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= L$ (or if
$lim_(x arrow.r - oo) f\(x\)= L$), where $L$ is a finite real number,
then the line $cal(D)$ with equation #strong[$y = L$] is called a
#strong[horizontal asymptote] to the curve $cal(C)_f$ in the
neighborhood of $+ oo$ (or $- oo$).
]

==== 3.2 Vertical Asymptote
<vertical-asymptote>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Vertical asymptote.
]
 If
$lim_(x arrow.r a^(+)) f\(x\)= plus.minus oo$ or if
$lim_(x arrow.r a^(-)) f\(x\)= plus.minus oo$ (where $a in bb(R)$), then
the line $Delta$ with equation #strong[$x = a$] is called a
#strong[vertical asymptote] to the curve $cal(C)_f$.
]

==== 3.3 Oblique Asymptote
<oblique-asymptote>
Sometimes the curve does not level off horizontally, but aligns itself
along a slanted line with equation $y = a x + b$ with $a eq.not 0$.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Oblique asymptote.
]
 The line $cal(D)$ with
equation #strong[$y = a x + b$] is an oblique asymptote to the curve
$cal(C)_f$ at $+ oo$ (resp. $- oo$) if:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] f\(x\)-\(a x + b\)#scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] = 0 quad upright("(resp. at ") - oo upright(")") . $
]

#emph[Geometric sense:] The vertical distance between the point on the
curve $M\(x\;f\(x\)\)$ and the point on the line $P\(x\;a x + b\)$
becomes infinitely small as one advances toward the ends of the
coordinate plane.

==== 3.4 Determining Relative Position
<determining-relative-position>
To determine whether the curve $cal(C)_f$ lies #strong[above] or
#strong[below] its asymptote (horizontal $y = L$ or oblique
$y = a x + b$), one applies the following universal method:

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Relative position of a curve and an asymptote:
]
 1.
Compute the difference: $d\(x\)= f\(x\)- y_(upright("asymptote"))$. 2.
Rigorously study the #strong[sign] of $d\(x\)$:

- If $d\(x\)> 0$ on an interval, then $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly
  above] the asymptote on that interval.

- If $d\(x\)< 0$ on an interval, then $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly
  below] the asymptote.

- If $d\(x\)= 0$ at a point $x_1$, the curve #strong[intersects /
  crosses] its asymptote at the point of abscissa $x_1$.
]

#divider()

=== 4. The Toolkit for Resolving Indeterminate Forms
<the-toolkit-for-resolving-indeterminate-forms>
Recall the four forms that prohibit immediate direct conclusions:
$ upright("I.F.: ") quad\[oo - oo\]\,quad\[0 times oo\]\,quad [oo / oo]\,quad [0 / 0] . $

Here are the four fundamental techniques to master in Grade 12:

==== Method 1: Factoring Out the Dominant Term
<method-1-factoring-out-the-dominant-term>
At $+ oo$ or $- oo$, in a polynomial or a rational function, the
highest-degree term dictates the law to all others.

- For a polynomial: factor out the highest power of $x$.
- For a fraction: factor out the respective dominant powers from both
  numerator and denominator, then simplify.

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution, trap! --- The sign under the square root at $- oo$.
]

Always remember that for any real number $x$:
$ sqrt(x^2) =\|x\|= cases(delim: "{", x & upright("if ") x gt.eq 0, - x & upright("if ") x < 0) $
As $x arrow.r - oo$, $x$ is a #strong[strictly negative] number.
Consequently:
$ sqrt(x^2) = - x quad\(upright("and not ") + x upright(" !")\). $ This
is the single most heavily penalized error on Baccalaureate exams!
]

==== Method 2: The Conjugate Expression
<method-2-the-conjugate-expression>
When square roots clash in an indeterminacy of type $\[oo - oo\]$ or
$\[0\/0\]$, use the difference of squares identity
$\(a - b\)\(a + b\)= a^2 - b^2$ to remove radicals from the problematic
site:

$ sqrt(A) - sqrt(B) = frac(\(sqrt(A) - sqrt(B)\)\(sqrt(A) + sqrt(B)\), sqrt(A) + sqrt(B)) = frac(A - B, sqrt(A) + sqrt(B)) . $

The delicate subtraction $\[oo - oo\]$ turns into a well-behaved
addition in the denominator $\[oo + oo = oo\]$.

==== Method 3: Recognizing a Difference Quotient (Derivative)
<method-3-recognizing-a-difference-quotient-derivative>
An indeterminate form of type $[0 / 0]$ at a point $x_0$ very often
conceals the very definition of the derivative:
$ lim_(x arrow.r x_0) frac(g\(x\)- g\(x_0\), x - x_0) = g'\(x_0\). $

Famous examples:

- $lim_(x arrow.r 0) frac(e^x - 1, x)$: with $g\(x\)= e^x$, $g\(0\)= 1$,
  the limit is $g'\(0\)= e^0 = 1$.
- $lim_(x arrow.r 0) frac(sin x, x)$: with $g\(x\)= sin x$, $g\(0\)= 0$,
  the limit is $cos\(0\)= 1$.

#divider()

=== 5. Growth Hierarchy: Comparative Growth of the Exponential Function
<growth-hierarchy-comparative-growth-of-the-exponential-function>
When the exponential function confronts a polynomial or a power of $x$
at infinity, the explosive growth of $e^x$ infinitely surpasses any
polynomial growth.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Comparative growth of the exponential function.
]

For any natural number $n gt.eq 1$: 1.
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x = + oo$ $quad$ and more generally $quad$
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x^n = + oo$ 2.
$lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x thin e^x = 0$ $quad$ and more generally $quad$
$lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x^n thin e^x = 0$
]

#emph[Rule of thumb:] At $+ oo$ as well as at $- oo$, #strong[the
exponential always dominates any power of $x$].

==== Required Baccalaureate Proof: $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x = + oo$
<required-baccalaureate-proof-lim_x-to-infty-fracexx-infty>
#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof (Required knowledge).
]
 The goal is to obtain a lower
bound for $e^x$ by a degree-2 polynomial, so that the quotient $e^x / x$
is bounded below by a degree-1 linear term tending to $+ oo$.

#strong[Step 1: Lower bound for the exponential function.] Consider the
function $f$ defined on $\[0 thin\;thin + oo\[$ by:
$ f\(x\)= e^x - x^2 / 2 . $ Differentiate $f$ twice:

- Its first derivative is $f'\(x\)= e^x - x$.
- Its second derivative is $f''\(x\)= e^x - 1$.

For all $x gt.eq 0$, $e^x gt.eq e^0 = 1$, so $f''\(x\)gt.eq 0$.
Consequently, the derivative function $f'$ is strictly increasing on
$\[0 thin\;thin + oo\[$. Now $f'\(0\)= e^0 - 0 = 1 > 0$. Since $f'$ is
increasing and $f'\(0\)> 0$, for all $x gt.eq 0$ we have:
$ f'\(x\)gt.eq f'\(0\)> 0 . $ The function $f$ is therefore itself
strictly increasing on $\[0 thin\;thin + oo\[$. Since
$f\(0\)= e^0 - 0 = 1 > 0$, it follows that for all $x gt.eq 0$:
$ f\(x\)> 0 arrow.r.double.long e^x > x^2 / 2 . $

#strong[Step 2: Passing to the quotient and conclusion.] For any real
number $x > 0$, divide this inequality by $x$ (since $x > 0$, the
inequality direction is preserved): $ e^x / x > x / 2 . $ Now:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) x / 2 = + oo . $ By the #strong[comparison
theorem] for limits, we conclude irrefutably:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x = + oo . quad square.filled.medium $
]

#emph[Proof of $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x e^x = 0$ via change of variable:]
Set $X = - x$. As $x arrow.r - oo$, we have $X arrow.r + oo$. Then:
$ x thin e^x =\(- X\)thin e^(- X) = - X / e^X = - 1 / e^X / X . $ Since
$lim_(X arrow.r + oo) e^X / X = + oo$, by the reciprocal of an infinite
limit:
$ lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x thin e^x = lim_(X arrow.r + oo) (- frac(1, e^X\/X)) = 0 . quad square.filled.medium $

#divider()

=== 6. Limit of a Composite Function
<limit-of-a-composite-function>
A composite function is a cascade: the output of the first machine $f$
becomes the input of the second machine $g$. This operation is denoted
$\(g compose f\)\(x\)= g\(f\(x\)\)$.

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Limit of a composite function.
]
 Let $a\,b\,c$
denote either real numbers, $+ oo$, or $- oo$. If:
$ lim_(x arrow.r a) f\(x\)= b quad upright("and") quad lim_(y arrow.r b) g\(y\)= c\, $
then: $ lim_(x arrow.r a) g\(f\(x\)\)= c . $
]

#methode(title: [Méthode Pas à Pas])[
#strong[🧭 Method --- Writing out the limit of a composite on the Bac:
]

Never compute everything all at once. Clearly separate into two steps by
introducing a dummy variable substitution: 1. #emph[Inner step:]
calculate $lim_(x arrow.r a) f\(x\)= b$. 2. #emph[Outer step:] set
$X = f\(x\)$, and since $X arrow.r b$, calculate
$lim_(X arrow.r b) g\(X\)= c$. 3. #emph[Conclusion:] By composition of
limits, $lim_(x arrow.r a) g\(f\(x\)\)= c$.
]

#divider()

=== 7. Traps to Avoid!
<traps-to-avoid>
#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution, trap! --- Crossing an asymptote.
]
 A widespread
misconception among students is that a curve “is never allowed to touch
its asymptote”. This is #strong[true for a vertical asymptote] $x = a$
(because the function is generally undefined there). But it is
#strong[completely false for a horizontal or oblique asymptote]! A
horizontal asymptote $y = L$ describes solely what occurs #strong[at the
far edge of the world] (for $x arrow.r plus.minus oo$). In the meantime,
for moderate values of $x$, the curve may intersect the horizontal line
once, ten times, or even infinitely many times! #emph[Example:]
$f\(x\)= frac(sin x, x)$ has the $x$-axis $y = 0$ as a horizontal
asymptote, and crosses this axis at every integer multiple of $pi$
($pi\,2 pi\,3 pi dots.h$).
]

#quote_box[
#strong[⚠️ Caution, trap! --- Comparative growth applies only at
infinity!
]
 Never say: “$lim_(x arrow.r 0) e^x / x = + oo$ by comparative
growth”! At 0, the numerator tends to $e^0 = 1$ and the denominator to
\0. It is a direct quotient of a finite non-zero number by zero
($1\/0^(+)$ or $1\/0^(-)$), not comparative growth! Comparative growth
compares the escape velocity toward infinity.
]

#divider()

=== 8. Step-by-Step Worked Examples
<step-by-step-worked-examples-13>
==== Worked Example 1 --- Asymptotes and Resolving Indeterminacy at $+ oo$ and $- oo$
<worked-example-1-asymptotes-and-resolving-indeterminacy-at-infty-and--infty>
#strong[Problem:] Consider the function $f$ defined on $bb(R)\\{ 2 }$
by: $ f\(x\)= frac(3 x - 1, x - 2) . $ 1. Determine the limits of $f$ at
the boundaries of its domain of definition ($+ oo$, $- oo$, and at 2
from below and from above). 2. Deduce the equations of the asymptotes to
the graph $cal(C)_f$. 3. Study the relative position of $cal(C)_f$ with
respect to its horizontal asymptote.

#strong[Solution:] 1. #strong[Limits at infinity:] For $x eq.not 0$:
$ f\(x\)= frac(x (3 - 1 / x), x (1 - 2 / x)) = frac(3 - 1 / x, 1 - 2 / x) . $
Since $lim_(x arrow.r plus.minus oo) 1 / x = 0$ and
$lim_(x arrow.r plus.minus oo) 2 / x = 0$, we obtain:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= 3 quad upright("and") quad lim_(x arrow.r - oo) f\(x\)= 3 . $

#strong[Limits at 2:] Numerator:
$lim_(x arrow.r 2)\(3 x - 1\)= 3\(2\)- 1 = 5 > 0$. Denominator:
$lim_(x arrow.r 2)\(x - 2\)= 0$.

- If $x > 2$, $x - 2 > 0$, so the denominator tends to $0^(+)$. By
  quotient: $ lim_(x arrow.r 2^(+)) f\(x\)= + oo . $

- If $x < 2$, $x - 2 < 0$, the denominator tends to $0^(-)$. By
  quotient: $ lim_(x arrow.r 2^(-)) f\(x\)= - oo . $

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Asymptotes:]

  - Since $lim_(x arrow.r plus.minus oo) f\(x\)= 3$, the line with
    equation #strong[$y = 3$] is a horizontal asymptote to $cal(C)_f$ at
    $+ oo$ and at $- oo$.
  - Since $lim_(x arrow.r 2) f\(x\)= plus.minus oo$, the line with
    equation #strong[$x = 2$] is a vertical asymptote to $cal(C)_f$.

+ #strong[Relative position with respect to the horizontal asymptote
  $y = 3$:] Compute the difference $d\(x\)= f\(x\)- 3$:
  $ d\(x\)= frac(3 x - 1, x - 2) - 3 = frac(\(3 x - 1\)- 3\(x - 2\), x - 2) = frac(3 x - 1 - 3 x + 6, x - 2) = frac(5, x - 2) . $

  - For all $x in\]2 thin\;thin + oo\[$, $x - 2 > 0$, so $d\(x\)> 0$:
    the curve $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly above] the asymptote
    $y = 3$.
  - For all $x in\]- oo thin\;thin 2\[$, $x - 2 < 0$, so $d\(x\)< 0$:
    the curve $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly below] the asymptote
    $y = 3$.
]

#divider()

==== Worked Example 2 --- The Sign Trap for $sqrt(x^2)$ at $- oo$
<worked-example-2-the-sign-trap-for-sqrtx2-at--infty>
#strong[Problem:] Determine the limit at $- oo$ and at $+ oo$ of the
function: $ f\(x\)= sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) + x . $

#strong[Solution:] #strong[At $+ oo$:]
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo)\(x^2 + 4 x + 5\)= + oo$, so
$lim sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) = + oo$. Since $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) x = + oo$,
by addition:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= + oo quad\(upright("no indeterminate form here!")\). $

#strong[At $- oo$:] $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) = + oo$
and $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x = - oo$. This is an indeterminate form
$\[oo - oo\]$. Use the #strong[conjugate expression]:
$ f\(x\)= frac((sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) + x) (sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) - x), sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) - x) = frac(\(x^2 + 4 x + 5\)- x^2, sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) - x) = frac(4 x + 5, sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) - x) . $
Now factor out the dominant term in the denominator for $x < 0$:
$ sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) = sqrt(x^2 (1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2)) =\|x\|sqrt(1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2) . $
As $x arrow.r - oo$, we have $x < 0$, so $\|x\|= - x$!
$ sqrt(x^2 + 4 x + 5) - x = - x sqrt(1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2) - x = - x [sqrt(1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2) + 1] . $
The numerator is $4 x + 5 = x (4 + 5 / x)$. Thus, for $x < 0$:
$ f\(x\)= frac(x (4 + 5 / x), - x [sqrt(1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2) + 1]) = frac(4 + 5 / x, - (sqrt(1 + 4 / x + 5 / x^2) + 1)) . $
Take the limit as $x arrow.r - oo$:

- The numerator tends to $4 + 0 = 4$.
- The denominator tends to $-\(sqrt(1 + 0 + 0) + 1\)= -\(1 + 1\)= - 2$.

By quotient: $ lim_(x arrow.r - oo) f\(x\)= frac(4, - 2) = - 2 . $
#emph[Geometric consequence:] The line with equation $y = - 2$ is a
horizontal asymptote to the curve at $- oo$.

#divider()

==== Worked Example 3 --- Comparative Growth and Factoring Exponentials
<worked-example-3-comparative-growth-and-factoring-exponentials>
#strong[Problem:] Determine the limit at $+ oo$ and at $- oo$ of the
function $f\(x\)=\(2 x - 3\)e^(- x) + 1$.

#strong[Solution:] #strong[At $+ oo$:] Write
$f\(x\)= 2 x e^(- x) - 3 e^(- x) + 1 = frac(2 x, e^x) - 3 / e^x + 1 = 2 (1 / e^x / x) - 3 / e^x + 1$.
By comparative growth theorems, $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x = + oo$,
so $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) x / e^x = 0$. Moreover,
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x = + oo$, whence
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) 3 / e^x = 0$. By addition of limits:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= 2\(0\)- 0 + 1 = 1 . $ The line with
equation $y = 1$ is a horizontal asymptote at $+ oo$.

#strong[At $- oo$:] Set the substitution $X = - x$. As $x arrow.r - oo$,
$X arrow.r + oo$. Then:
$f\(x\)=\(- 2 X - 3\)e^X + 1 = - 2 X e^X - 3 e^X + 1 .$ As
$X arrow.r + oo$, $e^X arrow.r + oo$ and $- 2 X - 3 arrow.r - oo$. By
product:
$ lim_(x arrow.r - oo)\(2 x - 3\)= - oo quad upright("and") quad lim_(x arrow.r - oo) e^(- x) = + oo arrow.r.double.long lim_(x arrow.r - oo)\(2 x - 3\)e^(- x) = - oo . $
Hence: $ lim_(x arrow.r - oo) f\(x\)= - oo . $

#divider()

==== Worked Example 4 --- Limit of a Composite with Radical and Rational Functions
<worked-example-4-limit-of-a-composite-with-radical-and-rational-functions>
#strong[Problem:] Determine the limit as $x arrow.r + oo$ of:
$ h\(x\)= sqrt(frac(4 x^2 + 1, x^2 - 3 x + 2)) . $

#strong[Solution:] Decompose the function by setting
$f\(x\)= frac(4 x^2 + 1, x^2 - 3 x + 2)$ and $g\(X\)= sqrt(X)$, so that
$h\(x\)= g\(f\(x\)\)$. 1. #emph[Inner limit:] For $x > 0$:
$ f\(x\)= frac(x^2 (4 + 1 / x^2), x^2 (1 - 3 / x + 2 / x^2)) = frac(4 + 1 / x^2, 1 - 3 / x + 2 / x^2) . $
Since $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) 1 / x^2 = 0$,
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) 3 / x = 0$, and
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) 2 / x^2 = 0$, we have:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= 4 / 1 = 4 . $ 2. #emph[Outer limit:] Set
$X = f\(x\)$. As $X arrow.r 4$:
$ lim_(X arrow.r 4) g\(X\)= lim_(X arrow.r 4) sqrt(X) = sqrt(4) = 2 . $
\3. #emph[Conclusion:] By composition of limits:
$ lim_(x arrow.r + oo) h\(x\)= 2 . $ The line with equation $y = 2$ is a
horizontal asymptote to the curve of $h$ at $+ oo$.

#divider()

==== Worked Example 5 --- Complete Determination of an Oblique Asymptote
<worked-example-5-complete-determination-of-an-oblique-asymptote>
#strong[Problem:] Consider the function $f$ defined on $bb(R)\\{ 1 }$ by
$f\(x\)= frac(2 x^2 - x + 3, x - 1)$. 1. Determine real numbers
$a\,b\,c$ such that for all $x eq.not 1$:
$f\(x\)= a x + b + frac(c, x - 1)$. 2. Prove that the line $cal(D)$ with
equation $y = 2 x + 1$ is an oblique asymptote to the curve $cal(C)_f$
at $+ oo$ and $- oo$. 3. Specify the relative position of $cal(C)_f$
with respect to $cal(D)$.

#strong[Solution:] 1. Expand the desired form:
$ a x + b + frac(c, x - 1) = frac(\(a x + b\)\(x - 1\)+ c, x - 1) = frac(a x^2 - a x + b x - b + c, x - 1) = frac(a x^2 +\(b - a\)x +\(c - b\), x - 1) . $
Equating coefficients with $2 x^2 - x + 3$: $ {a = 2\
b - a = - 1 arrow.r.double.long b = a - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1\
c - b = 3 arrow.r.double.long c = 3 + b = 3 + 1 = 4 $ Thus for all
$x eq.not 1$: $ f\(x\)= 2 x + 1 + frac(4, x - 1) . $

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Compute the difference between $f\(x\)$ and $y = 2 x + 1$:
  $ f\(x\)-\(2 x + 1\)= frac(4, x - 1) . $ Now
  $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) frac(4, x - 1) = 0$ and
  $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) frac(4, x - 1) = 0$. By definition, the line
  $cal(D)$ with equation $y = 2 x + 1$ is an #strong[oblique asymptote]
  to the curve $cal(C)_f$ at $+ oo$ and at $- oo$.

+ #strong[Relative position:] The sign of $f\(x\)-\(2 x + 1\)$ is the
  sign of $frac(4, x - 1)$:

  - If $x > 1$, $x - 1 > 0 arrow.r.double.long frac(4, x - 1) > 0$:
    $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly above] $cal(D)$.
  - If $x < 1$, $x - 1 < 0 arrow.r.double.long frac(4, x - 1) < 0$:
    $cal(C)_f$ is #strong[strictly below] $cal(D)$.
]

#divider()

==== Worked Example 6 --- Form $0 / 0$ Resolved via Difference Quotient
<worked-example-6-form-frac00-resolved-via-difference-quotient>
#strong[Problem:] Determine without a calculator
$lim_(x arrow.r 0) frac(e^(3 x) - 1, x)$.

#strong[Solution:] At $x = 0$, $e^0 - 1 = 0$ and the denominator is 0.
This is an indeterminate form $\[0\/0\]$. Define the function
$g\(x\)= e^(3 x)$. The function $g$ is differentiable on $bb(R)$, and
its derivative is $g'\(x\)= 3 e^(3 x)$. Notice that $g\(0\)= e^0 = 1$.
The expression can be rewritten as:
$ frac(e^(3 x) - 1, x) = frac(g\(x\)- g\(0\), x - 0) . $ By definition
of the derivative:
$ lim_(x arrow.r 0) frac(g\(x\)- g\(0\), x - 0) = g'\(0\)= 3 e^(3 times 0) = 3 times 1 = 3 . $
The requested limit is therefore equal to $3$.

#divider()

=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-20>
+ #strong[Definition of limit at infinity:]
  $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) f\(x\)= L$ if every open interval containing $L$
  contains all values $f\(x\)$ for sufficiently large $x$.

+ #strong[Asymptotes:]

  - $lim_(x arrow.r plus.minus oo) f\(x\)= L arrow.l.r.double y = L$ is
    a horizontal asymptote.

  - $lim_(x arrow.r a^plus.minus) f\(x\)= plus.minus oo arrow.l.r.double x = a$
    is a vertical asymptote.

  - $lim_(x arrow.r plus.minus oo)\[f\(x\)-\(a x + b\)\]= 0 arrow.l.r.double y = a x + b$
    is an oblique asymptote.

+ #strong[Relative position:] determined by the sign of
  $f\(x\)- y_(upright("asymptote"))$.

+ #strong[Indeterminate Forms:] $oo - oo$, $0 times oo$, $oo / oo$,
  $0 / 0$.

+ #strong[Comparative Growth:] $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x / x^n = + oo$
  and $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) x^n e^x = 0$. In any direct confrontation,
  exponential growth dominates polynomial growth.

+ #strong[Composite functions:] $lim_(x arrow.r a) f\(x\)= b$ and
  $lim_(X arrow.r b) g\(X\)= c arrow.r.double.long lim_(x arrow.r a) g\(f\(x\)\)= c$.

