=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-12>
We have just seen that some functions undergo sudden disruptions:
vertical asymptotes where curves tear apart toward infinity, or
staircase steps where curves jump abruptly from one elevation to
another. Yet most laws of nature --- the trajectory of a tennis ball,
the water level rising in a vase, the growth of a plant --- know no such
instantaneous leaps. How can we express with the highest mathematical
rigor the idea that a curve can be drawn #strong[in a single stroke of
the pen, without ever lifting the nib from the page]? And why does this
fundamental property guarantee that in crossing a river from one bank to
the other, one is mathematically certain to pass through every
intermediate current?

This is the essence of #strong[continuity] and the prestigious
#strong[Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT)]. See you in Chapter 46.

