=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-13>
We have just seen that continuity ensures a curve never tears. But a
continuous curve can be filled with sharp corners (like $\|x\|$ at zero)
or abrupt kinks. To speak of #strong[velocity], #strong[acceleration],
and #strong[smooth trajectory], we need the supreme instrument of
differential calculus: differentiation. What happens when we
differentiate a function of a function, like a mechanical gearbox where
a first cog drives a second, which in turn drives a third? How does the
second derivative reveal whether a curve cups upward toward the sky like
a bowl catching rain (#strong[convexity]) or slices through its own
tangent (#strong[inflection point])?

This is the art of gears and curvature: see you in Chapter 47.

