=== What You Will Discover
<what-you-will-discover-3>
- Why differentiating a composite function operates exactly like a
  #strong[bicycle gearbox]: the celebrated chain rule
  $\(g compose u\)' = u' dot.op\(g' compose u\)$.
- The three indispensable powerhouse formulas for the Baccalaureate: the
  derivatives of $e^(u\(x\))$, $sqrt(u\(x\))$, and $\(u\(x\)\)^n$.
- The #strong[second derivative] $f''\(x\)$: after instantaneous
  velocity comes #strong[acceleration] and the curvature of the
  trajectory.
- #strong[Convexity]: understanding intuitively and geometrically what
  it means for a curve to be “bowl-shaped” (opening upward) or
  “bell-shaped” (opening downward).
- The fundamental curriculum equivalences: secant lines lying above the
  curve, tangent lines lying below the curve, and the positivity of
  $f''\(x\)$.
- #strong[Inflection points]: the exact spot where a cyclist straightens
  the handlebars to switch curves, where the curve #strong[crosses its
  own tangent].
- #strong[Convexity inequalities], including the celebrated
  $e^x gt.eq x + 1$ for all real $x$.

