==== Worked Example 6 --- Finding the Inflection Point of an Economic Cost Function
<worked-example-6-finding-the-inflection-point-of-an-economic-cost-function>
#strong[Problem:] A chemical company produces an output quantity
$q in\[0 thin\;thin 10\]$ (in tons) of a polymer. The total production
cost in thousands of euros is modeled by:
$ C\(q\)= q^3 - 6 q^2 + 15 q + 10 . $ 1. Prove that the marginal cost
$C'\(q\)$ is strictly increasing on $\[2 thin\;thin 10\]$. 2. Determine
the inflection point of the total cost curve and interpret it
economically (law of diminishing returns).

#strong[Solution:] 1. Compute the marginal cost (first derivative) and
its rate of change (second derivative): $ C'\(q\)= 3 q^2 - 12 q + 15 . $
$ C''\(q\)= 6 q - 12 = 6\(q - 2\). $ For all $q > 2$, $C''\(q\)> 0$.
Thus $C'$ is #strong[strictly increasing] on $\[2 thin\;thin 10\]$. 2.
#strong[Inflection point:]
$C''\(q\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double 6 q - 12 = 0 arrow.l.r.double q = 2$.

- For $q < 2$, $C''\(q\)< 0$: the cost function is concave.

- For $q > 2$, $C''\(q\)> 0$: the cost function is convex. The second
  derivative vanishes while changing sign at $q = 2$. The point with
  $q = 2$ is therefore the #strong[inflection point] of the total cost
  curve. #emph[Economic interpretation:]

- For $q in\[0 thin\;thin 2\]$, production benefits from economies of
  scale: each additional ton costs less to produce than the preceding
  one (marginal cost is decreasing).

- Past $q = 2$ tons, plant capacity begins to strain, machinery
  experiences wear, overtime wages accrue: each additional ton becomes
  progressively more expensive (diminishing returns). The point $q = 2$
  marks the structural tipping point.

