=== The Question That Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-14>
We have seen that the exponential function $x mapsto e^x$ is the
sovereign of functions: it turns addition into multiplication, its
derivative is equal to itself, and its curve is entirely convex above
its tangent $y = x + 1$. Yet what happens if an astronomer or a
navigator needs to retrace the steps in reverse? What if one knows the
final power and seeks #strong[the exponent that produced it]? How did a
graduated slide rule and printed calculation tables enable
seventeenth-century sailors to navigate across oceans without performing
a single long multiplication by hand?

This is the saga of #strong[John Napier] and the #strong[natural
logarithm] function. See you in Chapter 48.

