=== 1. The Problem: The Sea, the Stars, and Endless Multiplications
<the-problem-the-sea-the-stars-and-endless-multiplications>
==== 1.1 The Lost Navigator
<the-lost-navigator>
Imagine yourself in the year #strong[1600], standing on the deck of a
galleon crossing the Atlantic. There is no GPS, no radio, no reliable
marine chronometer. To determine the ship's coordinates, the navigator
uses a #strong[quadrant] to measure the angular elevation of the Sun or
a guide star above the horizon, and must then solve a #strong[spherical
triangle]: a triangle inscribed on the celestial sphere.

The formulas of spherical trigonometry look like this:
$ cos a = cos b cos c + sin b sin c cos A . $

Sines and cosines are read from printed #strong[tables], computed to
seven or eight decimal places for navigational accuracy. The navigator
must therefore multiply, entirely by hand, numbers such as:
$ 0.8746197 times 0.4848096 . $

Such long multiplication involves dozens of intermediate products and
additions, with carries at every step. A single calculation error, and
the vessel believes itself to be a hundred kilometers from its actual
position---heading straight for submerged reefs. Astronomers, too, such
as #strong[Tycho Brahe] and #strong[Johannes Kepler], spent whole
#strong[decades] of their lives grinding through tedious multiplications
to determine planetary orbits.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did you know?
]
 Before logarithms, mathematicians used a
computational shortcut known as #strong[prosthaphaeresis] (from the
Greek for “addition and subtraction”), which exploited the
product-to-sum trigonometric identity
$cos a cos b = 1 / 2 [cos \( a + b \) + cos \( a - b \)]$ to convert
products into sums… but it was cumbersome, error-prone, and restricted
to numbers between $- 1$ and $1$.
]

==== 1.2 The Dream: Replacing × with +
<the-dream-replacing-with>
Addition is straightforward: one aligns the digits and adds.
Multiplication is laborious and prone to error. Hence the great dream of
Renaissance computers:

#quote_box[
#emph[To find a “dictionary” that pairs every positive number with
another number in such a way that the translation of a product is simply
the sum of the translations.
]

]

The German monk #strong[Michael Stifel] caught a glimpse of this dream
in 1544 by tabulating two sequences, one beneath the other:

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Arithmetic progression :   0    1    2    3    4     5     6      7      8
Geometric progression  :   1    2    4    8   16    32    64    128    256
    ```
  ]
]

To multiply $8 times 32$, look above: $8$ corresponds to $3$, and $32$
corresponds to $5$. Add them: $3 + 5 = 8$. Look back down: under $8$
sits $256$. And indeed, $8 times 32 = 256$! #strong[The top row converts
multiplications of the bottom row into simple additions.]

This is simply the rule of exponents you have known since middle school:
$ 2^3 times 2^5 = 2^(3 + 5) . $

Yet there was a critical flaw: between $8$ and $16$, there are vast
gaps. How could one multiply $11 times 13$? One would need a geometric
progression #strong[with steps so densely packed] that virtually every
real number is represented. One had to #strong[fill the gaps].

==== 1.3 Twenty Years of Calculation: The Baron of Merchiston
<twenty-years-of-calculation-the-baron-of-merchiston>
This monumental feat was accomplished by #strong[John Napier]
(1550--1617), 8th Laird of Merchiston in Scotland. He chose a geometric
progression whose common ratio was exceedingly close to $1$, namely
$1 - 10^(- 7) = 0.9999999$, so that consecutive terms barely differed:
the gaps shrank to microscopic proportions. For #strong[twenty years],
by hand, he computed his tables. In #strong[1614], he published
#emph[Mirifici Logarithmorum Canonis Descriptio] (“Description of the
Wonderful Rule of Logarithms”).

The word #strong[logarithm], which he coined, derives from the Greek
#emph[logos] (ratio, proportion) and #emph[arithmos] (number): “the
number that measures ratios”. The logarithm of a number is, in essence,
#strong[the number of steps] one must take along the geometric
progression to reach it.

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History ---
]
 The English mathematician #strong[Henry
Briggs], reading Napier's work, was so astonished that he journeyed from
London to Scotland in 1615 to meet him. It is said that when they met,
the two men stood looking at one another in speechless admiration for a
quarter of an hour before uttering a word. Together, they agreed that
logarithms should be recast in “base 10” (where $log 10 = 1$), which was
far more practical for decimal notation: these became the #strong[common
(or decimal) logarithms]. Kepler, who received Napier's tables in 1617,
immediately used them to complete his monumental #emph[Rudolphine
Tables] of planetary motions and dedicated one of his works to Napier…
unaware that Napier had passed away in the interim.
]

For more than #strong[three centuries], logarithmic tables and
#strong[slide rules] (pairs of logarithmic scales sliding against each
other to add lengths mechanically) accompanied engineers, sailors,
architects, and scientists---all the way to NASA engineers working on
the Apollo moon landings in the 1960s!

==== 1.4 The Same Problem, Seen from the Laboratory
<the-same-problem-seen-from-the-laboratory>
Recall the bacterial colony from Chapter 40. A population governed by
the law $N\(t\)= N_0 thin e^(k t)$ grows exponentially. The biologist
faces the #strong[inverse] question:

#quote_box[
#emph[“The population has grown by a factor of 1000. How long ago did
the infection begin?”
]

]

This requires solving $e^(k t) = 1000$---that is, finding the
#strong[exponent] that produces a desired result. The exponential
function maps the exponent to the output; we need the reciprocal machine
that maps the #strong[output back to the exponent]. That machine is the
#strong[natural logarithm]. The two narratives---that of navigators at
sea and that of biologists in the laboratory---converge at the exact
same concept.

#divider()

=== 2. Definition: The Machine that Inverts the Exponential
<definition-the-machine-that-inverts-the-exponential>
==== 2.1 What We Already Know About the Exponential
<what-we-already-know-about-the-exponential>
Let us summarize what Chapters 40 and 46 established regarding the
function $exp : x mapsto e^x$:

- It is defined, continuous, and differentiable on $bb(R)$, with
  $\(e^x\)' = e^x$\;
- It is #strong[strictly positive]: $e^x > 0$ for all real numbers $x$\;
- It is therefore #strong[strictly increasing] on $bb(R)$\;
- $lim_(x arrow.r - oo) e^x = 0$ and $lim_(x arrow.r + oo) e^x = + oo$.

The #strong[corollary to the Intermediate Value Theorem] (the Bijective
Theorem, Chapter 46) then guarantees: a function that is continuous and
strictly monotonic on an interval defines a #strong[bijection] from that
interval onto its image. Here:

#quote_box[
#strong[For every real number $a > 0$, the equation $e^x = a$ has a
unique real solution.
]

]

Visually: the exponential curve intersects every horizontal line $y = a$
(with $a > 0$) #strong[at exactly one point]. And it never intersects
the lines $y = a$ with $a lt.eq 0$, because the curve lies entirely
above the horizontal axis.

#figure(image("figures/vol_6/fig_48_exp_log_reciproques.svg", alt: "Réciprocité des fonctions exponentielle et logarithme népérien"),
  caption: [
    Réciprocité des fonctions exponentielle et logarithme népérien
  ]
)

==== 2.2 The Definition
<the-definition-1>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition ---
]
 For every strictly positive real number $a$,
the #strong[natural logarithm] of $a$, denoted by $ln a$ (or $ln\(a\)$),
is the #strong[unique] real number $x$ such that $e^x = a$. The function
$ln : thick\]0 thin\;+ oo\[thick arrow.r bb(R)\,thick x mapsto ln x$ is
the #strong[natural logarithm function]. It is the #strong[inverse
function] of the exponential function.
$ upright("For ") a > 0 upright(" and ") x in bb(R) : #h(2em) e^x = a arrow.l.r.double x = ln a . $
]

#strong[Notation.] The symbol $ln$ abbreviates #emph[logarithmus
naturalis] (Latin for “natural logarithm”). It is read “the natural
logarithm of $x$”, or informally “el-en of $x$”. Parentheses are
optional when there is no ambiguity: $ln 2$, $ln x$\; but one writes
$ln\(x + 1\)$, and never $ln x + 1$, which represents $\(ln x\)+ 1$.

#decouvre(title: [Découverte & Histoire])[
#strong[💡 Did you know?
]
 Napier did not use the symbol $ln$, nor did he
view it as the inverse of $e^x$: the base $e$ had not yet been
identified or named! It was #strong[Grégoire de Saint-Vincent] and
#strong[Alphonse Antonio de Sarasa] (around 1647--1649) who discovered
that the area under the hyperbola $y = 1 / x$ exhibits logarithmic
behavior (as we will explore in Chapter 50), and #strong[Leonhard
Euler], in 1748, who systematically established the exponential and
logarithm as mutual inverse operations. Napier's original tables
corresponded closely to the function $x mapsto 10^7 ln (10^7 / x)$.
]

==== 2.3 Immediate Consequences
<immediate-consequences>
From this definition follow directly:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
