==== 4.4 Boundary Limits
<boundary-limits>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Boundary Limits Theorem ---
]
 1.
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) ln x = + oo$ 2.
$lim_(x arrow.r 0^(+)) ln x = - oo$

The vertical axis $x = 0$ (the $y$-axis) is therefore a #strong[vertical
asymptote] to the graph of $ln$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proofs (Required Curriculum Proofs) ---
]
 1. #strong[Limit at
$+ oo$:] For every natural number $n$, we have $ln\(2^n\)= n ln 2$.
Since $2 > 1$, $ln 2 > 0$. Consequently,
$lim_(n arrow.r + oo) ln\(2^n\)= lim_(n arrow.r + oo)\(n ln 2\)= + oo$.
Now choose an arbitrarily large real threshold $M > 0$. Since
$n ln 2 arrow.r + oo$, there exists an integer $N$ such that
$N ln 2 > M$. For every real number $x gt.eq 2^N$, the strict
monotonicity of $ln$ implies: $ ln x gt.eq ln\(2^N\)= N ln 2 > M . $
Hence, any interval $\]M thin\;+ oo\[$ contains all values of $ln x$ for
all sufficiently large $x$. Therefore,
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) ln x = + oo$. $square.filled.medium$

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Limit at $0^(+)$:] Substitute $X = 1 / x$. As
  $x arrow.r 0^(+)$, we have $X arrow.r + oo$. Then
  $ln x = ln (1 / X) = - ln X$. Since
  $lim_(X arrow.r + oo) ln X = + oo$, composite limit rules yield:
  $ lim_(x arrow.r 0^(+)) ln x = lim_(X arrow.r + oo)\(- ln X\)= - oo . quad square.filled.medium $
]
]

