=== 6. Real-World Logarithmic Scales
<real-world-logarithmic-scales>
Why are logarithms ubiquitous throughout all experimental sciences?
Because both nature and human perception frequently operate not by
#strong[additive differences], but by #strong[multiplicative ratios]
(the Weber-Fechner psycho-physical law). When our ears perceive a sound
as twice as loud, the underlying acoustic power has not increased by a
few milliwatts: it has increased tenfold!

==== 6.1 Acidity and pH in Chemistry
<acidity-and-ph-in-chemistry>
In aqueous chemistry, the oxonium ion concentration $\[H_3 O^(+)\]$
ranges from $1 thin upright("mol") dot.op upright("L")^(- 1)$
(concentrated hydrochloric acid) down to
$10^(- 14) thin upright("mol") dot.op upright("L")^(- 1)$ (concentrated
sodium hydroxide). This spans a dynamic ratio of $10^14$---one hundred
trillion! To compress this enormous span into convenient numbers between
0 and 14, the Danish chemist #strong[Søren Sørensen] introduced the
#strong[pH] (potential of hydrogen) in 1909 using the common logarithm
$log_10\(x\)= frac(ln x, ln 10)$:

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition of pH ---
]

$ upright("pH") = - log_10 (frac(\[H_3 O^(+)\], c_0)) = - frac(ln\(\[H_3 O^(+)\]\/c_0\), ln 10) $
where $c_0 = 1 thin upright("mol") dot.op upright("L")^(- 1)$ is the
standard reference concentration. Inversely:
$\[H_3 O^(+)\]= 10^(- upright("pH")) thin upright("mol") dot.op upright("L")^(- 1)$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[Direct Consequence:
]
 When the pH drops by a single unit (for
instance from 7 to 6), the concentration of $\[H_3 O^(+)\]$ ions
#strong[multiplies by 10]. If the pH drops from 7 to 4, it multiplies by
$10^3 = 1 thin 000$!
]

==== 6.2 Acoustics and Decibels
<acoustics-and-decibels>
The human ear detects acoustic intensities spanning from the faintest
threshold of hearing
($I_0 = 10^(- 12) thin upright("W") dot.op upright("m")^(- 2)$) to the
roar of a rocket launch
($100 thin upright("W") dot.op upright("m")^(- 2)$)---a dynamic range of
$10^14$. The #strong[sound intensity level] $L$, measured in
#strong[decibels (dB)], is defined as:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Sound Level in Decibels ---
]

$ L = 10 log_10 (I / I_0) = 10 frac(ln\(I\/I_0\), ln 10) . $
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 14 --- Do Two Vacuum Cleaners Make Twice the
Noise?
]
 Suppose a vacuum cleaner emits an acoustic intensity $I_1$
resulting in a sound level of $L_1 = 70 thin upright("dB")$. Two
identical vacuum cleaners operating simultaneously produce double the
acoustic intensity: $I_2 = 2 I_1$. What is the resulting sound level
$L_2$?
$ L_2 = 10 log_10 (frac(2 I_1, I_0)) = 10 [log_10 \( 2 \) + log_10 (I_1 / I_0)] = 10 log_10\(2\)+ L_1 . $
Since
$10 log_10\(2\)approx 10 times 0.30103 approx 3 thin upright("dB")$, we
have: $ L_2 approx 70 + 3 = 73 thin upright("dB") ! $ #strong[Doubling
the physical acoustic power increases the perceived sound level by only
3 decibels!]
]

==== 6.3 Earthquakes and the Richter Magnitude Scale
<earthquakes-and-the-richter-magnitude-scale>
The Richter scale relates the seismic energy $E$ released at an
earthquake's epicenter via a formula of the form:
$ M = 2 / 3 log_10 (E / E_0) . $ If the magnitude increases by 1 unit,
we have $Delta M = 1 = 2 / 3 log_10 (E' / E)$, whence:
$ log_10 (E' / E) = 3 / 2 = 1.5 arrow.l.r.double E' / E = 10^1.5 = 10 sqrt(10) approx 31.6 . $
A magnitude 7 earthquake releases #strong[31.6 times more energy] than a
magnitude 6 earthquake, and $\(31.6\)^2 approx 1 thin 000$ times more
energy than a magnitude 5 earthquake!

==== 6.4 Radiocarbon Dating and Half-Life
<radiocarbon-dating-and-half-life>
In any living organism, the ratio of radioactive carbon-14 to stable
carbon-12 remains constant. Upon death, metabolic intake ceases and
carbon-14 decays exponentially according to:
$ N\(t\)= N_0 thin e^(- lambda t)\, $ where $lambda > 0$ is the
radioactive decay constant of the isotope.

The #strong[half-life] $t_(1\/2)$ is the duration after which half of
the original radioactive nuclei have decayed:
$ N\(t_(1\/2)\)= N_0 / 2 arrow.l.r.double N_0 e^(- lambda t_(1\/2)) = N_0 / 2 arrow.l.r.double e^(- lambda t_(1\/2)) = 1 / 2 . $
Applying the natural logarithm:
$ - lambda t_(1\/2) = ln (1 / 2) = - ln 2 arrow.l.r.double t_(1\/2) = frac(ln 2, lambda) . $
For carbon-14, $t_(1\/2) approx 5 thin 730$ years. If a sample of
charcoal recovered from a prehistoric cave retains $30 %$ of its
original carbon-14 activity, its age $t$ is obtained by solving:
$ frac(N\(t\), N_0) = 0.30 arrow.l.r.double e^(- lambda t) = 0.30 arrow.l.r.double - lambda t = ln\(0.30\)arrow.l.r.double t = - frac(ln\(0.30\), lambda) = - 5 thin 730 times frac(ln\(0.30\), ln 2) approx 9 thin 950 upright(" years") . $

