#strong[Solution 48.13:] 1. $lim_(x arrow.r + oo)\(x - ln x\)$:
Indeterminate form $oo - oo$. Factor out $x$:
$x - ln x = x (1 - frac(ln x, x))$. Since
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) frac(ln x, x) = 0$, the term in parentheses tends
to $1$. Thus the product tends to $+ oo$. 2.
$lim_(x arrow.r + oo) frac(ln\(x^2 + 1\), x)$: For $x > 0$,
$ln\(x^2 + 1\)= ln [x^2 (1 + 1 / x^2)] = 2 ln x + ln (1 + 1 / x^2)$. The
quotient becomes:
$frac(ln\(x^2 + 1\), x) = 2 frac(ln x, x) + frac(ln\(1 + 1\/x^2\), x)$.
As $x arrow.r + oo$, $frac(ln x, x) arrow.r 0$ and
$frac(ln\(1 + 1\/x^2\), x) arrow.r frac(0, + oo) = 0$. The limit is $0$.
\3. $lim_(x arrow.r 0^(+)) x ln (1 + 1 / x)$: Substitute
$u = 1 / x arrow.r + oo$. The expression becomes
$frac(ln\(1 + u\), u) = frac(ln u + ln\(1 + 1\/u\), u) = frac(ln u, u) + frac(ln\(1 + 1\/u\), u) arrow.r 0 + 0 = 0$.
\4. $lim_(x arrow.r 1) frac(ln x, x^2 - 1)$: Form $0 / 0$. Factor the
denominator:
$frac(ln x, \(x - 1\)\(x + 1\)) = frac(ln x - ln 1, x - 1) times frac(1, x + 1)$.
Since $lim_(x arrow.r 1) frac(ln x - ln 1, x - 1) = ln'\(1\)= 1$ and
$lim_(x arrow.r 1) frac(1, x + 1) = 1 / 2$, the product limit is
