#strong[Solution 48.14:] 1. With an annual interest rate of $3.5 %$, the
capital multiplies each year by $1 + 0.035 = 1.035$. Thus
$C_n = 10 thin 000 times 1.035^n$. 2. Doubling occurs when
$C_n gt.eq 20 thin 000 arrow.l.r.double 1.035^n gt.eq 2$. Applying $ln$:
$n ln\(1.035\)gt.eq ln 2 arrow.l.r.double n gt.eq frac(ln 2, ln\(1.035\))$
(since $ln\(1.035\)> 0$).
$frac(ln 2, ln\(1.035\)) approx 0.69315 / 0.03440 approx 20.15$. It
takes $21$ full years for the capital to double. 3. Rule of 70: For an
annual rate of $t %$, the multiplier is $1 + t / 100$. The doubling time
satisfies $n = frac(ln 2, ln\(1 + t\/100\))$. For small $t\/100$, the
first-order approximation gives $ln\(1 + u\)approx u$, hence
$ln\(1 + t\/100\)approx t / 100$. Moreover,
$ln 2 approx 0.693 approx 0.70$. Thus:
$n approx frac(0.693, t\/100) = 69.3 / t approx 70 / t$. For $t = 3.5$,
this yields $70 / 3.5 = 20$ years, remarkably close to the exact $20.15$
years!

