$phi\(x\)lt.eq 0 arrow.l.r.double ln x lt.eq x - 1$ for all $x > 0$. 2.
Substituting $x = 1 + t$ with $1 + t > 0 arrow.l.r.double t > - 1$
yields $ln\(1 + t\)lt.eq\(1 + t\)- 1 = t$. 3. - Apply
$ln\(1 + t\)lt.eq t$ with $t = 1 / n > 0$:
$ln (1 + 1 / n) lt.eq 1 / n arrow.l.r.double n ln (1 + 1 / n) lt.eq 1$.
Applying the increasing exponential function: $(1 + 1 / n)^n lt.eq e$.

- Apply $ln\(1 + t\)lt.eq t$ with $t = - frac(1, n + 1) > - 1$:
  $ ln (1 - frac(1, n + 1)) lt.eq - frac(1, n + 1) arrow.l.r.double ln (frac(n, n + 1)) lt.eq - frac(1, n + 1) arrow.l.r.double - ln (frac(n + 1, n)) lt.eq - frac(1, n + 1) . $
  Multiplying by $-\(n + 1\)< 0$: $ \(n + 1\)ln (1 + 1 / n) gt.eq 1 . $
  Applying the exponential function: $(1 + 1 / n)^(n + 1) gt.eq e$.
  Combining both yields the celebrated bounds:
  $(1 + 1 / n)^n lt.eq e lt.eq (1 + 1 / n)^(n + 1)$.

