#strong[Solution 48.19:] 1. For all $x > 0$, $ln\(1 + x\)lt.eq x$.
Setting $x = 1 / k$ for $k gt.eq 1$:
$ ln (1 + 1 / k) lt.eq 1 / k arrow.l.r.double ln (frac(k + 1, k)) lt.eq 1 / k arrow.l.r.double ln\(k + 1\)- ln k lt.eq 1 / k . $
\2. For all $x in thick\]0 thin\;1\[$,
$ln\(1 - x\)lt.eq - x arrow.l.r.double - ln\(1 - x\)gt.eq x$. Setting
$x = 1 / k$ for $k gt.eq 2$:
$ 1 / k lt.eq - ln (1 - 1 / k) = - ln (frac(k - 1, k)) = ln k - ln\(k - 1\). $
\3. - Summing inequality 1 for $k = 1$ to $n$:
$ sum_(k = 1)^n\(ln\(k + 1\)- ln k\)lt.eq sum_(k = 1)^n 1 / k = H_n . $
The left sum telescopes:
$\(ln 2 - ln 1\)+\(ln 3 - ln 2\)+ dots.h +\(ln\(n + 1\)- ln n\)= ln\(n + 1\)$.
Thus $ln\(n + 1\)lt.eq H_n$.

- Summing inequality 2 for $k = 2$ to $n$:
  $ sum_(k = 2)^n 1 / k lt.eq sum_(k = 2)^n\(ln k - ln\(k - 1\)\)= ln n - ln 1 = ln n . $
  Since $H_n = 1 + sum_(k = 2)^n 1 / k$, we obtain
  $H_n - 1 lt.eq ln n arrow.l.r.double H_n lt.eq 1 + ln n$. We have
  established the bounds: $ln\(n + 1\)lt.eq H_n lt.eq 1 + ln n$.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 4)
+ Since $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) ln\(n + 1\)= + oo$, the comparison theorem
  immediately implies $lim_(n arrow.r + oo) H_n = + oo$. The harmonic
  series diverges, even though its individual terms $1 / n$ tend to
  zero!
+ Divide the bounds by $ln n$ (which is strictly positive for
  $n gt.eq 2$):
  $ frac(ln\(n + 1\), ln n) lt.eq frac(H_n, ln n) lt.eq frac(1, ln n) + 1 . $
  Since
  $frac(ln\(n + 1\), ln n) = 1 + frac(ln\(1 + 1\/n\), ln n) arrow.r 1$
  and $frac(1, ln n) + 1 arrow.r 1$, the Squeeze Theorem gives:
  $ lim_(n arrow.r + oo) frac(H_n, ln n) = 1 . $ (The difference
  $H_n - ln n$ converges to the celebrated #strong[Euler-Mascheroni
  constant] $gamma approx 0.577215$).
]

#strong[Solution 48.20:] 1.
