#strong[Solution 48.22:] 1. Take the natural logarithm of both sides:
$ln f\(x\)= ln\(u_1\(x\)u_2\(x\)dots.h u_n\(x\)\)= sum_(k = 1)^n ln\(u_k\(x\)\)$.
Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$:
$ frac(f'\(x\), f\(x\)) = sum_(k = 1)^n frac(u'_k\(x\), u_k\(x\)) . $ 2.
For $g\(x\)=\(x + 1\)^3\(2 x - 1\)^4\(x^2 + 5\)^2$:
$ frac(g'\(x\), g\(x\)) = frac(3, x + 1) + frac(4 times 2, 2 x - 1) + frac(2 times 2 x, x^2 + 5) = frac(3, x + 1) + frac(8, 2 x - 1) + frac(4 x, x^2 + 5) . $
Multiplying this sum by $g\(x\)$ gives $g'\(x\)$ immediately without an
unwieldy generalized product rule!

