=== 1. The Problem: The Pisa Chandelier and the Shadow of the Circle
<the-problem-the-pisa-chandelier-and-the-shadow-of-the-circle>
==== 1.1 Galileo's Insight
<galileos-insight>
In #strong[1583], a nineteen-year-old medical student named
#strong[Galileo Galilei] attended a religious service in the Cathedral
of Pisa. His attention drifted toward the vaulted ceiling. A sacristan
had just lit a large chandelier suspended from an immense iron chain
and, in releasing it, set it swaying in a slow, steady rhythm.

Galileo noticed something extraordinary: whether the amplitude of the
swing was broad at the beginning or shrank to a barely perceptible
tremor as friction slowed it down, #strong[the time required to complete
a full back-and-forth cycle appeared precisely constant].

Lacking a pocket watch---high-precision mechanical timekeepers had not
yet been invented---Galileo took his own pulse at his wrist. He counted
beats: four heartbeats for a wide swing, four heartbeats for a medium
swing, four heartbeats for a tiny flutter. This was the discovery of the
#strong[isochronism of small pendular oscillations].

This phenomenon is universal: a child's swing at the playground, a
plucked violin string, water sloshing in an enclosed bay, alternating
electrons surging through a radio antenna---all obey this very same
fundamental rhythmic cadence.

==== 1.2 From the Circle to the Wave: Optical Projection
<from-the-circle-to-the-wave-optical-projection>
How can we represent this oscillatory back-and-forth movement
mathematically? Consider a simple thought experiment: 1. A large
vertical wheel rotates at constant speed in a darkened room. A single
bright luminous LED $M$ is fixed to its outer rim. 2. A distant light
source projects the shadow of the dot $M$ horizontally onto a flat
vertical wall behind the wheel.

What path does the shadow trace on the wall?

- When the dot reaches the top of the wheel, its shadow climbs to the
  highest point on the wall, momentarily slows down, comes to an
  infinitesimal standstill, and reverses downward.
- As the shadow passes through the horizontal center line, it travels at
  its maximum speed.
- At the bottom, it decelerates, halts instantaneously, and accelerates
  back upward.

The shadow on the wall executes #strong[the exact motion of a pendulum
or a swing]! If we pull a strip of paper vertically past the wall at a
constant speed, the moving shadow traces a pristine periodic curve:
#strong[a sinusoid].

