=== 2. The Trigonometric Circle and Fundamental Symmetries
<the-trigonometric-circle-and-fundamental-symmetries>
==== 2.1 The Radian: The Natural Measure of Arc Length
<the-radian-the-natural-measure-of-arc-length>
In elementary school, you learned to measure angles in degrees
($360^compose$ for a full revolution). The number 360 was chosen by
ancient Babylonian astronomers because it is divisible by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,
8, 9, 10, 12, etc., and closely approximates the 365 days of a solar
year.

In calculus and physics, however, degrees are an arbitrary and
cumbersome choice: differentiating trigonometric functions expressed in
degrees would introduce an unsightly scaling factor of $pi / 180$ into
every single derivative!

The intrinsic, natural mathematical unit is the #strong[radian]:

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition of the Radian ---
]
 In the Cartesian plane equipped
with an orthonormal coordinate system $\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\)$, the
#strong[trigonometric circle] (or unit circle) $cal(C)$ is the circle
centered at the origin $O\(0 thin\;0\)$ with radius $R = 1$, oriented in
the #strong[counter-clockwise direction] (the positive or direct
mathematical orientation). The measure of an angle in #strong[radians]
is defined as the #strong[arc length] subtended by that angle along the
unit circle of radius 1.
]

Since the circumference of a circle of radius 1 is $2 pi R = 2 pi$, a
full revolution of $360^compose$ corresponds to an angle of
#strong[$2 pi$ radians].

