  )

==== 2.2 Wrapping the Real Line Around the Circle
<wrapping-the-real-line-around-the-circle>
Imagine a graduated real number line tangent to the trigonometric circle
at the base point $I\(1 thin\;0\)$. Wrap this infinite line around the
circumference:

- Positive numbers wrap counter-clockwise (upward and around);
- Negative numbers wrap clockwise (downward and around).

To every real number $x in bb(R)$ corresponds a unique point $M$ on the
unit circle.

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition of the Cosine and Sine Functions ---
]
 For every
real number $x$, let $M$ be the point on the unit circle paired with $x$
by wrapping the real number line.

- The #strong[cosine] of $x$, denoted $cos x$, is the #strong[abscissa]
  ($x$-coordinate) of point $M$.
- The #strong[sine] of $x$, denoted $sin x$, is the #strong[ordinate]
  ($y$-coordinate) of point $M$.

$ M\(x\)= #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cos x thin\;thin sin x #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
]

==== 2.3 Immediate Consequences and Special Angles
<immediate-consequences-and-special-angles>
+ #strong[Absolute Bounds:] Because the circle has unit radius, the
  coordinates of any point $M$ are bounded between $- 1$ and $1$:
  $ upright("For all ") x in bb(R) : quad - 1 lt.eq cos x lt.eq 1 quad upright("and") quad - 1 lt.eq sin x lt.eq 1 . $

+ #strong[The Circle Equation (Pythagorean Identity):] The right
  triangle formed by the coordinates of $M$ has perpendicular legs of
  lengths $\|cos x\|$ and $\|sin x\|$, and hypotenuse equal to the
  radius $O M = 1$. The Pythagorean theorem directly gives:
  $ cos^2 x + sin^2 x = 1 . $ #emph[\(Notation reminder: $cos^2 x$ means
  $\(cos x\)^2$\.)]

+ #strong[Table of Special Values (Must be Mastered):]

