=== 4. Comprehensive Analysis of the Sine and Cosine Functions
<comprehensive-analysis-of-the-sine-and-cosine-functions>
Because sine and cosine are periodic with period $2 pi$, it suffices to
study them on an interval of length $2 pi$, such as $\[- pi thin\;pi\]$.
Exploiting parity:

- For cosine (even): analyze on $\[0 thin\;pi\]$, then reflect across
  the vertical axis $\(O y\)$ onto $\[- pi thin\;pi\]$.
- For sine (odd): analyze on $\[0 thin\;pi\]$, then reflect through the
  origin $O\(0 thin\;0\)$ onto $\[- pi thin\;pi\]$.

Finally, translate the pattern indefinitely left and right by integer
multiples of $2 pi arrow(i)$.

==== 4.1 Analysis of the Cosine Function
<analysis-of-the-cosine-function>
On the interval $\[0 thin\;pi\]$:

- $cos'\(x\)= - sin x$.
- For all $x in thick\]0 thin\;pi\[$, $sin x > 0$, so $cos'\(x\)< 0$.
- The cosine function is therefore #strong[strictly decreasing] on
  $\[0 thin\;pi\]$.
- $cos 0 = 1$, $cos (pi / 2) = 0$, and $cos pi = - 1$.

