=== 6. The Realm of Signals and Waves: Concrete Modeling
<the-realm-of-signals-and-waves-concrete-modeling>
In real physical systems, oscillations do not necessarily complete a
cycle in $2 pi$ seconds, nor is their amplitude restricted to 1 unit. We
describe general periodic phenomena using the canonical form of a
#strong[sinusoidal signal]:

$ s\(t\)= A cos\(omega t + phi\) $

where:

- #strong[$A > 0$] is the #strong[amplitude] (peak excursion from
  equilibrium);
- #strong[$omega > 0$] is the #strong[angular frequency] (in radians per
  second, $upright("rad") dot.op upright("s")^(- 1)$);
- #strong[$T = frac(2 pi, omega)$] is the #strong[period] (duration of
  one complete cycle in seconds);
- #strong[$f = 1 / T = frac(omega, 2 pi)$] is the #strong[frequency]
  (cycles per second, in Hertz, $upright("Hz")$);
- #strong[$phi$] is the #strong[initial phase] (phase offset at
  $t = 0$).

==== 6.1 Alternating Mains Current
<alternating-mains-current>
In European residential electrical outlets, the voltage is a sinusoidal
wave with frequency $f = 50 upright(" Hz")$. The angular frequency is
$omega = 2 pi f = 2 pi times 50 = 100 pi approx 314.16 upright(" rad") dot.op upright("s")^(- 1)$.
The period of oscillation is
$T = 1 / 50 = 0.02 upright(" s") = 20 upright(" milliseconds")$. An RMS
voltage of $230 upright(" V")$ corresponds to a peak amplitude of
$U_max = 230 sqrt(2) approx 325 upright(" V")$:
$ u\(t\)= 325 cos\(100 pi t\). $

==== 6.2 Musical Notes and Acoustics
<musical-notes-and-acoustics>
A pure tone produced by a tuning fork is a sinusoidal pressure wave
traveling through air. The standard orchestral reference note #emph[A4]
(concert pitch) vibrates at 440 cycles per second
($f = 440 upright(" Hz")$). Its acoustic wavelength in air (at speed of
sound $c approx 340 upright(" m") dot.op upright("s")^(- 1)$) is:
$ lambda = c / f = 340 / 440 approx 0.77 upright(" m") . $

#quote_box[
#strong[💻 Python Simulation --- Sampling a Sinusoid:
]

]

#quote_box[
```python
import math
>
def sinusoidal_signal(amplitude, frequency, duration, num_points):
    omega = 2 * math.pi * frequency
    step = duration / num_points
    points = [
]

    for i in range(num_points):
        t = i * step
        value = amplitude * math.sin(omega * t)
        points.append((round(t, 4), round(value, 3)))
    return points
>
# Generate a 50 Hz signal over 0.02 seconds (1 complete period)
sampled = sinusoidal_signal(amplitude=325, frequency=50, duration=0.02, num_points=5)
print("Sampled points:", sampled)
```
