=== The Question that Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-16>
We have learned to measure slopes, rates of change, and velocities at
every point along a curve: this is differential calculus. But what
happens if we wish to travel in the opposite direction? If we know the
instantaneous velocity of a rocket at every single second of flight, how
do we reconstruct the total distance traveled? If we know the curved
profile of a barrel or the wavy contour of an undulating landscape, how
do we calculate its exact enclosed volume or surface area?

How do we add together an infinity of infinitely thin slices to rebuild
a whole?

Join us in Chapter 50 with #strong[The Barrel Filled with Slices of
Water] and the discovery of integral calculus.

