=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-45>
- How astronomer #strong[Johannes Kepler], outraged by a wine merchant
  during his own wedding in 1615, invented the method of infinitely thin
  slices to measure curved volumes.
- The concept of the #strong[definite integral]
  $integral_a^b f\(t\)thin d t$: from Riemann sums of approximating
  rectangles to the exact area under a curve.
- The historical origin and meaning of the elongated symbol $integral$,
  introduced by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in 1675.
- The #strong[Fundamental Theorem of Calculus] and its rigorous proof:
  how and why integration is the exact #strong[inverse operation] of
  differentiation.
- Finding #strong[antiderivatives]: standard tables and mastering
  composite patterns ($u' u^n$, $u' / u$, $u' e^u$, etc.).
- Essential algebraic and geometric properties of integrals:
  #strong[linearity], #strong[Chasles' relation], #strong[positivity],
  #strong[area between two curves], and the #strong[mean value].
- The cornerstone technique of integral calculus: #strong[Integration by
  Parts] (IBP), derived directly from the product rule of
  differentiation.
- A clean #strong[Python] implementation of the midpoint Riemann sum
  numerical approximation.

