=== 1. The Problem: Kepler, the Wine Merchant, and the Rigged Dipstick
<the-problem-kepler-the-wine-merchant-and-the-rigged-dipstick>
==== 1.1 The Wedding in Linz (1615)
<the-wedding-in-linz-1615>
In the autumn of #strong[1615], in the city of Linz on the banks of the
Danube, the renowned astronomer #strong[Johannes Kepler]---the man who
unlocked the laws of planetary motion around the Sun---was preparing the
banquet for his second wedding. To feast his guests, he purchased
several large barrels of Austrian wine.

When the vintner delivered the bulging wooden casks, he pulled a simple
notched iron rod, called a #strong[gauging rod] or dipstick, from his
pocket. He inserted the rod diagonally through the bung-hole (the
opening situated at the widest bulge of the barrel) all the way to the
farthest seam of the opposite base, pulled out the wet rod, read the
notch, and announced the volume in liters and the price to be paid.

Kepler was dumbfounded: \> #emph[“How can you possibly measure the
capacity of a curved, bulging body with a single diagonal straight line?
If a cask has steeply swollen flanks or nearly straight cylindrical
flanks, your rod plunges to the exact same depth, yet the volume of wine
inside is completely different! You are selling me air, or you are
robbing me blind!”]

The merchant merely shrugged: wine merchants had gauged casks this way
for generations. Kepler retreated to his study. For weeks, he set aside
his astronomical calculations to resolve the enigma of the casks.

His breakthrough, published in his landmark treatise #emph[Nova
Stereometria Doliorum Vinariorum] (“New Stereometry of Wine Casks”,
1615), was brilliant in its simplicity: To measure a volume bounded by
curved surfaces, #strong[one must mentally slice it into an infinity of
flat, infinitely thin circular disks], calculate the volume of each disk
(which is virtually a perfect flat cylinder), and then #strong[add all
these disks together] from the bottom to the top!

