+ #strong[The Case of Variable Velocity:] In real-world driving,
  velocity fluctuates constantly: accelerating, braking, idling. The
  curve $v\(t\)$ undulates. To calculate total distance, partition time
  into tiny intervals of duration $Delta t$. During a split second
  $Delta t$, the velocity $v\(t\)$ barely has time to change: it is
  practically constant. The small incremental distance covered during
  that slice is the area of a slender rectangle of width $Delta t$ and
  height $v\(t\)$: $ Delta d approx v\(t\)times Delta t . $ The total
  distance traveled is the sum of these millions of tiny rectangles. And
  as the width $Delta t$ shrinks to an infinitesimal increment (denoted
  $d t$), the sum of the rectangular areas converges to the
  #strong[exact area under the velocity curve]!
]

