=== 3. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus: The Miraculous Bridge
<the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-the-miraculous-bridge>
Computing sums of millions of rectangles by hand would be an agonizing,
impossible task. Here enters what is widely considered the crown jewel
of mathematical analysis, discovered independently by #strong[Isaac
Newton] and #strong[Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz] between 1666 and 1675.

==== 3.1 The Area Accumulation Function
<the-area-accumulation-function>
Let $f$ be a continuous, positive function on $\[a thin\;b\]$. Hold the
lower limit $a$ fixed, and let the upper limit vary as a free variable
$x in\[a thin\;b\]$. Define the #strong[area accumulation function] $A$:
$ A\(x\)= integral_a^x f\(t\)thin d t . $

$A\(x\)$ measures the #strong[accumulated area swept under the curve
from $a$ to $x$]. What is the rate of change of this accumulated area
when the upper boundary advances by a tiny increment $h > 0$?

When moving from $x$ to $x + h$, the total area increases by the slender
strip spanning from $x$ to $x + h$:
$ Delta A = A\(x + h\)- A\(x\)= integral_x^(x + h) f\(t\)thin d t . $
This strip has base width $h$ and a nearly constant height equal to
$f\(x\)$ (since $f$ is continuous). Its area is therefore approximately:
$ A\(x + h\)- A\(x\)approx f\(x\)times h arrow.l.r.double frac(A\(x + h\)- A\(x\), h) approx f\(x\). $
As $h arrow.r 0$, the difference quotient of the area function becomes
#strong[precisely the height of the curve $f\(x\)$]!

#quote_box[
#strong[The derivative of the accumulation function is the original
function itself:
]
 $ A'\(x\)= f\(x\). $
]

==== 3.2 Rigorous Proof of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus
<rigorous-proof-of-the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (Required Curriculum Proof)
---
]
 Let $f$ be a continuous function on an interval $I$, and let
$a in I$. The function $A$ defined on $I$ by:
$ A\(x\)= integral_a^x f\(t\)thin d t $ is differentiable on $I$, and
for every $x in I$: $ A'\(x\)= f\(x\). $
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Proof ---
]
 Let $x in I$ and let $h > 0$ such that
$x + h in I$. By Chasles' relation for integrals:
$ A\(x + h\)- A\(x\)= integral_a^(x + h) f\(t\)thin d t - integral_a^x f\(t\)thin d t = integral_x^(x + h) f\(t\)thin d t . $
Because $f$ is continuous on the closed interval $\[x thin\;x + h\]$,
the Extreme Value Theorem guarantees that $f$ attains an absolute
minimum $m_h$ and an absolute maximum $M_h$ on $\[x thin\;x + h\]$:
$ upright("For all ") t in\[x thin\;x + h\]: quad m_h lt.eq f\(t\)lt.eq M_h . $
Integrating this bounding across the interval $\[x thin\;x + h\]$ of
length $h$:
$ integral_x^(x + h) m_h thin d t lt.eq integral_x^(x + h) f\(t\)thin d t lt.eq integral_x^(x + h) M_h thin d t arrow.l.r.double m_h times h lt.eq A\(x + h\)- A\(x\)lt.eq M_h times h . $
Dividing by $h > 0$:
$ m_h lt.eq frac(A\(x + h\)- A\(x\), h) lt.eq M_h . $ Because $f$ is
continuous at $x$, as $h arrow.r 0^(+)$, the interval
$\[x thin\;x + h\]$ collapses down to the single point $x$. The minimum
$m_h$ and maximum $M_h$ both converge to $f\(x\)$:
$ lim_(h arrow.r 0^(+)) m_h = f\(x\)quad upright("and") quad lim_(h arrow.r 0^(+)) M_h = f\(x\). $
By the #strong[Squeeze Theorem]:
$ lim_(h arrow.r 0^(+)) frac(A\(x + h\)- A\(x\), h) = f\(x\). $ An
identical argument for $h < 0$ yields
$lim_(h arrow.r 0^(-)) frac(A\(x + h\)- A\(x\), h) = f\(x\)$. We
conclude that $A$ is differentiable at $x$ and $A'\(x\)= f\(x\)$.
$square.filled.medium$
]

