=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-46>
- Why most fundamental laws in physics, biology, and economics do not
  directly specify the formula for what happens, but rather #strong[the
  relationship between a quantity and its instantaneous rate of change].
- Newton's phenomenological #strong[Law of Cooling] (1701): why your hot
  coffee cools very rapidly at first, then progressively more slowly.
- The homogeneous linear differential equation #strong[$y' = a y$]: its
  rigorous proof and general solutions $y\(t\)= C e^(a t)$.
- The non-homogeneous linear differential equation with constant forcing
  #strong[$y' = a y + b$]: decomposing into #emph[general solution of
  the homogeneous equation + constant particular solution]:
  $y\(t\)= C e^(a t) - b / a$.
- The Cauchy initial value problem and how the #strong[initial
  condition] $y\(t_0\)= y_0$ guarantees uniqueness of the trajectory.
- Foundational real-world models: radioactive decay, electronic RC
  circuits, terminal velocity of a skydiver, and population dynamics.
- Step-by-step #strong[Euler's method]: how computers numerically
  simulate any differential equation with a clean #strong[Python]
  script.

