=== 4. Foundational Real-World Models
<foundational-real-world-models>
==== 4.1 Charging a Capacitor (RC Circuit)
<charging-a-capacitor-rc-circuit>
In an electrical circuit comprising a DC voltage source $E$, a resistor
$R$, and a capacitor $C$ in series, Kirchhoff's voltage law yields:
$ u_R\(t\)+ u_C\(t\)= E . $ Ohm's law gives $u_R = R i\(t\)$, while the
current through the capacitor is $i\(t\)= C frac(d u_C, d t)$. This
yields the differential equation:
$ R C frac(d u_C, d t) + u_C\(t\)= E arrow.l.r.double u'_C\(t\)= - frac(1, R C) u_C\(t\)+ frac(E, R C) . $

Defining the #strong[time constant] $tau = R C$ (measured in seconds),
the equation becomes: $ u'_C\(t\)= - 1 / tau u_C\(t\)+ E / tau . $ With
the initial condition of an initially uncharged capacitor
($u_C\(0\)= 0$), the unique solution is:
$ u_C\(t\)= E (1 - e^(- t\/tau)) . $

#quote_box[
#strong[Notable Properties of Time Constant $tau$:
]
 At $t = tau$, the
voltage reaches
$u_C\(tau\)= E\(1 - e^(- 1)\)approx E\(1 - 0.368\)= 0.632 E$. The
capacitor is charged to #strong[$63.2 %$] of its capacity. At
$t = 3 tau$, it reaches $95 %$. At $t = 5 tau$, it reaches $99.3 %$
(effectively full charge).
]

==== 4.2 Terminal Velocity with Linear Drag
<terminal-velocity-with-linear-drag>
A skydiver of mass $m$ falls under the influence of gravity ($P = m g$,
downward) and linear aerodynamic air drag ($F = - k v$, upward).
Newton's second law ($m a = sum F$) gives:
$ m v'\(t\)= m g - k v\(t\)arrow.l.r.double v'\(t\)= - k / m v\(t\)+ g . $

This is of the form $y' = a y + b$ with $a = - k / m$ and $b = g$. The
steady-state equilibrium velocity is the #strong[terminal velocity]
$v_(upright("lim"))$:
$ v_(upright("lim")) = - b / a = - frac(g, - k\/m) = frac(m g, k) . $ If
the skydiver drops from rest ($v\(0\)= 0$), the velocity over time is:
$ v\(t\)= frac(m g, k) (1 - e^(- k / m t)) . $ The skydiver accelerates
initially at $g$, but as speed rises, drag increases until it balances
weight, causing velocity to level off at $v_(upright("lim"))$.

