=== The Question that Opens What Follows
<the-question-that-opens-what-follows-18>
Until now, we have described movement and physical change along a single
line (time $t$) or across a two-dimensional flat plane (a sheet of paper
with coordinates $x$ and $y$). Yet our physical universe is not flat:
the Earth floats in three-dimensional space, medieval cathedrals loft
their vaults into the sky, satellites traverse inclined orbital planes,
and aircraft navigate using latitude, longitude, and altitude.

How do we pinpoint a position, trace a line, and establish the algebraic
equation of a tilted plane in three-dimensional space? How do we
calculate the shortest distance from an airborne drone to the wall of a
cathedral?

Join us in Chapter 52 with #strong[The Cathedral in Space] and vector
geometry in three dimensions.

