=== 1. The Problem: The Builders of Chartres Cathedral
<the-problem-the-builders-of-chartres-cathedral>
==== 1.1 Leaving the Flat Parchment
<leaving-the-flat-parchment>
In the Middle Ages, the architects and stonecutters who designed Gothic
cathedrals---such as Chartres or Notre-Dame de Paris---faced a
monumental challenge. On their parchments and on the plaster floor of
the tracing room, they could only draw figures in #strong[two
dimensions]: circles, triangles, rectangles. But the cathedral is a
#strong[three-dimensional] edifice:

- The ground plan provides the length of the nave and the width of the
  transept;
- But the pillars soar toward the sky, the flying buttresses span empty
  air diagonally, and the ribs of the ribbed vaults cross at a
  #strong[keystone suspended 37 meters above the floor].

If two flying buttresses cut in the quarry do not meet at the exact same
point in space, the vault fails and hundreds of tons of stone come
crashing down. In the plane, two non-parallel lines #strong[necessarily
intersect]. In space, however, two lines can be non-parallel and yet
#strong[never intersect]: picture two airplanes flying in different
directions at different altitudes, or two cables crossing in a timber
frame without touching. Such lines are said to be #strong[skew
(non-coplanar)].

#figure(image("figures/vol_6/fig_52_vecteur_espace_3d.svg", alt: "Coordonnées et norme d’un vecteur dans l’espace à 3 dimensions"),
  caption: [
    Coordonnées et norme d'un vecteur dans l'espace à 3 dimensions
  ]
)

To build toward the heavens without risk of error, we need an infallible
system to locate every grain of stone throughout the volume of
three-dimensional space.

