=== 2. The 3D Coordinate System and Coordinates
<the-3d-coordinate-system-and-coordinates>
==== 2.1 The Orthonormal Frame $\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\,arrow(k)\)$
<the-orthonormal-frame-o-veci-vecj-veck>
To determine the position of a point in space, we choose an origin
$O\(0 thin\;0 thin\;0\)$ and three mutually perpendicular axes, each
equipped with a unit vector of equal length:

- The $\(O x\)$ axis directed by $arrow(i)$: the #strong[x-axis] or axis
  of abscissas (often pointing forward);
- The $\(O y\)$ axis directed by $arrow(j)$: the #strong[y-axis] or axis
  of ordinates (pointing to the right);
- The $\(O z\)$ axis directed by $arrow(k)$: the #strong[z-axis] or axis
  of altitudes / cotes (pointing upward).

The triplet $\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\,arrow(k)\)$ constitutes an
#strong[orthonormal coordinate frame of space].

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Coordinates of a Point and a Vector:
]


- For any point $M$ in space, there exists a unique triplet of real
  numbers $\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$ such that:
  $ accent(O M, ⃗) = x arrow(i) + y arrow(j) + z arrow(k) . $ The point
  $M$ has coordinates $M\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$. $x$ is the abscissa, $y$
  the ordinate, and $z$ the altitude (z-coordinate).

- If $A\(x_A thin\;y_A thin\;z_A\)$ and $B\(x_B thin\;y_B thin\;z_B\)$
  are two points, the vector $accent(A B, ⃗)$ has coordinates:
  $ accent(A B, ⃗) vec(x_B - x_A, y_B - y_A, z_B - z_A) . $

- The midpoint $I$ of segment $\[A B\]$ has coordinates:
  $ I (frac(x_A + x_B, 2) thick \; thick frac(y_A + y_B, 2) thick \; thick frac(z_A + z_B, 2)) . $
]

==== 2.2 Norm and Distance: The Double Pythagorean Theorem
<norm-and-distance-the-double-pythagorean-theorem>
What is the distance between two points in space? To travel from point
$A\(x_A thin\;y_A thin\;z_A\)$ to point $B\(x_B thin\;y_B thin\;z_B\)$,
we can construct a rectangular cuboid (box) whose space diagonal is the
segment $\[A B\]$:

+ In the bottom horizontal plane, the right triangle yields by
  Pythagoras: $ A C^2 =\(x_B - x_A\)^2 +\(y_B - y_A\)^2 . $
+ In the vertical right triangle $A C B$, the height is
  $Delta z = z_B - z_A$, and the hypotenuse is the diagonal $\[A B\]$. A
  second application of Pythagoras gives:
  $ A B^2 = A C^2 + B C^2 =\(x_B - x_A\)^2 +\(y_B - y_A\)^2 +\(z_B - z_A\)^2 . $

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem --- Vector Norm and Distance Between Two Points:
]


- The norm (length) of a vector $arrow(u)\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$ in an
  orthonormal frame is:
  $ parallel arrow(u) parallel = sqrt(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) . $

- The distance between two points $A$ and $B$ is:
  $ A B = parallel accent(A B, ⃗) parallel = sqrt(\(x_B - x_A\)^2 +\(y_B - y_A\)^2 +\(z_B - z_A\)^2) . $
]

