=== 3. Lines in Space: Parametric Representation
<lines-in-space-parametric-representation>
==== 3.1 Why a Single Equation Does Not Suffice
<why-a-single-equation-does-not-suffice>
In the 2D plane, a line has a Cartesian equation of the form
$a x + b y + c = 0$. What does that same equation represent in 3D space?
If we write $2 x + 3 y - 6 = 0$ in space, the variable $z$ does not
appear: this means that $z$ can take #strong[any real value]! All points
above and below the line form an infinite vertical wall: #strong[it is a
plane, not a line]! In space, a single linear equation always defines a
surface (a plane). To define a line, one needs the intersection of two
planes, or even better: #strong[a time-dependent (parametric)
trajectory].

==== 3.2 Parametric Representation of a Line
<parametric-representation-of-a-line>
Let $A\(x_A thin\;y_A thin\;z_A\)$ be a point in space and
$arrow(u)\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)$ a non-zero vector. The line $cal(D)$
passing through $A$ with direction vector $arrow(u)$ is the set of
points $M\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$ such that the vectors $accent(A M, ⃗)$
and $arrow(u)$ are #strong[collinear]:
$ M in cal(D) arrow.l.r.double upright("there exists a real number ") t in bb(R) upright(" such that ") accent(A M, ⃗) = t arrow(u) . $

Projecting onto the three coordinate components:

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Parametric Representation of a Line:
]
 The line
$cal(D)$ passing through $A\(x_A thin\;y_A thin\;z_A\)$ and directed by
$arrow(u)\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)$ admits as a parametric representation
the system: $ {x = x_A + a t\
y = y_A + b t\
z = z_A + c t quad\(t in bb(R)\). $ The real number $t$ is called the
#strong[parameter].
]

#strong[Kinematic Interpretation:] Think of an aircraft taking off at
time $t = 0$ from point $A$, with constant velocity vector $arrow(u)$.
The parameter $t$ plays the role of time: at every second $t$, the
aircraft occupies a precise position along the line!

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 1 --- Line passing through two points.
]
 Let
$A\(1 thin\;- 2 thin\;3\)$ and $B\(4 thin\;2 thin\;- 1\)$. 1. Determine
a parametric representation of the line $\(A B\)$. 2. Does the point
$C\(7 thin\;6 thin\;- 5\)$ lie on the line $\(A B\)$? 3. Does the point
$D\(2 thin\;0 thin\;1\)$ lie on the line $\(A B\)$?

#emph[Solution:] 1. The direction vector is
$accent(A B, ⃗) vec(4 - 1, 2 -\(- 2\), - 1 - 3) = vec(3, 4, - 4)$. The
line $\(A B\)$ passing through $A$ is given by: $ {x = 1 + 3 t\
y = - 2 + 4 t\
z = 3 - 4 t quad\(t in bb(R)\). $ 2. For the point
$C\(7 thin\;6 thin\;- 5\)$:
$ {7 = 1 + 3 t arrow.r.double.long 3 t = 6 arrow.r.double.long t = 2\
6 = - 2 + 4 t arrow.r.double.long 4 t = 8 arrow.r.double.long t = 2\
- 5 = 3 - 4 t arrow.r.double.long - 4 t = - 8 arrow.r.double.long t = 2 $
The same value $t = 2$ satisfies all three equations simultaneously.
Therefore #strong[$C in\(A B\)$]. 3. For the point
$D\(2 thin\;0 thin\;1\)$:

- $2 = 1 + 3 t arrow.r.double.long t = 1 / 3$.
- Check with $y$: $- 2 + 4 (1 / 3) = - 2 + 4 / 3 = - 2 / 3 eq.not 0$.
  The equations are incompatible. Therefore #strong[$D in.not\(A B\)$].
]

==== 3.3 Relative Positions of Two Lines in Space
<relative-positions-of-two-lines-in-space>
Consider two lines $cal(D)_1$ directed by $arrow(u)_1$ and $cal(D)_2$
directed by $arrow(u)_2$.

