=== 5. Planes in Space: Normal Vector and Cartesian Equation
<planes-in-space-normal-vector-and-cartesian-equation>
==== 5.1 The Concept of a Normal Vector
<the-concept-of-a-normal-vector>
How can we orient a plane in space? Think of a tabletop: to orient the
surface of the table, the simplest and most direct method is to drive a
peg perpendicular to the surface. If we know the direction of the peg
and the position of one point on the table, the entire surface is
completely locked in space!

#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Normal Vector to a Plane:
]
 A non-zero vector
$arrow(n)$ is said to be #strong[normal] to a plane $cal(P)$ if it is
orthogonal to the direction of every line in the plane $cal(P)$. In
practice: $arrow(n)$ is normal to $cal(P)$ if and only if it is
orthogonal to #strong[two non-collinear direction vectors] lying in
plane $cal(P)$.
]

