n (normal vector)
Plane P
A \                 /
u/    \ v             /
    ```
  ]
]

==== 5.2 Cartesian Equation of a Plane
<cartesian-equation-of-a-plane>
Let $A\(x_A thin\;y_A thin\;z_A\)$ be a fixed point and
$arrow(n)\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)$ a non-zero vector. The plane $cal(P)$
passing through $A$ with normal vector $arrow(n)$ is the set of points
$M\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$ such that the vector $accent(A M, ⃗)$ is
orthogonal to $arrow(n)$:
$ M in cal(P) arrow.l.r.double accent(A M, ⃗) dot.op arrow(n) = 0 . $

Expanding using the coordinates
$accent(A M, ⃗) vec(x - x_A, y - y_A, z - z_A)$ and
$arrow(n) vec(a, b, c)$:
$ a\(x - x_A\)+ b\(y - y_A\)+ c\(z - z_A\)= 0 arrow.l.r.double a x + b y + c z -\(a x_A + b y_A + c z_A\)= 0 . $
Setting the constant $d = -\(a x_A + b y_A + c z_A\)$, we obtain the
fundamental theorem:

#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Theorem (Cartesian Equation of a Plane) ---
]


- Every plane $cal(P)$ in space admits a Cartesian equation of the form:
  $ a x + b y + c z + d = 0 $ where
  $\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)eq.not\(0 thin\;0 thin\;0\)$.

- Conversely, for any real numbers $a\,b\,c\,d$ with
  $\(a\,b\,c\)eq.not\(0\,0\,0\)$, the set of points
  $M\(x thin\;y thin\;z\)$ satisfying $a x + b y + c z + d = 0$ is a
  #strong[plane], a normal vector of which is:
  $ arrow(n) vec(a, b, c) . $
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 3 --- Equation of a plane given a point and a
normal vector.
]
 Determine the Cartesian equation of the plane $cal(P)$
passing through $A\(2 thin\;- 1 thin\;3\)$ with normal vector
$arrow(n)\(3 thin\;4 thin\;- 2\)$.

- The equation has the form $3 x + 4 y - 2 z + d = 0$.

- Point $A\(2 thin\;- 1 thin\;3\)$ belongs to the plane, so its
  coordinates satisfy the equation:
  $ 3\(2\)+ 4\(- 1\)- 2\(3\)+ d = 0 arrow.l.r.double 6 - 4 - 6 + d = 0 arrow.l.r.double - 4 + d = 0 arrow.l.r.double d = 4 . $

- The Cartesian equation of $cal(P)$ is therefore:
  $ 3 x + 4 y - 2 z + 4 = 0 . $
]

#exemple_resolu(title: [Exemple Résolu Pas à Pas])[
#strong[Worked Example 4 --- Equation of a plane passing through three
non-collinear points.
]
 Determine a Cartesian equation of the plane
passing through $A\(1 thin\;0 thin\;0\)$, $B\(0 thin\;2 thin\;0\)$, and
$C\(0 thin\;0 thin\;3\)$.

- Form two vectors in the plane: $accent(A B, ⃗) vec(- 1, 2, 0)$ and
  $accent(A C, ⃗) vec(- 1, 0, 3)$. These vectors are clearly not
  collinear.

- Seek a normal vector $arrow(n)\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)$ orthogonal to
  both $accent(A B, ⃗)$ and $accent(A C, ⃗)$:
  $ {arrow(n) dot.op accent(A B, ⃗) = 0 arrow.l.r.double - a + 2 b = 0 arrow.l.r.double a = 2 b\
  arrow(n) dot.op accent(A C, ⃗) = 0 arrow.l.r.double - a + 3 c = 0 arrow.l.r.double a = 3 c $

- Choose a convenient non-zero value to set the scale: let $a = 6$. Then
  $b = 3$ and $c = 2$. The vector $arrow(n)\(6 thin\;3 thin\;2\)$ is a
  normal vector!

- The equation takes the form $6 x + 3 y + 2 z + d = 0$. Substitute
  $A\(1 thin\;0 thin\;0\)$:
  $6\(1\)+ 0 + 0 + d = 0 arrow.l.r.double d = - 6$.

- Cartesian equation: $6 x + 3 y + 2 z - 6 = 0$ (or
  $x / 1 + y / 2 + z / 3 = 1$).
]

