=== Key Takeaways
<key-takeaways-27>
==== 1. Coordinates and Distance in Space
<coordinates-and-distance-in-space>
- Orthonormal frame $\(O\;arrow(i)\,arrow(j)\,arrow(k)\)$.
- Vector: $accent(A B, ⃗) vec(x_B - x_A, y_B - y_A, z_B - z_A)$.
  Midpoint:
  $I (frac(x_A + x_B, 2) thin \; frac(y_A + y_B, 2) thin \; frac(z_A + z_B, 2))$.
- Norm and distance:
  $parallel arrow(u) parallel = sqrt(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)$ and
  $A B = sqrt(\(x_B - x_A\)^2 +\(y_B - y_A\)^2 +\(z_B - z_A\)^2)$.

==== 2. Lines in Space
<lines-in-space>
- Parametric representation of the line passing through
  $A\(x_A\,y_A\,z_A\)$ with direction vector $arrow(u)\(a\,b\,c\)$:
  $ {x = x_A + a t\
  y = y_A + b t\
  z = z_A + c t quad\(t in bb(R)\). $

- Two lines can be: parallel (collinear direction vectors), intersecting
  (a single common point), or #strong[skew / non-coplanar] (neither
  parallel nor intersecting).

==== 3. Dot Product and Orthogonality
<dot-product-and-orthogonality>
- $arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = x x' + y y' + z z'$.
- $arrow(u) perp arrow(v) arrow.l.r.double arrow(u) dot.op arrow(v) = 0$.

==== 4. Planes in Space
<planes-in-space>
- Every plane is determined by a point and a #strong[normal vector]
  $arrow(n)\(a thin\;b thin\;c\)$.
- Cartesian equation: $a x + b y + c z + d = 0$.
- A line $cal(D)$ is perpendicular to a plane $cal(P) arrow.l.r.double$
  its direction vector is collinear with the normal vector of $cal(P)$.
- Two planes are perpendicular $arrow.l.r.double$ their normal vectors
  are orthogonal ($arrow(n)_1 dot.op arrow(n)_2 = 0$).

==== 5. Orthogonal Projection and Distance
<orthogonal-projection-and-distance>
- The orthogonal projection $H$ of $M$ onto $cal(P)$ is the intersection
  of the plane $cal(P)$ with the perpendicular line passing through $M$.
- Distance formula:
  $d\(M\,cal(P)\)= frac(\|a x_M + b y_M + c z_M + d\|, sqrt(a^2 + b^2 + c^2))$.

