=== What You Are About to Discover
<what-you-are-about-to-discover-48>
- How to count without ever enumerating millions of objects one by one,
  using two foundational laws: the #strong[addition principle] and the
  #strong[multiplication principle].
- The crucial distinction governing all combinatorics: #strong[does
  order matter?] and #strong[can elements be repeated?]
- #strong[$k$-tuples], #strong[permutations], and #strong[arrangements
  (partial permutations)], articulated around the spectacular factorial
  function $n !$.
- #strong[Combinations] $binom(n, k)$ (“$n$ choose $k$”),
  #strong[Pascal's triangle], and their properties of symmetry and
  recurrence.
- The power of #strong[Newton's binomial theorem], expanding instantly
  any power of a binomial sum $\(a + b\)^n$.
- Classic overcounting pitfalls and the strategy of taking the
  #strong[complementary event].

