==== 6.2 Fatal Pitfalls
<fatal-pitfalls>
#alerte(title: [Attention au Piège !])[
#strong[⚠️ Warning, Trap! --- Overcounting via False Decomposition
]

#emph[Example:] In a class of 10 boys and 12 girls, we wish to form a
group of 4 students containing #strong[at least one girl].

- #emph[Tempting False Reasoning:] “I choose 1 girl among 12 (12 ways),
  then choose the remaining 3 students from the 21 remaining students
  ($binom(21, 3)$ ways). Total: $12 times binom(21, 3)$\.” #strong[This
  is wrong!] You have overcounted groups that contain more than one
  girl. For example, selecting Clara as the designated girl, then Sophie
  among the other 3, produces the exact same group as selecting Sophie
  first and Clara among the other 3.

- #emph[The Correct Method (Complementary Event):] Count all possible
  groups of 4 without restriction: $binom(22, 4)$. Subtract groups
  containing #strong[no girls at all] (i.e., 4 boys): $binom(10, 4)$.
  Exact count:
  $binom(22, 4) - binom(10, 4) = 7 thin 315 - 210 = 7 thin 105$.
]

#quote_box[
#strong[🔍 True History --- The Dice of Chevalier de Méré (1654)
]

Antoine Gombaud, known as the Chevalier de Méré---a nobleman and avid
gambler---brought a puzzle to Blaise Pascal: #emph[“Rolling a fair
6-sided die 4 times, I have better than an even chance of getting at
least one 6. But rolling a pair of dice 24 times, I seem to have less
than an even chance of getting a double 6. Yet $4\/6 = 24\/36$! Can
mathematics be inconsistent?”] Pascal wrote to Pierre de Fermat. Through
their historic correspondence, they analyzed the problem using the
complement: 1. With 1 die in 4 rolls: probability of no 6s =
$\(5\/6\)^4 approx 0.482$. Hence the probability of at least one 6 =
$1 - 0.482 = 0.518 > 1\/2$. (Favorable bet!) 2. With 2 dice in 24 rolls:
probability of no double 6s = $\(35\/36\)^24 approx 0.509$. Hence at
least one double 6 = $1 - 0.509 = 0.491 < 1\/2$. (Unfavorable bet!) The
Chevalier was losing his money because of a naive ratio. Modern
probability and combinatorics were born!
]

