=== The Question That Opens the Next Step
<the-question-that-opens-the-next-step-1>
We have learned to count the number of ways to choose $k$ successes out
of $n$ trials: that is the binomial coefficient $binom(n, k)$. What
happens when each individual trial is governed by chance, with a
probability $p$ of success and $1 - p$ of failure? If we toss a coin a
thousand times, how often will "Heads" turn up? Can we be certain that
the observed frequency will stabilize around $50 %$? This is the
breathtaking leap from discrete combinatorics to the binomial
distribution and the great limit theorems: welcome to #strong[Chapter 54
and The Thousand Coin Tosses].

