=== The Question That Opens the Next Step
<the-question-that-opens-the-next-step-2>
We have seen how statistical laws govern the empirical reality of coin
tosses and sample surveys. What happens when algebra itself hits an
impenetrable wall? Recall the equation $x^2 = - 1$: no real number, no
tally of pebbles, no probability can possess a negative square. Yet in
16th-century Italy, while seeking formulas to solve ordinary cubic
equations with obvious integer roots, algebraists were compelled to
imagine a ghostly phantom number. A number capable of pivoting the
coordinate plane by a ninety-degree right angle. Welcome to the
fascinating world of complex numbers: turn the page to discover
#strong[Chapter 55 and The Right-Angled Whirlwind].

