=== 2. The Geometric Breakthrough of Argand and Gauss
<the-geometric-breakthrough-of-argand-and-gauss>
==== 2.1 Multiplying by $i$ Means Rotating by 90°
<multiplying-by-i-means-rotating-by-90>
In 1806, a Swiss accountant and bookseller living in Paris,
#strong[Jean-Robert Argand], dispelled the metaphysical fog with
illuminating clarity.

Recall the real number line:

- Start at $+ 1$.
- Multiplying by $- 1$ brings us to $- 1$. Geometrically, this
  corresponds to a #strong[half-turn ($180^compose$ or $pi$ radians)]
  around the origin.

$ times\(- 1\)arrow.l.r.double upright("Rotation by ") 180^compose $

By definition of the imaginary unit: $ i times i = - 1 $ This algebraic
identity states: #emph[“Applying multiplication by $i$ twice in
succession produces the exact same geometric outcome as a $180^compose$
half-turn!”] What elementary geometric transformation, when applied
twice consecutively, produces a half-turn? #strong[A rotation by a
quarter-turn ($90^compose$ or $pi / 2$ radians) in the counterclockwise
(positive) direction!]

#align(center)[
  #block(
    fill: rgb("#f8fafc"),
    stroke: 1pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
    inset: (x: 16pt, y: 10pt),
    radius: 6pt,
    breakable: false,
  )[
    #set text(font: "Liberation Mono", size: 9.5pt, fill: rgb("#0f172a"))
    ```text
Imaginary Axis (North)
i  (after 1 quarter-turn of 90°)
Real Axis (East)
1 (after 2 quarters)     0                           +1  (start)
180° half-turn
i  (after 3 quarters: 270°)
    ```
  ]
]

Observe the cyclic progression of powers of $i$:

- $i^0 = 1$ (East)
- $i^1 = i$ (North, quarter-turn)
- $i^2 = - 1$ (West, half-turn)
- $i^3 = i^2 times i = - i$ (South, three-quarter turn)
- $i^4 =\(i^2\)^2 =\(- 1\)^2 = 1$ (East, full turn)

The number $i$ is not fictitious: #strong[$i$ is the gateway allowing
numbers to break free from the line and inhabit the entire
two-dimensional plane!]

