=== 1. The Problem: Sending a Secret in Plain Sight
<the-problem-sending-a-secret-in-plain-sight>
==== 1.1 Caesar's Courier and the Vulnerability of the Shared Key
<caesars-courier-and-the-vulnerability-of-the-shared-key>
Around 50 BCE, Julius Caesar sent military orders to his legions in
Gaul. To prevent an intercepted courier from revealing his plans, he
shifted each letter by 3 positions in the alphabet:

- A becomes D, B becomes E… X becomes A, Y becomes B, Z becomes C.

The recipient performs the inverse shift (-3).

This procedure has a glaring flaw: anyone who knows the rule can
decipher all messages. Even when complicating the formula (affine
cipher), frequency analysis of letters (with 'E' being the most common
letter in French and English) breaks the code within minutes.

Yet the fundamental problem of classical cryptography is even more
profound: #strong[the key exchange problem]. If Alice and Bob want to
exchange secrets, they must first agree on a shared secret key. But how
can they agree on this key without an eavesdropper sitting between them
intercepting it?

==== 1.2 The Asymmetric Revolution (1977)
<the-asymmetric-revolution-1977>
For two thousand years, monarchs and diplomats wrestled with this
paradox. Until 1977, when three researchers at MIT --- #strong[Ron
Rivest, Adi Shamir, and Leonard Adleman] --- pulled off a masterstroke:
inventing a padlock with two distinct keys: 1. A #strong[public key],
which anyone can inspect and use to lock the padlock (encrypt the
message). 2. A #strong[private key], possessed solely by the recipient,
indispensable for opening the padlock (decrypting the message).

The foundation of this unbreakable padlock relies on no mechanical
apparatus: it rests on a theorem of arithmetic discovered by a Toulouse
magistrate in #strong[1640], Pierre de Fermat, and on the theory of
#strong[congruences] developed by Carl Friedrich Gauss.

