=== 4. Graphs and Adjacency Matrices
<graphs-and-adjacency-matrices>
==== 4.1 Graph Terminology
<graph-terminology>
A #strong[graph] $G =\(V\,E\)$ consists of:

- A set of #strong[vertices] (or nodes) denoted ${ 1\,2\,dots.h\,n }$.
- A set of #strong[edges] (bidirectional links in an undirected graph)
  or #strong[arcs] (directed arrows from one vertex to another in a
  directed graph).
- The #strong[degree] of a vertex is the number of edges connected to
  it.
- A #strong[walk] (or path) of length $k$ is a sequence of $k$
  consecutive edges connecting an initial vertex to a destination
  vertex.

==== 4.2 The Adjacency Matrix
<the-adjacency-matrix>
#principe(title: [Définition Fondamentale])[
#strong[📐 Definition --- Adjacency Matrix
]
 Let $G$ be a graph of order
$n$ whose vertices are labeled from 1 to $n$. The #strong[adjacency
matrix] of $G$ is the square matrix $M in cal(M)_n\(bb(R)\)$ whose entry
in row $i$ and column $j$ is:
$ m_(i\,j) = upright("number of edges (or arcs) going from vertex ") i upright(" to vertex ") j $
#emph[\(In a simple undirected graph, $m_(i\,j) in { 0\,1 }$ and $M$ is
symmetric: $m_(i\,j) = m_(j\,i)$).]
]

