==== 4.3 The Fundamental Theorem of Paths of Length $k$
<the-fundamental-theorem-of-paths-of-length-k>
#quote_box[
#strong[📐 Fundamental Theorem --- Powers of the Adjacency Matrix
]
 Let
$M$ be the adjacency matrix of a graph with $n$ vertices. For every
positive integer $k gt.eq 1$, the entry in the $i$-th row and $j$-th
column of the matrix #strong[$M^k$] equals the #strong[number of paths
of length $k$ from vertex $i$ to vertex $j$].
]

#quote_box[
#strong[✍️ Required Proof by Induction
]
 Let $c_(i\,j)^(\(k\))$ denote
the $\(i\,j\)$ entry of the matrix $M^k$, and $N_(i\,j)^(\(k\))$ denote
the number of paths of length $k$ from $i$ to $j$. Let us prove by
mathematical induction on $k gt.eq 1$ the property
$cal(P)\(k\): upright("“For all ") i\,j\,med c_(i\,j)^(\(k\)) = N_(i\,j)^(\(k\)) upright("”")$.

- #strong[Base case ($k = 1$):] $M^1 = M$. By definition of the
  adjacency matrix, $c_(i\,j)^(\(1\)) = m_(i\,j)$ is exactly the number
  of direct edges from $i$ to $j$, which is the number of paths of
  length 1. Thus $cal(P)\(1\)$ holds.

- #strong[Inductive step:] Assume the property holds for some
  $k gt.eq 1$: for all vertices $i$ and $m$,
  $c_(i\,m)^(\(k\)) = N_(i\,m)^(\(k\))$. Consider a path of length
  $k + 1$ from $i$ to $j$. Such a path consists of a path of length $k$
  from $i$ to some intermediate vertex $m$, followed by a direct edge
  from $m$ to $j$ (of length 1). By the addition and multiplication
  counting principles (Chapter 53), the total number of such paths is
  the sum over all possible intermediate vertices
  $m in { 1\,dots.h\,n }$:
  $ N_(i\,j)^(\(k + 1\)) = sum_(m = 1)^n N_(i\,m)^(\(k\)) times m_(m\,j) $
  By the induction hypothesis, $N_(i\,m)^(\(k\)) = c_(i\,m)^(\(k\))$.
  Hence:
  $ N_(i\,j)^(\(k + 1\)) = sum_(m = 1)^n c_(i\,m)^(\(k\)) dot.op m_(m\,j) $
  This sum is precisely the formula for the matrix product of row $i$ of
  $M^k$ with column $j$ of $M$! It is exactly the entry $\(i\,j\)$ of
  the matrix $M^k times M = M^(k + 1)$. Therefore
  $N_(i\,j)^(\(k + 1\)) = c_(i\,j)^(\(k + 1\))$, proving the inductive
  step.

- #strong[Conclusion:] By induction, the property holds for all
  $k gt.eq 1$.
]

