=== 6. Google's PageRank: A Giant Matrix Network
<googles-pagerank-a-giant-matrix-network>
In 1998, two Stanford doctoral students, #strong[Larry Page and Sergey
Brin], founded the search engine #strong[Google]. Their stroke of genius
was dubbed #strong[PageRank]. The World Wide Web is modeled as a massive
directed graph where each web page is a vertex and each hyperlink is an
arc. A virtual surfer traverses the Web at random:

- From a page with $k$ outgoing links, the surfer chooses one with
  probability $1 / k$.
- With a small damping probability $alpha approx 0.15$, the surfer gets
  bored and jumps to a completely random page anywhere on the Web.

This random walk is a Markov chain on a matrix measuring billions by
billions! The steady state of this matrix assigns an occupancy
probability to each webpage: #strong[the most important pages are those
whose component in the steady state is the largest]. A simple equation
$P = P T$ laid the foundation for the most powerful technological empire
of the 21st century!

