- almost sure convergence, martingales. tensor calculus of Relativity.
]

+ #strong[Abstract Linear Algebra:] In high school, you manipulated
  vectors with 2 or 3 coordinates. In higher education, you will explore
  general #strong[vector spaces]. Functions, sequences, polynomials, and
  audio signals are all vectors! One can add two functions, multiply
  them by scalars, and compute their dimension (which can be infinite,
  as in the Hilbert spaces of quantum mechanics).

+ #strong[General Topology:] What does continuity mean when there is no
  longer a coordinate grid or a real number line? Topology defines
  proximity, compactness, and connectedness using collections of subsets
  called “open sets.” It is the study of properties preserved under
  continuous stretching and bending without tearing.

+ #strong[Complex Analysis (Holomorphic Functions):] What happens when
  we differentiate a function not of a real variable, but of a complex
  variable $z in bb(C)$? An absolute wonder occurs: if a complex
  function is differentiable just once, it is automatically infinitely
  differentiable and can be expanded as a power series! This is the
  magic of Augustin-Louis Cauchy's contour integrals and the residue
  theorem.

+ #strong[Measure Theory and Lebesgue Integration:] The Riemann integral
  has limits: functions oscillating too erratically cannot be
  integrated. At the dawn of the 20th century, Henri Lebesgue rethought
  integration by partitioning the $y$-axis rather than the $x$-axis.
  This integral underpins the modern theory of continuous probability
  formalized by Andrey Kolmogorov.

+ #strong[Foundational Logic and Gödel's Incompleteness (1931):] Can
  everything in mathematics be proven? In 1931, at age 25, Kurt Gödel
  published his landmark #strong[Incompleteness Theorems]: in any
  consistent formal axiomatic system capable of arithmetic, there exist
  true statements that the system cannot prove using its own rules.
  Mathematics is not a locked room: it is an infinite, inexhaustible
  territory forever open to discovery.

